\id PSA Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Psalms
\toc1 The Book of Psalms.
\toc2 Psalms
\toc3 PSA
\mt1 The Book of Psalms.
\is Concerning interpretation of holy Scriptures.
\ip As Prophecy \it (or other holy Scripture)\it* was not at any time by man's will, but the \it (Prophets)\it* holy men of God spake , inspired by the Holy Ghost: so no prophecy \it (nor explication)\it* of Scripture, is \f - \ft Scriptures are to be expounded by the common spirit of the Church, not by private men.\f* made by private interpretation. \it 2. Pet. 1.\it* but by the same Spirit wherewith it was written, which our Saviour gave to the Church, to abide for ever, the Spirit of truth, to teach all truth. \it Ioan. 14. 16. Neither\it* pertaineth it to every one, to discern the true spirit, but to some. \it 1. Cor. 12.\it*
\ip Holy Scriptures consist not in reading, but in understanding. \it S. Jerome Dialogo advers. Luciferianos.\it* The words of holy Scripture are so to be understood , as holy men, the Saints of God, have \f - \ft They consist in understanding.\f* understood them , \it S. Aug. Ser. 18. de verb. Domini.\it* Men must learn of men, not expect \f - \ft Holy Fathers do best expound them.\f* knowledge immediately of God, nor only by Angels. \it Idem, in Prologo Doct. Christ.\it*
\ip There be some things, mentioned in holy Scripture , which God will have hidden, and those are \f - \ft Some Mysteries are hidden.\f* not to be curiously searched. \it S. Amb. li. 1. c. 7. de vocat. Gentium.\it*
\ip By those things, which to us are hidden in holy Scripture, our humility is proved . \it S. Greg. ho. 17.\it* \f - \ft They prove our humility.\f* \it super Ezech.\it*
\is PROEMIAL ANNOTATIONS UPON THE BOOK OF PSALMS.
\ip The authority of this Book was ever authentical, and certain, as the assured word of God, and Canonical Scripture . But concerning the Author, there be diverse opinions. For although it be \f - \ft This book undoubtedly is canonical Scripture.\f* manifest by the testimony of Philo and Josephus, that in their time, and always before, only King David was by all Hebrew Doctors holden for Author of all the Psalms; yet after that learned \f - \ft Late Hebrew Doctors and some Catholics hold diverse Authors of sundry Psalms.\f* Origen, and other Christian Doctors, expounded many Psalms of Christ, the Jews being pressed therewith, began to deny that all were David's: alleging for their new opinion the title of diverse Psalms, and some other difficulties, ministering occasion of much needless dispute, still acknowledging the whole book to be Canonical. Whereupon S. Jerome and S. Augustine , \f - \ft \+it Prefat. in Psal. Epist. 134. 139.\+it*\f* sometimes admitted those as Authors of certain Psalms, whose names are in the title thereof. S. Cyprian, S. Cyril, S. Athanasius, and others agree in general, that David writ not all: but differ much in particular, touching other supposed authors. In so much that \it Melchisedech, Moyses, Asaph, Eman, Idithun, The sons of Core, Salomon, Jeremie, Ezechiel, Aggaeus, and Zacharias, are all\it* (with more or less probability) \it reputed Authors of several Psalms\it*. Nevertheless it seemeth that S. \f - \ft It is much more probable that David was Author of all.\f* Jerome rather related other men's judgement, than shewed his own; as we shall note by and by. And S. Augustine \it (l. 17. c. 14. de civit.)\it* maturely discussing this doubt, saith plainly, that \it their Judgement seemed to him more credible, who attribute all the hundred fifty Psalms to David alone\it*. Further explicating that whereas some Psalms have David's own name in their titles, some \f - \ft Proved by S. Augustin, S. Chrysostome and greatest part of Doctors.\f* have other men's names, some none at all, \it this diversity importeth not diverse Authors, but signifieth other things, either pertaining to the same persons, or by interpretation of their names, belonging to the present matter, as our Lord inspired him\it*. Likewise S. Chrysostome resolutely judgeth, that only King David was Author of this whole Book. Moved especially by this argument, for that Christ and the Apostles alleging the Psalms, do oftentimes name David as Author, and never any other . \f - \ft \+it Matt. 22. v. 16. Act. 4. v. 24. Ro. 4. v. 6. Ro. 11. v. 9. Heb. 4. v. 7.\+it*\f* Also Origen, S. Basil, Saint Ambrose, S. Hilary, Theodoretus, Cassiodorus, Beda, Eutimius, and most part of ancient and late writers, with the most common voice of Christians , call this book \f - \ft The common voice of Christians & some general Councels, call it David's Psalter.\f* the Psalms of David: and the General Councels of Carthage, Florence, and Trent, in the Catalogue of Canonical Scriptures recite this book by the name of David's Psalter.
\ip Moreover it is clear, \it Act. 2.\it* that the second Psalm, though it want his name, is David's. And other Scriptures , \it 2. Paral. v. 6.\it* and \it 1. Esd. 3. v. 10.\it* say plainly, that David made the Psalms, \it 104. 105.\it* \f - \ft Proved by other Scriptures.\f* \it 106. 117. 135.\it* beginning: \it Confess to our Lord, because he is good, because his mercy is for ever\it*. Which he appointed the Levites to sing, or play on instruments, \it 1. Paral. 15. & 16.\it* and yet they have not his name in their title. Again, \it 2. Reg. 23. David\it* is only entitled \it the egregious\it*, or excellent \it Psalmist of Israel\it*. Neither were Asaph, Eman, and Idithun any where called Prophets (as are all the writers of holy Scriptures) but only masters of music, \it 1. Paral. 25.\it* And the sons of Core were only porters, \it 1. Paral. 26.\it* Finally, S. Jerome (whose judgement the whole Church singularly esteemeth in all questions belonging to holy Scriptures (seemeth as much inclined, that only the Royal Prophet David was Author of this whole book, as to the contrary . For in his Epistle to Paulinus, \f - \ft S. Jerome attributeth the sum of this book to David only. key of each Psalm.\f* prefixed before the Latin Bible, comprehending the principal arguments of several books, when he cometh to the Psalms, without mention of other Authors, saith: \it David our Simonides, Pindarus, and Alceus, Flaccus also, Catullus, and Cerenus, soundeth out Christ, with harp & ten stringed Psalter, rising up from hell:\it* so attributing the sum of this whole book to the Royal Prophet David, as if he supposed no other Author.
\ip Touching therefore the argument or contents of this divine Psalter, all Catholic Doctors \f - \ft The Psalms are a sum of all other Scriptures.\f* uniformly agree that it is the abridgement, sum, and substance of all holy Scriptures, both old and new Testament. As may first be probably collected, by that Christ himself often \f - \ft \+it Mat. 5. 7. 11. 22. Luc. 16.\+it*\f* comprehending all the old Testament by the terms of the Law and Prophets, in one place \it (Luc. 24.\it*
\ip \it v. 44.)\it* seemeth not only to reduce all to \it the Law and Prophets jointly\it*, but also to \it the Psalms alone\it*, or severally. But whether this be our Saviour's divine meaning or no in that place, out of this and many other places, all the ancient Fathers teach expressly, that the Psalms are an Epitome of all other holy Scriptures. For examples, S. Denys. \it li. de Eccles. Hierar. contemplat. 2.\it* after brief recital of the contents of other holy Scriptures, saith: \it This sacred book of divine Canticles, doth exhibit both a general song, and exposition of divine things\it*. S. Basil calleth the \it Psalmody of David the common and most plentiful store-house of all sacred doctrine, the treasure of perfect Theology\it*. S. Ambrose accounteth it \it The register of the whole Scripture\it*. Origen, S. Cyprian, S. Jerome, S. Chrysostome, \f - \ft \+it S. Greg. in Psal. penitent.\+it*\f* S. Gregory, S. Beda, S. Bernard, Cassiodorus, Eutimius, and others use the same, or very like terms. S. Augustine particularly distinguishing all the Scriptures into four sorts of books, sheweth that the Psalms contain all : \it The Law\it* (saith he) \it teacheth some things, the History some things, the\it* \f - \ft They contain the sum of Legal, Historical, Sapiential, and Prophetical doctrine.\f* \it Proverbs also and Prophets teach some things: but the Book of Psalms teacheth all. It proposeth the law, recounteth things of old, prescribeth the due ordering of men's actions, and prophesieth things to come. Briefly, it is a common treasure of good doctrine, aptly administering that is necessary to every one\it*. And a little after, exemplifying in particular points: \it Is not here\it* (saith he) \it all greatness of virtue, and is not here the right square of justice? Is not the comeliness of chastity, the consummation of prudence, is not whatsoever may be called good, learned in the Psalms? Here is the knowledge of God, the clear pronunciation of Christ to come in flesh, the hope of general Resurrection, fear of torments, promise of glory, revelation of mysteries. Even all good things are here, as in a common great treasure laid up and heaped together\it*.
\ip See then and observe here (Christian reader) the admirable wisdom and goodness of God . The \f - \ft God's providence in sweetly drawing our consent & cooperation of freewill, which is necessary to salvation.\f* means of man's salvation being so disposed, that his own free consent and cooperation is thereto necessarily required, according to that most approved doctrine of the same S. Augustine: \it Qui creavit te sine te, non iustificat te sine te . He that created thee without thee, doth not justify thee\it* \f - \ft \+it Ser. 15. de verb. Apost.\+it*\f* \it without thee\it*: to help our weakness, and sweetly to draw our minds, otherwise averse from travail and pain, the Holy Ghost hath ordained that in small room , and in pleasant manner, we may \f - \ft \+it S. Basil in prolog.\+it*\f* attain necessary knowledge of God, & ourselves, easily keep the same in memory, and daily put in practice our chiefest duty, in serving and praising God, by singing, reading, or hearing these divine Psalms. Which one book (as every one shall be able to learn it, more or less perfectly) openeth and sheweth the way to understand all other Scriptures, and so to find, & enjoy the hidden treasures of God's word, in like manner as a key openeth a lock. For the whole sacred \it Bible is a sealed Book\it*, \f - \ft Holy Scripture a sealed book.\f* and not rightly understood, till the seal , or lock be opened, by the key of God's spirit, giving \f - \ft \+it Apoc. 5.\+it*\f* knowledge; which the Holy Ghost, amongst other ways, inspireth very often by sacred Music or Psalmody. As S. Gregory noteth in holy Scripture \it (4. Reg. 3. v. 15.)\it* where Eliseus not yet \f - \ft \+it li. 4. dialogi. c. 42.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The Psalter is the key of other Scriptures.\f* knowing God's will in a particular case, called for a Psalmist (or player on instruments) and \it when the Psalmist sang, the hand of our Lord came upon Eliseus\it*, and presently he prescribing what should be done, procured plenty of water without rain, where was none before, and prophesied victory against the enemies. Reason also and experience teach, that as \it men of cheerful heart are apt to sing\it*, so the exercise of reading, singing, or playing Psalms, is a convenient and a special means, to \f - \ft \+it Iac. 5.\+it*\f* attain quietness or cheerfulness of mind.
\ip But as this holy Psalter is the key of other Scriptures, so itself is most especially a sealed, and locked Book , requiring many keys. \it Every Psalm\it* (saith S. Hilary) \it hath a peculiar key, and\it* \f - \ft But itself is also sealed.\f* \it oftentimes there be so many locks and keys of one Psalm, as there be diverse persons that speak, to diverse ends and purposes\it*. For albeit diverse mysteries are sometimes connected , and so require \f - \ft But one principal \+it merit.\+it*\f* sundry keys, yet there is but one principal & proper key of each Psalm: otherwise it should be divided into many Psalms. Our first endeavour therefore must be, to find the proper key of every Psalm, that is, to know what is principally therein contained. To this purpose the learned \f - \ft Ten keys of the Psalter.\f* Expositors of this book, have observed ten general points, or several matters, to which all the contents may be reduced, as it were, so many keys, and means of entrance into the sense, and true understanding of all the Psalms. And the same may likewise be called the ten strings of this divine instrument . Upon one of which, every Psalm principally playeth, touching the rest more or \f - \ft Also ten strings.\f* less, as cause requireth, for more melodious harmony and perfect music.
\ip The first key or string is God himself , One in Substance, Three in Persons, Almighty, \f - \ft 1. Key. One God the B. Trinity.\f* All-perfect, Power, Wisdom, Goodness, Majesty, Justice, Mercy, & other Divine Attributes. The second is God's works of Creation , Conservation, and Governing of the whole world. The \f - \ft 2. God's works.\f* third, God's Providence , especially towards man, in protecting and rewarding the just, in \f - \ft 3. God's providence.\f* permitting and punishing the wicked. The fourth, is the peculiar calling of the Hebrew people , \f - \ft 4. The Hebrew people.\f* their beginning in Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: their marvelous increase in Aegypt: diverse estates, many admirable and miraculous things done amongst them, with their ingratitude, rejection, and reprobation. The fifth principal key and string is Christ , the promised Redeemer of mankind: \f - \ft 5. Christ our Redeemer.\f* prophesying his Incarnation, Nativity, Travails, Sufferings, Death, Resurrection, Ascension, and Glory. The sixth is the propagation of Christ's name and Religion , with Sacrifice and \f - \ft 6. Conversion of Gentiles, the Catholic Church.\f* Sacraments, in the multitude of Gentiles believing in him, even to the uttermost coasts of the earth: the Catholic Church ever visible. The seventh is the true manner of serving God , with sincere \f - \ft 7. Faith & good works.\f* faith and good works. The eight, holy David interposeth many things concerning himself . As \f - \ft 8. David's own acts.\f* God's singular benefits towards him, for which he rendereth thanks and divine praises, recounteth his enemies, dangers, and afflictions of mind & body, namely by Saul, Absalom, and others, humbly beseeching and obtaining God's protection. He also expresseth in himself a perfect image and pattern of a sincere and hearty penitent: bewailing, confessing, and punishing his own sins. The ninth is the end and renovation of this world , with the general Resurrection, and Judgement. \f - \ft 9. General Resurrection, & judgement.\f* The tenth is eternal felicity, and punishment, according as every one deserveth in this life . \f - \ft 10. Eternal glory and pain.\f* These are the ten keys of this holy Book; and ten strings of this Divine Psalter.
\ip Moreover to find which of these is the proper key and principal string of every Psalm , learned \f - \ft Four ways to find the proper key of every psalm.\f* Divines use four especial ways. First , by the title added by Esdras, or the Seventy two \f - \ft 1. By the title.\f* Interpreters, for an introduction to the sense of the same Psalm. So it appeareth that the third Psalm treateth literally of David's danger, and delivery from his son Absalom: which is the eighth key: though mystically it signifieth Christ's Persecution, & Resurrection, which is the fifth key. Secondly , if there be no title, or if it declare not sufficiently they key, or principal matter \f - \ft 2. Allegation in the new Testament.\f* contained, it may sometimes be found by allegation and application of some special part thereof in the new Testament. So it is evident \it Act. 4. v. 25. c. 13. v. 33. Heb. 1. v. 5. & Heb. 5. v. 5.\it* that the second Psalm pertaineth to Christ, impugned and persecuted by diverse adversaries. Which is the fifth key. Thirdly , when greater things are affirmed of any person, or people, as of David, \f - \ft 3. Greatness of things affirmed.\f* Salomon, the Jewish nation, or the like, than can be verified of them, it must necessarily be understood of Christ, or his Church, in the new Testament or in Heaven. So the \it conclusion\it* of the \it 14. Psalm: He that doth these things, shall not be moved for ever\it*, cannot be verified of the tabernacle, nor temple of the Jews, but of eternal Beatitude in heaven. Which is the tenth key. Though the greater part of the Psalm sheweth, that just and true dealing towards our neighbours, is necessary for attaining of eternal Glory. Fourthly , when both the title and Psalm, or part thereof seem \f - \ft 4. Conference of places.\f* hard and obscure, some part being more clear, the true sense of all may be gathered by that which is more evident. According to S. Augustin's rule \it li. 2. c. 9. & li. 3. c. 26. Doct. Christ\it*. So the title \f - \ft \+it li. 3. c. 4. de pecca.\+it* more pleasant.\f* and former part of the fifth Psalm, being more obscure, are explained by the last verses, shewing plainly that God will justly judge all men, both just and wicked, in the end of this world. Which is the ninth key. By these and like means the principal key being found, it will more easily appear, what other keys belong to the same, and what other strings are also touched. At least the studious may by these helps make some entrance, and for more exact knowledge search the judgement of ancient Fathers and other learned Doctors.
\ip But besides this singular great commodity, of compendious handling much Divine matter in small room , this book hath another special excellency, in the kind of style, and manner of uttering, \f - \ft The style of this book is Poetry.\f* which is Meter, and Verse, in the original Hebrew tongue. And though in Greek, Latin, and other languages, the same could not in like form be exactly translated, yet the number, and distinction of verses is so observed, that it is apt for music, as well voices as instruments, and to all other uses of God's servants. Neither is musical manner of uttering God's word and praises less to be esteemed , because profane Poets have in this kind of style uttered light, vain, and false things. For the \f - \ft Abuse derogateth not from good things.\f* abuse of good things, doth not derogate from the goodness thereof, but rather commendeth the same, which others desire to imitate. And clear it is, that this holy Psalmody was before any profane poetry now extant . For Homer, the most ancient of that sort, writ his poem at least \f - \ft David's Psalter more ancient than any profane poetry now extant.\f* two hundred and forty years after the destruction of Troy, as Apolidorus witnesseth; others, namely Solinus, Herodotus, and Cornelius Nepos say longer. Whereas King David our Divine Psalmist, reigned within one hundred years after the Trojans' wars. There were indeed Amphion, Orpheus, and Muscus before David, but their verses either were not written, or shortly perished, only a confuse memory remaining of them, recited, altered, and corrupted by word of mouth: but before them were the sacred History of Job, almost all in verse; and the two Canticles of Moyses, \it Exodi. 15.\it* and \it Deut. 32.\it* It is moreover recorded that Jubal (long before Noe's flood) was the \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4.\+it*\f* father of them , that sang on harp, and organ. Music therefore is marvelous ancient. But sacred \f - \ft Music very ancient.\f* Poetry is in many other respects most excellent, and most profitable. \it This holy Psalmody\it* (saith \f - \ft Sacred poetry most excellent.\f* S. Augustine ) \it is a medicine of old spiritual sores, it bringeth present remedy to new wounds: it\it* \f - \ft \+it Prefat.\+it*\f* \it maketh the good to persevere in well doing, it cureth at once all predominating passions, which vex men's souls\it*. A little after: \it Psalmody driveth away evil spirits, inviteth good Angels to help us, it is a shield in night terrors, a refreshing of day travails, a guard to children, an ornament to youngmen, a comfort to oldmen, a most seemly grace to women. Unto beginners it is an introduction, an augmentation to them that go forward in virtue, a stable firmament to the perfect: It conjoineth the whole Church militant in one voice, and is the spiritual eternal sweet perfume of the celestial Armies, all Saints and Angels in heaven.\it*
\ip To all this we may add other causes, which moved the Royal Prophet to write this divine poetry.
\ip First, he had from his youth (by God's special providence) a natural inclination to Music ; \f - \ft Why King David writ divine poetry\f* \f - \ft The first cause his natural inclination to music.\f* wherein he shortly so excelled, that before all the Musicians in Israel, he was selected to recreate King Saul, whom an evil spirit vexed. And his skill, together with his devotion, had such effect, that \it when he played on the harp , Saul was refreshed, and waxed better. For the evil spirit departed\it* \f - \ft \+it 1. Reg. 16. v. 23.\+it*\f* \it from him\it*, saith the holy text. Wherefore he made these Psalms, that himself and others might by singing them, employ this gift of God to his more honour. Secondly, verse being more easy to \f - \ft 2. Verse more easy &\f* learn, more firmly kept in mind, and more pleasant in Practice (for \it as wine, so music doth recreate the heart of man\it* ) the Holy Ghost condescending to man's natural disposition, inspired David \f - \ft \+it Eccli. 40.\+it*\f* to write these psalms in meter, \it mixing the power of divine doctrine, with delectable melody of song, that whiles the ear is allured with harmony of music, the heart is endued with heavenly knowledge, pleasant to the mind, and profitable to the soul.\it* Thirdly, David singularly illuminated with \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. S. Basil. in praefat.\+it*\f* \f - \ft 3. Most special, great, and memorable things writ in verse.\f* knowledge of great, and most divine Mysteries, endued also with most gracious disposition of mind, \it the man chosen according to God's own heart (1. Reg. 13.)\it* would utter the same Mysteries, with godly instructions, and praises of God, in the most exquisite kind of style; that is in verse. For otherwise he was also very eloquent in prose, as well appeareth by sundry his excellent, and effectual discourses, in the books of Kings, and Paralipomenon. For which cause Moyses also described the passage of Israel forth of Aegypt through the red sea in a Canticle , after that he \f - \ft \+it Exo. 15.\+it*\f* had related the same whole history more at large in prose, that all might sing, and so render thanks with melodious voice, and musical instruments praising God. Likewise in another Canticle he comprised the whole law, a little before his death . So also Barac and Debora, and after them \f - \ft \+it Deut. 32.\+it*\f* Judith , sung praises to God for their victories in verse. Salomon writ the end of his Proverbs, \f - \ft \+it Iudic. 5. Iudith. 16. Pro. 21. 2. Reg. 2. Isa. 38. Isa. 12. 26. Ezech. 38. Ioan. 2. Abac. 3. Dan. 3. Luc. 1. 2.\+it*\f* and a whole book (entitled Canticles) & the Prophet Jeremy his Lamentations in verse. Anna having obtained her prayer for a son, gave thanks to God with a Canticle. The like did King Ezechias for recovery of health. The Prophets Isaias, Ezechiel, Jonas, Abacuc, and the three children in the furnace: again in the new Testament , the B. Virgin-Mother, just Zachary, & \f - \ft Canticles in the new Testament.\f* devout Simeon gave thanks, & sang praises to God in Canticles.
\ip Fourthly, albeit the holy King was not permitted to build the gorgeous Temple for God's \f - \ft 4. Both divine music and ditty in God's temple.\f* service, as he greatly desired to have done, yet he provided both store of musicians (four thousand in number, of which 288. were masters to teach) & made these Psalms as godly ditties, for this \f - \ft \+it 1. Par. 23. 25.\+it*\f* holy purpose, in all solemnities of feasts, and daily sacrifice, when the Temple should afterward be built.
\ip Fifthly, he made these Psalms not only for his own, & other's private devotion, nor yet so \f - \ft 5. The great use of these Psalms in the Catholic Church.\f* especially for the public Divine service in the Temple, and other Synagogues of the Jews, but most principally for the Christian Catholic Church, which he knew should be spread in the whole earth. Foreseeing the marvelous, great, and frequent use thereof in the Christian Clergy, and Religious people of both sexes. As he prophesieth in diverse Psalms. \it All the earth sing to thee: sing Psalms to thy name.\it* Again , \it I will sing Psalms to thee\it* (o God) \it in the Gentiles, in all peoples and Nations\it*. \f - \ft \+it 56. 117. 65.\+it*\f* Which himself never did, but his Psalms are ever since Christ, sung by Christians, converted from gentility, as we see in the Church's Service. For the whole Psalter is distributed to be sung, in \f - \ft The whole Psalter in the ordinary office every week.\f* the ordinary office of our Breviary every week. And though extraordinarily, for the variety of times, and feasts, there is often alteration, yet still the greater part is in Psalms. Certain also of the same Psalms , are without change or intermission repeated every day . And such as have \f - \ft Certain Psalms every day.\f* \f - \ft 4. \+it (30. ad v. 7) 53. 62. 66. 90. 94. 118. 133. 148. 149. 150.\+it*\f* obligation to the Canonical Hours, must at least read the whole Office privately, if they be not present where it is sung. The Office also of Mass , ordinarily beginneth with a Psalm. In \f - \ft Many Psalms in other Ecclesiastical offices.\f* Litanies, and almost all public Prayers, and in administration of other Sacraments, and Sacramentals, either whole Psalms, or frequent verses are inserted. Likewise the greatest part of the Offices of our B. Lady, and for the dead, are Psalms. Besides the seven Penitential, and fifteen Gradual Psalms, at certain times. So that Clergy men's daily office consisteth much in singing, or reading Psalms. And therefore all Bishops especially, are strictly bound by a particular Canon \it (Dist. 38. cap. Omnes psallentes)\it* to be skillful in the Psalms of David: and to see that other Clergy \f - \ft Bishops bound to be skillful in David's Psalter. Other Priests to have competent knowledge therein.\f* men be well instructed therein. According to the Holy Ghost's admonition, by the pen of the same Royal Prophet \it (Psal. 46.) Psallite sapienter\it*, or, \it intelligenter\it*, that is: \it Sing Psalms with knowledge, and understanding them\it*. Not that every one is bound to know, and be able to discuss all difficulties, but competently, according to their charge undertaken in God's Church. Otherwise every one that is, or intendeth to a Priest, may remember what God denounceth to him, by the Prophet Osee \it (c. 4.) Because thou hat repelled knowledge, I will repel thee, that thou do not the function of Priesthood unto me.\it* Thus much touching the Author, the contents, the poetical style, & final cause of this holy Psalter.
\ip As for the name , S. Jerome, S. Augustine, and other Fathers teach, that whereas amongst \f - \ft Why this book is called the psalter.\f* innumerable musical instruments, six were more specially used in David's time, mentioned by him in the last Psalm, \it Trumpet, Psalter, Harp, Timbrel, Organ, and Cymbal\it*: This book hath his name of the instrument called Psalter, which hath ten strings, signifying the ten commandments, and is made in form (as S. Jerome, and S. Bede suppose) of the Greek letter Δ \it delta\it*, because as that instrument rendereth sound from above, so we should attend to heavenly virtues, which come from above: Likewise using the harp, which signifieth mortification of the flesh, & other instruments , which signify and teach other virtues, we must finally refer all to God's glory, \f - \ft Other instruments make consort with the Psalter.\f* rejoice spiritually in heart, and render all praise to God. \f - \ft All virtues are referred to God's honour.\f*
\c 1
\cd Christ's glory, the world repining thereat, 4. shall be propagated in all the world. 7. His divine power as well spiritual in converting men's hearts, as external, in severe justice, is prophesied. \f - \ft Of Christ, The 5. key. Also of his Church, The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Blessed \f a \fr 1.1 \ft He is in the right way to eternal felicity.\f*\f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft Mat. 5.\f* is the man, that hath \f b \fr 1.1 \ft not consented to evil suggestions.\f* not gone in the counsel of the impious, & hath \f c \fr 1.1 \ft not continued in sin.\f* not stood in the way of sinners, and hath \f d \fr 1.1 \ft not finally persisted in wicked life.\f* not sit in the chair of pestilence: \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq Hath not gone, not stood, not sit. \ft ¹ The Hebrew style and manner of discourse differeth here from other nations, in mentioning first the less evil, and the greatest last. Whereas we would say in the contrary order: He is happy that hath not \+it sit\+it*, that is, hath not settled himself in wickedness, nor finally persisted obstinate: more happy, that hath not \+it stood\+it*, any notable time continued in sin: and most happy, that hath not \+it gone\+it*, not given any consent at all to evil suggestions. \fq ¹ \ft They are happy (in hope) that decline from evil.\ef*
\v 2 But \f e \fr 1.2 \ft He is wholly occupied & delighted in keeping God's commandments.\f* his will is in the way of our Lord, and in his law he will meditate day and night. \x - \xt Ios. 1. 1. Hath not gone, not stood, not sit.]\x* \ef - \fr 1.2 \fq His will in the way of our Lord. \ft As one part of happiness consisteth in declining from evil: so the other is in doing good ¹; the will desiring, and diligently endeavouring to walk in the way of virtue, and law of God. Which is true justice, and right form of good life, proposed in this Psalm, for attaining eternal beatitude. \fq ¹ \ft Justice consisteth in flying evil and doing good.\ef*
\v 3 And he shall be as a tree, that is planted nigh to \f f \fr 1.3 \ft To him that useth God's grace well, more grace is continually given.\f* the streams of waters, which shall give his fruit in his time. \x - \xt Jere. 17.\x* And \f g \fr 1.3 \ft Through such grace he shall persevere.\f* his leaf shall not fall: and \f h \fr 1.3 \ft all things work to the good of them that love God sincerely.\f* all things whatsoever he shall do, shall prosper.
\v 4 The impious not so, not so: but \f i \fr 1.4 \ft The wicked are carried with every light tentation.\f* as dust, which the wind driveth from the face of the earth.
\v 5 Therefore the impious shall \f k \fr 1.5 \ft All rising at the last day, the wicked shall not rise with hope nor comfort, but in desolation.\f* not rise again in judgment: nor sinners in the \f l \fr 1.5 \ft the happy congregation of the blessed.\f* Council of the just.
\v 6 For our Lord \f m \fr 1.6 \ft approveth & rewardeth.\f* knoweth the way of the just, and the way of the impious \f n \fr 1.6 \ft in eternal damnation.\f* shall perish.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 Why did the \f a \fr 2.1 \ft Both gentiles\f* Gentiles rage, and \f b \fr 2.1 \ft and Jews strive in vain against Christ.\f* peoples meditate vain things? \x - \xt Act. 4.\x*
\v 2 The \f c \fr 2.2 \ft Pilate and Herod.\f* kings of the earth stood up, and the \f d \fr 2.2 \ft Annas and Caiphas.\f* Princes came together in one against our Lord, and against his Christ. \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq Kings and Princes against Christ. \ft When Christ, and his Apostles preached the Gospel ¹, both Jews and Gentiles, with their Princes, Kings, and Emperors most furiously resisted, but all in vain. For they could not hinder the will and power of God. But the more they persecuted, the more was increased the zeal and number of Christians. \fq ¹ \ft Persecution cannot hinder the glory of Christ.\ef*
\v 3 Let \f e \fr 2.3 \ft The voice of wicked men,\f* us break their bonds a-sunder: and let us cast away their \f f \fr 2.3 \ft & especially libertines striving to shake off all discipline.\f* yoke from us.
\v 4 He that dwelleth in the heavens shall \f g \fr 2.4 \ft God for all this will turn the hearts of many.\f* laugh at them: and our Lord shall scorn them.
\v 5 Then shall he speak to them in his \f h \fr 2.5 \ft severely reprehend,\f* wrath, & in his \f i \fr 2.5 \ft and justly punish the obstinate.\f* fury he shall trouble them.
\v 6 But \f k \fr 2.6 \ft Christ shall reign in his Kingdom the Church.\f* I am appointed king by him over Sion his holy hill, preaching his precept.
\v 7 The \f l \fr 2.7 \ft God the Father speaketh to\f* Lord said to me: Thou art my \f m \fr 2.7 \ft God the Son.\f* Son, I this day have begotten thee. \x - \xt Act. 13. Heb. 1. & 5.\x*
\v 8 Ask of me, and I will give \f n \fr 2.8 \ft Christ as man hath the Church for his inheritance.\f* thee the Gentiles, for thine inheritance, and thy possession \f o \fr 2.8 \ft Spread through the whole world.\f* the ends of the earth. \ef - \fr 2.8 \fq The gentiles thine inheritance. \ft By this promise of God to Christ ¹, S. Augustine convinced the Donatists ², & in them the Protestants, that say, the Church of Christ failed, and became small, or invisible; as though Christ the Son of God could sometimes lose his inheritance, which is the Catholic Church, gathered of the Gentiles, and \+it his possession\+it* extended \+it to the ends of the earth.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Church never faileth. \fq ² \ft \+it in hunc Psal.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 Thou shalt rule them in \f p \fr 2.9 \ft God's inflexible will & power.\f* a rod of iron, and as a potter's vessel thou shalt break them in pieces. \x - \xt Apoc. 3. & 19.\x* \ef - \fr 2.9 \fq As a potter's vessel. \ft If a potter's vessel (saith S. Jerome \+it in hunc Psal. tom. 8.\+it* ) be broken whiles it is soft, it may easily be repaired, but after it is hard ¹, it cannot be made whole again. So sinners are more easily restored to grace, shortly repenting, than long obdurate: yet that which is unpossible to man, is possible to God. \+it Mat. 10. For as clay in the potter's hand, so are you in my hand, saith our Lord, Jere. 18\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Custom in sin more hardly cured.\ef*
\v 10 And \f q \fr 2.10 \ft A prophecy that Kings shall be converted, and submit themselves to Christ's discipline.\f* now ye kings understand: take instruction you that judge the earth. \ef - \fr 2.10 \fq Ye Kings understand. \ft Not only innumerable other people of all nations, but also after a while, Kings and Emperors believed in Christ ¹. And such as at first persecuted, became most Christian, Catholic, Defenders of the faith. \fq ¹ \ft Kings converted to Christianity.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.10 \fq Take instruction you that judge the earth. \ft Petilianus, Gaudentius, & other Donatists inveighing against Christian Kings ¹, for punishing heretics, most falsely avouched, that Christianity never found Kings but envious, enemies, and persecuters. To whom S. Augustine answereth in several books ², that Christian Kings and Princes are not enemies to Christianity: but are enemies to heretics, the rebels of Christ and his Church ³. For according to his prophecy of King David, Christian \+it Kings are instructed\+it* ⁴, and know it is their duty, in the service of God, to defend the Church against Heretics, and other Infidels. And it is the property of Apostates to favour heretics: So good Constantine the Great maintained Catholic unity; and Iulian the Apostata to make greater division, took Churches from Catholics and gave them to Donatists, to nourish dissension, and so to overthrow all Christians. But God still protecteth the true Church, against all such subtle, and malicious devices: \+it because it is Christ's inheritance.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Defend Catholics and punish heretics. \fq ² \ft \+it Ad Petil. li. 2. c. 92.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Apostates favour heretics and schismatics, because they hate all Christians. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ad Gaud. li. 2. c. 26.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 Serve our Lord in \f r \fr 2.11 \ft None is secure before death.\f* fear: and rejoice to him with trembling. \ef - \fr 2.11 \fq Rejoice with trembling. \ft God's service is tempered with two affections: with joy ¹, in consideration of his goodness, mercy, & meekness; and with fear, in respect of his justice, and severe judgement. The one is a remedy against desperation: the other against presumption. \fq ¹ \ft Joy and fear.\ef*
\v 12 Apprehend discipline \f s \fr 2.12 \ft Some fall from the way of salvation.\f* lest sometime our Lord be wrath, and you perish out of the just way.
\v 13 when his wrath \f t \fr 2.13 \ft God will judge justly in the end of this short life both the evil and good. So this Psalm concludeth with the ninth key.\f* shall burn in short time, blessed are all that trust in him.
\c 3
\cd King David recounteth his danger, when his son Absalom conspired against him: 4. and thanketh God for his delivery: 9. acknowledging all help to be from God. Mystically, Christ's persecution, Death, Burial, and Resurrection. \f - \ft David Persecuted by his son. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Psalm of David, when he fled from the face of Absalom his son. \ef - \fr 3.1 \fq Psalm of David. \ft All interpreters agreeably teach, that King David made not the titles, which are before the Psalms ¹. Nevertheless they are authentical, as indited by the Holy Ghost. And it is most probable Esdras added those titles which are in the Hebrew: and the Seventy interpreters writ the other, in their Greek Edition. Both which S. Jerome translated into Latin. In these titles five things may be noted ². First, the former two having no title at all, the general name of Psalm, common to all, is particularly appropriated to some, and other names to others. Which in all are twelve: to wit, Psalm, Inscription, Prayer, Canticle, Psalm of Canticle, Canticle of Psalm, Hymn, Testimony, Understanding, Praise of Canticle, Alleluia, & Gradual Canticle. Secondly, in the titles of some Psalms are the names of certain persons, which by S. Augustine's judgement, cited in the Proemial Annotations, and others, proveth not the same persons to be authors of those Psalms, but signifieth some other thing. Thirdly, in some titles the time is signified, when the Psalm was made, or sung. Fourthly, the matter contained in the Psalm ³, or upon what occasion it was made, is expressed in some titles. Fifthly, diverse other terms are often used in the titles of sundry Psalms, as \+it To the end, For the Octave, For presses\+it*, and the like, all which we shall briefly explicate, where they first occur. \fq ¹ \ft Titles of the Psalms added by Esdras and the Septuagint. \fq ² \ft Five things to be noted in the titles. \fq ³ \ft \+it Psal. 4. 6. 8. 15. 16. &c.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 3.1 \ft first therefore this third Psalm is called \+it the Psalm of David\+it* ¹, not because he is author thereof, for he is also author of the former, where his name is not expressed, as is evident by the testimony of all the Apostles, \+it Act. 4. v. 25.\+it* but because it treateth particularly and literally of him. \fq ¹ \ft The time and occasion of making this Psalm.\ef* \ef - \fr 3.1 \fq When he fled from the face of Absalom. \ft Here the time is signified ¹, when this Psalm was made, to wit, immediately after the overthrow of his rebellious son Absalom, mentioned \+it 2. Reg. 18.\+it* before his return to Jerusalem. For albeit of human, natural, and fatherly affection, he greatly lamented the death of his son, yet he rendered thanks and praises to God, as reason and duty bound him. \fq ¹ \ft Why this is called the Psalm of David.\ef*
\v 2 Lord \f a \fr 3.2 \ft O God let me know how grievously I have sinned,\f* why are they \f b \fr 3.2 \ft that all Israel (1. Reg. 15. v. 13.) with all their heart followeth Absalom. So against Christ, the Priests, the People, & Gentiles are conspired.\f* multiplied that trouble me? Many rise up against me. \x - \xt Ioan. 2.\x*
\v 3 Many say to \f c \fr 3.3 \ft my life.\f* my soul: There is \f d \fr 3.3 \ft he cannot escape.\f* no salvation for him in his God.
\v 4 But \f e \fr 3.4 \ft But I avouch that God always defendeth me,\f* thou Lord art my protector, my \f f \fr 3.4 \ft giving me victory,\f* glory, & exalting \f g \fr 3.4 \ft & confirming my Kingdom.\f* my head.
\v 5 With my voice I have cried to our Lord: and he hath heard me from his \f h \fr 3.5 \ft heaven.\f* holy hill.
\v 6 I have \f i \fr 3.6 \ft Ilay down\f* slept, and have \f k \fr 3.6 \ft and rested in expectation of thy help.\f* been at rest; and have \f l \fr 3.6 \ft And am delivered. Christ died, was buried, & rose again.\f* risen up, because our lord hath taken me. \ef - \fr 3.6 \fq I have slept, and have been at rest, and have risen up. \ft King David by his sleeping in persecution ¹, and by his resting, and delivery from his persecutors, prefigured Christ's Death, Burial, & Resurrection. As appeareth, Ioan. 2. v. 22. Where the Evangelist saith: that after Christ's Resurrection, \+it his disciples believed the scripture\+it*, to wit, this and other like prophecies. For otherwise the old Testament doth not so expressly declare such Mysteries, as the Gospel doth: but one thing in the proper and grammatical signification of the words, and another thing in shadows and figures, and both literal. Whereupon S. Gregory teacheth \+it (li. 20. c. 1. Moral.)\+it* that holy Scripture (amongst other incomparable excellencies) surpasseth all other doctrines ², in the very manner of speaking: because by one and the same speech, it reporteth a thing done, and proclaimeth a Mystery: so relating things past, that with the very same words, it foresheweth things to come. \fq ¹ \ft King David prefigured Christ. \fq ² \ft The same Scripture hath diverse literal senses.\ef*
\v 7 I will not fear thousands of people compassing me: \f m \fr 3.7 \ft Iknow thou wilt help me, and so I beseech thee to do.\f* arise Lord, save me my God.
\v 8 Because thou hast strucken all that are my adversaries without cause: thou hast broken the \f n \fr 3.8 \ft The strength and fury.\f* teeth of sinners.
\v 9 Salvation \f o \fr 3.9 \ft health and safety cometh from God.\f* is our Lord's: and thy \f p \fr 3.9 \ft Aboundance of grace promised to God's servants.\f* blessing upon thy people.
\c 4
\cd The holy Prophet teacheth, by his own example, to fly to God in all tribulation: 3. that other refuges are insufficient, 9. and God's help most assured. \f - \ft Confidence in God necessary. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end in \f a \fr 4.1 \ft In an instrument apt for verses.\f* songs, the Psalm \f b \fr 4.1 \ft This Psalm pertaineth to the beloved, signified by the word David. S. Aug. li. 17. c. 14. civit. S. Beda in Psal.\f* of David. \ef - \fr 4.1 \fq Unto the end. \ft The Hebrew word \+it Lamnatsea\+it*, signifieth \+it to him that overcometh\+it*. And so the Hebrews interpret, that the Psalms ¹, which have this word in their titles, were directed either to him that excelled others in skill of music ²; or had authority over other musicians: or to him, whose office was to sing victories and triumphs. But the Latin, according to the Greek, hath \+it In finem, Unto the end\+it* which (most commonly signifying perpetuity, or continuance unto the end of any thing) in the titles of the Psalms rather signifieth, that the matter contained in the Psalm, pertaineth to future times, or persons; especially to the new Testament. And so S. Augustine expoundeth it here of \+it Christ\+it*, who is \+it the end\+it* (or perfection) \+it of the law\+it* ³. Not that the principal contents belong to Christ, in his own Person, but to his mystical body the Church, and faithful people, whom the prophet here teacheth to have confidence in God, moderation in their affections, & patience in tribulation, which is the seventh key, proposing his own example, & prophetically Christ's. The same whereto Christ exhorteth, saying: Ioan. 16. v. ult. \+it Have confidence, I have overcome the world.\+it* Signifying that his servants, through his grace, may also overcome it. \fq ¹ \ft The signification of this phrase, \+it To the end\+it* in the titles of Psalms. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Par. 15.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Rom. 10.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 When \f c \fr 4.2 \ft When Saul unjustly persecuted just David, God heard his prayers.\f* I invocated, the God of my justice heard me: in \f d \fr 4.2 \ft being straitly besieged \+it (1. Reg. 23. 26.)\+it*\f* tribulation thou hast enlarged to me. \f e \fr 4.2 \ft Likewise help me whensoever I shall need.\f* Have mercy on me, and hear my prayer.
\v 3 Ye sons of men how long are you of \f f \fr 4.3 \ft Why do you still harden your hearts?\f* heavy heart? Why love you \f g \fr 4.3 \ft honour, and transitory glory,\f* vanity, and seek \f h \fr 4.3 \ft false and deceitful riches?\f* lying? \x - \xt Eph. 4.\x*
\v 4 And know ye that our Lord hath made his \f i \fr 4.4 \ft Every godly soul.\f* holy one \f k \fr 4.4 \ft Rich with virtues.\f* marvelous: \f l \fr 4.4 \ft Every just soul hath confidence in God, that he will here his cry.\f* our Lord will hear me, when I shall cry to him.
\v 5 Be ye \f m \fr 4.5 \ft Just anger is good & necessary against sin.\f* angry, and \f n \fr 4.5 \ft but then is most need to beware not to exceed in passion, have therefore a continual purpose never to sin.\f* sin not: the things that you say in \f o \fr 4.5 \ft Evil cogitations\f* your hearts, in your \f p \fr 4.5 \ft bewail & repent before you sleep.\f* chambers be ye sorry for.
\v 6 Sacrifice ye the sacrifice of \f q \fr 4.6 \ft Not only external but most especially internal sacrifice of justice, and observation of God's commandments is most necessary.\f* justice, and hope in our Lord. Many say: \f r \fr 4.6 \ft The solid rewards promised by God?\f* Who sheweth us good things? \ef - \fr 4.6 \fq Sacrifice of Justice. \ft Not only external Sacrifice of diverse kinds were necessary in the law of nature ¹, and of Moyses, and one most excellent and complement of all, in the new Testament, but also spiritual sacrifice was ever, and is required, and that of three sorts. First ², Sacrifice of sorrow, and contrition for sins \+it (Psal. 50.) An afflicted spirit is a sacrifice to God\+it*. The second is sacrifice of Justice, here mentioned. The third is Sacrifice of praise \+it (Psal. 49.) Immolate to God the sacrifice of praise\+it*. Concerning the second proposed in this place: He offereth sacrifice of Justice, that rendereth to every one that is due. First ³, to God as our Creator, a resignation of ourselves, even our lives, at his divine pleasure; as to our Master, we must render faith and belief, in all that he proposeth; as to our Father, hope, confidence, & reverential fear; as to our Lord and King, payment of tribute, that is observation of his law and commandments; as to our Captain, the travail of warfare in this life; as to our Physician, patience and toleration, when he cureth our wounds, by chastisement for sins; as to our Spouse, chastity of body and mind, flying all carnal and spiritual fornication; as to our Friend, frequent conversation in all acts of devotion. We owe to ourselves ⁴, that seeing we consist of soul and body, we keep due subordination, that the soul and reason command, & the body, and inferior appetite obey: as the servant must obey his master, and the handmaid her mistress. We owe to our neighbour ⁵, love from the heart, instruction also from the mouth, and assistance by our help, according to his necessary, and our ability; yea though our neighbour be our enemy. But to other enemies ⁶ contrary things are due. To the world ⁷, contempt: because the goods of this world are small, few, short, uncertain, deceitful, not satisfying the mind, and mixed with many evils and dangers. To the flesh ⁸ we owe chastisement, and daily care, so to feed it, that it serve the soul, & rebel not. To the devil ⁹ we must render the shame, that cometh by sin, acknowledging our faults, and all truths, and so return upon him \+it all vanity and lying\+it*, wherewith he allureth & seduceth. Finally, to sin ¹⁰ itself, we owe hate, and revenge, because it is the only evil, that hurteth us; and due punishment with zeal of justice, because it dishonoureth God. He that thus offereth \+it sacrifice of justice\+it*, may justly (as it followeth in the Psalm) hope (yet not in himself but) in our Lord. And lest any should pretend ignorance, saying: \+it who sheweth\+it* (or teacheth) \+it us good things?\+it* ¹¹ as though they lacked instruction, the Prophet preventeth this vain excuse, saying: \+it The light of thy countenance,\+it* o Lord (the light of reason, which is the image of God, whereto we are created like) \+it is signed upon us\+it*, fixed in our understanding, that we may see there is a God, that ought to be served, and that he will reward his servants. \+it Heb. 11.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Three spiritual sacrifices necessary. \fq ² \ft Of penance, Justice. Praise. \fq ³ \ft What is due to God. \fq ⁴ \ft To ourselves. \fq ⁵ \ft To our neighbour. \fq ⁶ \ft To our enemies. \fq ⁷ \ft The world. \fq ⁸ \ft The flesh. \fq ⁹ \ft The devil. \fq ¹⁰ \ft To sin. \fq ¹¹ \ft Light of reason sheweth there is a God that rewardeth.\ef*
\v 7 The \f s \fr 4.7 \ft reason and grace are freely given to man, whereby he may know that God will reward the just. \+it Heb. 11. v. 6.\+it*\f* light of thy countenance, o Lord is signed upon us: thou hast given \f t \fr 4.7 \ft Wherein a just man inwardly rejoiceth.\f* gladness in my heart.
\v 8 By the fruit of their \f v \fr 4.8 \ft For example and in figure of heavenly rewards, God gave temporal wealth in the old testament.\f* corn, and \f w \fr 4.8 \ft See previous note\f* wine, and \f x \fr 4.8 \ft See previous note\f* oil, they are multiplied.
\v 9 In \f y \fr 4.9 \ft In this confidence the just may rest contented.\f* peace in the self same I will sleep, and rest:
\v 10 Because thou Lord hast \f z \fr 4.10 \ft God so promiseth every just person in particular.\f* singularly settled me in hope.
\c 5
\cd Just men in affliction appeal to God, the revenger of injuries, 5. knowing and professing that God hateth iniquity. 9. Therefore remit their cause to him, 11. recite certain enormous vices of the wicked, 13. and expect God's final judgement of good and bad. \f - \ft The general judgement. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, for \f a \fr 5.1 \ft The faithful just soul that overcometh her enemies by virtue.\f* her that obtaineth the \f b \fr 5.1 \ft eternal glory.\f* inheritance. The Psalm of David.
\v 2 Receive, o Lord \f c \fr 5.2 \ft The prayer of the whole Church, or of any faithful (ever beloved) soul.\f* my words with thine ears, understand my cry.
\v 3 Attend to the voice of my prayer, my King and my God.
\v 4 Because I will pray to thee: Lord in \f d \fr 5.4 \ft God's help is presently granted of his part, though it be sometimes deferred for the more good of his servants.\f* the morning thou wilt hear my voice.
\v 5 In \f e \fr 5.5 \ft Before all other affairs we must pray to God. S. Cyprian. in fine orat. Dominicæ.\f* the morning I will stand by thee and will see: because thou art not a God that wilt iniquity. \ef - \fr 5.5 \fq Not a God that wilt iniquity. \ft Seeing \+it God will not iniquity\+it* ¹, as these words testify in plain terms, it followeth necessarily, that he is not author, nor cause of any sin. For God doth nothing contrary to his own will. But he hateth iniquity, and in respect thereof \+it hateth all that work iniquities\+it*, as the authors of iniquity, though he loveth them as his creatures, and of his part requireth their salvation. \fq ¹ \ft God is not author nor cause of sin.\ef*
\v 6 Neither shall the malignant \f f \fr 5.6 \ft The wicked and wickedness have no conversation with God.\f* dwell near thee: neither shall the unjust abide \f g \fr 5.6 \ft in the day of judgement.\f* before thine eyes.
\v 7 Thou hatest all that work iniquity: thou wilt \f h \fr 5.7 \ft by final sentence of eternal damnation.\f* destroy all that speak lie. The bloody and deceitful man our Lord will abhor:
\v 8 But I in the multitude of \f i \fr 5.8 \ft Not in man's power, but in God's mercy must the just trust.\f* thy mercy. I will enter into thy house: I will adore toward \f k \fr 5.8 \ft In the Church of God.\f* thy holy temple in thy \f l \fr 5.8 \ft with reverential fear, as in God's presence.\f* fear.
\v 9 Lord conduct me in thy justice: because of mine enemies direct my way in thy sight.
\v 10 Because there is \f m \fr 5.10 \ft No true nor solid goodness in the wicked.\f* no truth in their mouth: their heart is \f n \fr 5.10 \ft They think nothing but vanity, and mischief.\f* vain.
\v 11 Their throat is an \f o \fr 5.11 \ft yielding loathsome stench, bitterness, and rancor,\f* open sepulchre, they did \f p \fr 5.11 \ft yet they flatter with feigned good words.\f* deceitfully with their tongues, \f q \fr 5.11 \ft Albeit the just desire the conversion of the wicked, yet if they will not repent, then the just conform their desires to God's just judgement: which shall be manifested in the end of the world.\f* judge them o God \x - \xt Psal. 13. Rom. 3.\x*. Let them fail of their cogitations, according to the multitude of their impieties expel them, because they have provoked thee o Lord.
\v 12 And let all be glad, that hope in thee, they shall rejoice for ever: and thou shalt dwell in them. And all that love thy name shall glory in thee,
\v 13 because thou wilt \f r \fr 5.13 \ft The just shall receive sentence of eternal glory.\f* bless the just. Lord, as with a shield of thy good will, thou hast crowned us.
\c 6
\cd David's earnest and hearty prayer after he had grievously sinned. 5. Which being grounded in filial, not servile fear, 9. concludeth with assured hope, and confidence in God's mercy. \f - \ft A pathetical prayer of a sinner & the first penitential Psalm, The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 6.1 \ft This Psalm pertaineth also to penitents in the new testament.\f* the end in songs, the Psalm of David for the octave. \x - \xt Psa. 37.\x* \ef - \fr 6.1 \fq For the octave. \ft Literally is seemeth that the Psalms which have \+it For the octave\+it* in their titles ¹, were to be sung on an instrument of eight strings. So the Chaldee paraphrasis translateth, \+it In citheris octo chordarum: in Citherns of eight strings\+it*. But prophetically S. Augustine, & others expound it, to pertain to the Resurrection in the end of this world. So David, and all penitent sinners bewail their sins, and do penance in this life, for the octave, that is for the world to come. \fq ¹ \ft The octave signifieth the world to come.\ef*
\v 2 Lord, \f b \fr 6.2 \ft condemn me not eternally.\f* rebuke me not in thy fury; nor \f c \fr 6.2 \ft Spare me also for part of the temporal pain, which I deserve.\f* chastise me in thy wrath. \ef - \fr 6.2 \fq In thy fury, nor in thy wrath. \ft By \+it fury\+it* is signified divine justice, irrevocably condemning the wicked to eternal damnation ¹: by \+it wrath\+it*, God's fatherly chastisement correcting sinners, whom he saveth. Whereupon S. Gregory teacheth, that the faithful soul not only feareth fury, but also wrath: because \+it after the death of the flesh, some are deputed to eternal torments, some pass to life through the fire of purgation\+it* ². Which doctrine the same holy father confirmeth, by the judgement of S. Augustine more ancient. Who likewise affirmeth, that all those which have not laid Christ their foundation, are rebuked in fury, because they are tormented in eternal fire: and those which upon right foundation (of true faith in Christ) have \+it built wood, hay, stubble\+it*, are chastised in wrath, because they are brought to rest of beatitude, but purged by fire. Let therefore the faithful soul (considering what she hath done, and contemplating what she shall receive) say: \+it Lord rebuke me not, in thy fury: nor chastise me in thy wrath\+it*. As if she said more plainly: This only with my whole intention of heart I crave, this incessantly with all my desires I covert, that in the dreadful judgement, thou neither strike me with the reprobate, nor afflict me with those, that shall be purged in burning flames. Thus S. Gregory, \+it in Psalm. penitent. v. 1.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Hell for some sinners. Purgatory for others. \fq ² \ft \+it in Psal. 37. 1. Cor. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 Have mercy on me Lord, because I am weak: \f d \fr 6.3 \ft Give me the medicine of grace.\f* heal me Lord, because all \f e \fr 6.3 \ft My sorrow hath inwardly pierced me even to the bones.\f* my bones be troubled.
\v 4 And my soul is \f f \fr 6.4 \ft with fear of thy just wrath.\f* troubled exceedingly: but thou Lord \f g \fr 6.4 \ft leavest thou me in this calamity?\f* how long?
\v 5 \f h \fr 6.5 \ft Shew again thy favourable countenance,\f* Turn thee, o Lord, and \f i \fr 6.5 \ft from this fearful affliction.\f* deliver my soul: save me for \f k \fr 6.5 \ft Though my sins have deserved the contrary yet shew thy mercy.\f* thy mercy.
\v 6 Because there is not in \f l \fr 6.6 \ft This life is the time of repentance, after death no\f* death that is mindful of thee: and in \f m \fr 6.6 \ft In hell nothing but blasphemy.\f* hell who shall confess to thee?
\v 7 I \f n \fr 6.7 \ft Ihave in part lamented.\f* have laboured in my sighing, I \f o \fr 6.7 \ft Iwill add more sorrow & penance.\f* will every night wash my bed; I will \f p \fr 6.7 \ft Iwill persist in my penance, till I be throughly watered with thy grace.\f* water my couch with my tears.
\v 8 My \f q \fr 6.8 \ft mine eyes are dim with weeping, for fear of thy just judgement.\f* eye is troubled for fury: I have waxen \f r \fr 6.8 \ft my hairs are gray with sorrow.\f* old \f s \fr 6.8 \ft whereat mine enemies rejoice.\f* among all mine enemies.
\v 9 \f t \fr 6.9 \ft After due sorrow the true penitent hath confidence in God, against his enemies.\f* Depart from me all ye that work iniquity: because our Lord \f v \fr 6.9 \ft Will most certainly accept of true repentance.\f* hath heard the voice of my weeping. \x - \xt Mat. 7. & 25. Luc. 13.\x*
\v 10 Our Lord hath heard my petition, our Lord hath received my prayer.
\v 11 Let all mine enemies be \f w \fr 6.11 \ft These are not imprecations, but threatenings, that the wicked may amend, or else predictions if they persist in sin.\f* ashamed, & very sore troubled: let them be converted and ashamed very quickly.
\c 7
\cd David in confidence of his just cause, and unjust persecution, prayeth for God's help, 7. and just revenge of his enemies: 15. describing their malicious intention, and ruin. \f - \ft David's prayer in tribulation. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Psalm of David which he sung to our Lord, for the words of \f a \fr 7.1 \ft This Chusi defeated the counsel of Achitophel, as S. Aug. S. Basil & S. Chris. expound it. Others think he speaketh of that Chusi (or Chusai) who reported to him the death of Absalom. \+it 2. Reg. 18. v. 31.\+it*\f* Chusi the son of Jemini. \f 1 \fr 7.1 \ft \+it (2. Reg. 16.)\+it*\f*
\v 2 O Lord my God I have hoped in thee: save me from all that persecute me, and deliver me.
\v 3 Lest sometime \f b \fr 7.3 \ft Absalom, or any other enemy, if God do not resist and hinder him.\f* he as a Lion violently take \f c \fr 7.3 \ft my life.\f* my soul, whilst there is none to redeem, nor to save.
\v 4 O Lord my God if I have done \f d \fr 7.4 \ft Such evil as mine enemies feign & object against me.\f* this, if there be iniquity in my hands:
\v 5 If I have rendered to them that repayed me evils, let me worthily fall \f e \fr 7.5 \ft Let me not have the victory of them.\f* empty from mine enemies.
\v 6 Let the enemy persecute my soul, and take it, and \f f \fr 7.6 \ft Let me die with ignominy.\f* tread down my life in the earth, and bring down my glory into the dust.
\v 7 Arise Lord in thy wrath: and \f g \fr 7.7 \ft Shew thy power.\f* be exalted in the coasts of mine enemies.
\v 8 And arise, o Lord my God, in the \f h \fr 7.8 \ft Seeing thou didst command to make me King, it behoveth thee to protect me.\f* precept which thou hast commanded: and a \f i \fr 7.8 \ft many will serve thee the only true God.\f* synagogue of peoples shall compass thee.
\v 9 And \f k \fr 7.9 \ft For this increase of faithful people.\f* for it return on high: our Lord judgeth peoples. Judge me, o Lord, according to \f l \fr 7.9 \ft My just cause against my particular enemies\f* my justice, and according to my innocency \f m \fr 7.9 \ft let fall upon me.\f* upon me.
\v 10 The wickedness of sinners shall be consumed, and thou shalt direct the just, which searchest the heart and reins, o God. \x - \xt 1. Par. 28. Iere. 11. 17.\x*
\v 11 My just help is from our Lord, who saveth those that be right of heart.
\v 12 God is a just judge, strong, & patient: is he angry \f n \fr 7.12 \ft God doth not punish all sinners presently, but often deferreth.\f* every day?
\v 13 \f o \fr 7.13 \ft Expecting if they will amend.\f* Unless you will be converted, he shall shake his sword, he hath bent his bow, and prepared it.
\v 14 And in it he hath prepared the vessels of death: he hath made his arrows for them that burn. \x - \xt Isa. 59. Iob. 15.\x*
\v 15 Behold \f p \fr 7.15 \ft The injurious persecutor purposing injustice, conceiveth envy, and bringeth forth iniquity.\f* he hath bred with injustice: he hath conceived sorrow, and brought forth iniquity.
\v 16 He hath opened a pit, and digged it up: and he is fallen into the ditch, which he made.
\v 17 His \f q \fr 7.17 \ft Envy & desire to hurt others turneth to the ruin of the envyers.\f* sorrow shall be turned upon his head: and his iniquity shall descend upon his crown.
\v 18 I will confess to our Lord according to his justice: and will sing to the name of our Lord most high.
\c 8
\cd God is magnified & praised for his marvelous work of creatures, 5. but especially of mankind, singularly exalted by the Incarnation of Christ. \f - \ft Christ's Incarnation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 8.1 \ft Belonging to the new Testament.\f* the end \f b \fr 8.1 \ft Suffering of Christ, and of Christians.\f* for presses, the Psalm of David. \ef - \fr 8.1 \fq Presses. \ft ¹ Most Hebrew Doctors say the word \+it Gittith\+it*, may either signify the place, where this Psalm was made, or the musical instrument, on which it was sung. But most Christian Doctors expound it literally of Christ's Passion, who was stretched on the Cross, and all his sacred blood pressed, and drawn out of his body. Which Metaphor Isaias also useth, demanding of Christ ²: Why is thy clothing red, and thy garments as theirs that tread in the \+it wine press?\+it* and answereth in Christ's person: I have trodden \+it the Press alone.\+it* S. Augustine also applieth it morally to the Church ³, where Christ is the vine, the Apostles are the branches, & spreaders (that is preachers) of the Gospel, Christians are the grapes, Christian virtues are the wine. Namely patience, and fortitude in afflictions. Whereby the good are purified, & severed from amidst the reprobate, as wine is pressed out of the grapes, barreled, and laid up in cellars, and ⁴ the husks and kernels cast to hogs, or other beasts. \fq ¹ \ft Presses signify Christ's Passion. \fq ² \ft \+it Isa. 63.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Morally it signifieth the travails of the Church militant. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it vinacia.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 O Lord \f c \fr 8.2 \ft God the Lord of all by creation, is our singular Lord,\f* our Lord, how marvelous is thy name in the whole earth! Because thy magnificence is elevated above \f d \fr 8.2 \ft God more excellent than the heavens, he being the Creator, they a creature.\f* the heavens.
\v 3 Out of the mouth of \f e \fr 8.3 \ft Fulfilled when Christ coming into Jerusalem, children sang \+it Ozanna. Mat. 21.\+it*\f* infants & sucklings, thou hast perfected praise because of thine enemies, that thou mayest destroy the enemy & revenger. \x - \xt Mat. 21.\x*
\v 4 Because I shall see thy heavens, the works of thy fingers: the moon and the stars, which thou hast founded.
\v 5 What is man, that thou art mindful of him? Or the son of man, that thou visitest him?
\v 6 Thou hast \f f \fr 8.6 \ft The Son in assumpted human nature, became less than Angels.\f* minished him a little less than Angels; with \f g \fr 8.6 \ft But in him man's nature is exalted above Angels.\f* glory and honour thou hast crowned him: \x - \xt Heb. 2.\x*
\v 7 And hast appointed him \f h \fr 8.7 \ft Christ the Lord of all creatures.\f* over the works of thy hands.
\v 8 Thou hast subjected \f i \fr 8.8 \ft Yea of Angels. \+it Heb. 2.\+it*\f* all things under his feet, all \f k \fr 8.8 \ft Not only all reasonable creatures, but all beasts, and other things obey him. The sea and the winds obey him. \+it Mat. 8.\+it*\f* sheep and oxen: moreover also the beasts of the field.
\v 9 The birds of the air, & fishes of the sea; that walk the paths of the sea.
\v 10 \f l \fr 8.10 \ft The same end & beginning signify, that as God was marvelous in creating man in so happy state, that if he would, he might have avoided both sin & death: so he is marvelous in that he so rewardeth the blessed in the resurrection, that they can neither sin nor die.\f* O Lord our Lord, how marvelous is thy name in the whole earth!
\c 9
\cd The Church praiseth God for her protection, 4. in repelling the enemy's force, 8. in punishing the wicked, and rewarding the just. \f - \ft God's providence in protecting the good and permitting evil. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, for the \f a \fr 9.1 \ft Christ's coming in humility, and Christians' afflictions, are hidden from the world, in God's providence.\f* secrets of the son, the Psalm of David.
\v 2 I will \f b \fr 9.2 \ft Give thanks.\f* confess to thee, o Lord, with all my heart: I will tell all thy marvelous things.
\v 3 I will be \f c \fr 9.3 \ft In mind\f* glad and \f d \fr 9.3 \ft and body.\f* rejoice in thee: I will sing to thy name, o most High.
\v 4 In \f e \fr 9.4 \ft God repelleth the enemy, when man is not able to resist.\f* turning mine enemy backward: they shall be weakened, and perish before my face.
\v 5 Because thou hast done \f f \fr 9.5 \ft Ajust man doth his endeavor, not of himself, but by God's grace overcometh the enemy.\f* my judgment and my cause: thou hast sit upon the throne which judgest justice.
\v 6 Thou hast rebuked the \f g \fr 9.6 \ft All sinners, called Gentiles, because they were generally accounted wicked.\f* Gentiles, and the impious hath perished: their \f h \fr 9.6 \ft The vain glorious fame of sinners partly decayeth in this world, but most especially in the world to come.\f* name thou hast destroyed for ever, and for ever and ever.
\v 7 The swords of the enemy have failed unto the end: and their cities thou hast destroyed.
\v 8 Their memory hath perished with a sound: and our Lord abideth for ever. He hath prepared his throne in \f i \fr 9.8 \ft Judicial seats of men are often corrupted, but God's never.\f* judgment:
\v 9 and he will judge the whole world in equity, he will judge the people in justice.
\v 10 And our Lord is made a refuge for the poor: an helper \f k \fr 9.10 \ft God doth not presently deliver the good from afflictions: but when it is to their spiritual profit.\f* in opportunities, in tribulation.
\v 11 And let them hope in thee that know thy name: because thou hast not forsaken them that seek thee, o Lord.
\v 12 Sing to our Lord, which dwelleth in Sion: declare his \f l \fr 9.12 \ft His precepts which men ought chiefly to study.\f* studies among the Gentiles.
\v 13 Because \f m \fr 9.13 \ft God revengeth the blood of Martyrs.\f* requiring blood he hath remembered them: he hath not forgotten the cry of the poor.
\v 14 Have mercy on me, o Lord: See my humiliation \f n \fr 9.14 \ft Procured by mine enemies.\f* by my enemies.
\v 15 Which exaltest me from the gates of death, that I may declare all thy praises in \f o \fr 9.15 \ft In the public view of the Church.\f* the gates of the daughter of Sion.
\v 16 I will rejoice in thy salvation: the Gentiles are \f p \fr 9.16 \ft The wicked are entangled in the snares which they lay for others.\f* fastened in the destruction, which they made. In this snare, which they hid, is their foot taken.
\v 17 Our Lord shall be known doing judgments: the sinner is taken in the works of his own hands.
\v 18 \f q \fr 9.18 \ft As the first works of Christ in all humility and patience were strange, and hidden to the world \+it (v. 1.)\+it* so his last judgement shall be in majesty and manifest to all in exalting the blessed and suppressing the wicked.\f* Let sinners be turned into hell, all nations that forget God.
\v 19 Because to the end there shall not be oblivion of the poor man: the patience of the poor, shall not perish in the end.
\v 20 Arise Lord, let not man be strengthened: Let the \f r \fr 9.20 \ft By Gentiles is often understood all great sinners. For the Jews despised Gentiles: as the Romans did all Barbarous nations.\f* Gentiles be judged in thy sight.
\v 21 Appoint Lord \f s \fr 9.21 \ft Suffer a tyrant to rule over them, that thereby they may learn what it is to use others unjustly. It seemeth to S. Augustine a Prophecy, that such as receive not Christ, shall believe Antichrist.\f* a Law-giver over them: that the Gentiles may know that they be men. \ef - \fr 9.21 \ft After the 21. verse the late Hebrew Doctors divide this Psalm ¹, beginning there the tenth, without any new title: but only this word \+it Sela\+it* ²: which the Septuagint, Theodotion, and Symmachus translate \+it Diapsalma\+it*, that is, change of meter, or music, also pause or rest in singing. Aquila, whom S. Jerome rather approveth, translateth \+it semper\+it* ever ³. Some English Bibles omit it ⁴, others leave it in the text, not translating it into English. It seemeth to most Interpreters to be added as a note to stir up attention. And it occurreth often, not only in the end of Psalms, but also in other places. For it is thrice in the third Psalm. And therefore maketh no argument, that this Psalm should be divided ⁵. And those which divide this into two, join two in the 147. Psalm. So that all agree in the number of 150. Psalms in the whole Psalter. \fq ¹ \ft Some divide this Psalm into two. \fq ² \ft Sela a note of change, or of rest in music, or rather of attention. \fq ³ \ft \+it Epist. ad Marcel.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Anno. 1577. 1552. 1603.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft All the Psalms are just 150.\ef*
\v 22 \f v \fr 9.22 \ft In great persecution it seemeth to the weak, that god deferreth his assistance very long.\f* Why Lord hast thou departed far off, despisest in opportunities, in tribulation?
\v 23 Whilst the impious is proud, the poor is \f w \fr 9.23 \ft Extremely vexed & tormented.\f* set on fire: \f x \fr 9.23 \ft The Prophet answereth to the complaint of the just, that indeed the wicked are caught in their own snares.\f* they are caught in the counsels which they devise.
\v 24 Because the sinner is praised in the desires of his soul: and the unjust man is blessed.
\v 25 The sinner hath exasperated our Lord, according to the multitude of his wrath he shall \f y \fr 9.25 \ft Not seek to recover God's favour.\f* not seek.
\v 26 There is no God in his sight: his ways are defiled at all time. Thy judgments are taken away from his face: he shall \f z \fr 9.26 \ft The wicked doth domineer for a time, and thinketh he shall do so still.\f* rule over all his enemies.
\v 27 For he hath said in his heart: I will not be moved from generation unto generation \f a \fr 9.27 \ft And never fall into any adversity, but still remain without misery or any evil.\f* without evil.
\v 28 Whose mouth is full of cursing, and bitterness, and guile: under his tongue labour and sorrow. \x - \xt Rom. 3.\x*
\v 29 He sitteth in wait with the rich in secret places, to kill the innocent.
\v 30 His eyes look upon the poor: he lieth in wait in secret, as a lion in his den.
\v 31 He lieth in wait to take the poor man violently, violently to take the poor man whilst he draweth him. In his snare he will humble himself, and shall fall when he shall have dominion over the poor.
\v 32 For he hath said in his heart: God hath forgotten, he hath turned away his face not to see for ever.
\v 33 \f b \fr 9.33 \ft The prayer of the just in tribulation.\f* Arise Lord God, let thy hand be exalted: forget not the poor.
\v 34 Wherefore hath the impious provoked God? For he hath said in his heart: He will not enquire.
\v 35 Thou seest, that thou considerest labour & sorrow: that thou mayest deliver them into thy hands. To thee is the poor left: to the orphan thou wilt be an helper.
\v 36 Break the arm of the sinner and malignant: his sin shall be sought, and shall not be found.
\v 37 Our Lord shall reign for ever, and for ever and ever: ye \f c \fr 9.37 \ft Ye vilest men.\f* Gentiles shall perish from his land.
\v 38 Our Lord hath heard the desire of the poor: thy ear hath heard the \f d \fr 9.38 \ft The just ought always to be ready prepared in heart, to suffer patiently all that shall happen unto them.\f* preparation of their heart.
\v 39 To judge \f e \fr 9.39 \ft As the first works of Christ in all humility and patience were strange, and hidden to the world (v. 1.) so his last judgement shall be in majesty and manifest to all in exalting the blessed and suppressing the wicked.\f* for the pupil and the humble, that man add no more to magnify himself upon the earth.
\c 10
\cd David's friends advising him to fly from the persecution of Saul, he answereth, that his trust is in God's protection. 2. Though the persecutor be very malicious, 4. yet God will overthrow him, 5. and deliver the just. \f - \ft God's provident care of the just. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 10.1 \ft S. Augustine applieth it to heretics, persuading Catholics to repair unto their separate congregation, falsely calling it the mountain.\f* the end the psalm of David. I Trust in our Lord, how say \f b \fr 10.1 \ft Ye my friends say thus.\f* ye to my soul: Pass over unto the mountain as a sparrow?
\v 2 For behold sinners \f c \fr 10.2 \ft Persecuters using all rigour and force,\f* have bent the bow, they have prepared their arrows in the quiver, that they may shoot in \f d \fr 10.2 \ft falsely pretend other causes against the innocent to destroy them.\f* the dark, at them that be right of heart.
\v 3 For they have \f e \fr 10.3 \ft It is the manner of persecuters, and especially of heretics, to destroy and pull down that others have built.\f* destroyed the things, which thou didst perfit: but the just what hath he done?
\v 4 Our Lord is in his holy temple, our Lord his seat is in heaven. \x - \xt Abac. 2.\x*
\v 5 His eyes have respect unto the poor: his \f f \fr 10.5 \ft Though God seem to wink or sleep, yet his providence still watcheth, and observeth all men's actions.\f* eye-lids examine the sons of men.
\v 6 Our Lord \f g \fr 10.6 \ft Proveth by tribulations.\f* examineth the just and the impious but he that \f h \fr 10.6 \ft Continuance in sin bringeth damnation to the soul.\f* loveth iniquity, hateth his own soul.
\v 7 \f i \fr 10.7 \ft God sparing for the time at last must needs of justice punish severely.\f* He shall rain snares upon sinners: fire and brimstone, and blast of storms the portion of their cup.
\v 8 Because our Lord is just and hath loved justice: his countenance hath seen equity.
\c 11
\cd The Prophet describeth the paucity of just men, and aboundance of wicked, both at Christ's first coming in flesh, 6. and second in majesty, in the end of the world. \f - \ft The state of the Church in the first and last times of Christ. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 11.1 \ft Christ's first\f* the end for \f b \fr 11.1 \ft and last coming,\f* the octave, the \f c \fr 11.1 \ft will bring joy to the elect.\f* Psalm of David.
\v 2 Save \f d \fr 11.2 \ft Christ calleth his mystical body himself. \+it Act. 9. v. 4.\+it*\f* me Lord, because the holy hath failed \f e \fr 11.2 \ft False and double dealing hinder from true faith.\f* because verities are diminished from among the children of men.
\v 3 They have spoken vain things every one to his neighbour, deceitful lips, they have spoken in heart and heart.
\v 4 Our Lord destroy all deceitful lips, & the tongue that speaketh \f f \fr 11.4 \ft Indolent & arrogant.\f* great things.
\v 5 Which have said: We will magnify our tongue, our lips are of us, who is our Lord?
\v 6 For the misery of the needy, and mourning of the poor, now will I arise, saith our Lord: I will put in \f g \fr 11.6 \ft When sin most abounded Christ came into this world: and in like case will come to judge.\f* a salvation, I will do confidently in him.
\v 7 Words of our Lord be chaste words: silver examined by fire, tried from the earth, purged seven fold. \x - \xt Pro. 30.\x*
\v 8 Thou Lord wilt \f h \fr 11.8 \ft Yet still there remain some just whom God preserveth.\f* preserve us: and keep us from this generation for ever.
\v 9 The impious walk round about: according to thy highness thou hast \f i \fr 11.9 \ft God sometimes suffereth the wicked to do what evil they desire.\f* multiplied the children of men. \ef - \fr 11.9 \fq The impious walk round about. \ft S. Augustine expoundeth this of worldly men desiring temporal things ¹, signified by the seven days, wherein this whole life is turned about, as in a wheel, not providing for the eighth day, which is eternity, after the day of Judgement. In another place he sheweth also ², that this sentence agreeth aptly to the Platonists, who taught that this world never endeth, but passeth & returneth round about, in a revolution of many years ³; so that all things should happen again even as they did before, contrary to this, and many other Scriptures, affirming that God \+it will preserve\+it* the just, and keep them \+it from this generation for ever\+it*. Whereas the reprobate, who set their whole mind on temporal things, or expect a revolution of all, shall eternally walk without the kingdom of heaven, & never enter in; though some may call with the foolish virgins, saith S. Jerome (or some other learned author) upon this place: \+it Lord Lord, open\+it* (the door) \+it to us. But he will answer: that I know you not. Mat. 25.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Temporal desires hinder the entrance into heaven. \fq ² \ft \+it li. 12. c. 11. civ.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Platonists' error.\ef*
\c 12
\cd After general gross ignorance and impiety in the world 7. Christ shall be incarnate, the Redeemer of mankind. \f - \ft Of Christ's Incarnation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 12.1 \ft It more pertaineth to the new testament than to the old.\f* the end, the Psalm of David. How long, o Lord wilt thou forget me unto the end? How long doest thou turn away thy face from me?
\v 2 How long shall I put \f b \fr 12.2 \ft Careful & almost perplex cogitations by reason of long persecution & man's weakness.\f* counsels in my soul, sorrow in my heart \f c \fr 12.2 \ft Very often every day.\f* by day?
\v 3 How long shall mine enemies be exalted over me? Regard and hear me, o Lord my God.
\v 4 Illuminate mine eyes that I sleep not \f d \fr 12.4 \ft Fall not into mortal sin,\f* in death at any time: lest sometime mine enemy say: I have prevailed against him.
\v 5 They that trouble me, will rejoice if I be moved: but I have hoped in thy mercy.
\v 6 My heart shall rejoice in thy salvation: I will sing to our Lord, which giveth me \f e \fr 12.6 \ft Patience in tribulation, and reward for victory.\f* good things: and I will sing to the name of our Lord most high.
\c 13
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, a Psalm of David. The \f a \fr 13.1 \ft Wicked men drowned in sin are at last so besotted in their understanding, that they think in their heart (though they dare not utter it) that there is no God: that is, none that hath divine providence in governing the world, nor that will judge all in the end.\f* fool hath said in his heart: There is no God. They are corrupt, and are become \f b \fr 13.1 \ft Defiled with all sorts of sin.\f* abominable in their studies: there is \f c \fr 13.1 \ft Not only the most wicked, but also all mankind were unable without a Redeemer to do good.\f* not that doth good no not one. \x - \xt Psa. 52.\x* \ef - \fr 13.1 \fq No not one. \ft S. Paul by this place, & the like \+it (Isaie 59. v. 7.)\+it* confirmeth his doctrine (Rom. 3.) that both the Jews and the Gentiles (meaning all mankind) were in that state, that none, no not one without the grace of Christ ¹, were just, nor could be justified, nor could be justified, nor saved by the law of Nature, nor of Moyses. Which proveth the necessity of faith. But neither that only faith justifieth, nor that the justest are still wicked, as Calvin and Beza falsely expound these Scriptures. For the Prophets and S. Paul speak in these places of men before they be justified, teaching that all mankind was once in sin, and none could be justified but by Christ ². Nevertheless they teach also that men being justified must and may \+it serve justice unto sanctification\+it*. And that their works are not then unprofitable. \+it For being made free from sin\+it* (saith the same Apostle to the Romans c. 6.) \+it and become servants to God, you have your fruit unto sanctification, and the end is life everlasting\+it* Which point of doctrine, how man is justified ³, S. Augustine excellently, & briefly explicateth \+it (li. 1. de Spiritu et lit. c. 9.)\+it* in these words: The just are \+it justified freely by Christ his grace\+it*. They are not before purified by the law ⁴: they are not justified by their proper will, \+it but justified freely by (Christ) his grace\+it*. Not that it is done without our will, but by the law our will is shewed weak, that grace might cure the will, and the will being cured might fulfil the law, not being under the law, nor needing the law. \fq ¹ \ft Without Christ's grace no man is nor can be just. \fq ² \ft The law shewed the insufficiency of man's will. \fq ³ \ft Grace cureth the will. \fq ⁴ \ft The will being cured cooperateth with grace.\ef* \ef - \fr 13.1 \ft Whereto we may here add (and so save labour of repeating this in other places) another document of the same Doctor, in the same book (de Spirit. & lit. c. 27.) ¹ that the just do not live without some sins, and yet remain in state of salvation: the wicked do sometimes certain good works ², & still remain in state of damnation. For even as (saith he) venial sins, without it which this life is not led, do not exclude the just from eternal life: so certain good works, without which the life of the very worst is hardly found, profit nothing the unjust man to eternal salvation, but in everlasting damnation, some shall have more and some less torment. \fq ¹ \ft Venial sins exclude not from heaven. \fq ² \ft Good works done in mortal sins avail not to salvation.\ef*
\v 2 Our Lord hath looked forth from heaven upon the children of men, to see if there be that understandeth, and seeketh after God.
\v 3 All have declined, they are become \f d \fr 13.3 \ft Without faith in Christ none had meritorious works. * These three verses being not in the Hebrew, nor Greek, yet are in the English, an. \+it 1577.\+it* and are three distinct verses in other psalms. \+it 5. 9. & 35.\+it*\f* unprofitable together: there is not that doth good, no not one. Their throat is an open sepulchre, with their tongues they did deceitfully, the poison of asps under their lips. Whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness: their feet swift to shed blood. \f e \fr 13.3 \ft They are wholly occupied in vexing others.\f* Destruction and infelicity in their ways, and the way of peace they have not known: there is no fear of God before their eyes. \x - \xt Isa. 59. v. 8.\x*
\v 4 \f f \fr 13.4 \ft The Prophet speaketh this in the person of God.\f* Shall not all they know that work iniquity, that devour my people \f g \fr 13.4 \ft With greediness to hurt the good.\f* as food of bread?
\v 5 They have not invocated our Lord, \f h \fr 13.5 \ft Not believing in God, they feared Idols, that is, devils:\f* there have they trembled for fear, \f i \fr 13.5 \ft who indeed cannot hurt God's servants.\f* where no fear was.
\v 6 Because our Lord is in \f k \fr 13.6 \ft Though innumerable be very wicked, yet some are just.\f* the just generation, you have \f l \fr 13.6 \ft Mocked & derided those that trust in God.\f* confounded the counsel of the poor man: because our Lord is his hope.
\v 7 \f m \fr 13.7 \ft The Prophet wisheth, and withal Prophesieth that Christ our Saviour will come, who is promised to Israel.\f* Who will give from Sion the salvation of Israel? When our Lord shall have \f n \fr 13.7 \ft Redeemed man from the captivity of the devil.\f* turned away the captivity of his people, \f o \fr 13.7 \ft Those that supplant vice\f* Jacob shall rejoice, and \f p \fr 13.7 \ft and contemplate God.\f* Israel shall be glad.
\c 14
\cd Christ, by the mouth of David, declareth his future victory and triumph over the world, 9. and death. \f - \ft Of eternal Beatitude. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Psalm of David. Lord who shall dwell in \f a \fr 14.1 \ft In heaven, as appeareth by the last verse.\f* thy tabernacle? Or who shall rest in thy holy hill? \ef - \fr 14.1 \fq The Psalm of David. \ft As the appropriating of the general name of Psalm unto some, doth not prejudice¹, but that the rest are also Psalms, though they be called Prayers, Canticles, Testimonies, and the like: so the application of David's name to certain Psalms, proveth not other Authors of the rest. But the name of Psalm sheweth a spiritual song, apt for musical instrument; and the name of \+it David\+it* by interpretation signifieth, that it particularly pertaineth to the \+it beloved\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Why this and certain others are called the Psalms of David.\ef*
\v 2 He that walketh \f b \fr 14.2 \ft One requisite thing is to be free or cleansed from sin.\f* without spot, and \f c \fr 14.2 \ft The second is to do good.\f* worketh justice.
\v 3 He that speaketh truth in his \f d \fr 14.3 \ft Sincerely in thought,\f* heart, that hath not done guile in his \f e \fr 14.3 \ft word, and\f* tongue. Nor hath \f f \fr 14.3 \ft deed.\f* done evil to his neighbour, and hath not taken \f g \fr 14.3 \ft Nor harkened to detraction.\f* reproach against his neighbour.
\v 4 The malignant is brought to nothing in his sight: but them that fear our Lord, he \f h \fr 14.4 \ft Glory is the reward of good works.\f* glorifieth. He that sweareth to his neighbour, and deceiveth not,
\v 5 That hath not given his money to \f i \fr 14.5 \ft Usury excludeth from heaven.\f* usury, and hath not taken \f k \fr 14.5 \ft Likewise doing wrong for bribes.\f* gifts upon the innocent, He that doth these things, shall not be moved for ever. \ef - \fr 14.5 \fq He that doth these things. \ft whereas this ¹, or any other place of holy Scripture, attributeth salvation to certain good works, neither faith, nor other works are thereby excluded, but presupposed as no less necessary, than those which are mentioned. Especially faith is always requisite ², without which it is impossible to please God, and other virtues either in practise, or in purpose, and preparation of mind, when and where occasion requireth. \fq ¹ \ft Both faith and good works necessary to salvation. \fq ² \ft \+it Heb. 11.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 14.5 \fq Shall not be moved for ever. \ft All states of this world are mutable, ¹and only eternal felicity in heaven shall continue for ever. Therefore this Psalm cannot be understood of the Tabernacle, nor temple of the old Testament, which were but figures of eternal glory. But if so much purity was then requisite, much more all sincerity, and great sanctity are necessary for entrance into heaven. \fq ¹ \ft Only the state of glory is immutable.\ef*
\c 15
\p
\v 1 The \f a \fr 15.1 \ft Stylographia signifieth a thing most worthy to be noted, to wit, Christ crucified:\f* inscription of the title \f b \fr 15.1 \ft and most worthy of the Prophet's consideration.\f* to David himself. Preserve \f c \fr 15.1 \ft Christ as man did often pray, as appeareth in the Gospel.\f* me, o Lord, because I have hoped in thee.
\v 2 I have said to our Lord: Thou art my God, because thou \f d \fr 15.2 \ft Christ's passion was not needful nor profitable to God but to man.\f* needest not my goods.
\v 3 \f e \fr 15.3 \ft God speaketh shewing that Christ should make his marvelous charity known to his Apostles, and other servants.\f* To the Saints that are in his land, he hath made all my wills marvelous in them.
\v 4 Their infirmities were \f f \fr 15.4 \ft Men feeling their infirmities and miseries,\f* multiplied: afterward they \f g \fr 15.4 \ft make haste in seeking remedies.\f* made haste. I will not assemble their \f h \fr 15.4 \ft Sacrifices of idols shall cease in Gentiles.\f* conventicles of blood: neither will I be mindful of their \f i \fr 15.4 \ft Their names shall be changed from heathen to be called Christians.\f* names by my lips.
\v 5 \f k \fr 15.5 \ft Eternal glory consisteth in seeing God.\f* Our Lord the portion of mine inheritance, and of my \f l \fr 15.5 \ft God is the reward of suffering pains for Christ.\f* cup: thou art he, that will restore mine inheritance unto me. \ef - \fr 15.5 \fq Our Lord the portion of mine inheritance. \ft Christ, whom the Jews expected as an earthly conqueror, that should advance himself and them temporally in this world ¹, was indeed, as the children and multitude called him, King of Israel \+it (Joan. 12.)\+it* At which time (as also before) he exercised temporal Jurisdiction, in correcting abuses in the Temple \+it (Mat. 21. Joan. 2.)\+it* And when Pilate demanded of him if he were a King \+it (Joan. 18. v. 37.)\+it* he answered: \+it Thou sayst, that I am a King\+it*. For this I was born, and for this came I into the world, that I should give testimony to the truth. And though he answered withal, that his Kingdom (to wit the possession and use thereof) was not of this world, yet Pilate by God's providence, writ the title, and would not alter it, \+sc Jesus\+sc* of Nazareth King of the Jews. ² But Christ's chief inheritance, and reward of his merits is God himself, as here he professeth by his Prophet David: which is also the only true & perfect inheritance of all Christ's servants, wherefore Clergy men more particularly profess the same, when they first enter into their spiritual state ³, addicting and dedicating themselves to serve God in Ecclesiastical function, not for temporal inheritance, but for a better lot, God himself, who is all Good, and most perfect goodness, true riches, and eternal inheritance. In which election of state to live and serve God in, every Clergy man sayth: \+it Our Lord is the portion of mine inheritance, and of my cup: Thou art he that will restore mine inheritance unto me\+it*. Man calleth it \+it his\+it* inheritance, because he was created to serve God, and for his service to inherit God: which reward though he lost by sin, yet every one returning to God's service, and persevering therein, recovereth by Christ new right and title to the same inheritance, performing their duties in their several vocations. Some travailing in the world, but not loving it ⁴: others sequestered from secular affairs duly administering sacred offices, more peculiarly called Divine service. \fq ¹ \ft Christ a King sometimes exercised temporal jurisdiction. \fq ² \ft God the proper inheritance of Christ, and Christians. \fq ³ \ft Clergy men profess expressly to serve God, for God himself not for temporal profit. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Psal. 61. v. 11. 2. Tim. 2. v. 4.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 \f m \fr 15.6 \ft In division of temporal inheritance, land is measured by cords, as Josue 10. so portions in heaven are given with large measure:\f* Cords are fallen to me in goodly places: for \f n \fr 15.6 \ft Christ also received all nations for his inheritance.\f* mine inheritance is goodly unto me.
\v 7 I will bless our Lord, who hath \f o \fr 15.7 \ft Wisdom to make good election of spiritual things rather than temporal.\f* given me understanding: moreover also even till \f p \fr 15.7 \ft Not only by day, but also by night.\f* night my \f q \fr 15.7 \ft Also my corporal pains give me instruction.\f* veins have rebuked me.
\v 8 I \f r \fr 15.8 \ft Christ had God continually before his eyes: every man ought to think frequently of God.\f* foresaw our Lord in my sight always: because he is \f s \fr 15.8 \ft For god still protecteth the just.\f* at my right hand, that I be not moved. \x - \xt Act. 2. v. 25.\x*
\v 9 For this thing my heart hath been glad, and my tongue hath rejoiced: moreover also my flesh shall rest in hope.
\v 10 Because thou wilt not leave my soul \f t \fr 15.10 \ft In limbo Patrum.\f* in hell: neither wilt give \f v \fr 15.10 \ft Christ's body corrupted not in the grave.\f* thy holy one to see corruption \x - \xt Act. 2. & 3.\x*. Thou hast made \f w \fr 15.10 \ft Death and resurrection is the way to life.\f* the ways of life known to me, thou shalt make me full of joy with \f x \fr 15.10 \ft Perfect glory consisteth in seeing God.\f* thy countenance: delectations on thy right hand, even to \f y \fr 15.10 \ft In eternity.\f* the end. \ef - \fr 15.10 \fq Not leave my soul in hell. \ft How Calvin and Beza sometimes corrupt this text, always pervert the sense ¹, and most absurdly oppose themselves against all ancient holy Fathers, concerning the Article of Christ's descending in soul into that part of hell called \+it Lymbus patrum\+it*, is largely noted \+it Gen. 37. Act. 2. & 1 Pet. 3.\+it* Only here we may not omit to advertise the reader, that some Protestant Bibles permitting the world \+it hell\+it* to remain in the text ², a latter Edition for \+it hell\+it*, putteth \+it grave\+it*, with this only note in the former place, that \+it this is chiefly meant of Christ, by whose Resurrection all his members have immortality\+it* And Act. 2. they repeat their new text by this paraphrasis: \+it Thou shalt not leave me in the grave.\+it* wresting that which pertaineth to the body rising from the grave, to the soul, which was not at all in the grave, all time the body lay there. \fq ¹ \ft Protestants denying that Christ descended into limbus, translate \+it grave\+it* for \+it hell\+it*. \fq ² \ft \+it 1552. 1577. 1603.\+it*\ef*
\c 16
\cd A just man's prayer in tribulation, 10. describing his enemies' cruelty, 13. by way of imprecation foresheweth their destruction, 15. and declareth that the just shall be satisfied in glory. \f - \ft God's providence protecting the just. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The prayer of David. Hear, o Lord my \f a \fr 16.1 \ft In my just cause hear my petition\f* justice: attend my petition. With thine ears hear my prayer, \f b \fr 16.1 \ft seeing I pray sincerely, not in feigned affection.\f* not in deceitful lips. \ef - \fr 16.1 \fq The prayer of David. \ft This Psalm of the matter contained is called a prayer. Which holy David so composed ¹, as was both convenient for himself, being molested with unjust afflictions by the wicked, and for any other just person, or the whole Church in persecution, serving as a spiritual sword to strike the enemies, and as a shield to bear off with patience and fortitude all their forces. \fq ¹ \ft This Psalm called a Prayer is both a sword & buckler in affliction.\ef*
\v 2 From \f c \fr 16.2 \ft Thou that seest all things declare my right against mine adversaries.\f* thy countenance let my judgment proceed: let thine eyes see equities.
\v 3 Thou hast proved \f d \fr 16.3 \ft My intention.\f* my heart, and visited it \f e \fr 16.3 \ft Most secret cogitations.\f* by night: \f f \fr 16.3 \ft By tribulations.\f* by fire thou hast examined me, \f g \fr 16.3 \ft Whose conscience is pure from grievous sin, may pray with this confidence, otherwise repentance is first necessary. But the whole Church may ever pray in this manner, because there be always some just and holy, in respect of whom it is truly called holy.\f* and there is no iniquity found in me.
\v 4 That my mouth speak not the works of men: for the \f h \fr 16.4 \ft For thy precepts.\f* words of thy lips I have kept the \f i \fr 16.4 \ft The narrow way of virtue.\f* hard ways.
\v 5 \f k \fr 16.5 \ft None can of themselves walk rightly, but by God's help.\f* Perfit my passes in thy paths, that my steps be not moved.
\v 6 I have cried, because thou hast heard me, o God: incline thine ear to me, and hear my words.
\v 7 Make thy mercies marvelous, which savest them that hope in thee.
\v 8 From them that resist \f l \fr 16.8 \ft Against thy omnipotent power.\f* thy right hand keep me, as the apple of the eye. Under the shadow of thy wings protect me:
\v 9 from the \f m \fr 16.9 \ft From their cruel and furious countenance.\f* face of the impious, that have afflicted me. Mine enemies have compassed my soul,
\v 10 they have shut up their \f n \fr 16.10 \ft They have shut out all pity or commiseration.\f* fat: their mouth hath spoken pride.
\v 11 Casting me forth now have they compassed me: they have set their eyes to bend them \f o \fr 16.11 \ft They intend utterly to destroy me even to the ground.\f* unto the earth.
\v 12 They have taken me as a lion ready to the prey: and as a lion's whelp dwelling in hid places.
\v 13 Arise Lord, \f p \fr 16.13 \ft Except God prevent, man's industry is not sufficient.\f* prevent him, and supplant him: deliver my soul from the impious, \f q \fr 16.13 \ft Refrain their power, which they have by thy permission, that they may not persecute so much as they intend.\f* thy sword
\v 14 from the enemies of thy hand. Lord from \f r \fr 16.14 \ft This is a prophecy, that the wicked, which are many, shall at the day of judgement be separated from the elect\f* a few out of the land divide them, \f s \fr 16.14 \ft which judgement beginneth sometimes in this life.\f* in their life: their belly is filled of \f t \fr 16.14 \ft The pleasures of this world, which God approveth not, nor acknowledgeth amongst good things.\f* thy secrets. They are filled with children: and they have left their remnants to their little ones.
\v 15 But \f v \fr 16.15 \ft The just shall be approved.\f* I in justice shall appear to thy sight: I shall be \f w \fr 16.15 \ft Nothing doth satiate man's mind, but the sight of God in eternal glory.\f* filled when thy glory shall appear?
\c 17
\cd King David's thanks to God for his often delivery from great dangers. First in general, 9. then more particularly describeth God's terrible manner of fighting for him, 18. against his cruel, and otherwise potent enemies: 22. attributing the same to God's good pleasure, and justice of his cause. 31. Praiseth God, 33. his only protector, 41. and depresser of his enemies. \f - \ft David singularly protected by God. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 17.1 \ft Though literally this Psalm pertained to David, yet in figure of Christ, and of the Church, or every just soul.\f* the end, \f b \fr 17.1 \ft The Holy Ghost inspired David to render these thanks for his often delivery from dangers.\f* to the servant of our Lord David, who spake to our Lord the words of this canticle, in the day, that our Lord delivered him out of the hand of all his enemies, and out of the hand of \f c \fr 17.1 \ft Saul is specially named, because he was his most potent worldly enemy.\f* Saul, and he said: \f 1 \fr 17.1 \ft (2. Reg. 22.)\f* I will \f d \fr 17.1 \ft These first words (as also diverse others) are added, and many changed in this and other Psalms by the Septuagint, who often leaving the Hebrew text render the sense, and so this agreeth in substance with the same Psalm recorded 2. Reg. 22.\f* love thee o Lord \f e \fr 17.1 \ft By whom I am strong.\f* my strength:
\v 2 Our Lord is my firmament, and my refuge, and my deliverer.
\v 3 My God is my helper, and I will hope in him. My protector and the \f f \fr 17.3 \ft High & firm salvation.\f* horn of my salvation, and my receiver. \x - \xt Heb. 2.\x*
\v 4 Praising I will invocate our Lord: & I shall be saved from mine enemies.
\v 5 The \f g \fr 17.5 \ft This is aptly applied to all mankind after his fall, declaring our state in sin, and inducing to penance, in the office of Mass on Septuagesima Sunday.\f* sorrows of \f h \fr 17.5 \ft Mortal flesh subject to death.\f* death have compassed me: and \f i \fr 17.5 \ft Violent incursions of tentations to sin.\f* torrents of iniquity have troubled me.
\v 6 The sorrows of \f k \fr 17.6 \ft Exceeding great afflictions of mind, like torments of hell, which I also fear.\f* hell have compassed me: the \f l \fr 17.6 \ft Secret tentations have deceived me.\f* snares of death have prevented me.
\v 7 In my tribulation I have invocated our Lord, and have \f m \fr 17.7 \ft Earnest prayer is the best remedy in all tribulations.\f* cried to my God: And \f n \fr 17.7 \ft As it is certain that God heard David's prayers, so he assuredly heareth all that sincerely fly unto him.\f* he hath heard my voice from his holy temple: and my cry in his sight, hath entered into his ears.
\v 8 The earth was shaken & trembled: the foundations of mountains were troubled, and were moved, \f o \fr 17.8 \ft God's anger against sin maketh high and lofty things to shake, even the most obstinate presumptuous sinners.\f* because he was wrath with them.
\v 9 \f p \fr 17.9 \ft Divine wrath is like to smoke of the nostrils, or flaming fire, & burning coals.\f* Smoke arose in his wrath: and fire flamed up from his face: coals were kindled from him.
\v 10 He \f q \fr 17.10 \ft God's punishment sometimes cometh so swiftly, as if the heavens bowed towards the earth.\f* bowed the heavens, and descended: and \f r \fr 17.10 \ft God's fury is as a dark desolate night, or horrible mist.\f* darkness under his feet.
\v 11 And he \f s \fr 17.11 \ft Yet when sinners repent, God most speedily, as flying with wings of mercy, comforteth & protecteth them.\f* ascended upon the cherubs, and flew: he flew upon the wings of winds.
\v 12 And he put darkness his \f t \fr 17.12 \ft God being in himself incomprehensible, is also secret in his determinations, and covert in his proceedings or acts.\f* covert, his tabernacle is round about him: darksome water in the clouds of the air.
\v 13 Because of the \f v \fr 17.13 \ft God's splendor oppressing man's sense, yet instructeth him by his marvelous works. Which mystically signifieth, that Christ illuminateth the world by his Apostles, and other preachers denouncing his justice, peace, and his will in all things pertaining to man.\f* brightness in his sight the clouds passed, hail and coals of fire.
\v 14 And our Lord thundered from heaven, and the Highest gave his voice: hail and coals of fire.
\v 15 And he shot his arrows, and dissipated them: he multiplied lightnings, and troubled them.
\v 16 And the fountains of waters appeared, and the foundations of the world were revealed. At thy rebuke, o Lord, at the blast of the spirit of thy wrath.
\v 17 He sent from on high, and took me: and he received me out of many \f w \fr 17.17 \ft From tribulations.\f* waters.
\v 18 He delivered me \f x \fr 17.18 \ft From Saul, Absalom, Moabites, Ammonites, and temporal & spiritual enemies.\f* from my most strong enemies, and from them that hated me: because they were made strong over me.
\v 19 They prevented me in the day of mine affliction: and our Lord was made my protector.
\v 20 And he brought me out into largeness: he saved me, because he \f y \fr 17.20 \ft Of his good pleasure without my deserts.\f* would me.
\v 21 And our Lord will reward me according to my justice, and according to the purity of my hands he will reward me.
\v 22 Because I have kept the ways of our Lord, neither have I done impiously from my God.
\v 23 Because all his judgments are in my sight: and his justices I have not repelled from me.
\v 24 And I shall be immaculate \f z \fr 17.24 \ft By his grace.\f* with him, and shall keep me from mine iniquity.
\v 25 \f z \fr 17.25 \ft He repeateth the 21. verse, inculcating that God will\f* And our Lord will reward me according to my justice: & according to the purity of my hands in the sight of his eyes.
\v 26 With the holy thou shalt be holy, and with the innocent man thou shalt be innocent.
\v 27 And with the elect thou shalt be elect: and with the perverse thou shalt be perverted.
\v 28 Because thou wilt save the humble people: and the eyes of the proud thou wilt humble.
\v 29 Because thou dost illuminate my lamp o Lord: my God illuminate my darkness.
\v 30 Because in thee I shall be delivered from tentation, and in my God I shall \f a \fr 17.30 \ft Pass over all difficulties.\f* go over the wall.
\v 31 My God his \f b \fr 17.31 \ft God's precepts.\f* way is unpolluted: the words of our Lord are examined by fire: he is protector of all that hope in him.
\v 32 For \f c \fr 17.32 \ft One only God, Creator and Saviour of all.\f* who is God but our Lord? Or who is God but our God?
\v 33 God that girded me with strength: and made my way immaculate.
\v 34 That perfited my feet as it were of harts: and setting me upon high things.
\v 35 That teacheth my hands to battle: and hast put mine arms \f d \fr 17.35 \ft Amongst other acts David killed a lion, and a bear, & Goliath. 1. Reg. 17.\f* as a bow of brass.
\v 36 And hast given me the protection of thy salvation: & thy right hand hath received me. And thy discipline hath corrected me unto the end: and thy discipline, the same shall teach me.
\v 37 Thou hast enlarged my passes under me: and my steps are not weakened:
\v 38 I will pursue mine enemies, and overtake them: and will not return till they fail.
\v 39 I will break them, neither shall they be able to stand: they shall fall under my feet.
\v 40 And thou hast girded me with strength to battle: and hast supplanted them that rise against me under me.
\v 41 And \f e \fr 17.41 \ft As God giveth strength to his servants, so he diminisheth the natural strength and courage of his enemies.\f* mine enemies thou hast given me their back, & them that hate me thou hast destroyed.
\v 42 They cried, neither was there that would save them, to our Lord, neither did he hear them.
\v 43 And I will break them to powder, as the dust before the face of wind: as the durt of the streets I will destroy them.
\v 44 Thou wilt deliver me from the contradictions of the people: thou wilt appoint me to be head of the Gentiles.
\v 45 A people \f f \fr 17.45 \ft Conversion of Gentiles to Christ, as the Moabites, Idumeans, and others were subdued by David. 1. Par. 11. 14. 18. 19. 20.\f* which I knew not hath served me: in the hearing of the ear it hath obeyed me.
\v 46 The \f g \fr 17.46 \ft The revolting and reprobation of the Jews, prefigured by Absalom's rebellion and others. \+it 2. Reg. 15. 16.\+it*\f* children being aliens have lied to me, the children aliens are inveterated, and have halted from their paths.
\v 47 Our Lord liveth, and blessed be my God, and the God of my salvation be exalted.
\v 48 O God \f h \fr 17.48 \ft God still protecteth the Church of Christ, as he preserved David.\f* which givest me revenges, & subduest peoples under me, my deliverer from mine angry enemies.
\v 49 And from them that rise up against me thou wilt exalt me: from the unjust man thou wilt deliver me.
\v 50 Therefore will I confess to thee among nations, o Lord: and will say \f i \fr 17.50 \ft Use of Psalms is most frequent in the Church of Gentiles. See the proemial Annotations. \+it page. 2.\+it*\f* a Psalm to thy name.
\v 51 Magnifying the salvations of his king, and doing mercy to his Christ David, and to his seed for ever.
\c 18
\cd God's perfect goodness and glory is shewed by his great works, and by his Apostles, sent with heavenly commission to preach in all tongues to all nations. 6. Christ's coming into the world, and return unto heaven: 8. his immaculate Law: 13. wherein notwithstanding the just shall have need to pray for remission of smaller and daily sins. \f - \ft Propagation of the Catholic faith.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 18.1 \ft Pertaining to the beloved of the new Testament.\f* the end, the Psalm of David.
\v 2 The \f b \fr 18.2 \ft The silent works of God declare his Majesty to them that consider thereof, his Preachers declare the same by words, to all that will hear.\f* heavens shew forth the glory of God, and the firmament declareth the works of his hands.
\v 3 \f c \fr 18.3 \ft The constant course of times sheweth that the same was disposed by God's power, and daily propagation of faith, especially of Christian doctrine, sheweth Christ's power, & assured perpetuity of his Church.\f* Day unto day uttereth word: and night unto night sheweth knowledge.
\v 4 There are \f d \fr 18.4 \ft Some of every language or nation have believed in Christ, receiving the Catholic Religion.\f* no languages, nor speeches, whose voices are not heard.
\v 5 \f e \fr 18.5 \ft S. Paul affirmeth that this hath been fulfilled by the Apostles preaching in all the world. Rom. 10.\f* Their sound hath gone forth into all the earth; and unto the ends of the round world the words of them.
\v 6 He put his tabernacle in \f f \fr 18.6 \ft By the sun a most excellent and superelemental creature, the Prophet describeth Christ, making his course through this world, illuminating, comforting, and strengthening the Church his tabernacle, wherein he perpetually dwelleth.\f* the sun: & himself as a \f g \fr 18.6 \ft Christ the bridegroom, & the Church his bride are never divorced; his love, wisdom, and power, ever conserving her by his immaculate law.\f* bridegroom coming forth of his bride-chamber. He hath rejoiced as a giant to run the way,
\v 7 his coming forth from the top of heaven: And his recourse even to the top thereof: neither is there that can hide himself from his heat.
\v 8 The law of our Lord is immaculate \f h \fr 18.8 \ft The old law was likewise pure in itself and holy, but the new also maketh the observers immaculate.\f* converting souls: the testimony of our Lord is faithful, giving wisdom to little ones. \ef - \fr 18.8 \fq The Law of our Lord is immaculate, converting souls. \ft God's law in it-self being most pure, and immaculate, is the proper means, whereby the Holy Ghost converteth souls from sins to justice ¹. Not that every one is justified, which readeth, heareth, or knoweth the law, but by keeping it, through grace of the Holy Ghost. Who is the author and worker of justice, by disposing the soul to cooperate in manner partly here described: \+it God's\+it* faithful and most true \+it testimony\+it* by his law \+it giveth wisdom\+it*, faith, and knowledge to the humble, his \+it right justices, comfort\+it* the \+it hearts\+it* of the faithful, his \+it clear precept teacheth them\+it* their duty; \+it his holy fear persevereth\+it* with them; \+it his true judgements\+it* are most delectable, and \+it desirable\+it* above all worldly riches or pleasures. So by these and like spiritual motives the soul freely desireth, accepteth, undertaketh, endureth, and by continual assistance of grace observeth God's law; and so meriteth the great reward, which is promised for keeping it. \+it v. 12. 2. Tim. 4. v. 6.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft God converteth and justifieth souls instructing them by his law, and sweetly drawing their free cooperation by grace.\ef*
\v 9 The justices of our Lord be right, making hearts joyful: the precept of our Lord lightsome, illuminating the eyes.
\v 10 The fear of our Lord is holy, permanent for ever and ever: the judgments of our Lord be true, justified in themselves.
\v 11 To be desired above gold and much precious stone: and more sweet above honey and the honey-comb.
\v 12 For thy servant \f i \fr 18.12 \ft How sweet the law of God is, his servants find not by reading, or by hearing only, but by keeping it.\f* keepeth them, in keeping them is \f k \fr 18.12 \ft Conformably to this text the Prophet professeth (Psa. 118. v. 12.) that he kept them for reward, in which place the heretics' translation is corrupted.\f* much reward.
\v 13 Sins \f l \fr 18.13 \ft None in this life knoweth perfectly his own estate, whether he be worthy of love or hate, Eccle. 9. but hopeth and feareth.\f* who understandeth? From my secret sins cleanse me:
\v 14 and from other mens spare thy servant. If \f m \fr 18.14 \ft If mortal sins have not dominion in the soul, it is just: and shall be in time immaculate from all sin.\f* they shall not have dominion over me, then shall I be immaculate; and shall be cleansed from the greatest sin.
\v 15 And the words of my mouth shall be such as may please: and the meditation of my heart in thy sight always. O Lord \f n \fr 18.15 \ft God's helping grace is still necessary to persevere,\f* my helper and my \f o \fr 18.15 \ft as his first grace redeeming man is necessary for our first conversion.\f* Redeemer.
\c 19
\cd Praise to God for Christ's exaltation after his passion: 9. and depression of his enemies. \f - \ft Christ's exaltation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 19.1 \ft Though such a prayer was very fitly made for David, Ezechias, or other Kings of Juda, yet it more properly serveth for Christian Kings and Prelates.\f* the end, the Psalm of David.
\v 2 Our Lord hear \f b \fr 19.2 \ft The King or other superior praying for himself, his subjects pray with him, and for him. It may also be applied to Christ, praying whilst he was mortal, or now praying for his mystical body the Church: but in both these cases our prayers are only necessary for his servants, not for him.\f* thee in the day of tribulation: the name of the God of Jacob protect thee.
\v 3 Send he aid to thee from the holy place: and from Sion defend he thee.
\v 4 Be he mindful of all thy \f c \fr 19.4 \ft The Hebrew word MINCA signifieth sacrifice of flour, and unbloody, so pertaineth to the Eucharist, in form of bread and wine.\f* sacrifice: and be thy holocaust made \f d \fr 19.4 \ft Be acceptable for him, for whom it is offered.\f* fat.
\v 5 Give he unto thee according to thy heart: and confirm he all thy counsel.
\v 6 We shall rejoice \f e \fr 19.6 \ft In the prosperous state of thee our superior.\f* in thy salvation: and in the name of our God we shall be magnified.
\v 7 Our Lord accomplish all thy petitions: now have I known that our Lord hath saved \f f \fr 19.7 \ft His anointed King, or Priest. Mystically faithful people acknowledge the victory of Christ our Saviour over death and all his enemies.\f* his \sc Christ\sc*. He shall hear him from his holy heaven: the salvation of his right hand is \f g \fr 19.7 \ft In great strength, or heavenly forces.\f* in powers.
\v 8 These in chariots, and these in horses: but we will invocate in the name of the Lord our God.
\v 9 \f h \fr 19.9 \ft Men trusting in human and worldly power fall into captivity.\f* They are bound, & have fallen: but we have risen & are set upright.
\v 10 Lord \f i \fr 19.10 \ft By protection of the head the body is also conserved.\f* save the king, and hear us in the day, that we shall invocate thee.
\c 20
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 20.1 \ft Pertaining to the new Testament, principally to Christ, partly to godly and victorious Kings, and generally to all the blessed, which overcome spiritual enemies.\f* the end, the Psalm of David.
\v 2 Lord in \f b \fr 20.2 \ft Christ our King as man overcoming his enemies by his divine power, rejoiceth in victory.\f* thy power the king shall be glad: and upon thy salvation he shall rejoice exceedingly.
\v 3 The \f c \fr 20.3 \ft Christ's most special desire was the salvation of his people.\f* desire of his heart thou hast given him: and of the will of his lips thou hast not defrauded him.
\v 4 Because thou hast \f d \fr 20.4 \ft This most principally verified in Christ, is also applied to Martyrs, which suffer, or are ready in preparation of mind to suffer death for the truth.\f* prevented him in blessings of sweetness, thou hast put on his head a crown of precious stone.
\v 5 He asked life of thee: and thou gavest him length of days for ever, and for ever and ever.
\v 6 Great is his glory in thy salvation: glory and great beauty thou shalt put upon him.
\v 7 Because thou shalt give him \f e \fr 20.7 \ft All Saints receive blessing of glory, but only Christ imparteth such grace to others: for in him all are blessed that are eternally glorified. \+it Gen. 21. Ioan. 1.\+it*\f* to be a blessing for ever and ever: thou shalt make him joyful in gladness with thy countenance.
\v 8 Because the king hopeth in our Lord: and in the mercy of the Highest he shall not be moved.
\v 9 Let \f f \fr 20.9 \ft The just conforming their desires to God's will, do pray that the wicked may be punished. It is also a Prophecy, that so it shall come to pass.\f* thy hand be found of all thy enemies: let thy right hand find all that hate thee.
\v 10 Thou \f g \fr 20.10 \ft And so it followeth in the next verse: \+it Thou shalt put them\+it* &c.\f* shalt put them as an oven of fire \f h \fr 20.10 \ft This also cannot be understood of any but of Christ, who in the general or particular judgement shall give sentence upon the wicked.\f* in the time of thy countenance: our Lord in his wrath \f i \fr 20.10 \ft And immediately hell fire shall devour them.\f* shall trouble them, and fire shall devour them.
\v 11 Their \f k \fr 20.11 \ft All the wicked, and namely the Jews who persecuted Christ, fail in their devices.\f* fruit thou shalt destroy from the land: and their seed from the children of men.
\v 12 Because they have turned the evils upon thee: they have devised counsels, which they could not establish.
\v 13 Because thou shalt put them back: in thy remnants thou shalt prepare their countenance.
\v 14 Be exalted Lord in thy power: \f l \fr 20.14 \ft Whilst the wicked perish, the just rejoice & praise God in song and Psalm.\f* we will chant and sing thy powers.
\c 21
\cd Christ prayeth in his Passion, 7. describeth the acerbity thereof; 20. foresheweth by way of prayer his Resurrection: 23. More clearly the foundation and propagation of his church (27. & 30. interposing the singular food of the most Blessed Sacrament) even to the ends of the earth in all nations. \f - \ft Christ's Passion & effects thereof. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end for the \f a \fr 21.1 \ft Redemption of mankind undertaken by Christ, and performed by his death, began to be shewed by his Resurrection in the morning of the third day. \+it S. Augu.\+it*\f* morning enterprise, the Psalm \f b \fr 21.1 \ft In figure of Christ the \+it beloved of God.\+it*\f* of David. \ef - \fr 21.1 \fq For the morning enterprise. \ft In respect of the end for which Christ suffered, this Psalm is entitled: \+it for the morning enterprise\+it*: that is, for Christ's glorious Resurrection ¹, and other effects of his Passion. Which holy David by the spirit of Prophecy so describeth here long before with diverse particular circumstances, as the Evangelists have since historically recorded, that it may not unfitly be called, \+it The Passion of Jesus Christ according to David.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Christ's Resurrection. The Passion of Christ according to David.\ef*
\v 2 God \f c \fr 21.2 \ft God is God of all his creatures, but in more peculiar sort God of Christ by personal union.\f* my God have respect to me: \f d \fr 21.2 \ft Thou neither deliverest me from dying: nor yieldest me such comfort as thou vouchsafest to other Saints. \+it v. 6. Mat. 27.\+it*\f* why hast thou forsaken me? \f e \fr 21.2 \ft Iam neither delivered from dying, nor comforted in my passion, having undertaken to die for the sins of mankind, & reputed them as mine own words or facts.\f* Far from my salvation are the words of my sins.
\v 3 My God I shall cry \f f \fr 21.3 \ft Albeit I cry by day,\f* by day, \f g \fr 21.3 \ft and by night, on the cross and in the garden, to have the chalice of death removed from me, & shall not be heard,\f* & thou wilt not hear: and by night, and \f h \fr 21.3 \ft yet there is no folly, nor fault in this petition, which is with submission of man's will to God's will.\f* not for folly unto me. \ef - \fr 21.3 \fq Thou wilt not hear. \ft Our B. Saviour seeing his most terrible death imminent, prayed conditionally ¹, \+it if it pleased his heavenly Father\+it*, to have the same removed from him, & was not heard, as the Psalmist here prophesieth. The principal reason was, because God of his divine charity had decreed, that mankind should be redeemed by this death of his Son. Christ also himself of his excellent charity, consented here unto, & therefore persisted not in his conditional prayer, but added absolutely & prayed, that not his own will, but his Fathers might be fulfilled. And in this he was heard ², to his own more glory and other infinite benefits of innumerable souls, as it followeth, \+it v. 25. When I cried to him he heard me\+it*. S. Paul also witnesseth \+it (Heb. 5. v. 7.)\+it* that Christ \+it offering prayers and supplications to him that could save him from death, was heard for his reverence\+it*: that is, in respect of his inestimable merit in human nature united in person to God. Another cause, why Christ was not delivered from violent death ³, as many holy persons were, when they cried to God in distress, as S. Augustine sheweth (Epist. 120. c. 11.) was for example to Christians, whom God will have to suffer temporal afflictions, and death, for the glory of life everlasting, according to S. Peter's doctrine, \+it Christ suffered for us, leaving an example that you may follow his steps\+it*. ⁴ \fq ¹ \ft Christ's conditional prayer was not heard. \fq ² \ft His absolute prayers were always heard. \fq ³ \ft Christ suffered for our example. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 1 Pet. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 But \f i \fr 21.4 \ft Thou from heaven wilt hear when it is convenient.\f* thou dwellest in the holy place, \f k \fr 21.4 \ft Whose wisdom and goodness the Church worthily praiseth.\f* the praise of Israel.
\v 5 In thee \f l \fr 21.5 \ft Patriarchs, Prophets, and other holy men praying in their distresses obtained their requests.\f* our fathers have hoped: they hoped, and thou didst deliver them.
\v 6 They cried to thee, and were \f m \fr 21.6 \ft Were temporally delivered by thy mighty hand from their persecutors.\f* saved: they hoped in thee, and were \f n \fr 21.6 \ft Not frustrate of their humble prayers.\f* not confounded.
\v 7 \f o \fr 21.7 \ft God that comforteth his servants in their tribulations, left Christ without his ordinary consolation, to suffer more than ever any other did.\f* But I am a \f p \fr 21.7 \ft Wicked persecuters respected not Christ as a man, but contemned him, as a very worm\f* worm and no man: a \f q \fr 21.7 \ft as most reproachful of all men\f* reproach of men and \f r \fr 21.7 \ft as the basest of all the people.\f* outcast of the people.
\v 8 \f s \fr 21.8 \ft Almost all became Christ's cruel enemies at the time of his passion, afflicting, blaspheming, and scorning him, as the Evangelists record.\f* All that see me have scorned me: they have spoken with the lips, & wagged the head.
\v 9 \f t \fr 21.9 \ft The Evangelists write how all these things were fulfilled by wicked men, speaking these blasphemies, and reproaches.\f* He hoped in the Lord, let him deliver him: let him save him, because he \f v \fr 21.9 \ft God seemed to be well pleased with Christ, as with his own Son: if it be so, let him deliver him from these afflictions, say these blasphemers.\f* will him.
\v 10 Because thou art he that \f w \fr 21.10 \ft Divine power without man formed me in the womb of my mother a Virgin.\f* hast drawn me out of the womb: my hope from the breasts of my mother.
\v 11 Upon thee I \f x \fr 21.11 \ft As I have no father but thee O God: so without intermission from mine incarnation to this time, I have had thee my protector:\f* have been cast from the matrice: \f y \fr 21.11 \ft leave me not now without comfort, seeing I must die as thou hast determined, and I freely consented: yet leave me not in death but raise me again to life. \+it Psal. 15. v. 9. 10.\+it*\f* from my mother's womb thou art my God,
\v 12 depart not from me. Because tribulation is very nigh: because there is \f z \fr 21.12 \ft Almost all are become mine enemies, and those few that would, cannot help me.\f* not that will help.
\v 13 Many \f a \fr 21.13 \ft Delicate lascivious young men,\f* calves have compassed me: \f b \fr 21.13 \ft and the scribes, Pharisees, and elders of the people have all conspired against me.\f* fat bulls have besieged me.
\v 14 They have \f c \fr 21.14 \ft Condemning me and persuading the people to cry: \+it Crucify, crucify him.\+it*\f* opened their mouth upon me, as a lion ravening and roaring.
\v 15 As \f d \fr 21.15 \ft So weakened with pains of torments, as fluid water not able to consist.\f* water I am poured out: and all \f e \fr 21.15 \ft My bones and strongest parts of my body are weakened, verified, when our Saviour fell down under his cross.\f* my bones are dispersed. \f f \fr 21.15 \ft The part that first and last liveth is weakened as soft wax by heat of the fire, and ready to fail.\f* My heart is made as wax melting in the midst of my belly.
\v 16 My strength is withered as a \f g \fr 21.16 \ft All my powers and radical humidity is dried up, as a potter's vessel is baked in the furnace.\f* potsherd, and my tongue \f h \fr 21.16 \ft Through exceeding great drought, which our Saviour professed on the cross saying: \+it I thirst.\+it*\f* cleaved to my jaws: and thou hast \f i \fr 21.16 \ft Thus thou O God has suffered me to come to the last breath of life, next to death. Yet finally our Saviour gave up his spirit before he should have died. \+it v. 21.\+it*\f* brought me down into the dust of death.
\v 17 Because many \f k \fr 21.17 \ft Again this Royal Prophet recounteth by whom and how our B. Saviour should suffer, even as clear as the Evangelists afterwards have written the history.\f* dogs have compassed me: the counsel of the malignant hath besieged me. They have digged my hands and my feet: \ef - \fr 21.17 \fq They have digged. \ft Of obstinate malice the Jews have corrupted this place (and God knoweth how many others) in the Hebrew text of some editions ¹, reading \+it caari\+it*, which signifieth \+it as a lion\+it*, without all coherence of the sense, for \+it caaru\+it*, they \+it digged\+it* or \+it pierced\+it*; to avoid so plain a prophecy of nailing Christ's hands and feet to the cross. \fq ¹ \ft The Hebrew text corrupted by the Jews.\ef*
\v 18 they have \f l \fr 21.18 \ft Our Saviour's body was so racked on the cross, that his bones might be seen and counted.\f* numbered all my bones. But themselves have \f m \fr 21.18 \ft The persecuters wittingly determined all this cruelty: beheld it with their eyes, and without all compassion persisted in malice, rejoiced, and blasphemed.\f* considered and beheld me:
\v 19 they have \f n \fr 21.19 \ft The soldiers that crucified our Saviour, taking his garments for their prey:\f* divided my garments among them: and upon my \f o \fr 21.19 \ft yet in mystery of his Church divided not his coat.\f* vesture they have cast lot.
\v 20 But thou Lord \f p \fr 21.20 \ft He prophesieth Christ's speedy resurrection.\f* prolong not thy help from me: look toward my defence.
\v 21 Deliver, o God, \f q \fr 21.21 \ft Christ's soul was not separated from his body by force of the torments, but he preventing death freely yielded up his spirit. \+it Ioan. 10. v. 9. & 10.\+it*\f* my soul from the sword: and mine \f r \fr 21.21 \ft The most pure, and sanctified soul, of whose fullness all other just souls are sanctified.\f* only one from \f s \fr 21.21 \ft That it stay not in hell, which devoured all other souls in the old Testament.\f* the hand of the dog.
\v 22 Save me out of the lion's mouth: and my humility from the horns of unicorns.
\v 23 I will \f t \fr 21.23 \ft The propagation of the Church of Christ in all nations.\f* declare thy name to my brethren: in the midst of the Church I will praise thee, \ef - \fr 21.23 \fq I will declare thy name to my brethren. \ft Here it is evident that this Psalm is of Christ ¹, not of David, by S. Paul's allegation \+it (Heb. 2. v. 11. 12.)\+it* saying: He that sanctifieth (to wit Christ) disdained not to call the sanctified his brethren. \fq ¹ \ft This Psalm is of Christ.\ef* \ef - \fr 21.23 \fq In the midst of the Church I will praise thee. \ft After Christ's Passion and Resurrection, in the rest of this Psalm, other two principal points of Christian Religion are likewise prophesied: His perpetual visible Church, and the B. Sacrament of his body. The former is here prophesied by way of inviting \+it all the seed of Jacob to glorify God\+it* (v. 24.) \+it all the seed of Israel to fear him\+it* (v. 25.) to wit, innumerable Christians ¹, the true Israelites, the universal Church in the whole world. As for heretical parts or parcels in the world, such as the Donatists, which going forth from the Catholic Church say: Christ hath lost his great Church, the devil hath taken the whole world from him, and he remaineth only in a part of Africa, they do not praise God (saith S. Augustine) but dishonour God and Christ, as if God were not faithful in his promise ², as if Christ were dispossessed of his Kingdom the Catholic Church. Lest any should reply, that Christ is praised though the Church be decayed, or be very small, the Holy Ghost hath prevented such arguments, saying v. 26: His praise is \+it in the great Church.\+it* Which could neither be verified in the part of Donatists in Afrike, nor now in the part of Protestants since Luther in Europe. Further S. Augustine explicateth ³, & urgeth the verses following in this Psalm, against the same blind, deaf, and obstinate Donatists, who did not, or would not see, nor hear, that \+it all the ends of the earth shall remember, and be converted to our Lord\+it*. The holy Scripture saith not, \+it the ends of the earth, but all the ends\+it*. Well go too (saith this great Doctor) peradventure there is but one verse, thou thoughtest upon something else, thou talkedst with thy brother, when one read this; mark, he repeateth and knocketh upon the deaf: \+it All the families of the Gentiles shall adore in his sight\+it*: Yet the heretic is deaf, he heareth not, let one knock again: \+it Because the Kingdom is our Lord's, and he shall have dominion over the Gentiles\+it*. Hold these three verses brethren. Thus and more S. Augustine against those that think the true Church may fail, or become invisible or obscure. And though it be not in like prosperous state at all times, and in all places, yet it is always conspicuous, and more general than any other congregation professing whatsoever pretended religion. \fq ¹ \ft Prophecy of the visible and universal Church. \fq ² \ft \+it in hunc Psal.\+it* \fq ³ \ft S. Augustine proveth the Church to be always visible and great by this Psalm.\ef*
\v 24 Ye that fear our Lord praise him: all the \f v \fr 21.24 \ft Not the carnal but spiritual children of Jacob, Isaac, and Abraham. \+it Rom. 9. v. 8.\+it*\f* seed of Jacob glorify ye him.
\v 25 Let all the seed of Israel fear him: because he hath not contemned nor despised the petition of the poor. Neither hath he turned away his face from me: and when I cried to him he heard me,
\v 26 With thee is my praise in \f w \fr 21.26 \ft The Church gathered both of Jews and Gentiles is very great and universal.\f* the great Church: I \f x \fr 21.26 \ft Our Saviour promised to give his own body, the bread of life. \+it Ioan. 6.\+it* and performed the same at his last supper.\f* will render my vows in the sight of them that fear him.
\v 27 The \f y \fr 21.27 \ft Those that be faithful, humble, and poor in spirit participate the fruit of this most excellent Sacrament.\f* poor shall eat, and shall be filled: and they shall praise our Lord that seek after him: their hearts \f z \fr 21.27 \ft The effect of this B. Sacrament is the resurrection in glory, and life everlasting.\f* shall live for ever and ever. \ef - \fr 21.27 \fq The poor shall eat. \ft Seeing this Psalm is of Christ, as is proved by S. Paul's allegation of 23. verse, and by the concordance thereof with the Evangelists ¹, it is necessarily deduced, that the \+it vows\+it* mentioned in the former verse, and these words, \+it the poor shall eat and be filled\+it*, cannot be referred to the sacrifices of the old Testament, but to the blessed Sacrifice and Sacrament of the Eucharist, which our Saviour promised, after he had replenished the people with five loaves, and which he instituted at his last supper in presence of his Apostles. So S. Augustine doubteth not to understand it, and to teach, as well in his double exposition of this Psalm as in his 120. Epistle. c. 27. The poor, that is the humble and poor in spirit, shall eat & be filled: the fat ones, or the rich being proud, do also adore and eat, but are not filled. They also are brought to the \+it table of Christ\+it* ², and participate his body and blood, but they \+it adore\+it* only, are not also filled, because they do not imitate (Christ's humility) they disdain to be humble. Where it is clear, this holy father by Christ's body and blood meaneth not bread and wine as signs of his body and blood, for bread and wine cannot be lawfully \+it adored\+it*: neither doth he mean our Lord's body as it was on the cross, or is in heaven, for so it is not eaten, but as it is in forms of bread and wine on \+it Christ's table\+it*, the Altar. \fq ¹ \ft The Eucharist prophesied in this place. \fq ² \ft Real presence of Christ in the Eucharist.\ef*
\v 28 All the ends of the earth \f a \fr 21.28 \ft Gentiles which have been idolaters shall recollect themselves, when they hear Christ preached, and shall turn to true Religion.\f* shall remember, and be converted to our Lord. And all the families of the Gentiles shall adore in his sight.
\v 29 \f b \fr 21.29 \ft Although men can neither deserve to be converted, nor to persevere in justice; yet Christ meriteth to have a continual Kingdom, which is the perpetual visible Catholic Church.\f* Because the kingdom is our Lord's: and he shall have dominion over the Gentiles.
\v 30 All the \f c \fr 21.30 \ft Not only the poor sort, but also the mighty ones of the world shall be converted to Christ, participate his B. Body in the Sacrament,\f* fat ones of the earth have eaten, and \f d \fr 21.30 \ft and religiously adore the same.\f* adored: in his sight shall all fall \f e \fr 21.30 \ft All that adore God, shall adore him in this Sacrament.\f* that descend into the earth.
\v 31 And \f f \fr 21.31 \ft Death being once overcome, it shall have no more power.\f* my soul shall live to him: and my \f g \fr 21.31 \ft Again the Prophet inculcateth the continuance of the Catholic Church.\f* seed shall serve him.
\v 32 The generation to come shall be shewed to our Lord: and the \f h \fr 21.32 \ft Apostles, and other preachers of Christ.\f* heavens shall shew forth his justice to the people that shall be born, whom our Lord hath made.
\c 22
\cd A form of thanks-giving for all spiritual benefits (described under the metaphor of temporal prosperity) even from a sinner's first conversion, to final perseverance, and eternal beatitude. \f - \ft Thanks-giving for God's protection. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Psalm of David. Our \f a \fr 22.1 \ft Christ the good pastor, governeth, protecteth,\f* Lord ruleth me, and nothing shall be wanting to me:
\v 2 in place \f b \fr 22.2 \ft and feedeth his faithful flock.\f* of pasture there he hath placed me \x - \xt Isa. 40. Iere. 23. Ezech. 34. Ioan. 10. 1. Pet. 2. & 5.\x*. Upon \f c \fr 22.2 \ft Baptism of regeneration,\f* the water of refection he hath brought me up:
\v 3 he hath \f d \fr 22.3 \ft which is the first justification.\f* converted my soul. He hath conducted me upon \f e \fr 22.3 \ft God's precepts which the baptised must observe \+it Mat. 28. v. 20.\+it*\f* the paths of justice, \f f \fr 22.3 \ft Salvation is in the name and power of Christ, not in man's own merit.\f* for his name.
\v 4 For although I shall walk in \f g \fr 22.4 \ft In great dangers of tentations to mortal sin,\f* the midst of the shadow of death, I will \f h \fr 22.4 \ft yet by God's grace we may resist.\f* not fear evils: because thou art with me. Thy \f i \fr 22.4 \ft God's direction and law is straight,\f* rod and thy \f k \fr 22.4 \ft and strong.\f* staff: they have comforted me.
\v 5 Thou hast prepared in my sight \f l \fr 22.5 \ft Christ hath prepared for our spiritual food the B. Sacrament of the Eucharist. \+it S. Cyprian, Epist. 65. Eutym. in hunc Psal.\+it*\f* a table \f m \fr 22.5 \ft Against all spiritual enemies, the world, the flesh, and the devil.\f* against them that trouble me. Thou \f n \fr 22.5 \ft Christian souls are also strengthened by the Sacraments of Confirmation, Penance, holy Orders, Matrimony, and Extreme Unction.\f* hast fatted my head with oil: and my \f o \fr 22.5 \ft The B. Sacrament and Sacrifice of Christ's body and blood.\f* chalice inebriating how goodly is it!
\v 6 And thy mercy shall follow me \f p \fr 22.6 \ft Continual and final perseverance is by God's special grace.\f* all the days of my life. And that I may dwell in the house of our Lord, \f q \fr 22.6 \ft In eternal life.\f* in longitude of days.
\c 23
\cd A general prayer of the faithful against all enemies, 4. with desire to be directed in the way of godliness, 7. and to be pardoned for sins past, 9. acknowledging God's meekness, 17. our weakness, necessity of help, and hope in God: 22. concludeth with prayer for the whole Church. \f - \ft A prayer of the faithful. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The \f a \fr 23.1 \ft Christ rising from death the first day of the week, had all power given him in heaven and in earth. \+it Mat. 28.\+it*\f* first of the Sabbath, the Psalm of David. The earth is our Lord's, and \f b \fr 23.1 \ft Not only the soil it-self, but all the fruit, and all that dwell therein are God's.\f* the fullness thereof: the round world, and all that dwell therein.
\v 2 Because he hath founded it upon the seas: and upon the rivers hath prepared it.
\v 3 Who \f c \fr 23.3 \ft Though Christ created and redeemed all, yet only the just shall inherit heaven.\f* shall ascend into the mount of our Lord? Or who shall stand in his holy place?
\v 4 The innocent of hands, and of clean heart, that hath not taken his soul \f d \fr 23.4 \ft Not occupied himself in vain and unprofitable things, but in commendable works.\f* in vain, nor sworn to his neighbour in guile.
\v 5 He shall receive blessing of our Lord: and \f e \fr 23.5 \ft God's mercy goeth before justification: just works follow, and so glory is the reward of all.\f* mercy of God his Saviour.
\v 6 This is \f f \fr 23.6 \ft This sort of people thus serving God, shall receive everlasting bliss.\f* the generation of them that seek him, of them that seek the face of the God of Jacob.
\v 7 Lift up your gates \f g \fr 23.7 \ft The Prophet contemplating in spirit Christ's Ascension, inviteth Angels to receive him, and (by prosopopoeia) speaketh also to the gates of heaven, by which he is to enter.\f* ye Princes, and be ye lifted up, o eternal gates: and the King of glory shall enter in.
\v 8 \f h \fr 23.8 \ft Angels answer admiring, & demanding as in a dialogue, how Christ is become so glorious.\f* Who is this King of glory? Our Lord \f i \fr 23.8 \ft The Prophet answereth that Christ by his power hath overcome all enemies in battle.\f* strong & mighty, our Lord mighty in battle.
\v 9 \f k \fr 23.9 \ft Again he willeth Angels to open the gates, and biddeth the gates to enlarge themselves.\f* Lift up your gates ye Princes, and be ye lifted up, o eternal gates: and the King of glory shall enter in.
\v 10 \f l \fr 23.10 \ft The Angels demand as before:\f* Who is this King of glory? \f m \fr 23.10 \ft The Prophet answereth, That Christ is Lord also of Angels, and all heavenly powers under God.\f* The Lord of powers he is the King of glory.
\c 24
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 24.1 \ft This Psalm pertaineth more properly to the new testament. And is artificially composed: the verses beginning with distinct letters in order of the Hebrew Alphabet, to the last verse.\f* the end, the Psalm of David. To thee, O Lord, I have lifted up \f b \fr 24.1 \ft My mind to be attentive.\f* my soul:
\v 2 my God in thee is my confidence, let me \f c \fr 24.2 \ft Not be frustrate of my petition.\f* not be ashamed.
\v 3 Neither let mine enemies scorn me: for all \f d \fr 24.3 \ft That patiently expect the time when God will assist.\f* that expect thee, shall not be confounded.
\v 4 \f e \fr 24.4 \ft This manner of praying is frequent in the Psalms, signifying as a prophecy, that so it will come to pass, and the conformity of the just to God's justice.\f* Let all be confounded that do unjust things in vain. Lord shew me thy ways: and teach me thy paths.
\v 5 Direct me \f f \fr 24.5 \ft In true faith and religion.\f* in thy truth, and teach me: because thou art God my Saviour, and thee have I expected \f g \fr 24.5 \ft All our life we must desire more and more knowledge of true doctrine.\f* all the day.
\v 6 Remember, O Lord, thy commiserations, and thy mercies: that are from the beginning of the world.
\v 7 The sins \f h \fr 24.7 \ft From the first use of reason, at which time many are careless,\f* of my youth, and \f i \fr 24.7 \ft & negligent to learn how to serve God.\f* my ignorances do not remember. According to thy mercy remember thou me: for thy goodness, O Lord.
\v 8 Our Lord is \f k \fr 24.8 \ft As God is sweet in giving good motions:\f* sweet, and \f l \fr 24.8 \ft so he is severe to them that resist his grace.\f* righteous: for this cause he will give a law to them that sin in the way.
\v 9 He will direct the mild in judgment: he will teach the meek his ways.
\v 10 All the ways of our Lord be \f m \fr 24.10 \ft God mercifully preventeth with his grace:\f* mercy and \f n \fr 24.10 \ft and justly rewardeth good works.\f* truth, to them that seek after his \f o \fr 24.10 \ft God's law is his covenant with man:\f* testament and his \f p \fr 24.10 \ft and testimony of his will.\f* testimonies.
\v 11 For thy name, o Lord, thou wilt be propitious to my sin: for \f q \fr 24.11 \ft Sin in respect of aversion from God is great, & needeth his grace.\f* it is much.
\v 12 Who is the man that \f r \fr 24.12 \ft He that feareth God, which is the beginning of wisdom, receiveth five spiritual commodities here mentioned:\f* feareth our Lord? He \f 1 \fr 24.12 \ft 1. God instructeth him by his law:\f* appointeth him a law in the way that he hath chosen.
\v 13 His soul \f 1 \fr 24.13 \ft 2. bestoweth all necessaries upon him:\f* shall abide in good things: and \f 2 \fr 24.13 \ft 3. others shall imitate his good example.\f* his seed shall inherit the land.
\v 14 Our Lord is \f 1 \fr 24.14 \ft 4. God will protect him:\f* a firmament to them that fear him: & \f 2 \fr 24.14 \ft 5. According to God's covenant he shall enjoy the manifest sight of God for his eternal reward.\f* his testament that it may be made manifest to them.
\v 15 Mine eyes are always to our Lord: because he will pluck my feet out of the snare.
\v 16 Have respect to me, and have mercy on me: because I am \f s \fr 24.16 \ft Man's weakness without God's help.\f* alone and poor.
\v 17 The tribulations of my heart are multiplied: deliver me from \f t \fr 24.17 \ft Tribulations cannot be avoided, but must necessarily be suffered: therefore, o God, give us grace to pass through them without sin.\f* my necessities.
\v 18 See my \f v \fr 24.18 \ft Mine affliction.\f* humiliation and my labour, and \f w \fr 24.18 \ft Take away the cause, and affliction will be mitigated.\f* forgive all my sins.
\v 19 Behold mine enemies, because they are multiplied, and with \f x \fr 24.19 \ft Wicked men of hatred do endeavour to draw others into sin.\f* unjust hatred hated me.
\v 20 Keep my soul, and deliver me: I shall \f y \fr 24.20 \ft Those that hope in God shall never be confounded.\f* not be ashamed, because I hoped in thee.
\v 21 The innocent and righteous have cleaved to me: because I expected thee.
\v 22 \f z \fr 24.22 \ft All the letters of the Alphabet being complete in this Psalm, this last verse beginneth with \+it Pere, Redeem,\+it* praying God to redeem and deliver Israel, that is, the whole Church, from tribulations.\f* Deliver Israel, o God, out of all his tribulations.
\c 25
\cd David in banishment among the Philistines, trusteth in the justice of his cause, 9. and prayeth God earnestly to deliver him, that he may with more freedom and commodity serve him as he desireth. \f - \ft David's prayer distressed in persecution. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 25.1 \ft This Psalm is also a convenient prayer for any Christian in tribulation.\f* the end the Psalm of David. Judge \f b \fr 25.1 \ft Be thou, o God, arbiter of the cause between Saul and me, thou knowest mine innocency in this behalf, though I am unjustly charged by Saul and his friends.\f* me, o Lord, because I have walked in my innocency: and hoping in our Lord I shall not be weakened.
\v 2 \f c \fr 25.2 \ft Lest perhaps I be not so innocent as I desire, and as in respect of Saul I hope that I am, do thou O God prove me as thou wilt by tribulations.\f* Prove me Lord, and tempt me: burn my reins and my heart.
\v 3 Because thy mercy is before mine eyes: and I am well pleased in thy truth.
\v 4 I \f d \fr 25.4 \ft David in confidence of a good conscience, and zeal against the wicked, allegeth his sincere proceeding, more than ordinary men may do, God so inspiring him extraordinarily.\f* have not sitten with the Council of vanity: and with them that do unjust things I will not enter in.
\v 5 I have hated the Church of the malignant: and with the impious I will not sit. \ef - \fr 25.5 \fq I have hated the Church of the malignant. \ft Holy David forced by reason of persecution to dwell amongst Infidels the Philistians ¹; after he had twice spared King Saul's life (1. Reg. 24. v. 5. &. c. 26. v. 9.) lamented (v. 9.) how great affliction it was to him, \+it to be cast out, that he could not dwell in the inheritance of our Lord\+it* (where God was rightly served) and that his enemies had done so much as in them lay, to make him fall into idolatry, by their fact, as it were saying: \+it Go serve strange gods\+it*. Nevertheless his zeal was such, that (as he here professeth) \+it he hated the Church of the malignant\+it*: that is, the congregations of all miscreants: & his immaculate religious purity was so perfect, that he would not so much as in external shew conform his actions to theirs, in matters of religion, nor yield his bodily presence in their conventicles, but said: \+it With the impious I will not sit\+it*. Instructing us Christians (for the word \+it to the end\+it* in the title sheweth that this Psalm pertaineth also to us) that we must both \+it hate the Church or congregations of the malignant\+it*, to wit, of Paynims, Jews, Turks, and Heretics, and \+it not sit\+it*, nor personally be present with them in the exercise of their false pretended religions. \fq ¹ \ft A singular great affliction to be hindered from God's true service. Christians must abhor, and abstain from all conventicles of Heretics and other Infidels.\ef*
\v 6 I \f e \fr 25.6 \ft The rest of this Psalm every Priest reciteth in Mass, before he offer the holy Sacrifice, professing & putting himself in memory, that he must only communicate with 5. \+it I have hated the Church of the malignant.]\+it* Holy David forced by reason of persecution to dwell\f* will wash my hands among innocents: \f f \fr 25.6 \ft and so approach to the Altar. \+it prefigured Levit. 6. v. 4.\+it*\f* and will compass thy altar, o Lord:
\v 7 That I \f g \fr 25.7 \ft Shutting the ears of my heart from evil and vain thoughts I will attend to godly inspirations,\f* may hear the voice of praise, and \f h \fr 25.7 \ft and so with mental prayer and external voice (as the holy order of this sacred office requireth) praise thee, o God, in thy marvelous works.\f* shew forth all thy marvelous works.
\v 8 Lord I have loved \f i \fr 25.8 \ft I cannot but singularly love the excellency of this place dedicated to thy service: where is true faith, unity, and charity of thy people, the guard of holy Angels, the administration of sacred mysteries, assistance of the Holy Ghost, real presence of Christ our Lord, all replenished with Divine majesty.\f* the beauty of thy house, and the place of \f k \fr 25.8 \ft This representeth unto me the glorious heavenly kingdom of God and all Saints.\f* the habitation of thy glory.
\v 9 \f l \fr 25.9 \ft Suffer me not therefore to be contaminate by the wicked, nor to be devoured with them.\f* Destroy not, o God, my soul with the impious, and my life with bloody men.
\v 10 In whose \f m \fr 25.10 \ft They are still ready to commit more and more iniquities.\f* hands are iniquities: their \f n \fr 25.10 \ft Themselves being corrupted, endeavour by gifts of worldly commodities to corrupt others.\f* right hand is replenished with gifts.
\v 11 But \f o \fr 25.11 \ft Every one ought so to purge his conscience, that he may be innocent or free from great sins.\f* I have walked in mine innocency: \f p \fr 25.11 \ft Deliver me from this necessity of dwelling among the wicked.\f* redeem me, and have mercy on me.
\v 12 My \f q \fr 25.12 \ft I intend to walk right.\f* foot hath stood in the direct way: in \f r \fr 25.12 \ft I desire to praise thee amongst thy true faithful servants.\f* the Churches I will bless thee o Lord.
\c 26
\cd David being in great distress through persecution, and having assured confidence in God, describeth the great security of God's protection, 7. sheweth the same experienced in himself, 12. prayeth for continuance thereof 13. and encourageth his own soul, in hope of life everlasting, to persevere in virtue. \f - \ft An other confident prayer of David in tribulation. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Psalm of David \f a \fr 26.1 \ft Before his second anointing, as is probable. \+it 2. Reg. 2.\+it*\f* before he was anointed. Our Lord is \f b \fr 26.1 \ft Against ignorance God illuminateth his servants.\f* my illumination, and \f c \fr 26.1 \ft Against infirmity he giveth strength.\f* my salvation, \f d \fr 26.1 \ft So he needeth not to fear any man's malice, subtlety, nor force. \+it Luc. 21. v. 15.\+it*\f* whom shall I fear? Our Lord is the protector of my life, of whom shall I be afraid?
\v 2 Whilst the harmful approach upon me, to eat my flesh. Mine enemies that trouble me, themselves are weakened and are fallen.
\v 3 If camps stand together against me, my heart shall not fear. If battle rise up against me, in this will I hope.
\v 4 One thing I have asked of our Lord, this will I seek for, that I \f e \fr 26.4 \ft How special a benefit David esteemed it to be in the Catholic Church, the only true house of God!\f* may dwell in the house of our Lord all the days of my life. That I may see the pleasantness of our Lord, and visit his temple.
\v 5 Because he hath \f f \fr 26.5 \ft Albeit the spiritual or carnal enemy seek to overthrow me, yet I am secure in the Catholic Church.\f* hid me in his tabernacle: in the day of evils he hath protected me, in the \f g \fr 26.5 \ft God either suffereth not the enemy to find his servant,\f* secret of his tabernacle.
\v 6 In \f h \fr 26.6 \ft or not to be able to hurt him spiritually.\f* a rock he hath exalted me: and now he hath \f i \fr 26.6 \ft When a martyr or confessor dieth, then he getteth the victory against the persecutors.\f* exalted my head over mine enemies. I \f k \fr 26.6 \ft Diligently recounting all thy benefits, I render thanks by sacrifice and praise.\f* have gone round about, and have immolated in his tabernacle an host of jubilation: I \f l \fr 26.6 \ft Not only in heart, but also singing with loud voice and instrument.\f* will sing, and say a Psalm to our Lord.
\v 7 Hear, o Lord, my voice, wherewith I have cried to thee: have mercy on me, and hear me.
\v 8 My \f m \fr 26.8 \ft In my inward sincere cogitations I desire and seek that I may see thee\f* heart hath said to thee, my face hath sought thee out: \f n \fr 26.8 \ft face to face. \+it 1. Cor. 13. v. 12.\+it*\f* thy face, o Lord, I will seek.
\v 9 Turn \f o \fr 26.9 \ft In the mean time, o Lord, grant me thy favour,\f* not away thy face from me: \f p \fr 26.9 \ft leave me not, though thou be angry with me.\f* decline not in wrath from thy servant. Be \f q \fr 26.9 \ft He speaketh in the person of orphans.\f* thou my helper: forsake me not, neither despise me, o God my Saviour.
\v 10 because \f r \fr 26.10 \ft Though carnal parents forsake the just man in tribulation, yet God hath then most special care of him.\f* my father and my mother have forsaken me: but our Lord hath taken me.
\v 11 Give me \f s \fr 26.11 \ft Establish my heart in thy law.\f* a law, o Lord, in thy way: \f t \fr 26.11 \ft Conserve me in the right way, which thou hast already taught me: and it is the more necessary, because mine enemies labour to pervert me.\f* and direct me in the right path, because of mine enemies.
\v 12 Deliver me not into \f v \fr 26.12 \ft The wills.\f* the souls of them that trouble me: because \f w \fr 26.12 \ft False witnesses accused David, others accused Christ: \+it Mat. 26.\+it* others do still accuse the just \+it Mat. 5.\+it*\f* unjust witnesses have risen up against me: and iniquity hath lied \f x \fr 26.12 \ft The wicked please themselves in lying, but the chief hurt finally turneth upon themselves.\f* to it-self.
\v 13 I \f y \fr 26.13 \ft The Prophet and all just men are comforted by God, and hope of reward in heaven.\f* believe to see the good things of our Lord, in the land of the living.
\v 14 \f z \fr 26.14 \ft The just exhorteth his own soul to patience,\f* Expect our Lord, do manfully: and \f a \fr 26.14 \ft fortitude,\f* let thy heart take courage, and \f b \fr 26.14 \ft and longanimity.\f* expect thou our Lord. \x - \xt Psal. 30.\x*
\c 27
\cd David prayeth to be defended from the eternal destruction of the wicked, 4. which (by way of imprecation, or conformity to God's justice) he prophesieth. 6. Feeling by inspiration that his prayer is heard, rendereth thanks to God, 9. and prayeth for all the people. \f - \ft Another prayer of David for delivery from evils. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A psalm to David himself. To thee, o Lord, I will cry, my God \f a \fr 27.1 \ft Omit not to comfort me.\f* keep not silence from me: lest at any time thou hold thy peace from me, & I shall be like to them that go down into the lake.
\v 2 Hear, o Lord, the voice of my petition whilst I pray to thee: whilst I lift up my hands to thy holy temple.
\v 3 \f b \fr 27.3 \ft Suffer me not to be overcome: for God tempteth none to evil. Iac. 1.\f* Draw me not together with sinners: and with them that work iniquity destroy me not. Which speak peace with their neighbour, but evils in their hearts.
\v 4 \f c \fr 27.4 \ft The just in zeal of justice pray that sin may be punished.\f* Give them according to their works, & according to the wickedness of their inventions. According to the works of their hands give unto them: render them their retribution.
\v 5 Because they have \f d \fr 27.5 \ft Ignorance doth not excuse when men may and will not understand.\f* not understood the works of our Lord, and in the works of his hands thou shalt destroy them, & \f e \fr 27.5 \ft God saveth not without our cooperation with his grace.\f* not build them up.
\v 6 blessed be our Lord: because he hath heard the voice of my petition.
\v 7 Our Lord is my helper, and my protector: in him my heart hath hoped, and I was holpen. And \f f \fr 27.7 \ft Being comforted in spirit my body is as it were refreshed.\f* my flesh flourished again: & \f g \fr 27.7 \ft Freely & gladly.\f* with my will I will confess to him.
\v 8 Our Lord is the strength of his people: and he is \f h \fr 27.8 \ft God protecteth and prospereth the King's good endeavours for his people.\f* the protector of the salvations of his anointed.
\v 9 Save \f i \fr 27.9 \ft As Psal. 19. and often elsewhere the subjects pray for their Superior: so mutually the superior prayeth for the subjects.\f* thy people, o Lord, and bless thine inheritance: and rule them, and extol them for ever.
\c 28
\cd The royal Prophet seeing in spirit the most sacred Mysteries, brought by Christ into this world, inviteth all to offer their best things, even themselves wholly, as sacrifice of thanks, for so excellent benefits, preached with magnificence. 5. Whereby innumerable are gathered into his Church, here replenished with grace, and in heaven with glory. \f - \ft The Church of Christ endowed with excellent mysteries. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Psalm of David, in the consummation \f a \fr 28.1 \ft \+it 2. Reg. 6. v. 17. 1. Par. 16. v. 1.\+it*\f* of the tabernacle. Bring \f b \fr 28.1 \ft Offer sacrifice of thanks for the singular benefits after recounted in this Psalm.\f* to our Lord ye children of God: bring to our Lord \f c \fr 28.1 \ft Rams were of the more principal things that were offered in the law of Moyses. But the sons of rams import in mystical sense better hosts than rams.\f* the sons of rams. \ef - \fr 28.1 \fq In consummation of the tabernacle. \ft The seventy Interpreters testify by adding this title ¹, that King David made this Psalm (as he did also some others) when the Ark of God was brought into the tabernacle ², which he had pitched for it in Sion. \+it 2 Reg. 6. 1. Paral. 16.\+it* Wherein he saw by prophetical spirit, and here uttereth other far greater mysteries, & more excellent benefits, brought into the world by Christ, and preached by him, and his Apostles, than agreed to the Ark, or the time of the old Testament; but are verified in the admirable fruit of innumerable people of all nations, and of many great Potentates converted to Christianity. \fq ¹ \ft Mysteries of the Catholic Church prophesied in this Psalm. \fq ² \ft \+it 104. 105. &c.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 Bring to our Lord \f d \fr 28.2 \ft The first thing in sacrifice is to glorify, honour, and adore God in sincerity of spirit,\f* glory and honour, bring to our Lord glory unto his name: adore ye our Lord \f e \fr 28.2 \ft in his holy Catholic Church.\f* in his holy court.
\v 3 The voice of our Lord \f f \fr 28.3 \ft Here is a greater matter intimated than happened in the bringing of the Ark into a tabernacle prepared in Sion, when David danced, and offered hosts for sacrifice, others joining with him in that solemnity. But this \+it voice of our Lord upon waters\+it* is rather verified of our Blessed Saviour's own preaching with\f* upon waters, the God of \f g \fr 28.3 \ft \+it majesty\+it*\f* majesty hath \f h \fr 28.3 \ft \+it thundering,\+it* & by his Apostles upon\f* thundered: Our Lord upon \f i \fr 28.3 \ft \+it many waters,\+it* many nations:\f* many waters.
\v 4 The voice of our Lord \f k \fr 28.4 \ft \+it in power\+it* of miracles,\f* in power: the voice of our Lord \f l \fr 28.4 \ft \+it in magnificence\+it* preaching, as \+it having\+it* indeed \+it power, not as the Scribes and Pharisees. Mat. 7. v. 29.\+it*\f* in magnificence.
\v 5 The voice of our Lord \f m \fr 28.5 \ft \+it Breaking cedars;\+it* among innumerable others, converting highest Potentates\f* breaking cedars: and our Lord shall break the \f n \fr 28.5 \ft \+it of Libanus;\+it* Emperors, Kings, and greatest Princes of the world:\f* cedars of Libanus:
\v 6 And he shall break them in pieces as a \f o \fr 28.6 \ft \+it as a calf of Libanus;\+it* so meekly submitting themselves to Christ's yoke, and spiritual obedience of his Church.\f* calf of Libanus. \f p \fr 28.6 \ft All which is done by Christ our Lord \+it the beloved\+it* of God,\f* And the beloved as \f q \fr 28.6 \ft \+it as the son of unicorns,\+it* is most tenderly beloved by the parents.\f* the son of unicorns.
\v 7 The voice of our Lord \f r \fr 28.7 \ft This \+it voice of our Lord dividing the flame of fire.\+it* The Holy Ghost proceeding from the Father and the Son, came upon the Apostles, as in divided tongues of fire.\f* dividing the flame of fire.
\v 8 The voice of our Lord \f s \fr 28.8 \ft Wherewith \+it the desert,\+it* the Gentiles of the wide and wild world, \+it were shaken\+it* and moved,\f* shaking the desert, and our Lord shall move \f t \fr 28.8 \ft the \+it desert of Cades,\+it* some of the Jews also compunct in heart with remorse of conscience, hearing the voice of Saint Peter and other Apostles.\f* the desert of Cades.
\v 9 The voice of our Lord \f v \fr 28.9 \ft \+it The same voice of our lord preparing harts,\+it* inspiring the minds of men with speed, like hinds and does to ascend the high hills of free and perfect life, in contemplative virtues.\f* preparing \f 1 \fr 28.9 \ft \+it cervos.\+it*\f* harts, and he shall discover thick woods \f w \fr 28.9 \ft So our Saviour shall \+it discover the thick woods,\+it* reveal the hidden Mysteries of the old Law, by preaching Christian doctrine, and use of Christian Religion\f* : and \f x \fr 28.9 \ft \+it in his holy Temple,\+it* the Catholic Church, wherein all true Christians shall \+it glorify God.\+it*\f* in his temple all shall say glory.
\v 10 Our Lord maketh \f y \fr 28.10 \ft Making the great aboundance of people, who are like \+it the sea when it overfloweth the land,\+it* to dwell in the same Church.\f* to inhabit the flood: and our Lord \f z \fr 28.10 \ft Christ \+it our Lord sitting,\+it* & ruling \+it King\+it* over all \+it for ever:\+it*\f* shall sit King for ever.
\v 11 Our Lord \f a \fr 28.11 \ft by his grace \+it giving strength to his people,\+it* to pass through the tentations of this life,\f* will give strength to his people: our Lord \f b \fr 28.11 \ft and \+it bless\+it* the just\f* will bless his people in peace.
\c 29
\cd King David by voice and instrument rendereth thanks to God for his peaceable state in the Kingdom, 5. inviteth others to rejoice in God's benefits, teaching by his own example that God sometimes giveth more comfort, sometimes sheweth his wrath, but all for our good. \f - \ft David rendereth thanks for his establishment in his Kingdom. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A \f a \fr 29.1 \ft The general name of Psalm common to this whole book containing in all 150. is more particularly appropriated to some, which more specially were played upon musical instruments, as on the Psalter, Harp, &c. Others are called Canticles, which were most usually sung with human voices. So this, called \+it a Psalm of Canticle,\+it* signifieth that voices began the music, and instruments were adjoined. As contrarywise others are called \+it Canticles of Psalms,\+it* where instruments began and voices followed.\f* Psalm of Canticle, \f b \fr 29.1 \ft After many great tribulations, King David prospering built an excellent house or palace \+it (2. Reg. 5. v. 11. Paralip. 14. v. 1.)\+it* And at his first dwelling therein, made this Psalm, beginning himself to sing the same with voice, other musicians joined with him in praises of God and thanks-giving for his benefits.\f* in the dedication of David's house.
\v 2 I will \f c \fr 29.2 \ft Though God in himself is most high, and neither needeth, nor can be exalted by men, yet the royal Prophet knew it was his duty to sing thanks and praises to him,\f* exalt thee, o Lord \f d \fr 29.2 \ft for his delivery from many troubles and dangers.\f* because thou hast received me: neither hast \f e \fr 29.2 \ft Not suffering his enemies to be delighted in his ruin.\f* delighted mine enemies over me.
\v 3 O Lord my God I have cried to thee, and thou hast \f f \fr 29.3 \ft Conserved my body in health amongst innumerable dangers.\f* healed me.
\v 4 Lord thou hast \f g \fr 29.4 \ft Preserved my soul from greater dangers of sins, and so from hell.\f* brought forth my soul out of hell: thou hast saved me from them that go down into the lake.
\v 5 Sing to our Lord \f h \fr 29.5 \ft Ye that are just and holy praise God for it, from whom it cometh, and not from yourselves:\f* ye his Saints: and \f i \fr 29.5 \ft confess his mere goodness without your deserts.\f* confess to the memory of his holiness.
\v 6 Because \f k \fr 29.6 \ft When he is angry,\f* wrath is in his indignation: \f l \fr 29.6 \ft yet he meaneth well unto us.\f* and life in his will. At \f m \fr 29.6 \ft The state of a just man's life is often changed from sorrow to comfort, and from comfort to sorrow.\f* evening shall weeping abide: and in the morning gladness.
\v 7 And I said in my aboundance: \f n \fr 29.7 \ft Though we suppose ourselves firmly established:\f* I will not be moved for ever.
\v 8 O Lord \f o \fr 29.8 \ft yet God of his good will towards us sometimes giveth \+it strength,\+it* and courage,\f* in thy will thou hast given strength to my beauty. Thou hast \f p \fr 29.8 \ft sometimes suffereth us to our own weakness,\f* turned away thy face from me, and I became troubled.
\v 9 To thee o Lord \f q \fr 29.9 \ft therefore we must still \+it cry and pray\+it* for God's help,\f* I will cry: and I will pray to my God.
\v 10 \f r \fr 29.10 \ft in manner here expressed or the like.\f* What profit is in my blood, whilst I descend into corruption? Shall dust confess to thee, or declare thy truth?
\v 11 Our Lord hath heard, and had mercy on me: our Lord is become my helper.
\v 12 Thou hast turned my mourning into joy unto me: thou hast cut my sackcloth, and hast compassed me with gladness.
\v 13 That \f s \fr 29.13 \ft Finally in this my good state\f* my glory may sing to thee, and I be not compunct: Lord my God for ever \f t \fr 29.13 \ft I shall always confess and praise thee.\f* will I confess to thee.
\c 30
\cd A prayer of a just man exceedingly afflicted, still confident in God. 11. Describing his many calamities (in respect of his enemies) undeserved, 18. prayeth for his own delivery, and their just punishment. 20. Praiseth and thanketh God for his goodness. 24. exhorteth all others to do the same. \f - \ft How to pray in affliction. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 30.1 \ft Pertaining to the new Testament:\f* the end, the Psalm of David, for \f b \fr 30.1 \ft especially to the just troubled: & almost distracted in mind in great affliction. \+it See. v. 23.\+it*\f* excess of mind.
\v 2 In thee o Lord \f c \fr 30.2 \ft How grievously soever I am afflicted yet I trust in thee,\f* have I hoped, \f d \fr 30.2 \ft therefore I pray thus.\f* let me not be confounded for ever: in thy justice deliver me. \x - \xt Psa. 70.\x*
\v 3 Incline thine ear to me, make haste to deliver me. Be unto me for a God protector: and for a house of refuge, that thou mayst save me.
\v 4 Because thou art my strength, and my refuge: and for thy name thou wilt conduct me, and wilt nourish me.
\v 5 Thou wilt bring me out of this snare, which they have hid for me: because thou art my protector.
\v 6 Into \f e \fr 30.6 \ft I offer and resign myself to thee.\f* thy hands I commend my spirit: thou hast \f f \fr 30.6 \ft Thou hast often delivered me. This verse is most aptly applied in all resignations of our souls to God's will, namely whensoever we receive the B. Sacrament: likewise in the hour of death, and at many other times.\f* redeemed me, o Lord God of truth. \x - \xt Luc. 23.\x*
\v 7 Thou hast hated them that observe vanities, unprofitably. But I have hoped in our Lord:
\v 8 I shall rejoice and be joyful in thy mercy. Because thou hast respected my humility, thou hast saved my soul out of necessities.
\v 9 Neither \f g \fr 30.9 \ft not suffered me to be shut up.\f* hast thou shut me up in the hands of the enemy: thou hast set my feet in a large place.
\v 10 Have mercy upon me, o Lord, because I am in tribulation: mine eye is troubled for wrath, my soul, and my belly:
\v 11 Because \f h \fr 30.11 \ft All my parts external and internal, body and mind are troubled.\f* my life is decayed for sorrow: and my years for groanings. My strength is weakened for poverty; and my bones are troubled.
\v 12 Above all mine enemies I am made a reproach, both to my neighbours exceedingly: and \f i \fr 30.12 \ft My friends dare not converse with me, lest they incur displeasure for my sake.\f* a fear to my acquaintance. They that saw me, fled forth from me:
\v 13 I am forgotten, from the heart as one dead. I am made as a vessel destroyed:
\v 14 because I have heard the reprehension of many that abide round about: In that whilst, they assembled together against me, they consulted to take my soul.
\v 15 But I have hoped in thee. O Lord: I said, Thou art my God:
\v 16 my lots are in thy hands. Deliver me out of the hands of my enemies, and from them that persecute me.
\v 17 \f k \fr 30.17 \ft Make thy light so shine in my soul, that I may understand that is right.\f* Illustrate thy face upon thy servant, \f l \fr 30.17 \ft And through thy mercy deliver me from the force of mine adversaries.\f* save me in thy mercy:
\v 18 Lord let me not be confounded, because I have invocated thee. Let the impious be ashamed, and brought down into hell:
\v 19 let the deceitful lips be made mute. Which speak iniquity against the just, \f m \fr 30.19 \ft So proudly behaving themselves, as if they had no superior, neither in earth nor in heaven, to whom they shall at last render account:\f* in pride and \f n \fr 30.19 \ft and abusing their present power and authority, which they have of God.\f* abuse.
\v 20 How great is the multitude of thy sweetness, o Lord which thou \f o \fr 30.20 \ft As yet in this present life the reward of the just is hid:\f* hast hid for them that fear thee. Thou hast perfited it for them that hope in thee, \f p \fr 30.20 \ft but shall be made manifest in sight of all men.\f* in the sight of the children of men.
\v 21 Thou \f q \fr 30.21 \ft In the mean time the just is in great estimation in the secret knowledge of God.\f* shalt hide them in the secret of \f r \fr 30.21 \ft title of honour, as we speak to a king; \+it your majesty:\+it* or to a noble man, \+it your Lordship.\+it*\f* thy face from the disturbance of men. Thou shall protect them in thy tabernacle from the contradiction of tongues.
\v 22 Blessed be our Lord: because he hath made his mercy marvelous to me in the fenced city.
\v 23 But I have said in \f s \fr 30.23 \ft In mine extreme affliction being almost distracted in my mind, I said that in reason I would not have said. Holy Job spake some things in such state of affliction. \+it cap. 3. & 42.\+it*\f* the excess of my mind: I am cast away from the sight of thine eyes. Therefore thou hast heard the voice of my prayer, whilst I cried to thee.
\v 24 \f t \fr 30.24 \ft The prophet, or other just person, exhorteth all the servants of God\f* Love our Lord all ye his Saints: because our Lord will require truth, & will repay them aboundantly that do proudly.
\v 25 Do ye \f v \fr 30.25 \ft to constancy.\f* manfully, and let your \f w \fr 30.25 \ft Longanimity,\f* heart take courage, all ye that \f x \fr 30.25 \ft and final perseverance to the end.\f* hope in our Lord.
\c 31
\cd Forgiveness of sins is a happy thing: 3. whereto many are brought by affliction giving them understanding, so moving them to confess their sins, 6. pray for remission, 10. not despair, but hope in God's mercy, and so rejoice with sincere heart. \f - \ft The second penitential Psalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 31.1 \ft This Psalm sheweth how David was brought to understand his sins, to confess, bewail, and obtain remission of them.\f* David himself understanding. Blessed are they, \f b \fr 31.1 \ft The first blessing of a sinner is the forgiveness of his sins.\f* whose iniquities are forgiven: and whose sins \f c \fr 31.1 \ft By charity, which covereth the multitude of sins. \+it 1. Pet. 4.\+it*\f* be covered. \x - \xt Rom. 4. 1. Pet. 4.\x* \ef - \fr 31.1 \fq Understanding. \ft When David had sinned and some-while neglected to confess his fault ¹, God's mercy by affliction made him to understand his own estate, who then repenting, confessing, and sorrowing for his sins, made this Psalm: which is therefore entitled \+it Understanding\+it* or \+it Instruction of David\+it* ². It giveth us also to understand, and to know, saith S. Augustine, that we must neither trust in our own merits, nor presume to escape punishment of sin. Thy first understanding therefore or lesson, must be, to know thyself to be a sinner. The next is ³, that when with faith thou beginnest to work well by love, thou attribute not this to thine own strength, but to the grace of God. \fq ¹ \ft Vexation giveth understanding. \fq ² \ft Sin must be punished. \fq ³ \ft Good works are of grace.\ef*
\v 2 Blessed is the man, to whom \f d \fr 31.2 \ft Satisfaction being made.\f* our Lord hath not imputed sin, neither is there \f e \fr 31.2 \ft When sinners repent sincerely without guile, then God forgiveth: without which cooperation none is justified.\f* guile in his spirit. \ef - \fr 31.2 \fq Whose sins are covered. 2. not imputed. \ft Calvin and his complices gather poison of these holy words ¹ ², denying that sins are truly taken away, but only covered, and still remain say they in the justest. Which sense would make this Scripture contrary to other places. Isaie 6. Thine iniquity shall be taken away, and thy sin shall be cleansed. Ioan. 1. The lamb of God which taketh away the sins of the world. Act. 3. Be penitent & convert, that your sins may be put out. 1. Cor. 6. You are washed, you are sanctified, you are justified: & the like, which shew the true real taking away of sins, true sanctification and justification. As S. Jerome (or some other ancient authentical Author) explicateth this place saying: Sins are so \+it covered\+it* by baptism & penance ³, that they are not to be revealed in the day of judgement, \+it nor imputed\+it* in him that diligently purgeth himself in this world, or by martyrdom. S. Augustine teacheth the same, saying: Sins are covered, are wholly covered, are abolished. Neither must you understand (saith he) that sins are covered, as though still they were, & lived. Why then did the Prophet say: sins are covered? They are not to be punished. More clearly li. 1. c. 13. cont. duas Epist. Pelag. The Pelagians calumniating Catholics, as if they taught, that sins are not taken away, but shaven, as hairs are cut with a razor; the roots remaining in the flesh: \+it which\+it* (he answereth) \+it none affirmeth but an infidel\+it* Likewise S. Gregory teacheth, that a sinner covereth his sins well when with contrary virtues he overwhelmeth former vices, and with good deeds blotteth out former evil deeds. He covereth them evil, when either for shame, or fear, or obstinacy, or desperation, he concealeth his sins, omitting to confess them. God covereth sins ⁴, as a physician covereth wounds, by applying medicinal plaster, which indeed cureth them. Thus ancient learned holy Fathers expound this text. Further explicating, that albeit things covered, and only thereby hid from men, do remain as they were before they were hid, yet whatsoever is hid to God, is indeed utterly taken away, for nothing that is, can be hid from God. And the contrary doctrine of Protestants is injurious either to God's power ⁵, if they say he cannot quite take away sins; or to his mercy, if he will not; or to his justice, if he never punish sins ever remaining; and to his truth, if he repute otherwise, than indeed the thing is. It is also injurious to Christ ⁶, to say, his blood and death is not effectual to take away sins: injurious to innumerable places of holy Scripture ⁷, which affirm plainly that sins by God's grace are utterly taken away: Finally, it is injurious to Saints in heaven ⁸, arguing them as still infected with sins, if indeed sins yet remain in them: which is most absurd, and blasphemy to speak. And yet followeth by necessary consequence. For if the justest lived & died in sin, they should remain eternally in sin. \fq ¹ \ft \+it in epist. ad Ro. c. 4.\+it* \fq ² \ft Protestants expound this place contrary to many other clear places. \fq ³ \ft Contrary to the exposition of ancient fathers. \fq ⁴ \ft God covering or not imputing sins doth quite take them away. \fq ⁵ \ft The contrary doctrine is injurious to God: \fq ⁶ \ft to Christ. \fq ⁷ \ft To holy Scriptures. \fq ⁸ \ft To glorified Saints.\ef* \ef - \fr 31.2 \fq Neither is there guile in his spirit. \ft In remission of sins the penitent necessarily must so cooperate ¹, that he have no guile in his spirit, or heart. For if he have, then he faileth of the foresaid blessedness, and his iniquities are not forgiven, nor his sins covered to God, but to be imputed and punished. Yet the repentance of a sinner be it never so sincere, hearty, and without guile, doth not merit remission of sin, but only disposeth thereto. But after remission it is satisfactory for the pain due for sins ², and meritorious of glory. According as S. Augustine here teacheth saying: Good (or meritorious) works go not before faith and remission, but follow the same. \fq ¹ \ft Sincere repentance is a necessary disposition to remission of sin. \fq ² \ft After remission it is satisfactory and meritorious.\ef*
\v 3 Because \f f \fr 31.3 \ft Because I acknowledged not my grievous sins, I was still sore afflicted:\f* I held my peace, my bones are \f 1 \fr 31.3 \ft \+it waxed as if they were old.\+it*\f* inveterated, \f g \fr 31.3 \ft though otherwise I ceased not to pray, but without any fruit or good effect.\f* whilst I cried all the day.
\v 4 Because day & night thy hand is made heavy upon me: I \f h \fr 31.4 \ft Thy divine providence reducing me,\f* am turned in my anguish, whilst \f i \fr 31.4 \ft by remorse of mine own conscience, which telleth me that I deserve all this affliction.\f* the thorn is fastened.
\v 5 \f k \fr 31.5 \ft Therefore I do no longer dissemble with men, nor am silent to thee, but expressly acknowledge my sins.\f* I have made my sin known to thee: and my injustice I have not hid. I said: I will confess against me my injustice to our Lord: and thou hast forgiven the impiety of my sin.
\v 6 For this \f l \fr 31.6 \ft As I do now recall myself being strucken with God's heavy hand; so must every one that will be purged from his sins and sanctified \+it pray to thee,\+it* when he is afflicted.\f* shall every holy one pray to thee, in time convenient. But yet \f m \fr 31.6 \ft Though calamities be marvelous great like \+it to a deluge:\+it*\f* in the flood of many waters, they shall \f n \fr 31.6 \ft yet they shall not oppress him, that relieth upon God.\f* not approach to him.
\v 7 Thou art my refuge from tribulation, which hath compassed me: my exultation, deliver me from them that compass me.
\v 8 I \f o \fr 31.8 \ft God speaketh, promising by these tribulations to give his servants \+it understanding\+it*, and instruction:\f* will give thee understanding, and will instruct thee in the way, that thou shalt go: I will fasten mine eyes upon thee.
\v 9 Do \f q \fr 31.9 \ft Be not therefore careless, like to brute beasts, but considerative of your actions.\f* not become as horse and mule, which have no understanding. In \f r \fr 31.9 \ft The Prophet or any just soul beseeching God to hold this strait hand of discipline over sinners, for their conversion.\f* bit and bridle bind fast their cheeks that approach not to thee.
\v 10 Many \f s \fr 31.10 \ft Sinners deserve much punishment.\f* are the scourges of a sinner, but \f t \fr 31.10 \ft But repenting, and trusting in God shall find his mercy.\f* him that hopeth in our Lord mercy shall compass.
\v 11 \f v \fr 31.11 \ft The end of true penance is joy, to which therefore the Prophet inviteth all penitents.\f* Be joyful in our Lord and rejoice ye just, and glory all ye right of heart.
\c 32
\cd The Prophet exhorteth to praise God, 4. describing his power, providence, mercy, and wisdom. 16. No salvation but by him: 20. And therefore prayeth for his help. \f - \ft The perfection of God's works described. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Psalm of David. Rejoice ye just \f a \fr 32.1 \ft Not in yourselves.\f* in our Lord; \f b \fr 32.1 \ft Praise is not comely in the mouth of a sinner. Eccli. 15.\f* praising becometh the righteous.
\v 2 Confess ye to our Lord on \f c \fr 32.2 \ft In mortification, offering your bodies a living host, Rom. 12.\f* the harp: on \f d \fr 32.2 \ft Of this instrument this book is called the Psalter: and it signifieth the observation of the ten commandments, without which no praise pleaseth God.\f* a psalter of ten strings sing to him.
\v 3 Sing ye to him \f e \fr 32.3 \ft Praising God for the grace of Christ in the new testament.\f* a new song: sing well to him in jubilation.
\v 4 Because the \f f \fr 32.4 \ft God's rules and precepts are most just and right.\f* word of our Lord is right, and all his works are \f g \fr 32.4 \ft He performeth whatsoever he promiseth.\f* in faith.
\v 5 He loveth \f h \fr 32.5 \ft God ever joineth these virtues together.\f* mercy and judgment: the earth is full of the mercy of our Lord.
\v 6 By \f i \fr 32.6 \ft God's word is omnipotent.\f* the word of our Lord the heavens are established: and by the spirit of his mouth all the power of them,
\v 7 Gathering together the waters of the sea, as it were in a bottle: putting the depths \f k \fr 32.7 \ft Unknown, as riches hid in secret places.\f* in treasures.
\v 8 Let all the earth fear our Lord: and let all the inhabitants of the world be moved at him.
\v 9 Because he said, and they were made: he commanded and they were created,
\v 10 Our Lord \f l \fr 32.10 \ft As in Babylon he confounded their tongues.\f* dissipateth the counsels of nations: and he reproveth the cogitations of people, and he reproveth the counsels of princes.
\v 11 But the counsel of our Lord abideth for ever, the cogitations \f m \fr 32.11 \ft His absolute will is always fulfilled.\f* of his heart in generation and generation.
\v 12 Blessed is the nation, whose God is our Lord: the people \f n \fr 32.12 \ft The people of Israel in the old testament: and Christians of all nations in the new.\f* whom he hath chosen for his inheritance.
\v 13 Our \f o \fr 32.13 \ft God's perpetual providence.\f* Lord hath looked from heaven: he hath seen all the children of men.
\v 14 From his prepared habitation he hath looked upon all that inhabit the earth
\v 15 Who \f p \fr 32.15 \ft God's wisdom infinite.\f* made their hearts severally: who understandeth all their works.
\v 16 The \f q \fr 32.16 \ft No power in earth is of any force without God.\f* king is not saved by much power: and the giant shall not be saved in the multitude of his strength.
\v 17 The horse faileth \f r \fr 32.17 \ft Of his master\f* to safety, \f s \fr 32.17 \ft or of himself.\f* and in the aboundance of his force he shall not be saved.
\v 18 Behold the eyes of our Lord be upon them that fear him: and on them, that hope upon his mercy.
\v 19 That he may deliver their souls from death: and nourish them in famine.
\v 20 Our soul expecteth our Lord: because he is our helper and protector.
\v 21 Because in him our heart shall rejoice: and we have trusted in his holy name.
\v 22 \f t \fr 32.22 \ft O God which hast all perfection, shew thy mercy in protecting & saving all that trust in thee.\f* Let thy mercy, o Lord, be made upon us, as we have hoped in thee.
\c 33
\cd King David by his own example being delivered from danger, exhorteth all men to render thanks for God's benefits: 12. shewing wherein justice consisteth, 16. and God's special providence towards the just. \f - \ft God's providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f 1 \fr 33.1 \ft This Psalm is also composed in order of the Alphabet.\f* David, when he changed his countenance before \f a \fr 33.1 \ft His proper name was Achis, \+it 1. Reg. 21.\+it* but all Kings of Palestina were called Abimelech, as Pharao in Ægypt, Nabuchodonosor in Babylon.\f* Abimelech, and he dismist him, and he went away \f 2 \fr 33.1 \ft \+it (1. Reg. 21)\+it*\f* \ef - \fr 33.1 \fq He changed his countenance. \ft S. Augustine by holy David's changing of his countenance, and by changing the King of Geth his name, who in the book of Kings (where the history is recorded) is called Achis, and here Abimelech, gathereth that here is an hidden, and great Mystery ¹. Which he explicateth partly by interpretation of the Hebrew names, but more especially by David's changing of his countenance, which prefigured Christ, eternal God, becoming also man, and so making great changes in the world. For as David killed Goliath, and for his good act got envy, so Christ killing the devil, and humility in Christ's members killing pride ², are persecuted by the wicked ³. For Christ was both to the ruin, and Resurrection of many. He changed Sacrifice and Priesthood. The Jews had sacrifice according to the order of Aaron, in victims of cattle, and this was in mystery. For there was not then the Sacrifice of the body and blood of our Lord: which the faithful, and those that have read the Gospel do know, which Sacrifice is now spread in all the round earth ⁴. A little after: The Sacrifice of Aaron is taken away, and the Sacrifice according to the order of Melchisedech began to be. He therefore, \+it I know not who\+it*, changed his countenance? Let it not be, \+it I know not who\+it*, for our Lord Jesus Christ is known. He would have our health to be in his body and blood. From whence did he commend his body and blood? From his humility. For unless he were humble, he would neither be eaten nor drunk. Behold his highness ⁵: In the beginning was \+it the Word\+it*, and \+it the Word\+it* was with God, and God \+it the Word\+it*. Lo the everlasting meat, and Angels eat it, supernal powers eat it, celestial spirits eat it, and they eat, and are fatted, and the thing remaineth whole, which satiateth and rejoiceth them. How then hath the wisdom of God fed us with the same bread? ⁶ The Word was made flesh and dwelt in us. It were too long to recite this great Doctor's whole discourse. He further sheweth that Christ dismissed the Jews, and went from them to the Gentiles. Thou seekest now Christ (saith he) among the Jews, and findeth him not: ⁷ because he hath changed his countenance. For they sticking to the sacrifice according to the order of Aaron held not the Sacrifice according to the order of Melchisedech, and have lost Christ: and the Gentiles have begun to have him. Again, this holy father willeth us to remember the Gospel: When our Lord Jesus Christ spake of his body, he said: Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man, ⁸ and drink his blood, you shall not have life in you: because he had changed his countenance, this seemed as fury and madness unto them, to give his flesh to be eaten of men, & his blood to be drunk, therefore David was reputed mad before Achis, when he said: you have brought this mad man unto me. Doth it not seem madness: Eat ye my flesh, and drink my blood? He seemed to be mad. Thus S. Augustine. Never imagining the figurative interpretation of our new Sacramentaries: who say Christ gave no more but a figure of his body & blood, for then it had been easily understood by the Capharnaites, and no such contradiction nor murmuring had happened. Yet S. Augustine saith more plainly, if more plain may be: \+it Christ carried himself in his own hands\+it*. ⁹ And how this can be done (brethren) in man, who can understand? For who is carried in his own hands? A man may be carried in the hands of others, no man is carried in his own hands. We find not how it can be understood in David according to the letter: but in Christ we find it. For Christ was carried in his own hands, when giving his very body, he said: This is my body; for he carried his body in his own hands. \fq ¹ \ft A secret great mystery in the title of this Psalm. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 2.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Some become worse, some better by Christ. \fq ⁴ \ft Sacrifice and Priesthood changed. \fq ⁵ \ft God most high is become low. \fq ⁶ \ft He is our sacramental meat. \fq ⁷ \ft Christ left the Jews & received the Gentiles. \fq ⁸ \ft The real presence of Christ in the Sacrament. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Catech. Ro. p. 2. c. 3. q. 24.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 I \f b \fr 33.2 \ft I praise God\f* will bless our Lord at \f c \fr 33.2 \ft both in prosperity and adversity.\f* all time: his praise always in my mouth.
\v 3 In our Lord \f d \fr 33.3 \ft When I serve our Lord, my soul shall be praised in his service.\f* my soul shall be praised: let the mild hear, and rejoice.
\v 4 Magnify ye our Lord with me: and let us exalt his name for ever.
\v 5 I have sought out our Lord, and he hath heard me: and from all my tribulations he hath delivered me.
\v 6 Come ye to him, and be illuminated: and your faces shall not be confounded.
\v 7 This \f e \fr 33.7 \ft Every man, be he how poor soever, when he prayeth shall be heard.\f* poor man hath cried, and our Lord hath heard him: and from all his tribulations he hath saved him.
\v 8 The \f f \fr 33.8 \ft The proper Guardian Angel of every one.\f* Angel of our Lord shall put in himself about them that fear him: and shall deliver them.
\v 9 Taste ye, and see that our Lord is sweet: blessed is the man, that hopeth in him.
\v 10 Fear \f g \fr 33.10 \ft With filial fear.\f* ye our Lord all ye his Saints: because there is no lack to them that fear him.
\v 11 The \f h \fr 33.11 \ft The rich of this world setting their mind upon their wealth, are poor in spiritual gifts.\f* rich have wanted, and have been hungry: but they that seek after our Lord shall not be diminished of any good.
\v 12 Come children, hear me: I will teach you the fear of our Lord.
\v 13 who is the man \f i \fr 33.13 \ft Every one desireth to be happy, but he indeed shall be happy, that flyeth from evil, and doth good.\f* that will have life: loveth to see good days?
\v 14 Stay thy tongue from evil: and thy lips that they speak not guile. \ef - \fr 33.14 \fq 15. Stay thy tongue, &c. \ft Both these verses, and frequent other places in the Psalms, shew plainly that justice consisteth not only in faith, ¹ but in abstaining from evil and doing good: yet requiring and presupposing true faith, without which no works are available to justice, nor to everlasting life. \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith, but good works with faith justify.\ef*
\v 15 Turn away from evil, and do good: seek after peace and pursue it.
\v 16 The eyes of our Lord upon the just: and his ears unto their prayers.
\v 17 But the \f k \fr 33.17 \ft God seeing all men's actions & intentions, will render as they deserve.\f* countenance of our Lord is upon them that do evil things: to destroy their memory out of the earth.
\v 18 The just have cried, and our Lord hath heard them: and out of all their tribulations he hath delivered them.
\v 19 Our Lord is nigh to them, that are of a contrite heart: and the humble of spirit he will save.
\v 20 Many are the tribulations of the just: and out of all these our Lord will deliver them.
\v 21 Our Lord keepeth all their \f l \fr 33.21 \ft Though the just seem for a time to be forsaken, yet God that giveth them internal strength, will at last reward and crown in them his own gifts.\f* bones: there shall not one of them be broken.
\v 22 The death of sinners is very ill: and they that hate the just \f m \fr 33.22 \ft For their sin they are suffered to fall into more sin.\f* shall offend.
\v 23 Our Lord will redeem the souls of his servants: and all that hope in him \f n \fr 33.23 \ft Contrarywise those that accept of his grace, shall finally not offend.\f* shall not offend.
\c 34
\cd David in figure of Christ, prophetically by way of invocating God's help, foresheweth his persecution, and the just revenge upon his persecutors, 9. with praise to God. 13. his charity towards his cruel adversaries, 17. whom nevertheless God punisheth, 20. for pretending peace in words, and in fact persecuting: 23. rendering to all as they deserve. \f - \ft Christ's persecution. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 34.1 \ft David signifieth beloved, desirable, or strong of hand, that is Christ, above all beloved of God, desired of man, the strong conqueror of death and hell. S. Aug. in hunc locum.\f* David himself. Judge \f b \fr 34.1 \ft By way of imprecation he prophesieth that God will overthrow the persecutors of Christ, and of Christians.\f* o Lord them that hurt me: overthrow them that impugn me.
\v 2 Take \f c \fr 34.2 \ft Offensive\f* armour and \f d \fr 34.2 \ft Defensive.\f* shield: and rise up to help me.
\v 3 Bring forth the sword, and \f e \fr 34.3 \ft Preoccupate and prevent the malice of the persecutor.\f* shut up against them, that persecute me: say to my soul: I am thy salvation.
\v 4 Let them \f f \fr 34.4 \ft Such a punishment and confusion shall fall in the end upon all the malicious, after that the just shall have overcome tribulations.\f* be confounded & ashamed, that seek my soul. Let them be turned backward, and be confounded that think evil against me.
\v 5 Be they made as dust before the face of wind; and the Angel of our Lord straitening them.
\v 6 Let their way be made darkness and slipperness: and the Angel of our Lord pursuing them.
\v 7 Because they have hid the destruction of their snare for me without cause: in vain have they upbraided my soul.
\v 8 Let the snare which he knoweth not, come on him; and the net, which he hath hid, catch him: and let him fall into the very same snare.
\v 9 But \f g \fr 34.9 \ft In the time of trouble in hope,\f* my soul shall rejoice in our Lord: and shall be delighted upon \f h \fr 34.9 \ft after delivery, in eternal salvation.\f* his salvation.
\v 10 All my bones shall say: Lord, who is like to thee? Delivering the needy from the hand of them that are stronger than he: the needy and poor from them that spoil him.
\v 11 \f i \fr 34.11 \ft Such false witnesses did rise against Christ. Mat. 26.\f* Unjust witnesses rising up, asked me things that \f k \fr 34.11 \ft Things that were not; for God himself that knoweth all things, knoweth not that which neither was, is, nor can be.\f* I knew not.
\v 12 They repayed me evil things for good, \f l \fr 34.12 \ft They made privation of my life: verified in Christ, not in David, for he was killed by his enemies.\f* sterility to my soul.
\v 13 But I when they were troublesome to me, \f m \fr 34.13 \ft All our Saviour's life was penance for others, needing none for himself.\f* did put on cloth of hair. I humbled my soul in fasting: and my prayer shall be turned into my bosom.
\v 14 As a \f n \fr 34.14 \ft Christ the good Samaritane, that relieved the wounded man. Luc. 10.\f* neighbour, as our brother, so did I please: as mourning and sorrowful so was I humbled.
\v 15 And they \f o \fr 34.15 \ft All this was fulfilled according to the letter in our Saviour's passion.\f* rejoiced against me, and \f p \fr 34.15 \ft See previous note\f* came together: \f q \fr 34.15 \ft See previous note\f* scourges were gathered together upon me, and I was \f r \fr 34.15 \ft Our Lord knoweth not any just cause, why the Jews so persecuted him, for they had no just cause but mere malice.\f* ignorant.
\v 16 They were dissipated, and not compunct, they tempted me, they scorned me with scorning: they gnashed upon me with their teeth.
\v 17 Lord \f s \fr 34.17 \ft As Psal. 21. and Mat. 27. God why has thou forsaken me? Not delivered me from temporal death, not yielded me such consolation, as thou gavest other Saints in their agonies?\f* when wilt thou regard? \f t \fr 34.17 \ft A Prophecy of Christ's resurrection.\f* Restore thou my soul from their malignity, mine only one from the lions.
\v 18 I will confess to thee in \f v \fr 34.18 \ft Prophecy of the Catholic Church. as Psal. 21.\f* the great Church, in a grave people I will praise thee.
\v 19 Let them not rejoice over me that are my adversaries unjustly: \f w \fr 34.19 \ft This place is applied by our Saviour to himself. Ioan. 13.\f* that hate me without cause, and twinkle with the eyes.
\v 20 Because they spake indeed \f x \fr 34.20 \ft The Pharisees and Herodians said: Master we know that thou art a true speaker &c. meaning to entrap him with treason. \+it Mat. 22.\+it*\f* peaceably to me: and in the anger of the earth speaking they meant guiles.
\v 21 And they \f y \fr 34.21 \ft The same Pharisees and Priests judged him worthy of death, and procured the people to cry: Crucify him, crucify him.\f* opened their mouth awide upon me: they said, Well, well, our eyes have seen.
\v 22 Thou hast seen, o Lord, keep not silence: \f z \fr 34.22 \ft Again his Resurrection is prophesied.\f* Lord depart not from me.
\v 23 Arise and attend to my judgment: my God, and my Lord unto my cause.
\v 24 Judge me according to thy justice, o Lord my God, and let them not rejoice over me.
\v 25 Let them not say in their hearts: Well, well, to our soul: neither let them say, We have devoured him.
\v 26 Let \f a \fr 34.26 \ft At the day of judgement the wicked shall receive sentence of damnation.\f* them blush and be ashamed together, that rejoice at my evils. Let them be clothed with confusion and shame, that speak great things upon me.
\v 27 Let \f b \fr 34.27 \ft The Blessed in eternal glory.\f* them rejoice and be glad, that will my justice: and let them say always: Our Lord be magnified, that will the peace of his servant.
\v 28 And my tongue shall meditate thy justice, thy praise all the day.
\c 35
\cd The Prophet describeth the wicked malice of obstinate sinners. 6. Against which he opposeth God's infinite goodness, 9. with his provident mercy towards the worst, and just reward of the good; 12. praying to escape the dangerous gulf of pride. \f - \ft God's providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 35.1 \ft More specially describing the state of men in the new testament, than in the old.\f* the end, to \f b \fr 35.1 \ft Instruction for David, not as a King, or a Prophet, but as the poor servant of God.\f* the servant of our Lord David himself.
\v 2 The unjust hath said within himself, that he \f c \fr 35.2 \ft Wittingly and resolutely preferring wicked life before virtuous.\f* would sin: there is no fear of God before his eyes.
\v 3 Because he hath done deceitfully in his sight: \f d \fr 35.3 \ft God so hateth sin committed of mere malice that he commonly rejecteth such sinners, and more often offereth new grace to those, that sin of frailty or ignorance.\f* that his iniquity may be found unto hatred.
\v 4 The words of his mouth are iniquity, and guile: he \f e \fr 35.4 \ft Some ignorance is invincible, when one hath a good will to learn, doing his endeavour to know the truth in doctrine, & his duty in manners, but cannot get knowledge thereof, and then he is excused before God, though he err in opinion, or in fact: others are negligent to learn, and their error is gross ignorance, and is a sin, greater or less, according to the importance of the thing, which they ought to know. Others, are more willful, desiring to be ignorant: that they may sin with the less remorse, or repining of their own conscience, and this is affected ignorance, and most heinous and odious sin. For which God often leaving them destitute of ordinary grace, which he giveth to others, they fall into reprobate sense, and into more horrible sins.\f* would not understand that he might do well.
\v 5 He hath meditated iniquity in his bed: he hath set himself on every way not good, and malice he hath not hated.
\v 6 Lord \f f \fr 35.6 \ft God doth not utterly shut up his mercy from the most willful & wicked sinners, but giveth them sometimes good motions, and sufficient help, that they may repent, be justified, & saved, if they do not wittingly harden their own hearts, and still willfully repel God's grace.\f* thy mercy is in heaven: and \f g \fr 35.6 \ft For so God promiseth (who is most faithful) that he will forgive sinners, and receive them into his favour again, whensoever they resolving to serve him, repent and cease to sin.\f* thy truth even to the clouds.
\v 7 Thy justice as the hills of God: thy judgments are great depth. Men & \f h \fr 35.7 \ft Thou, o God, that hast care of all creatures, not only of men but also of brute beasts, art ever ready of thy part to save both moderate men, in whom the light of reason remaineth, and also gross senseless persons, which are become brutish like horse and mule or other beasts.\f* beasts thou wilt save, o Lord,
\v 8 as thou \f i \fr 35.8 \ft For so our Lord multiplieth his mercy.\f* hast multiplied thy mercy, o God. But \f k \fr 35.8 \ft Yet with condition that senseless or brutish men, must become reasonable men, the children of men, not colts, whelps, pigs &c.\f* the children of men \f l \fr 35.8 \ft Sinners thus converted shall not only have all necessaries in this life, as all living creatures have in this world, but also shall hope of spiritual, heavenly, & eternal glory prepared for Angels, and children of men: as in the verses following.\f* shall hope in the covert of thy wings.
\v 9 They shall be inebriated with the plenty of thy house: and with the torrent of thy pleasure thou shalt make them drink.
\v 10 Because with thee is the fountain of life: and in thy light we shall see light.
\v 11 Extend thy mercy to them that know thee, and thy justice to them, that are of a right heart.
\v 12 Let \f m \fr 35.12 \ft Lest any impediment hinder the obtaining and possession of eternal reward, the just must specially pray not to be infected with pride:\f* not the foot of pride come to me: and let not \f n \fr 35.12 \ft nor be overcome by the forcible tentations of other sinners, by persuasion, nor evil example.\f* the hand of a sinner move me.
\v 13 There \f o \fr 35.13 \ft The first sin, to wit of devils, was pride, and man's sin was by persuasion of the devil.\f* have they fallen that work iniquity: they were expelled, \f p \fr 35.13 \ft Neither of which could escape punishment.\f* neither could they stand.
\c 36
\cd An exhortation not to envy nor imitate the evil, who for most part prosper in this world, and are damned eternally: but to fly evil and do good, duly considering that God diversly permitteth and punisheth the wicked, and likewise comforteth and afflicteth the just, all for their good. \f - \ft An exhortation to contempt of this world. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 36.1 \ft For David's and every just man's instruction.\f* to David himself. Have \f b \fr 36.1 \ft Neither be thou offended that the wicked do prosper in this world, nor imitate them that thou mayst also prosper.\f* no emulation toward the malignant: neither envy them that do iniquity.
\v 2 Because \f c \fr 36.2 \ft For all this life & consequently the prosperity thereof is short, and uncertain.\f* they shall quickly wither as grass: and as the blossoms of herbs they shall soon fall.
\v 3 \f d \fr 36.3 \ft Put thy trust in God, living content in this world:\f* Hope in our Lord and do good: and inhabit the land, and thou \f e \fr 36.3 \ft & he will give thee that is necessary.\f* shalt be fed in the riches thereof.
\v 4 Be delighted in our Lord: & he will give thee the petitions of thy heart.
\v 5 \f f \fr 36.5 \ft Commend all thine affairs to God.\f* Reveal thy way to our Lord, and hope in him: and he will do it.
\v 6 And he will \f g \fr 36.6 \ft Partly making virtue appear to the comfort of the virtuous, and example of others in this life, but especially in the next world.\f* bring forth thy justice as light: and thy judgment as midday:
\v 7 Be subject to our Lord, and pray him. Have no emulation in him, that prospereth in his way: in a man that doth injustices.
\v 8 Cease from wrath, and leave fury: have not emulation that thou be malignant.
\v 9 Because they that are malignant, shall be cast out: but they that expect our Lord, the same shall inherit \f h \fr 36.9 \ft The land of the living.\f* the land.
\v 10 And yet a little while, and the sinner shall not be: and thou shalt seek his place, and shall not find it.
\v 11 \x - \xt Mat. 5.\x* But the meek shall inherit the land, and shall be delighted in multitude of peace.
\v 12 The sinner shall observe the just: & shall gnash upon him with his teeth.
\v 13 But our Lord shall scorn him: because he foreseeth that his day shall come.
\v 14 Sinners have drawn out the sword: they have bent their bow; That they may deceive the poor and needy: that they may murder the right of heart.
\v 15 Let \f i \fr 36.15 \ft By way of imprecation (as in many other places) the Prophet foresheweth that wicked men shall fall into the evils, which they prepare for others.\f* their own swords enter into their hearts: and let their bow be broken.
\v 16 Better is a little to the just, above much riches of sinners.
\v 17 Because the arms of sinners shall be broken in pieces: but our Lord confirmeth the just.
\v 18 Our Lord knoweth the days of the immaculate: and their inheritance shall be for ever.
\v 19 They shall not be confounded in the evil time, and in the days of famine they shall be filled:
\v 20 because the sinners shall perish. But the enemies of our Lord forthwith as they shall be honoured and exalted, vanishing shall vanish as smoke.
\v 21 The sinner shall borrow, and not pay: but the just is merciful and will give.
\v 22 Because they that bless him shall inherit the land: but they that curse him shall perish.
\v 23 With our Lord the step of man shall be directed: and he shall like well of his way.
\v 24 When \f k \fr 36.24 \ft Though the just fall of frailty or ignorance into venial sin, yet God's grace shall stay him that he fall not into mortal. The just falleth seven times in the day, & riseth. Prov. 24.\f* he shall fall, he shall not be bruised: because our Lord putteth his hand under.
\v 25 I have been young, for I am old: and I have not seen the just forsaken, \f l \fr 36.25 \ft So King David observed: and it very rarely happeneth, that the just or their children are destitute of necessary sustenance in this world\f* nor his seed seeking bread.
\v 26 All the day he is merciful and lendeth: & his seed shall be in blessing.
\v 27 \f m \fr 36.27 \ft In these two principles, Declining from evil and Doing good, true justice consisteth.\f* Decline from evil, & do good: and \f n \fr 36.27 \ft And he that finally observeth these two points, meriteth and shall possess heaven.\f* inhabit for ever & ever.
\v 28 Because our Lord loveth judgment, and he will not forsake his saints: they shall be preserved for ever. The unjust shall be punished: and the seed of the impious shall perish.
\v 29 But the just shall inherit the land: and shall inhabit for ever and ever upon it.
\v 30 The mouth of the just shall meditate wisdom, and his tongue shall speak judgment. \x - \xt Pro. 31. Isa. 51.\x*
\v 31 The law of his God in his heart: and his steps shall not be supplanted.
\v 32 The sinner considereth the just: and seeketh to \f o \fr 36.32 \ft To draw him to mortal sin, which is death of the soul.\f* murder him.
\v 33 But our Lord will not leave him in his hands: neither will he condemn him, when judgment shall be given of him.
\v 34 Expect our Lord, and keep his way: and he will exalt thee, that thou mayst inherit the land: when the sinners shall perish thou shalt see.
\v 35 I have seen the impious highly exalted, and advance as the cedars of Libanus.
\v 36 And I passed by, and behold he was not: and I sought him, and his place was not found.
\v 37 Keep innocency, and see equity: because there are \f p \fr 36.37 \ft Rewards.\f* remains for the peaceable man.
\v 38 But the unjust shall perish together: the remains of the impious shall perish.
\v 39 But the salvation of the just is \f q \fr 36.39 \ft Man's justice and well doing is not of his own power but of God's grace.\f* of our Lord: and he is their protector in the time of tribulation.
\v 40 And our Lord will help them, and deliver them: and he will take them away from sinners, and save them, because they have hoped in him.
\c 37
\cd King David, or any other penitent, earnestly prayeth God to remit his sins, and mitigate the pains which he acknowledgeth himself to have deserved, 12. lamenting the afflictions which he suffereth by such as sometimes were his friends, 14. whose tentations he now resisteth, trusting in God, resigning himself to God's will, confessing his own iniquity, and humbly praying for God's help. \f - \ft The third penitential Psalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm of David, in \f a \fr 37.1 \ft In remembrance that by sin we lost the rest and peace, which man had in the state of innocency; secondly we lost the peace of conscience; thirdly the rest and peace of eternal felicity.\f* recordation of the sabbath.
\v 2 Lord \f b \fr 37.2 \ft Condemn me not to eternal pain:\f* rebuke me not in thy fury: \f c \fr 37.2 \ft nor punish me in purgatory fire; but purge me so in this life, that the purging fire be not needful. By which fire (saith S. Aug.) though some shall be saved (gravior tamen erit ille ignis, quam quicquid potest homo pati in hac vita) yet that fire shall be more grievous, than whatsoever a man can suffer in this life. S. Gregory also expoundeth this same place, as if David said thus: I know it will come to pass, that after the end of this life, some shall be cleansed by purging flames, some shall be under the sentence of eternal damnation. But because I do esteem that transitory fire more intolerable than all present tribulation, I desire not only not to be\f* nor chastise me in wrath.
\v 3 Because \f d \fr 37.3 \ft Afflictions of mind and body sent by thy just judgment.\f* thy arrows are fast sticked in me: and thou hast \f e \fr 37.3 \ft Thou hast struck me with an heavy hand.\f* fastened thy hand upon me.
\v 4 There is \f f \fr 37.4 \ft I already feel in my flesh, in all my bones, and powers, great affliction,\f* no health in my flesh at \f g \fr 37.4 \ft considering thy justice\f* the face of thy wrath: my bones have no peace at \f h \fr 37.4 \ft and my sins.\f* the face of my sins.
\v 5 Because mine iniquities are gone \f i \fr 37.5 \ft Which are exceedingly increased, almost overwhelming my spirit.\f* over my head: and as a \f k \fr 37.5 \ft Sins not washed away by penance by their weight carry the soul into more and more wickedness.\f* heavy burden are become heavy upon me.
\v 6 My \f l \fr 37.6 \ft Still corrupting those parts which were whole before, as a pestered sore that is not cured.\f* scars are putrefied and corrupted, because of my foolishness.
\v 7 I am become miserable, and am made \f m \fr 37.7 \ft Not able to go straight to do any good work, being guilty of grievous sin.\f* crooked even to the end: I went sorrowful all the day.
\v 8 Because \f n \fr 37.8 \ft Concupiscence striving in me.\f* my loins are filled with illusions: and there is no health in my flesh.
\v 9 I am afflicted and am humbled exceedingly: I \f o \fr 37.9 \ft From the sorrow of my heart my voice hath broken out into clamour.\f* roared for the groaning of my heart.
\v 10 Lord \f p \fr 37.10 \ft O God thou knowest my desire, to be restored to thy favour.\f* before thee is all my desire: and my groaning is not hid from thee.
\v 11 My heart is troubled, my strength hath forsaken me: and the light of mine eyes, and the same is not with me.
\v 12 My \f q \fr 37.12 \ft Those that were my friends and companions in sin are become mine enemies, because I forsake them.\f* friends, and my neighbours have approached, & stood against me. And they that were near me, stood far off:
\v 13 and they did violence which sought my soul. And they that sought me evils \f r \fr 37.13 \ft Sought by all means to entangle me again.\f* spake vanities: and meditated guiles all the day.
\v 14 But I as \f s \fr 37.14 \ft Inow renounce all sin.\f* one deaf did not hear: and as one dumb not opening his mouth.
\v 15 And I became as a man not hearing: and not having reproofs in his mouth.
\v 16 Because \f t \fr 37.16 \ft Inow rely upon thee, o God.\f* in thee, o Lord have I hoped, thou wilt hear me, o Lord my God.
\v 17 \f v \fr 37.17 \ft For this cause I am returned to thee and do pray that mine enemies may not prevail against me.\f* Because I said: Lest sometime mine enemies rejoice over me, and whilst my feet are moved, they spake great things upon me.
\v 18 Because I \f w \fr 37.18 \ft I resign myself to thee.\f* am ready for scourges: and my sorrow is in my sight always:
\v 19 Because \f x \fr 37.19 \ft Though thou knowest all, yet with mouth confession is made to salvation.\f* I will declare my iniquity: and I will \f y \fr 37.19 \ft And I meditate of that which my sin hath deserved.\f* think for my sin.
\v 20 But mine enemies live, and are confirmed over me: and they are multiplied that hate me unjustly.
\v 21 They that repay evil things for good, \f z \fr 37.21 \ft One kind of detraction is in revealing secret faults, another in feigning and imputing false crimes, the third (here mentioned) in calling virtue vice, as penance hypocrisy.\f* detracted from me: because I followed goodness.
\v 22 \f a \fr 37.22 \ft Grant me Lord final perseverance in thy grace and service.\f* Forsake me not, o Lord my God, depart not from me.
\v 23 Attend unto my help, o Lord the God of my salvation.
\c 38
\cd Christ's faithful members after long expectation congratulate his coming in flesh. 6. He directing his speech to his Father, professeth to perform the
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 38.1 \ft Some expound this Psalm of the Jews in captivity in Babylon, but this title, and the matter contained shew, that it rather pertaineth to the new Testament.\f* the end, to \f b \fr 38.1 \ft To be sung by Idithun and his scholars & successors, or rather by Christians.\f* Idithun himself, a canticle of David.
\v 2 I \f c \fr 38.2 \ft Weak men in affliction, not able to deliver themselves, seek revenge, by murmuring and other evil speeches, but the perfect resolve to rule their tongues:\f* have said: I will keep my ways, that I offend not in my tongue. I have set a guard to my mouth, when the sinner stood against me.
\v 3 I was dumb and humbled, and kept silence \f d \fr 38.3 \ft Even to forbear sometimes from their own just defence:\f* from good things: and \f e \fr 38.3 \ft though thereby they endure more persecution.\f* my sorrow was renewed.
\v 4 My \f f \fr 38.4 \ft Sorrow suppressed maketh the heart to burn with zeal and indignation.\f* heart waxed hot within me: & in my meditation a fire shall burn.
\v 5 I have spoken in my tongue: Lord \f g \fr 38.5 \ft If it may please thee, let me know how long I shall live, desiring to die; as Elias desired. \+it 3. Reg. 19.\+it*\f* make mine end known to me. And the number of my days what it is: that I may know what is lacking to me.
\v 6 Behold thou hast put my days measurable, and my \f h \fr 38.6 \ft My life and all that I have is as nothing compared to thy eternity.\f* substance is as nothing before thee. Doubtless all things are vanity, every man living.
\v 7 Surely man passeth as \f i \fr 38.7 \ft As a shadow or image appearing in a glass, which is quickly forgot.\f* an image, yea and he is troubled in \f k \fr 38.7 \ft Therefore there is no cause man should be troubled in mind for temporal miseries.\f* vain. He gathereth treasure, and knoweth not to whom he shall gather them.
\v 8 And now what is my expectation? Is not our Lord? And my substance is with thee.
\v 9 From all mine iniquities deliver me: \f l \fr 38.9 \ft Thou hast suffered me to be reproached by the foolish that prosper in this world.\f* a reproach to the foolish thou hast given me.
\v 10 I was dumb, and opened not my mouth, \f m \fr 38.10 \ft Iknow my tribulation is by thy providence.\f* because thou didst it:
\v 11 Remove thy scourges from me.
\v 12 By the strength of thy hand I have fainted in reprehensions, for iniquity thou hast chastised man. And thou hast made his \f o \fr 38.12 \ft My life decayeth as a spider having spent all her moisture.\f* soul pine away as a spider: but vainly is every man troubled.
\v 13 Hear my prayer, o Lord, and my petition; with thine ears receive my tears. Keep not silence: because I am a \f p \fr 38.13 \ft All men are strangers in this life, heaven being our home.\f* stranger with thee, and a pilgrim, as my fathers.
\v 14 Forgive me, that I may \f q \fr 38.14 \ft That I may recover spiritual strength in this life:\f* be refreshed before I depart: and \f r \fr 38.14 \ft after which I shall not be in state to do free works of satisfaction nor merit.\f* shall be no more.
\c 39
\cd Redemption of mankind, and to denounce the same in \it the whole world: 12. prayeth for his servants, undertaking to satisfy for their sins.\it* \f - \ft Christ's coming and redeeming of mankind. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 39.1 \ft Pertaining unto the new Testament.\f* the end, a Psalm to David himself.
\v 2 Expecting \f b \fr 39.2 \ft The faithful of the old and new Testament rejoice in the coming of Christ.\f* I expected our Lord, and he hath attended to me.
\v 3 And he heard my prayers, and brought me out of the lake of misery, and from the mire of dregs. And hath set my feet upon a rock: and hath directed my steps.
\v 4 And he hath put a new canticle into my mouth: a song to our God. Many shall see, and shall fear: and they shall hope in our Lord.
\v 5 Blessed is the man, whose hope is in the name of our Lord: and hath not had regard to vanities and false madness.
\v 6 Thou hast done many thy marvelous things, o Lord my God: & in thy cogitations there is none that may be like to thee. I \f c \fr 39.6 \ft Christ by himself and by others preached the Gospel of salvation.\f* have declared and have spoken: they \f d \fr 39.6 \ft The multiplication of Christians thereby.\f* multiplied above number.
\v 7 \f e \fr 39.7 \ft No sacrifice of the old testament sufficed to satisfy God's justice for the sin of man.\f* Sacrifice and oblation thou wouldest not: but \f f \fr 39.7 \ft Christ by the ear of obedience performed the redemption of man by his death, as was determined from eternity. S. Paul for (ears) saith (body). See Annotations Heb. 10.\f* ears thou hast perfited to me. \x - \xt Heb. 10.\x* Holocaust and for sin thou didst not require:
\v 8 then said I, Behold I come. In the \f g \fr 39.8 \ft The sum of holy Scripture is of Christ's Incarnation & death for redemption of man.\f* head of the book it is written of me,
\v 9 that I should do thy will: my God I would, and thy law in the midst of my heart.
\v 10 I have \f h \fr 39.10 \ft Again Christ inculcateth the preaching and receiving of his Gospel in the whole world.\f* declared thy justice in the great Church, lo I will not stay my lips: Lord thou hast known it.
\v 11 Thy justice I have not hid in my heart: thy truth and thy salvation I have spoken. I have not hid thy mercy, and thy truth from the \f i \fr 39.11 \ft In the greatest and wisest congregations of this world, Christ concealeth not his mercy and truth. So himself professed before Annas, Caiphas, Pilate, and their counsels. Saint Paul preached Christ at Athens, and in many nations, and so the other Apostles. For their voice went into all the coasts of the earth.\f* great Council.
\v 12 \f k \fr 39.12 \ft The Prophet now speaketh in the name of Christ's mystical body the Church: praying to be made partaker of mercy, and to be delivered from evils.\f* But thou, o Lord, make not thy commiserations far from me: thy mercy, and thy truth have always received me.
\v 13 Because evils have compassed me, which have no number: mine iniquities have overtaken me, & I was \f l \fr 39.13 \ft The sins also of those which believe in Christ are so many, that they cannot be fully seen in particular.\f* not able to see. They are multiplied above the hairs of my head: and \f m \fr 39.13 \ft I almost faint in considering so many and so great iniquities amongst those that profess Christ.\f* my heart hath forsaken me.
\v 14 It may please thee, o Lord, to deliver \f n \fr 39.14 \ft The whole Church prayeth in the name of all for the infirm members.\f* me: Lord have respect to help me.
\v 15 Let \f o \fr 39.15 \ft The Prophet foresheweth that the reprobate for their obstinate malice, seeking to hurt others shall be confounded.\f* them be confounded and ashamed together, that seek my soul to take it away. Let them be turned backward, and be ashamed that will me evils.
\v 16 Let them forthwith receive their confusion, that \f p \fr 39.16 \ft That scornfully say: \+it Well, well:\+it* wishing all evil to good men.\f* say to me: Well, well.
\v 17 Let all \f q \fr 39.17 \ft Which not only in mouth and outward profession, but also in sincerity of heart seek thee, may with confidence rejoice, and praise God.\f* that seek thee rejoice and be glad upon thee: and let them that love thy salvation, say always: Our Lord be magnified:
\v 18 But \f r \fr 39.18 \ft Christ speaketh in the name of sinners truly repenting, whose sins he undertaketh to redeem, and wash away by his passion.\f* I am a beggar, and poor: Our Lord is careful to me. Thou art \f s \fr 39.18 \ft The faithful of the old testament pray for Christ's first coming into this world, & the faithful now pray for his second coming to purge his Church, and to reward the good.\f* my helper, & my protector: my God be not slack.
\c 40
\cd The Prophet pronounceth them happy that will believe in Christ, coming in humility and poverty. 5. Christ describeth his own poor afflicted state in this life, by reason he is to satisfy for the sins of the world; the malice of his adversaries, 10. especially of Judas, 11. and by way of prayer, prophesieth his own Resurrection. \f - \ft Christ's Passion and Resurrection. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 40.1 \ft Pertaining to the new testament, as appeareth by the 10. verse alleged by our Saviour.\f* the end, a Psalm to David himself. \x - \xt Io. 13. v. 18. This Psalm is also applied by the Church in the office of the sick, whom whosoever assisteth in that case, may hope to have assistance in their own like necessity.\x*
\v 2 Blessed is the man that understandeth concerning \f b \fr 40.2 \ft He is happy that is not scandalized in Christ (Luc. 7. v. 23.) coming in poverty, & suffering extreme afflictions.\f* the needy, and the poor: in \f c \fr 40.2 \ft He that trusteth in Christ, notwithstanding the contrary motives of his worldly misery, shall be delivered by him in all distress.\f* the evil day our Lord will deliver him.
\v 3 Our Lord \f d \fr 40.3 \ft Our Lord will give to such servants more grace in this life, and glory in the next.\f* preserve him, and give him life, and make him blessed in the land: and \f e \fr 40.3 \ft Not suffer him to be overcome in tentations.\f* deliver him not unto the will of his enemies.
\v 4 Our Lord help him \f f \fr 40.4 \ft When such constant servants are sick to death, Christ will most especially comfort and help them.\f* upon the bed of his sorrow: thou hast turned all his couch in his infirmity.
\v 5 I said: \f g \fr 40.5 \ft Christ in the behalf of his mystical body confesseth their sins, and prayeth for them.\f* Lord have mercy on me: heal my soul, because I have sinned to thee.
\v 6 Mine enemies have spoken evils to me: When shall he die \f h \fr 40.6 \ft After death suffered for mankind, Christ riseth, and his name and kingdom is glorious.\f* and his name perish?
\v 7 And if \f i \fr 40.7 \ft Those that came not of good will, but of malice to observe Christ's deeds and words, carped at both, sometimes saying, he taught against the law, and against Moyses; sometimes that he cast out devils in the power of Beelzebub.\f* he came in to see, he spake vain things: his heart hath gathered together iniquity to himself. He went forth, and spake together.
\v 8 All mine enemies whispered against me: they did think evils to me.
\v 9 They \f k \fr 40.9 \ft At last they resolved that he should die.\f* have determined an unjust word against me: \f l \fr 40.9 \ft But they could not so suppress his power, for he rose again in glory.\f* shall not he that sleepeth add to rise again?
\v 10 For \f m \fr 40.10 \ft By our Saviour's application of this verse, it is certain that the traitor Judas is here described. Ioa. 13. v. 18.\f* the man also of my peace, in whom I hoped: who did eat my breads, hath greatly trodden me under-foot. \x - \xt Io. 13. Act. 1.\x*
\v 11 But thou, o Lord, have mercy upon me, and raise me up again: and I \f n \fr 40.11 \ft In the day of judgement Christ Judge of all will render to every one as they deserve.\f* will repay them.
\v 12 In this I have known that thou wouldest me: because mine enemy shall not rejoice over me.
\v 13 But me thou hast received \f o \fr 40.13 \ft As before in respect of sinners, Christ Judge of all will render to every one: so here in his own person he avoucheth his own innocency, which made him apt to satisfy for others.\f* because of innocency: and thou hast confirmed me in thy sight for ever.
\v 14 Blessed be our Lord the God of Israel \f p \fr 40.14 \ft For this mercy of Almighty God in saving the elect by his Son's death he is to be praised for ever eternally.\f* from the beginning of the world, and for evermore: \f q \fr 40.14 \ft All the Blessed agree in this, that God is eternally to be praised and thereto say \+it Amen,\+it* So be it, so be it. Some divide the Psalms into five books, supposing the first book to end here with these words, \+it Be it, be it:\+it* not observing that the last Psalm hath not this ending. S. Jerome confuteth this opinion by our Saviour's and S. Peter's naming it the book, not books of Psalms. Luc. 20. v. 42. Act. 1. Moreover if this were the end of one book, then the Psalm following should not be called the 41. Psalm but the first Psalm of the second book.\f* Be it, be it.
\c 41
\cd The fervent desire of the just, 6. much afflicted in this life, 12. and assured hope of eternal joy. \f - \ft Eternal glory. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, understanding to the sons of \f a \fr 41.1 \ft The sons of Core repented, and departed from their father's schism, and so escaped miraculously the horrible pit of damnation, into which their father and his complices fell. Num. 26. v. 10. By which example all seduced and deceived Christians are admonished, not to persist in schism or other sins. And worldly men, ambitious of honour be warned to desire & seek God above all things, first of all the kingdom of heaven (to be living members of the Catholic Church) and the justice thereof: to seek things which are above, not which are upon the earth; lest hell devour them, as it devoured the complices of Core. Num. 16. v. 31.\f* Core.
\v 2 Even as the hart \f b \fr 41.2 \ft Ahart waxing old, and burdened with much hair, and great horns, draweth a serpent into his nostrils: so being infected with poison, desireth most ardently to drink, and afterwards casteth his horns, and hair, and becometh as it were young again.\f* desireth after the fountains of waters: \f c \fr 41.2 \ft With such fervent desire a true penitent, feeling himself infected with poison of sins, seeketh the water of God's grace.\f* so doth my soul desire after thee, o God.
\v 3 My soul hath thirsted after God \f d \fr 41.3 \ft God is omnipotent, and indeed the only true living God: devils who are honoured in idols, can do no more than God permitteth, and so they can kill the souls, that consent unto their tentations, but cannot restore spiritual life again.\f* the strong \f e \fr 41.3 \ft See previous note\f* living: \f f \fr 41.3 \ft The soul being justified, and still assaulted with new tentations desireth to be with God.\f* when shall I come and appear before the face of God?
\v 4 My \f g \fr 41.4 \ft Ihave had no other refection, but to lenify my sorrow with weeping.\f* tears have been breads unto me day and night: whilst it is said to me daily: \f h \fr 41.4 \ft The wicked exprobate the just, as though God would never help them, because he suffereth them to be sometimes long in tribulation.\f* Where is thy God?
\v 5 These things have I remembered, and have poured out my soul in me, because I shall pass into the place \f i \fr 41.5 \ft King David was not permitted to build the temple, much less did he enter into any such marvelous tabernacle in his mortal life, but must needs be understood, to speak here of the heavenly tabernacle, prepared by Christ for his servants.\f* of a marvelous tabernacle, even to the house of God. In the voice of exultation, and confession: the sound of one feasting.
\v 6 Why \f k \fr 41.6 \ft The Prophet comforteth himself, or any just soul, in\f* art thou sorrowful my soul? & why dost thou trouble me? Hope in God, because yet I \f l \fr 41.6 \ft I render thanks and praises.\f* will confess to him: the salvation of my countenance,
\v 7 and my God. My soul is troubled toward myself; therefore will I be mindful of thee from the land \f m \fr 41.7 \ft All this life is like to the small strait place between Jordan and a little hill called Hermoniim, but from this straitness the hope of the just is, to be placed in heaven.\f* of Jordan, and Hermoniim from the little mountain.
\v 8 Depth \f n \fr 41.8 \ft One tentation still succeedeth another:\f* calleth on depth, in the voice of \f o \fr 41.8 \ft and the same so great, as if God opened the gates, and suffered them to overflow like floods of water.\f* thy flood-gates. All thy high things, and thy ways have passed over me.
\v 9 In \f p \fr 41.9 \ft But God helpeth in opportunity, not suffering his servants to be tempted above their strength, giving them fruit with tentations:\f* the day our Lord hath commanded his mercy: and \f q \fr 41.9 \ft yea in the greatest tribulation, he giveth ordinarily most comfort, making them sing spiritually in heart, if not also in voice.\f* in the night a song of him. With me \f r \fr 41.9 \ft One special means to procure divine consolation is prayer in distress.\f* is prayer to the God of my life:
\v 10 I will say to God: Thou art my defender. Why hast thou forgotten me? And why go I sorrowful, whilst mine enemy afflicteth me?
\v 11 Whilst my bones are broken, mine enemies that trouble me have upbraided me: Whilst they say to me day by day: Where is thy God?
\v 12 Why art thou heavy, o my soul? & why dost thou trouble me? \f s \fr 41.12 \ft Still the just soul taketh comfort in assured hope of salvation, the eternal vision of God.\f* Hope in God, because yet I will confess to him: the salvation of my countenance, and my God.
\c 42
\cd The just invocateth God's sentence against the deceitful, that seek his spiritual overthrow, 4. acknowledgeth his help, from Almighty God the B. Trinity, in whose vision glory consisteth. \f - \ft One God the B. Trinity. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 42.1 \ft Holy David often prefiguring Christ, here representeth every faithful servant of God, and particularly when they begin a great and holy work; as when Priests celebrate the divine Sacrifice, they with their assistants recite by interchangeable verses this Psalm.\f* of David. Judge \f b \fr 42.1 \ft After that we have examined, and prepared ourselves to the most holy Sacrifice and Sacrament, according to S. Paul's admonition (let a man prove himself, and so eat this bread, & drink this chalice, 1. Cor. 11.) we pray God, to judge between our true sincere intention, and the unjust deceitful endeavours of our enemy:\f* me, o God, & discern my cause from the nation not holy, from the unjust and deceitful man \f c \fr 42.1 \ft and so to deliver and protect us from subtle malice.\f* deliver me.
\v 2 Because thou art God \f d \fr 42.2 \ft With thee I can do any thing, without thee nothing,\f* my strength: \f e \fr 42.2 \ft thou seemest sometimes not to regard me,\f* why hast thou repelled me? And why go I sorrowful \f f \fr 42.2 \ft whilst tentations are more sensible than thy grace.\f* whilst the enemy afflicteth me.
\v 3 Send forth \f g \fr 42.3 \ft As thou hast sent Christ the light and truth into this world, grant us the same now in particular.\f* thy light and thy truth: \f h \fr 42.3 \ft These two gifts of God, the light of knowing our duties and truth, with sincere intention to perform the same, have brought us into thy Church and unto thy Altar.\f* they have conducted me, and have brought me into thy holy hill, and into thy tabernacles.
\v 4 And \f i \fr 42.4 \ft Accompanied with light of truth, & sincere intention, we confidently approach to thine Altar, o God,\f* I will go in to the altar of God: to God, which \f k \fr 42.4 \ft who changest our old corruption into newness of life.\f* maketh my youth joyful. I will confess to thee \f l \fr 42.4 \ft But to this purpose we praise God on the harp, mortifying our affections.\f* on the harp \f m \fr 42.4 \ft The former word is of the plural number in Hebrew, Eloim, the other of the singular, signifying the Blessed Trinity, one God.\f* o God \f n \fr 42.4 \ft See previous note\f* my God:
\v 5 \f o \fr 42.5 \ft Thou needest not therefore my soul, to be pensive or desolate.\f* why art thou sorrowful, o my soul? And why dost thou trouble me? \f p \fr 42.5 \ft But trust in God,\f* Hope in God, because yet \f q \fr 42.5 \ft praise him,\f* will I confess to him: the salvation \f r \fr 42.5 \ft whom I hope to see face to face,\f* of my countenance, and \f s \fr 42.5 \ft the true eternal God.\f* my God.
\c 43
\cd The Prophet describeth the first calling, and difficult state of the Jewish nation, 6. their prosperity at other times. 10. Again their afflictions in captivity, and persecutions. \f - \ft The state of the Jews. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 43.1 \ft Though this Psalm doth first and literally pertain to the people of Israel, yet all things happening to them, were in figure of the Christian Catholic Church, which began with difficulties, afterwards prospered, and again suffereth much persecution.\f* the end, for the sons of \f b \fr 43.1 \ft Core signifieth caluus, bald; also calvaria a scull, or place of sculls: the name of the place where our Saviour was crucified: so the children of Core signify the children of Christ. S. Aug.\f* Core to understanding.
\v 2 O God we have heard with our ears: our fathers have declared to us. The \f c \fr 43.2 \ft The particular calling of Abraham out of Chaldea, protection of him, and Isaac, and Jacob, the delivery of all Israel out of Ægypt, and establishing them in the promised land of Chanaan, with innumerable, great, & strange things done for them.\f* work that thou hast wrought in their days: & in the days of old.
\v 3 Thy hand destroyed the nations, and thou didst plant them: thou didst afflict the peoples, and expel them.
\v 4 For \f d \fr 43.4 \ft The Israelites conquered not by ordinary power, but by the miraculous hand of God. See Iosue 23.\f* not by their own sword did they possess the land, and their own arm did not save them: But thy right hand, and thine arm, and the illumination of thy countenance: because \f e \fr 43.4 \ft Not that this people deserved of themselves, but of God's free election, all the world being wicked, he gave peculiar grace to Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and some others, & then for their sakes protected the whole people, in them conserving a visible Church.\f* thou wast pleased in them.
\v 5 Thou art the same my King and my God: which commandest the salvations of Jacob.
\v 6 In \f f \fr 43.6 \ft As in former examples, so in David's time, not man's strength, but God's hand gave them great victories.\f* thee we shall turn out our enemies with \f g \fr 43.6 \ft As an oxe with his horn casteth a small thing into the wind.\f* the horn, & in thy name we shall contemn them that rise up against us.
\v 7 For I will not hope in my bow: and my sword will not save me.
\v 8 For thou hast saved us from them that afflict us: and them that hate us thou hast confounded. \x - \xt Ios. 24. 1. Reg. 17. 2. Reg. 8.\x*
\v 9 In God we shall be praised all the day: and in thy name we will confess for ever.
\v 10 But \f h \fr 43.10 \ft The Prophet foretelleth that after prosperity God would suffer the Jews to fall into captivity, & many afflictions, which also signified allegorically diverse states of Christ's Church.\f* now thou hast repelled and confounded us: and thou wilt not go forth, o God, in our hosts.
\v 11 Thou hast turned us back behind our enemies: and they that hated us spoiled for themselves.
\v 12 Thou hast given us as sheep that are to be eaten: and thou \f i \fr 43.12 \ft The Jews are now marvelously dispersed and depressed.\f* hast dispersed us among the nations.
\v 13 Thou hast sold thy people \f k \fr 43.13 \ft In the destruction of Jerusalem the remnant of the people were sold for small, as it were for no price. They had sold Christ for thirty pence;\f* without price: and there was \f l \fr 43.13 \ft and now no multitude, nor number of money at all was given for them, but thirty of them were sold for one penny. Iosephus de bello Iudaico.\f* no multitude in the exchanges of them.
\v 14 Thou hast made us a reproach to our neighbours, a scorn and mocking stock to them that are round about us.
\v 15 Thou hast made us for a parable to the Gentiles: a wagging of the head among the peoples.
\v 16 All the day my shame is against me, and the confusion of my face hath covered me.
\v 17 At the voice of the upbraider, and the reproacher: at the face of the enemy and persecutor.
\v 18 All these things have come upon us, \f m \fr 43.18 \ft Until Christ's passion the Jewish people did not wholly fall from God and true religion. And of them were chosen the Apostles, and many others, that founded and propagated the Church of Christ.\f* neither have we forgotten thee: and we have not done wickedly in thy testament.
\v 19 And our heart hath not revolted backward: & \f n \fr 43.19 \ft The negative particle is here understood by zeugma, according to the Hebrew, thus: Our heart hath not revolted backward, neither hast thou suffered our paths to decline from the way.\f* thou hast declined our paths from thy way.
\v 20 \f o \fr 43.20 \ft Another Hebrew phrase, if we have, for, we have not.\f* Because thou hast humbled us in the place of affliction, and the shadow of death hath covered us.
\v 21 If we have forgotten the name of our God, and if we have spread forth our hands to a strange god,
\v 22 Will not God inquire of these things? For he knoweth the secrets of the heart. Because \f p \fr 43.22 \ft The Prophets and others, persecuted partly before Christ, much more the Apostles, and other Christians in the new Testament.\f* for thee we are killed all the day: we are esteemed as sheep of slaughter. \x - \xt Rom. 8.\x*
\v 23 \f q \fr 43.23 \ft A prayer in affliction.\f* Arise, why sleepest thou, o Lord? Arise, and expel us not to the end.
\v 24 Why dost thou turn away thy face, forgettest our poverty and our tribulation?
\v 25 Because \f r \fr 43.25 \ft We are at death's door, ready to become dust.\f* our soul is humbled in the dust: our \f s \fr 43.25 \ft Lying as groveling sorrowing on the earth: Arise Lord, help us: and redeem us for thy name, till thou deliver us from these tribulations.\f* belly is glued in the earth.
\v 26 Arise Lord, help us: and redeem us for thy name.
\c 44
\cd David singularly moved in heart and tongue, 3. prophesieth Christ's excellency, endowing his Church with most worthy dowries. 11. By way of exhortation foreshewing her internal and external beauty: 17. with perpetual succession of Pastors feeding the flock even to the world's end. \f - \ft Christ most excellently endowing his Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 44.1 \ft Pertaining to the new Testament.\f* the end, for them, \f b \fr 44.1 \ft Gentiles converted from paganism to Christianity:\f* that shall be changed, \f c \fr 44.1 \ft and all others returning from schism, or other sins,\f* to the sons of Core \f d \fr 44.1 \ft for their instruction.\f* for understanding, \f e \fr 44.1 \ft This Psalm is a marriage song of the beloved bridegroom and bride, Christ and his Church.\f* a Canticle for the beloved.
\v 2 My \f f \fr 44.2 \ft Ihave received by divine inspiration in my heart and cogitation,\f* heart hath uttered \f g \fr 44.2 \ft a most high Mystery.\f* a good word: I tell my works \f h \fr 44.2 \ft To the honour therefore and glory of this King (whom I secretly see in my heart) I utter and refer all my works, and this particular Canticle.\f* to the king. My \f i \fr 44.2 \ft From the aboundance of my heart, my tongue also speaketh,\f* tongue is the pen of a scribe, that \f k \fr 44.2 \ft & that presently without delay.\f* writeth swiftly.
\v 3 \f l \fr 44.3 \ft Description of Christ, most excellent in all internal and external gifts.\f* Goodly of beauty above the sons of men, grace is poured abroad in thy lips: therefore hath God blessed thee for ever.
\v 4 Be \f m \fr 44.4 \ft The Prophet seeing in spirit the perfections which he wisheth in Christ, in manner of congratulating, describeth his fortitude, fighting against the devil for the Church.\f* girded with thy sword upon thy thigh, o most mighty.
\v 5 With thy beauty and fairness \f n \fr 44.5 \ft Purposing,\f* intend, \f o \fr 44.5 \ft prosecuting,\f* proceed prosperously, and \f p \fr 44.5 \ft and perfecting the conquest, and so establishing thy spiritual Kingdom.\f* reign, Because of \f q \fr 44.5 \ft Not with warlike armour of this world, but by assaulting the adversary with truth:\f* truth, and \f r \fr 44.5 \ft defending thyself and thy soldiers with the shield of mildness,\f* mildness, and \f s \fr 44.5 \ft and striking the enemy with the sword of justice. Which right force of spiritual fight hath marvelous good success.\f* justice: and thy right hand shall conduct thee marvelously.
\v 6 Thy \f t \fr 44.6 \ft Preaching of Christ's Gospel, his grace moving the hearts of the hearers, is lively and forcible, more piercing than any two edged sword.\f* sharp arrows, the \f v \fr 44.6 \ft The example of people converted, shall move the hearts of the adversaries to come also unto the truth.\f* peoples underneath thee shall fall into the hearts of the king's enemies.
\v 7 Thy seat o God \f w \fr 44.7 \ft Christ's Kingdom shall have no end. Luc. 1. v. 33.\f* for ever and ever: a rod of direction the rod of thy Kingdom. \ef - \fr 44.7 \fq Thy seat, o God, for ever and ever. \ft Seeing S. Paul (Heb. 1. v. 8.) affirmeth expressly that these words are spoken of the Son of God, Christ our Saviour, and thereby proveth his excellency above Angels: John Calvin is wonderful bold to avouch that in the simple & proper sense ¹, David spake of his son Salomon, and the daughter of Pharao, as if that were the literal sense, and S. Paul only expounded it mystically. But first, the solemn preface in the two first verses importeth far greater things, than agree to any terrestrial King. Secondly, this excellent beauty described (v. 3) above the sons of men, cannot be verified of Salomon: for Absalom (2. Reg. 14.) and Adonias were also very beautiful (3. Reg 1. v. 6.) As for Salomon's wisdom, or other virtues, he persevered not therein, and so he was not blessed for ever. Thirdly, the Prophet here calleth the person of whom, and to whom he speaketh, God. v. 7. & 12. Fourthly, not only the ancient Fathers, and Doctors of the Church, but also the Hebrew Rabbins, and the Chaldee paraphrasis, expound this Psalm literally of the promised Messias, and his kingdom the Church. \fq ¹ \ft Calvin expoundeth this Psalm contrary to S. Paul.\ef*
\v 8 Thou \f x \fr 44.8 \ft Thou defendest and rewardest the good, finally forsakest and punishest the wicked.\f* hast loved justice, and hast hated iniquity: therefore God, \f y \fr 44.8 \ft More peculiarly the God of Christ, by hypostatical union.\f* thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness \f z \fr 44.8 \ft Diverse Kings (as David himself, Iosaphat, Ezechias, and Iosias) were as godly as Salomon, and persevered good to the end, which is doubted Salomon did not; but Christ incomparably was anointed, & endued with all graces above all Kings.\f* above thy fellows.
\v 9 \f a \fr 44.9 \ft Mortification which conserveth from putrefying.\f* Myrrh, and \f b \fr 44.9 \ft humility assuaging pride,\f* Aloes, and \f c \fr 44.9 \ft being small in the first spring, groweth great.\f* Cassia from thy \f d \fr 44.9 \ft Humanity assumpted, and sanctified persons, in whom Christ dwelleth as in clean, shining, odoriferous houses.\f* garments, from houses of ivory, out of the which
\v 10 \f e \fr 44.10 \ft Sincere faithful souls more dear to their spouse Christ, than daughters of temporal Kings.\f* the daughters of kings have delighted thee in thy honour.
\v 11 The \f f \fr 44.11 \ft The Catholic Church, in faith purified as gold:\f* Queen stood on thy right hand in golden raiment: compassed with \f g \fr 44.11 \ft with variety of states, as Clergy, Laity, and diverse sorts of religious Orders, and other professions, all united in the same faith, hope, and charity.\f* variety. \f h \fr 44.11 \ft Carefully all that Christ thy spouse speaketh to thee by his Spirit.\f* Hear daughter, and \f i \fr 44.11 \ft Diligently put the same in practice:\f* see, and \f k \fr 44.11 \ft with all obedience and readiness, and return not to former infidelity, nor to corrupt life.\f* incline thine ear: and forget thy people, and the house of thy father.
\v 12 And the King \f l \fr 44.12 \ft Christ loveth the Church adorned with his gifts,\f* will covet thy beauty: because he is the Lord thy God, and \f m \fr 44.12 \ft and mutually his true children love and serve him.\f* they shall adore him.
\v 13 And the daughters \f n \fr 44.13 \ft Many of all nations submit themselves, and all that they have to Christ.\f* of Tyre with gifts, all the rich of the people shall beseech thy countenance.
\v 14 All the glory of that daughter of the King is \f o \fr 44.14 \ft Internal virtues are most special ornaments:\f* within, in \f p \fr 44.14 \ft exterior are required to edify others in diverse sorts of virtues.\f* borders of gold,
\v 15 clothed round about with varieties. \f q \fr 44.15 \ft By this means many more are converted to Christianity:\f* Virgins shall be brought to the King after her: her \f r \fr 44.15 \ft and one country inviteth and draweth another.\f* neighbours shall be brought to thee.
\v 16 They shall be brought in joy and exultation: they shall be brought into the temple of the King. \ef - \fr 44.16 \fq They shall be brought into the temple of the King. \ft The temple of the King, saith S. Augustine, is the Church ¹, the temple of the King is in unity, the temple of the King is not ruinous, not cut insunder, not divided: the joining of living stones is charity. Nothing is more evident. Attend now the very temple of the King, for from thence he speaketh, because of the unity spread in the round earth. For those that would be virgins (faithful souls) unless they be brought into the temple of the King (the Catholic Church) they cannot please the bridegroom. \fq ¹ \ft No salvation out of the Church.\ef*
\v 17 \f s \fr 44.17 \ft As Apostles came in place of Patriarchs and Prophets: so still Bishops and Priests succeed in the Church, Pastors, and governors thereof.\f* For thy fathers there are born sons to thee: thou shalt make them Princes over all the earth. \ef - \fr 44.17 \fq For thy fathers there are born sons to thee. \ft The apostles begot thee (o Christ and Church) they were sent, they preached, they are the fathers ¹. But could they be always corporally with us? Could any of them tarry here till this time? Could they tarry to the time yet to come? But was therefore the Church left desolate by their departure? God forbid. For thy fathers, sons are born to thee. What is this, for thy fathers, sons are born to thee? The Apostles were sent fathers, in place of the Apostles sons are born to thee; Bishops are appointed. For whence were the Bishops born, that are at this day through the world? The Church herself calleth them fathers, she begat them, & appointed them in the seats of the fathers. Do not therefore think thyself desolate (o Christian Church) because thou seest not Peter, seest not Paul: for thou seest not them by whom thou wast born, but of thine issue fatherhood is sprung to thee: For thy fathers, sons are born to thee, thou shalt make them Princes over all the earth. This is the Catholic Church. Her children are made Princes over all the earth: her sons are constituted for fathers. Let them acknowledge this that are cut off: let them come to the unity, be they brought into the temple of the King. Thus S. Augustine. \fq ¹ \ft Perpetual succession of Bishops in place of the Apostles.\ef*
\v 18 They shall \f t \fr 44.18 \ft These Pastors shall still teach the true Christian doctrine.\f* be mindful of thy name in all generation and generation. Therefore shall \f v \fr 44.18 \ft And still there shall be Christian people that will follow and profess the same.\f* peoples confess to thee for ever: and for ever and ever.
\c 45
\cd The Church in persecution acknowledgeth God's perpetual defence, 5. making her thereby more glorious, 10. sometimes granting rest (11. God himself checking the persecuters) and ever protecting her. \f - \ft The Church prospereth also in persecution. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 45.1 \ft Belonging to the Church of Christ.\f* the end, to the sons of Core, for \f b \fr 45.1 \ft As well the cause, why God suffereth his Church to be persecuted, as his assured protection in difficulties, are hidden secrets to the world.\f* the secrets.
\v 2 Our God is a refuge \f c \fr 45.2 \ft All refuge is not secure, for one man is not able always to defend another: but God is a sure and strong refuge.\f* and strength: an \f d \fr 45.2 \ft Ever able and in convenient time willing to help.\f* helper in tribulations, which \f e \fr 45.2 \ft This whole world is full of tribulations, but the Church suffered the greatest in the first persecutions, & shall suffer as great in the time of Antichrist. English Catholics suffer most of all nations in this age, and cannot be suppressed, but still increase in number and fortitude.\f* have found us exceedingly.
\v 3 Therefore will we \f f \fr 45.3 \ft Therefore all Catholics may assuredly know, that the whole Church cannot fail\f* not fear when \f g \fr 45.3 \ft though very many, as now in England,\f* the earth shall be troubled: and \f h \fr 45.3 \ft and very eminent persons, as some noblemen, and some Priests have revolted, yet all will not.\f* mountains transported into the heart of the sea.
\v 4 Their waters have sounded, and were troubled: the mountains were troubled in his strength.
\v 5 The violence of the river \f i \fr 45.5 \ft Such bad examples make the good to recollect themselves more diligently, and to rejoice in God's grace, by which they stand fast.\f* maketh the city of God joyful: the Highest hath sanctified his tabernacle.
\v 6 God is in the midst thereof, it shall not be moved: God will help it \f k \fr 45.6 \ft Before the heat of persecution shall invade all, for the elect the days of tribulation are shortened.\f* in the morning early.
\v 7 Nations are troubled, and \f l \fr 45.7 \ft Sometimes one nation or Kingdom rebelleth against the Church, but cannot destroy it.\f* Kingdoms are inclined: he gave \f m \fr 45.7 \ft By the spirit of Christ, Antichrist, and all his members shall be destroyed.\f* his voice, the earth was moved.
\v 8 The Lord of hosts is with us: the God of Jacob is our defender.
\v 9 Come ye, & see the works of our Lord, what wonders he hath put upon the earth:
\v 10 \f n \fr 45.10 \ft The Church sometimes hath great peace and tranquility.\f* taking away wars even unto the end of the earth. He shall destroy bow, & break weapons: and shields he shall burn with fire.
\v 11 \f o \fr 45.11 \ft God himself restraineth the wicked, suddenly abating their fury, or cutting off their forces.\f* Be quiet, and see that I am God: I shall be exalted among the gentiles, and I shall be exalted in the earth.
\v 12 The Lord of hosts is with us: the God of Jacob is our defender.
\c 46
\cd Gentiles are called, and invited to praise God for his magnificence: for Christ's Ascension, and power. \f - \ft Vocation of Gentiles. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, for \f a \fr 46.1 \ft For Christians that leave the sins of their fathers, and rejoice in Christ crucified. See Annotations. Psal. 4.\f* the sons of Core.
\v 2 All ye Nations \f b \fr 46.2 \ft True joy of the heart sheweth it-self both in voice of exultation, and also in gesture of body, by clapping of hands, dancing (as King David did before the Ark 2. Reg. 6.) likewise with instruments.\f* clap hands: make jubilation to God in the voice of exultation.
\v 3 Because our Lord is high, \f c \fr 46.3 \ft To all the wicked,\f* terrible: a great King over \f d \fr 46.3 \ft not only of one or few kingdoms, but of all the earth.\f* all the earth.
\v 4 He hath made peoples subject \f e \fr 46.4 \ft When Kings and countries become Christians, they are made subjects to the Church that was before, not heads and rulers therefore.\f* to us: & gentiles under our feet.
\v 5 He hath chosen his inheritance in us: the beauty of Jacob which he loved.
\v 6 \f f \fr 46.6 \ft Christ God & man, after his Passion, rose from death and ascended:\f* God is ascended in \f g \fr 46.6 \ft Not leaving his Church desolate, but making her joyful by another comforter, the Holy Ghost.\f* jubilation: and our Lord in the voice of trumpet.
\v 7 Sing ye to our \f h \fr 46.7 \ft The same Christ is our God, by his Divinity:\f* God, sing ye: Sing ye to our \f i \fr 46.7 \ft and our King by his Humanity.\f* King, sing ye.
\v 8 Because God is King of all the earth: sing ye \f k \fr 46.8 \ft Do your endeavour to understand what you sing, read, or hear in God's word. At least to know the principal Mysteries, and points of Christian doctrine, every one according to their capacity and state or profession.\f* wisely.
\v 9 God shall reign over the gentiles: God sitteth upon his holy seat.
\v 10 Princes of peoples are gathered together with \f l \fr 46.10 \ft The faithful of the old and new Testament are united in the service of one & the same eternal God.\f* the God of Abraham: because the strong \f m \fr 46.10 \ft In respect of the Blessed Trinity, holy Scripture here, and in many places useth names of the plural number, as \+it Eloim,\+it* Gods, not dividing God's substance, which is one, but insinuating distinction of Divine Persons, The Father, the Son, and the Holy Ghost. Which Mystery is more expressly mentioned in Baptism, & professed by Christian Gentiles, than it was by the people of the Jews.\f* gods of the earth are exceedingly advanced.
\c 47
\cd God most, and every-where laudable, is especially praised in the Church of Christ (prefigured by Sion, and there begun.) 9. All things being fulfilled in the Church, even as they were prophesied and promised, 12. the faithful are exhorted to consider and congratulate the same. \f - \ft The Church founded and protected by God. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 47.1 \ft Voices beginning the music instruments prosecuted.\f* of Canticle to the sons of Core, the \f b \fr 47.1 \ft Especially for the second day of the week, the day after the sabbath, which is our Sunday, called Dominica, our Lord's day.\f* second of the Sabbath.
\v 2 Great is our Lord, and to be praised exceedingly, in \f c \fr 47.2 \ft Jerusalem, and mount Sion were most obliged to praise God, for greatest benefits received: so the Catholic Church thereby prefigured, & having received far greater, is most of all bound to be grateful.\f* the city of our God, in his holy mount.
\v 3 Mount Sion is founded with the exultation of \f d \fr 47.3 \ft This cannot be affirmed of Sion, or Jerusalem, but is only verified of the Catholic Christian Church:\f* the whole earth, \f e \fr 47.3 \ft whose coasts do extend to the North, & to all quarters of the round earth.\f* the sides of the North, the city of the great King.
\v 4 God shall be known in \f f \fr 47.4 \ft The same one God, one Christ, one Faith, & one Religion in all particular Churches of the whole militant Church.\f* the houses thereof, \f g \fr 47.4 \ft And this Universality and Unity shall be, after that Christ taking man's nature shall be ascended, and shall send the Holy Ghost, to found & begin this Church.\f* when he shall receive it.
\v 5 For behold the kings of the earth \f h \fr 47.5 \ft For the assured certainty of that is foreshewed, the Prophet speaketh in the pretertense, as if it were already done in his time, which he then saw in spirit.\f* were gathered together: they assembled in one.
\v 6 They seeing it so were in admiration, were troubled, were moved:
\v 7 Trembling took them. Their sorrows \f i \fr 47.7 \ft Nothing more moveth the heart, & affecteth all the body and soul, than spiritual cogitations of faith and religion, and therefore it is compared to a woman travailing with child, who hath most careful and grievous pains.\f* as a woman travailing.
\v 8 In a vehement spirit \f k \fr 47.8 \ft In which great conflict of man's spirit, God by his grace giveth force, to break through the contrary assaults of our enemy, to remove all impediments, and to overcome the difficulties.\f* thou shalt break the ships of Tharsis.
\v 9 \f l \fr 47.9 \ft This consideration, that all is now done, that was of old prophesied, is a marvelous confirmation to Christians.\f* As we have heard, so have we seen in the city of the Lord of hosts, in the city of our God: God hath founded it for ever.
\v 10 We have received thy mercy, o God, \f m \fr 47.10 \ft Grace and mercy is only granted to those that are within, or come unto the Catholic Church.\f* in the midst of thy temple.
\v 11 According to thy name, o God, so also is thy praise unto the ends of the earth: thy right hand is full of \f n \fr 47.11 \ft As God is praised for his mercy, so also for his justice: which do never prejudice the one the other.\f* justice.
\v 12 Let mount Sion be glad, and the daughters of Juda rejoice, because of thy judgments, o Lord.
\v 13 Compass Sion, and embrace ye her: \f o \fr 47.13 \ft Consider the fortresses or the Church, which are the holy Fathers, and Doctors, that watch and defend her walls.\f* tell ye in her towers.
\v 14 Set your hearts \f p \fr 47.14 \ft So rest you assured for all matters of faith in this pillar of truth.\f* on her strength: and \f q \fr 47.14 \ft Observe and mark diligently how many particular Churches were speedily founded in the world,\f* distribute ye her houses, that you \f r \fr 47.14 \ft and declare this to other generations, that they may also hold fast the same faith, or return unto it, if they be relapsed; or at last embrace it, if sooner they have not.\f* may declare it in another generation.
\v 15 Because \f s \fr 47.15 \ft Christ God incarnate that worketh all this, is our very God and Saviour, not for a few years, an hundred, six hundred, or a thousand, but for ever & ever.\f* this is God, our God for ever, and for ever and ever: he \f t \fr 47.15 \ft He shall rule as a King, and consequently have a Kingdom, his militant Church, evermore, to the very end of this world. As he shall likewise have his triumphant Church in eternity.\f* shall rule us evermore.
\c 48
\cd The royal Prophet inviting all states and sorts of men to hear him attentively, 6. sheweth that all ought to fear eternal damnation, that live wickedly; 9. vainly and foolishly seeking (13. even like brute beasts) carnal pleasures, which they cannot long enjoy, nor long escape hell: 16. confidently animating himself, and all good men, that trust not in this world. \f - \ft Exhortation to fly from sin for fear of hell. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, \f a \fr 48.1 \ft In this and diverse other titles, both before and yet ensuing, is said, To the sons, or, for the sons of Core, a Psalm, or Canticle, or understanding & the like; but in no place, a Psalm, Canticle &c. of the sons of Core: which no way proveth that they were the authors of such Psalms, but rather the contrary.\f* to the sons of Core a Psalm.
\v 2 Hear these things \f b \fr 48.2 \ft All ye nations and sorts of people\f* all ye Gentiles: receive with your ears all ye that \f c \fr 48.2 \ft that dwell upon the earth, learn this lesson which I will teach you.\f* inhabit the earth.
\v 3 All ye earthly persons, and children of men: together in one the rich and the poor.
\v 4 My mouth shall speak wisdom, and the meditation of my heart prudence.
\v 5 I will \f d \fr 48.5 \ft Holy David harkened to God inspiring him,\f* incline mine ear unto a parable: I will \f e \fr 48.5 \ft and declared to others that which he received from God,\f* open my proposition \f f \fr 48.5 \ft not only by his pen or tongue, but also for better instilling it into their minds he sounded it upon the instrument called the Psalter, which had ten strings, signifying the observation of the ten commandments.\f* on a Psalter.
\v 6 \f g \fr 48.6 \ft What especial thing is there in this life, why or for which I or any have cause to fear the dreadful day of judgement?\f* Why shall I fear in the evil day? \f h \fr 48.6 \ft Marry this we must fear, iniquity, by which any supplanteth, defraudeth, oppresseth, or any way wrongeth others, for that will involve the offender in the sentence of eternal damnation.\f* The iniquity of my heel shall compass me.
\v 7 They \f i \fr 48.7 \ft Such be they that trust in their present power, riches, or other worldly things.\f* that trust in their strength: and glory in the multitude of their riches.
\v 8 A \f k \fr 48.8 \ft Aman's own brother cannot help a sinner in that day,\f* brother doth not redeem, \f l \fr 48.8 \ft much less any other man: so the Hebrew phrase by zeugma, understandeth another negative particle.\f* man shall redeem: he shall not give unto God his reconciliation.
\v 9 And the price of the redemption of his own soul: and he shall \f m \fr 48.9 \ft Still suffer pain,\f* labour for ever,
\v 10 and \f n \fr 48.10 \ft and not die, but live in eternal torments.\f* shall live yet unto the end.
\v 11 He shall not see death, when he shall see \f o \fr 48.11 \ft All both wise and foolish do die temporally: but the wise living in eternal joy, the foolish live in eternal pain.\f* the wise dying, \f p \fr 48.11 \ft Those that believe not any other life after this,\f* the unwise, and \f q \fr 48.11 \ft and those that believing another life, yet live badly in this, shall perish in eternal damnation.\f* the fool shall perish together. And they shall leave their riches to strangers:
\v 12 and their \f r \fr 48.12 \ft They shall never return from their sepulchers\f* sepulchres their \f s \fr 48.12 \ft to enjoy again their houses and earthly possessions.\f* houses for ever. Their Tabernacles in generation and generation: they have renowned their \f t \fr 48.12 \ft Which vainly they labour to establish in their posterity.\f* names in their lands.
\v 13 And \f v \fr 48.13 \ft Amost pithy and brief consideration, for man to think, how absurdly, he being endowed with reason, understanding, & free will, like unto Angels, and capable of eternal glory, setteth his whole study and care upon corporal and temporal things, so making himself like unto brute beasts.\f* man when he was in honour, did not understand: he was compared to beasts without understanding, and became like to them.
\v 14 This their way is \f w \fr 48.14 \ft This care of worldly things is the stumbling block, and cause of eternal ruin:\f* a scandal to them: and \f x \fr 48.14 \ft yet they shall be obstinate, and praise their own desires, still persisting therein.\f* afterward in their mouth they shall take pleasure.
\v 15 As \f y \fr 48.15 \ft Among other creatures a sheep can least help herself in misery: even so the damned in hell are altogether unable to deliver themselves from thence, or to get any relief.\f* sheep they are put in hell: death shall feed upon them. And the just shall rule over them \f z \fr 48.15 \ft In the general resurrection they shall be most of all in misery, as ever dying and never dead: the just whom they wronged, shall be their judges, all friends shall fail them, after they have passed their glory and pleasure in this world.\f* in the morning: and their aid shall wax old in hell from their glory.
\v 16 Nevertheless \f a \fr 48.16 \ft The confidence of the just.\f* God will redeem my soul out of the hand of hell, when he shall take me,
\v 17 Fear not when a man shall be made rich: and when the glory of his house shall be multiplied.
\v 18 Because when he shall die, he shall not take \f b \fr 48.18 \ft He shall leave all worldly things and take nothing with him.\f* all things: neither shall his glory go down with him.
\v 19 Because his soul in his life shall be \f c \fr 48.19 \ft Temporally:\f* blessed: he will confess to thee \f d \fr 48.19 \ft so long as he enjoyeth worldly profits, he will seem grateful to God.\f* when thou shalt do him good.
\v 20 He shall enter in, even to the progenies of his fathers: and he shall \f e \fr 48.20 \ft But they shall not see the true light of heaven.\f* not see light for ever.
\v 21 \f f \fr 48.21 \ft Remember and consider, o worldly man, that God made thee an excellent creature: which thou neglecting makest thyself like to a beast. As, \+it v. 13.\+it*\f* Man, when he was in honour, did not understand: he was compared to beasts without understanding, and became like to them.
\c 49
\cd Christ in his first coming calleth all Nations: 3. in his second will judge the world. 7. In the mean time God exhorteth all men to serve him in purity of virtue, which he much preferreth before external sacrifice of the old law: 17. reprehending such as profess or teach the right way, and live wickedly. \f - \ft General Judgement. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 49.1 \ft To be sung or tuned by Asaph a master of music.\f* to Asaph. The \f b \fr 49.1 \ft God Almighty, who is greater than all falsely supposed gods; or holy persons, that participating of his goodness are called gods (as Kings, Priests, Judges) coming into this world in man's nature, calleth all men to salvation.\f* God of gods our Lord hath spoken: and he hath called the earth, from the rising of the sun even to the going down.
\v 2 Out \f c \fr 49.2 \ft The Church of Christ began in Sion.\f* of Sion the beauty of his comeliness.
\v 3 God will come \f d \fr 49.3 \ft Christ that came in humility, & more obscurely to suffer, and to redeem us, will come in majesty, and manifestly to judge.\f* manifestly: our God and he will not keep silence. \f e \fr 49.3 \ft Immediately before the general judgement, fire shall burn all transitory things.\f* Fire shall burn forth in his sight: and round about him a mighty tempest.
\v 4 He shall \f f \fr 49.4 \ft Give signs in the firmament,\f* call the heaven from above: and \f g \fr 49.4 \ft and in earth.\f* the earth to discern his people.
\v 5 Gather ye together his Saints unto him: which ordain his testament \f h \fr 49.5 \ft Which know that to keep God's commandments in following virtues, is above the oblation of external sacrifice.\f* above sacrifices.
\v 6 And the heavens shall shew forth his justice: because God is Judge.
\v 7 \f i \fr 49.7 \ft God instructeth his people.\f* Hear, o my people, and I will speak: Israel, and I will testify to thee: God thy God am I.
\v 8 I will not rebuke thee in thy sacrifices: and thy holocausts \f k \fr 49.8 \ft Sacrifices are grateful to God:\f* are in my sight always.
\v 9 I will \f l \fr 49.9 \ft But in regard that God needeth not these earthly things, he rather requireth a grateful mind. For otherwise man indeed can give nothing to God: seeing all that is in the whole world is God's own in propriety.\f* not take calves out of thy house: nor buck-goats out of thy flocks.
\v 10 Because all the wild beasts of the woods be mine, the cattle in the mountains and oxen.
\v 11 I have known all the fowls of the air: and the beauty of the field is with me.
\v 12 If I shall be hungry, I will not tell thee: for the round earth is mine, and the fullness thereof.
\v 13 Will I eat the flesh of oxen? Or will I drink the blood of buck-goats?
\v 14 \f m \fr 49.14 \ft Spiritual sacrifices of praise,\f* Immolate to God the sacrifice of praise, and \f n \fr 49.14 \ft & due payment of voluntary vows made in honour of God,\f* pay thy vows to the Highest. \ef - \fr 49.14 \fq 23. The sacrifice of praise. \ft For better and more due performing of external sacrifice ¹, it is requisite, that those which offer it, or desire to participate, do bring with them necessary internal virtues, or disposition; as sorrow and repentance for their sins, which is a kind of improper sacrifice (mentioned in the next Psalm;) the sacrifice of justice, which rendereth to every one that is due (Psal. 4.) and sacrifice of praise, or thanks-giving, for all God's benefits received or expected; which kinds of internal and improper sacrifices, do nothing prejudice, but rightly prepare men of the fruit of external sacrifice, ever used in the law of nature, the law of Moyses, and of Christ. This place also hath another higher and prophetical sense of the Sacrifice of Christ's body in the Eucharist ², which is both propitiatory, and Sacrifice of praise and thanks-giving. So S. Augustine (orat. adversus Judeos c. 6.) teacheth, that here certainly is a plain change of the old sacrifices. The same he affirmeth Ep. 120. c. 18. God foreshewing that the old sacrifices should be changed, which were offered in shadow of a sacrifice to come. I will not take (saith God of Israel calves nor goats at thy hand &c. but appointeth that all Israel (all nations from the rising of the sun to the setting) shall immolate the sacrifice of praise, the same Christ whom old Simeon knew an infant, whom he received into his hands. Likewise li. contra advers. legis & Prophet. c. 20. The Church offereth to God in the body of Christ the sacrifice of praise. \fq ¹ \ft Sacrifice of praise disposeth men to the fruit of external sacrifice. \fq ² \ft The Sacrifice of the Eucharist prophesied.\ef*
\v 15 And \f o \fr 49.15 \ft and praying to him for help in tribulation are most grateful.\f* invocate me in the day of tribulation: I will deliver thee, and thou shalt glorify me.
\v 16 But to the sinner God hath said: \f p \fr 49.16 \ft He that will teach others, must especially fly from sin, & serve God sincerely.\f* Why doest thou declare my justices, and takest my testament by thy mouth?
\v 17 But thou hast hated discipline & cast my words behind thee.
\v 18 If thou didst see a thief, thou didst run with him: and with adulterers thou didst put thy portion.
\v 19 Thy mouth hath abounded with malice: and thy tongue forged guiles.
\v 20 Sitting thou spakest against thy brother, and against thy mother's son thou didst put a scandal:
\v 21 these things hast thou done, and I have held my peace. Thou hast thought unjustly that I will be like thee: I will reprove thee, and set it against thy face.
\v 22 Understand these things you that forget God: lest sometime he take you violently and there be none to deliver you.
\v 23 The sacrifice of praise \f q \fr 49.23 \ft God is honoured by man's gratitude, and other good works.\f* shall glorify me: and there is the way, by which I will shew him the salvation of God.
\c 50
\cd King David in great sorrow for his sins: of adultery and murder, most seriously prayeth God of his manifold mercy to remit and purge all his offences, and pains due for them: 12. to restore unto him the grace of the Holy Ghost, lost by his sins; 15. that he may teach others (as indeed his singular example may teach the whole world true penance) 19. contrition of heart, worthily to offer sacrifice, for the whole Church. \f - \ft The fourth penitential Psalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 50.1 \ft Pertaining not only to David, but also to all penitents, especially of the new testament.\f* the end, a Psalm of David, \x - \xt 2. Reg. 12.\x*
\v 2 when Nathan the Prophet came to him, after that he had sinned with Bethsabee. \ef - \fr 50.2 \fq When Nathan came to David. \ft As Nathan denouncing to David that our Lord had (upon his repentance and confession) taken away his sin ¹, added nevertheless, that because he had made the enemies of God to blaspheme, his son should die: so David knowing that more was required than only confession, for that the bond of satisfaction remained after his sins were remitted, persisted in penance, praying, lamenting, and beseeching God according to his great and manifold mercies, to take away his iniquity, albeit the prophet Nathan had now told him, that our Lord had taken away his sin, because there yet remained temporal pain due for the same. He prayeth also v. 4. that God will, \+it wash him more amply, from his iniquity, and cleanse him from his sin\+it*. ² For albeit the gilt of mortal sin be washed and taken away, yet besides temporal punishment that is due, the soul that was so polluted, needeth to be washed, and cleansed from the evil habit, or proneness to fall again, gotten by the former custom, or delectation in sin. \fq ¹ \ft Temporal punishment is due after remission of sins. \fq ² \ft Custom of sin maketh more proneness to fall again.\ef*
\v 3 Have mercy on me, o God, \f b \fr 50.3 \ft My sins being very great, need thy great mercy.\f* according to thy great mercy. And according \f c \fr 50.3 \ft Yea many sorts of thy mercies: not only remission of the crimes, but also mitigation of the pains due for the same: Thy merciful grace, to be truly sorry, to make some part of satisfaction, to beware hereafter not to fall again, to give better example of penance, and of virtuous life, and to persevere to the end.\f* to the multitude of thy commiserations, take away mine iniquity.
\v 4 Wash me \f d \fr 50.4 \ft O God thou hast forgiven me, and taken away my sins, as thy Prophet hath told me \+it (2. Reg. 12. v. 13.)\+it* but my soul so foully polluted, needeth yet more washing.\f* more amply from mine iniquity: & \f e \fr 50.4 \ft Cleanse also the dregs that remain, and all habits and inclinations to sin. So our Saviour afterwards taught. \+it (Ioan. 13. v. 10.)\+it* He that is washed needeth not saving to wash his feet (ill affections and relics of former sins) but is clean wholly.\f* cleanse me from my sin.
\v 5 \f f \fr 50.5 \ft Whilst I did not know, nor consider, nor acknowledge my sins, I could not be forgiven, but now I know and acknowledge them:\f* Because I do know mine iniquity: and my sin is \f g \fr 50.5 \ft and I cease not to consider of them with sorrow.\f* before me always.
\v 6 To thee \f h \fr 50.6 \ft Principally (for so this particle, only, here signifieth) the enormities of my sins consist in that I have offended thy Divine Goodness and Majesty, the King of the worlds, immortal, invisible, only God, to whom is due all honour and glory for ever and ever, \+it 1. Tim. 1. v. 17.\+it*\f* only have I sinned, and have done evil before thee: that thou mayst \f i \fr 50.6 \ft Thou which hast promised forgiveness to all sinners that truly convert, shall herein be justified by receiving me again to grace:\f* be justified in thy words, and mayst \f k \fr 50.6 \ft and overthrow thy calumniators, that judge wickedly of thy proceedings, as if either thy justice or mercy were perverted.\f* overcome when thou art judged.
\v 7 For behold I \f l \fr 50.7 \ft I & all are born in original sin, the relics whereof, concupiscence and weakness, incline us to other sins, which we have added. In regard of which our infirmity, thy mercy is ready to recall us and help us.\f* was conceived in iniquities: & my mother conceived me in sins. \ef - \fr 50.7 \fq I was conceived in iniquities. \ft Another reason why sinners after remission of all mortal sins ¹, need to be washed, and cleansed, is, because being born in original sin, after remission thereof, there remaineth concupiscence, that striveth against virtue, and inclineth to sin, from which we must pray, and labour to be more and more washed and cleansed. \fq ¹ \ft Concupiscence remaineth after original sin.\ef*
\v 8 For behold thou \f m \fr 50.8 \ft Besides thou hast also given me knowledge of true faith, and right doctrine, which thou ever lovest, and art accustomed to reduce, and direct such into the true way of penance.\f* hast loved truth: \f n \fr 50.8 \ft Yea thou hast moreover shewed to me things uncertain, or unknown to many others, given me the gift and spirit of prophecy, to know hid mysteries, and to every one God giveth some particular benefits, which he loveth in him, and is ready of his part to confirm and maintain the same, that they be not lost.\f* the uncertain and hidden things of thy wisdom thou hast made manifest to me.
\v 9 Thou shalt sprinkle me with \f o \fr 50.9 \ft Most merciful Lord thou wilt (as I see in the spirit of prophecy) sprinkle me, and all men with thy blood from the Cross, where they shall give thee vinegar about hyssop to drink \+it (Ioan. 9.)\+it*\f* hyssop, and I shall be cleansed: thou shalt wash me, and I shall be made \f p \fr 50.9 \ft by which washing I shall be clean from sin, and become in time pure, yea whiter than snow. A figure of this hyssop was observed in Moyses' Law. Num. 16. signifying the lively heat of Christ's infinite charity.\f* whiter than snow.
\v 10 To \f q \fr 50.10 \ft When mine affections shall be clean purged, I shall take singular great delight to hear of thee,\f* my hearing thou shalt give joy and gladness, and \f r \fr 50.10 \ft and all my powers of mind and body, which are now afflicted, shall rejoice.\f* the bones humbled shall rejoice.
\v 11 \f s \fr 50.11 \ft Leave off thy cogitation of punishing, to which purpose first take away mine iniquities, for otherwise if they remain, God's justice cannot but punish them.\f* Turn away thy face from my sins: and wipe away all mine iniquities.
\v 12 \f t \fr 50.12 \ft Create in me new grace, whereby my heart shall be pure. So S. Paul calleth a just soul a new creature, \+it Galat. 6. v. 15.\+it*\f* Create a clean heart in me, o God: and renew a right spirit in my \f v \fr 50.12 \ft In my inward thoughts.\f* bowels.
\v 13 Cast me not away from thy face: and thy Holy spirit \f w \fr 50.13 \ft Suffer me not so to fall again, that thy grace depart from me.\f* take not from me.
\v 14 Render unto me \f x \fr 50.14 \ft Which I had before my fall, of Christ promised of my seed, and alter not the same for my sins. David also and other penitents pray here, that God will restore unto them the joy, which they had in the state of grace, of eternal salvation promised.\f* the joy of thy salvation? And \f y \fr 50.14 \ft Confirm & conserve in me hereafter a strong, constant, and willing spirit to persevere.\f* confirm me with the principal spirit.
\v 15 I \f z \fr 50.15 \ft No way can a penitent better shew himself grateful to God, for remission of his sins, than by instructing, exhorting, & persuading other sinners to repentance, to leave their former ill ways, and turn to God.\f* will teach the unjust thy ways: and the impious shall be converted to thee.
\v 16 Deliver me \f a \fr 50.16 \ft From the guilt and punishment of murder, causing Urias and others with him to be slain. Other penitents pray to be delivered from what sins soever they have committed, by shedding blood, or other wrongs & injuries, promising to praise God's justice, in offering and giving grace, according to his promise to sinners, that they may repent.\f* from bloods, o God, the God of my salvation: and my tongue shall exult thy justice.
\v 17 Lord thou \f b \fr 50.17 \ft Thou, o God, first stirring me up, opening my lips, which of myself I cannot do, then my tongue and mouth will praise thee.\f* wilt open my lips: & my mouth shall shew forth thy praise.
\v 18 Because if thou \f c \fr 50.18 \ft If thou wouldest especially legal sacrifice, I would easily have offered great store:\f* wouldest have had sacrifice, I had verily given it: with holocausts thou wilt \f d \fr 50.18 \ft but the best of that kind is not sufficient:\f* not be delighted.
\v 19 A sacrifice to God is \f e \fr 50.19 \ft true contrition of heart pleaseth thee far better.\f* an afflicted spirit: a contrite and humbled heart, o God, thou wilt not despise. \ef - \fr 50.19 \fq Sacrifice. \ft Holy Scriptures make often comparison between two kinds of sacrifices ¹, preferring internal before external, as more grateful to God. And of spiritual sacrifices, this of a contrite spirit is first in order, and maketh the way to the sacrifice of justice, because justice presupposeth repentance, and finally succeedeth sacrifice of praise, and thanks-giving. \fq ¹ \ft Spiritual sacrifice preferred before external.\ef*
\v 20 Deal favourably, o Lord, in thy good will \f f \fr 50.20 \ft After a penitent hath remission of his own sins, he must pray for the whole Church.\f* with Sion: that the walls of Jerusalem may be built up.
\v 21 \f g \fr 50.21 \ft The Church prospering, her faithful children shall offer\f* Then shall thou accept sacrifice of \f h \fr 50.21 \ft the sacrifice of justice, rendering to every one that is due;\f* justice, \f i \fr 50.21 \ft also free offerings without obligation,\f* oblations, & \f k \fr 50.21 \ft yea holocausts, which is the chiefest,\f* holocausts: \f l \fr 50.21 \ft holocausts, which is the chiefest, (1) calves, and like hosts upon the altar, according to the state of the old law: but in the law of Christ, the most B. Sacrifice by him instituted.\f* then shall they lay calves upon thine altar.
\c 51
\cd Holy David inveigheth against wicked Doeg a traitor, 7. prophesieth his ruin, 10. and his own exaltation. \f - \ft David's invective against Doeg. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, understanding to David,
\v 2 When Doeg \f a \fr 51.2 \ft Of the race of Esau, half a Jew, but either an Infidel, or fautor of Infidels, a spy for Saul, a persecutor of David, & a murderer of Innocents. 1. Reg. 22. v. 9. 18.\f* the Idumeite came and told Saul: David is come into the house of \f b \fr 51.2 \ft High Priest, slain with 84. more Priests and others, because they were supposed to favour David. ibidem.\f* Achimelech. \f 1 \fr 51.2 \ft (1. Reg. 22.)\f*
\v 3 Why doest thou \f c \fr 51.3 \ft Thou persecutor Doeg, why art thou so malicious, to abuse thy credit with King Saul, to the murdering of innocents?\f* glory in malice, which are mighty in iniquity?
\v 4 All the day hath thy tongue thought injustice: as a sharp razor thou \f d \fr 51.4 \ft Playing the part of a spy, in betraying to Saul, that I was with Achimelech?\f* hast done guile.
\v 5 Thou hast loved malice more than benignity: \f e \fr 51.5 \ft Though he told a truth, yet it was iniquity to betray innocents.\f* iniquity rather than to speak equity.
\v 6 Thou hast loved all words of precipitation, a deceitful tongue.
\v 7 Therefore will God destroy thee for ever, he will \f f \fr 51.7 \ft Thou shalt utterly be destroyed,\f* pluck thee out, & remove thee out of thy tabernacle: & \f g \fr 51.7 \ft & all thy race.\f* thy root out of the land of the living.
\v 8 The just shall see, and fear, and shall laugh at him, and they shall say:
\v 9 Behold the man, that hath not put God for his helper. But hath hoped in the multitude of his riches, and hath \f h \fr 51.9 \ft for a short time in this world.\f* prevailed in his vanity.
\v 10 But I as \f i \fr 51.10 \ft David prophesieth his own exaltation, and conservation of his seed in the Kingdom of Israel.\f* a fruitful olive tree in the house of God, have hoped in the mercy of God for ever: and for ever and ever.
\v 11 I will \f k \fr 51.11 \ft Sing praise & thanks to thee.\f* confess to thee for ever, because thou hast done it: and I will expect thy \f l \fr 51.11 \ft Thy Goodness, which agreeth to thy name.\f* name, because it is good in the sight of thy Saints.
\c 52
\cd David in distress cryeth to God for help, 6. Confidently trusting therein, 8. and promising sacrifice of thanks-giving. \f - \ft A prayer in distress. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, for \f a \fr 52.1 \ft Weakness, or mourning.\f* Mæleth, \f b \fr 52.1 \ft S. Augustin expoundeth this Psalm as an instruction to those that suffer persecution and injuries, especially near the end of the world.\f* understandings of David. The fool hath said in his heart: There is no God.
\v 2 They are corrupt, and become abominable in iniquities: there is not that doth good.
\v 3 God hath looked forth from heaven, upon the children of men: to see if there be that understandeth, or seeketh after God.
\v 4 All have declined, they are become unprofitable together: there is not that doth good, no there is not one.
\v 5 Shall they not all know that work iniquity, that devour my people as food of bread?
\v 6 God they have not invocated: there have they trembled for fear, where no fear was. Because God hath \f c \fr 52.6 \ft God will overthrow all the counsels and forces\f* dissipated the bones of them \f d \fr 52.6 \ft of worldly politics.\f* that please men: they are confounded, because God hath despised them.
\v 7 \f e \fr 52.7 \ft The true Church afflicted desireth Christ's coming to deliver the oppressed.\f* Who will give out of Sion the salvation of Israel? When God shall convert the captivity of his people: Jacob shall rejoice, and Israel shall be glad.
\c 53
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 53.1 \ft Though historically this Psalm\f* the end, \f b \fr 53.1 \ft was sung by David the author thereof, shewing how he prayed in danger, and rendered thanks for his delivery,\f* in songs understanding for David
\v 2 when the Zipheites were come, and said to Saul: \f c \fr 53.2 \ft when upon notice given that he abode in the mountains, Saul straitly besieged him, with a great army, but the Philistiims invading the country, Saul was forced to leave David, and to turn his forces against them, \+it 1. Reg. 23.\+it* yet it pertaineth also literally to all just men in distress, especially to the Church of Christ, praying in like dangers, and God by his like providence delivering his servants in extremities.\f* Is not David hid with us? \f 1 \fr 53.2 \ft \+it 1. Reg. c. 23. & 26.\+it*\f*
\v 3 O God save me \f d \fr 53.3 \ft For the glory of thy name\f* in thy name: and in thy strength \f e \fr 53.3 \ft for the justness of my cause defend me.\f* judge me.
\v 4 O God hear my prayer with thine ears: receive the words of my mouth.
\v 5 Because \f f \fr 53.5 \ft Barbarous highland men have betrayed the place of mine abode to the persecuters.\f* strangers have risen up against me, & the strong have sought my soul: and they have not set God before their eyes.
\v 6 For \f g \fr 53.6 \ft But I fear them not, because I am in God's protection.\f* behold God helpeth me: and our Lord is the receiver of my soul.
\v 7 \f h \fr 53.7 \ft Ajust prayer, that God will turn intended mischief upon the devisers' heads,\f* Turn away the evils of mine enemies: and in \f i \fr 53.7 \ft according to his promise, that he will defend the innocent.\f* thy truth destroy them.
\v 8 \f k \fr 53.8 \ft Offering voluntary sacrifice, more than is commanded.\f* I will voluntarily sacrifice to thee, and \f l \fr 53.8 \ft And praise thee, o God.\f* will confess to thy name o Lord, because it is good:
\v 9 \f m \fr 53.9 \ft As I am bound\f* Because thou hast delivered me out of all tribulation: and \f n \fr 53.9 \ft I rejoice in thy just judgements against the wicked.\f* mine eye hath looked down upon mine enemies.
\c 54
\cd The Prophet (as well in his own, as other just men's person) describeth great calamities suffered, 10. prayeth against the wicked, 13. lamenting especially that those which profess friendship, are adversaries, 17. and declareth God's providence in protecting the good, and destroying the bad. \f - \ft God's providence towards the good and bad. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 54.1 \ft Asong as well for King David himself, as others of all times\f* the end \f b \fr 54.1 \ft to sing\f* in songs, \f c \fr 54.1 \ft and consider God's providence, in suffering one man to afflict another in this life.\f* understanding to David.
\v 2 Hear my prayer, o God, despise not my petition.
\v 3 Attend to me and hear me.
\v 4 I am made sorrowful in my \f d \fr 54.4 \ft This life is a warfare, and a continual combat.\f* exercise: and am troubled at the voice of the enemy: and at the tribulation of the sinner. Because they have \f e \fr 54.4 \ft Calumniated me,\f* wrested iniquities upon me: & \f f \fr 54.4 \ft and persecuted me in great fury.\f* in anger they were troublesome to me.
\v 5 My heart is troubled in me: & \f g \fr 54.5 \ft So inwardly afflicted, as if death were at hand.\f* the fear of death is fallen upon me.
\v 6 Fear and trembling are come upon me: and \f h \fr 54.6 \ft Ihave scarce sense, or discourse of reason, being almost overwhelmed with trouble.\f* darkness hath covered me.
\v 7 And I said: \f i \fr 54.7 \ft Would God I could fly, that in the simplicity of a dove, I might speedily part away from these afflictions.\f* Who will give me wings as of a dove, and I will fly: and rest?
\v 8 \f k \fr 54.8 \ft Ihave fled so far as I could from troubles:\f* Lo I have gone far flying away: and I abode in the wilderness.
\v 9 I \f l \fr 54.9 \ft For the rest I remitted to God's will and good pleasure,\f* expected him, that \f m \fr 54.9 \ft and he suffered me not to be overthrown,\f* saved me from \f n \fr 54.9 \ft though I am weak,\f* pusillanimity of spirit, and \f o \fr 54.9 \ft and the tentations are great.\f* tempest.
\v 10 \f p \fr 54.10 \ft O God abate the pride of arrogant persecuters,\f* Precipitate, o Lord, and \f q \fr 54.10 \ft suffer them not to agree amongst themselves.\f* divide their tongues: because I have seen \f r \fr 54.10 \ft They are full of all iniquity,\f* iniquity, and \f s \fr 54.10 \ft they have also contentions among themselves, turn the same to our good.\f* contradiction in the city.
\v 11 Day and night shall iniquity compass it upon the walls thereof, and \f t \fr 54.11 \ft With their continual great iniquity, they have their troubles,\f* labour in the midst thereof, and \f v \fr 54.11 \ft but leave not their injustice.\f* injustice.
\v 12 And there hath not ceased out of the streets thereof \f w \fr 54.12 \ft They are still usurers, and deceitful oppressors of the poor.\f* usury and guile.
\v 13 For \f x \fr 54.13 \ft It is a greater grief to suffer injuries of those that seem to be friends.\f* if mine enemy had spoken evil to me, I would verily have born it. And if he that hated me had spoken great things upon me: I would perhaps have hid myself from him.
\v 14 But \f y \fr 54.14 \ft Aman that was, or seemed of the same mind, faith, and religion,\f* thou a man of the same mind: my \f z \fr 54.14 \ft whom I so trusted, that I would have gone, whithersoever he should have led me.\f* guide, and my familiar.
\v 15 Which didest \f a \fr 54.15 \ft Thou that didst participate the same holy sacraments with me,\f* take sweet meats together with me: in the \f b \fr 54.15 \ft in the Catholic Church.\f* house of God we walked with consent.
\v 16 Let \f c \fr 54.16 \ft As Core & his complices: spoken of just zeal, not of desire to revenge: verified in those that sin wittingly and knowing, for they descend, as it were, alive into hell.\f* death come upon them: and let them go down quick into hell. Because there is wickedness in their habitations, in \f d \fr 54.16 \ft The whole crew of the wicked conspire in iniquity.\f* the midst of them.
\v 17 But I have cried to God, and our Lord will save me.
\v 18 In \f e \fr 54.18 \ft The Prophet alludeth to three more specially appointed hours of Divine service, the daily sacrifice at morning, and evening, and other sacrifices commonly about midday. Which also are the three principal times of Divine Service in the Church of Christ: Matins, Evensong, and the Sacrifice of Mass. Which Eutymius and other Grecians call Liturgiam. S. Clement also \+it (li. 7. c. 25. Apost. Instit.)\+it* testifieth that the Apostles ordained three set hours of common prayer every day.\f* the evening, and morning, & at midday, I will \f 1 \fr 54.18 \ft \+it * or pray\+it*\f* speak, and declare, and he will hear my voice.
\v 19 He will redeem my soul in peace from them, that approach to me: because among many \f f \fr 54.19 \ft Many enemies combined together approached unto me, to overthrow me.\f* they were with me.
\v 20 God will hear, and he \f g \fr 54.20 \ft Eternal God.\f* which is before the worlds will humble them.
\v 21 For there is \f h \fr 54.21 \ft They will never repent of their wickedness.\f* no change with them, & they feared not God: he hath stretched forth his hand in repaying.
\v 22 They have contaminated his testament, they are \f i \fr 54.22 \ft They harden their hearts against his threatened wrath:\f* divided by the wrath of his countenance; and \f k \fr 54.22 \ft but God's providence illuminateth others to know and teach the truth, when it is impugned or contemned.\f* his heart hath approached. \f l \fr 54.22 \ft God's words, which in themselves are meek and sweet,\f* His words are made softer than oil: and \f m \fr 54.22 \ft are hard to the incredulous, & as darts that wound them. Christ said (Ioan. 6.) Unless one eat my flesh, & drink my blood, he shall not have life in him, which the Capharnaites not understanding said one to another: This is a hard speech, who can abide it? Which S. Augustin here saith was the first heresy against our Saviour's preaching. It was not hard to S. Peter, who in the name of the rest, answered, that Christ had the words of eternal life. He yet understood not the secret of our Lord's speech, but he piously believed that the words were good, which he understood not.\f* the same are darts.
\v 23 \f n \fr 54.23 \ft Therefore in all doubts of doctrine, in all distresses of persecution, and other difficulties which surpass thy weakness, cast thy care upon our Lord, and he will nourish thee.\f* Cast thy care upon our Lord: and he will nourish thee: he will not give \f o \fr 54.23 \ft He will not suffer the just to remain always in fluctuation, that is, in doubtful, dangerous, and wavering thoughts or perplexities, as when a ship is tossed in the waves of the sea, but will give quiet repose of mind, as in a sure haven without danger of drowning.\f* fluctuation to the just for ever.
\v 24 But thou, o God, wilt bring \f p \fr 54.24 \ft Contrarywise the wicked and obstinate shall fall into destruction.\f* them down into the pit of destruction. \f q \fr 54.24 \ft Often or for most part blood-suckers die before the course of nature requireth, as Saul, Absalom, Achitophel, Achab, Jezabel, and the like.\f* Bloody and deceitful men shall not live half their days, but I will hope in thee, o Lord.
\c 55
\cd David being in danger before Achis King of Geth, confidently imploreth God's help, against the great malice and power of his enemies; 8. foresheweth their ruin, his own exaltation, 12. and offereth praises and thanks. \f - \ft David's prayer in danger. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 55.1 \ft This Psalm pertaineth also to future times,\f* the end, \f b \fr 55.1 \ft for the use of any just persons, or people, that are against their will separated from the public divine Service of holy Church:\f* for a people, that is made far from the Saints, David in \f c \fr 55.1 \ft most worthy to be noted with title, for perpetual memory,\f* the inscription of the title, \f d \fr 55.1 \ft made by David when the Philistiims detected him to their King in Geth.\f* when the foreigners held him in Geth. \x - \xt 1. Reg. 12. v. 12.\x*
\v 2 Have mercy on me, o God, because \f e \fr 55.2 \ft Now one sort of ill disposed men, now another,\f* man hath trodden upon me: \f f \fr 55.2 \ft never cease to seek my destruction.\f* all the day impugning he hath afflicted me.
\v 3 Mine enemies have trodden upon me all the day: because they are \f g \fr 55.3 \ft Saul with his great army, the Philistiims, & other strangers, some in manifest hostility, others detecting and betraying me to mine adversaries. So all that live godly in Christ have many enemies visible and invisible.\f* many that war against me.
\v 4 From \f h \fr 55.4 \ft Of these most eminent great dangers I am indeed afeared,\f* the height of the day I shall fear: \f i \fr 55.4 \ft but so that my trust and assured confidence is in thee, o God.\f* but I will trust in thee.
\v 5 In God I will praise \f k \fr 55.5 \ft Words and promises made to me, or the good which I speak or do by God's grace.\f* my words, in God have I hoped: I will not fear what flesh may do to me.
\v 6 All the day did they \f l \fr 55.6 \ft They calumniate whatsoever I say,\f* detest my words; against me \f m \fr 55.6 \ft wrestling all my words to evil sense.\f* all their cogitations are unto evil.
\v 7 They will \f n \fr 55.7 \ft They meet together, and secretly conspire to entrap me or catch me tripping.\f* inhabit and keep secret: they will observe my heel. As they have expected \f o \fr 55.7 \ft To take my life:\f* my soul,
\v 8 \f p \fr 55.8 \ft for this their vain purpose to destroy me, thou wilt save them, as they deserve, that is,\f* for nothing shalt thou save them: \f q \fr 55.8 \ft thou wilt break them in pieces.\f* in wrath thou wilt break peoples. O God,
\v 9 I have shewed my life to thee: thou hast set my tears in thy sight. As also in thy promise:
\v 10 then shall mine enemies be turned backward. In what day soever I shall invocate thee: lo I have known that thou art my God.
\v 11 In God I will praise \f r \fr 55.11 \ft Iwill always gratefully acknowledge thy promises, and sayings, for they are assured.\f* the word, in our Lord will I praise thee saying: I have hoped in God, I will not fear what man can do to me.
\v 12 In me, o God \f s \fr 55.12 \ft Ihave purposed and vowed to offer sacrifice of praise, and by thy help will perform it.\f* are thy vows, which I will render, praises to thee.
\v 13 Because thou hast delivered my soul from death, and my feet from falling: that I may \f t \fr 55.13 \ft Do that pleaseth God,\f* please before God, in \f v \fr 55.13 \ft in true faith and pious works.\f* the light of the living.
\c 56
\cd Holy David inveigheth against dissembling wicked men: 7. describeth their manifold punishment, 11. wherein the just shall be comforted. \f - \ft God's providence in suffering evil. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 56.1 \ft The heroical facts of David are for example to all Christians.\f* the end, \f b \fr 56.1 \ft Innocent David having opportunity to kill his unjust persecutor, obeyed the motion of God, suggesting unto him, not to destroy his enemy, contrary to the counsel of his friends:\f* destroy not, to David in \f c \fr 56.1 \ft a thing most worthy to be recorded for perpetual memory,\f* the inscription of the title, \f d \fr 56.1 \ft being in so great and unjust trouble, as to lie in the cave of a mountain, yet spared to kill, or hurt him, that drive him into such straits. \+it See the history 1. Reg. 24.\+it*\f* when he fled from the face of Saul into the cave. \x - \xt 1. Reg. 22. 24.\x*
\v 2 Have mercy on me, o God, have mercy on me: because my soul hath trusted in thee. And I will hope in the shadow of thy wings, until iniquity pass.
\v 3 I will cry to God the highest: God that hath done me good.
\v 4 He sent \f e \fr 56.4 \ft Extraordinary divine help, passing man's power.\f* from heaven, and delivered me: he hath given into \f f \fr 56.4 \ft It fell reproachfully to Saul, that David might have slain him if he would, yet did neither hurt him, nor insult upon him, but meekly and piously admonished him of his error, and injurious persecution.\f* reproach them that trod upon me. God hath sent his mercy and his truth,
\v 5 and hath delivered my \f g \fr 56.5 \ft My life\f* soul out of the midst of \f h \fr 56.5 \ft from most mighty and ravenous persecuters.\f* lions' whelps: I slept troubled. The sons of men, their \f i \fr 56.5 \ft Though they have not lion's natural teeth, yet they exercise cruelty by artificial weapons,\f* teeth are weapons and arrows: and their \f k \fr 56.5 \ft and with their cruel tongues incite their followers to the same fury. \+it 1. Reg. 22. v. 16.\+it*\f* tongue a sharp sword.
\v 6 Be exalted above the heavens, o God: and thy glory upon all the earth.
\v 7 They prepared a snare for my feet: and bowed down my soul. They \f l \fr 56.7 \ft Saul endeavoured many ways to overthrow David, amongst other means provoked him to set upon the Philistiims, thinking they should have slain him. \+it 1. Reg. 18. v. 17.\+it* but the same Philistiims overthrew Saul. \+it 1. Reg. 31.\+it*\f* digged a pit before my face: and they are fallen into it.
\v 8 My heart is ready, o God, my heart is ready: I will sing, and say \f 1 \fr 56.8 \ft Psalms more in use with Christian Gentiles, than they were with the Jews. See page. 12.\f* a Psalm.
\v 9 Arise my glory, arise psalter and harp: I will arise early.
\v 10 I will confess to thee among \f m \fr 56.10 \ft God's benefits bestowed upon David, and upon faithful Christians prefigured by him, are for ever to be praised by all people's and nations.\f* peoples, o Lord: and I will say a Psalm to thee among \f n \fr 56.10 \ft See previous note\f* the Gentiles.
\v 11 Because thy mercy is magnified even to the heavens, and thy truth even to the clouds.
\v 12 Be exalted above the heavens o God: and thy glory upon all the earth.
\c 57
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 57.1 \ft This Psalm was made upon the same occasion, and to the same purpose as the former,\f* the end, \f b \fr 57.1 \ft to exhort the just and innocent to patience,\f* destroy not, to David, in \f c \fr 57.1 \ft by David's memorable example.\f* the inscription of the title.
\v 2 If \f d \fr 57.2 \ft Few are so wicked, but they speak and pretend just things:\f* in very deed you speak justice: judge right things ye sons of men.
\v 3 For in \f e \fr 57.3 \ft But neither think well,\f* the heart you work iniquities: in the earth your \f f \fr 57.3 \ft nor do well, but both contrary, which feigned sanctity is double iniquity.\f* hands forge injustice.
\v 4 \f g \fr 57.4 \ft These wicked sinners that flatter and incite King Saul, seem to have spent all their life from their infancy in malice.\f* Sinners are alienated from the matrice, they have erred from the womb: they have spoken false things.
\v 5 They have fury according to the similitude of \f h \fr 57.5 \ft Their fury is unquiet, till they may wound the innocent with their poisonful sting,\f* a serpent: as of \f i \fr 57.5 \ft neither will they harken to good admonitions, but stop their ears like an asp, that layeth one ear close to the ground, & stoppeth the other with his tail.\f* the asp that is deaf, and stoppeth his ears.
\v 6 Which will not hear the voice of the enchanters, and of the sorcerer enchanting wisely.
\v 7 God shall break their \f k \fr 57.7 \ft But God will break their cruel force,\f* teeth in their mouth, the \f l \fr 57.7 \ft though it seemeth most strong and insuperable.\f* cheek-tooth of the lions, our Lord will break in pieces.
\v 8 They shall come to nothing as water running down: he hath \f m \fr 57.8 \ft God's just determination of punishing the wicked still remaineth bent and ready, though execution be some while deferred.\f* bent his bow till they be weakened.
\v 9 As \f n \fr 57.9 \ft That force and power which is now invincible, hard and strong like a lion's strongest teeth, shall then be as impotent and soft as wax:\f* wax that melteth, shall they be taken away: \f o \fr 57.9 \ft God's wrath, like fire, the most forcible element, shall fall upon them, and they shall be cast into utter darkness, deprived of the sun and all comfortable light.\f* fire hath fallen on them, and they have not seen the sun.
\v 10 Before your \f p \fr 57.10 \ft Before their malice can bring to effect the great mischiefs which they plot and purpose, God suddenly cutteth them off, before they fully understand of either sickness or death, casteth them as it were alive into hell.\f* thorns did understand the old briar: as living so in wrath he swalloweth them.
\v 11 The just \f q \fr 57.11 \ft The just rejoice in the punishment of the wicked for three causes, first in zeal of justice, conforming his will and mind to God's judgement; secondly, for that himself through God's mercy hath escaped that terrible damnation; thirdly, for that he is now delivered from molestation, and continual tribulation.\f* shall rejoice when he shall see revenge: he shall wash his hands in the blood of a sinner.
\v 12 And man shall say: If certes there be fruit to the just: \f r \fr 57.12 \ft The just seeing, or by faith knowing what punishment remaineth for the wicked, is thereby assured that the good shall reap fruit for his well-doing, and that in the mean time God ruleth and judgeth on the earth, though as yet it appeareth not so evidently.\f* there is a God certes judging them on the earth.
\c 58
\cd Holy David (being besieged in his own house by men sent to kill him) confidently prayeth God to deliver him, 6. and all faithful nations in like danger: 7. and praiseth God. \f - \ft Another prayer of David in danger. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, destroy not, to David in the inscription of the title, \f a \fr 58.1 \ft King Saul having thrice attempted in vain to kill David ( \+it 1. Reg. 18. v. 11.\+it* &c. \+it 19. v. 9.\+it* ) sent some of his guard to fetch him, from his own house, that he might be slain: but God moved the mind of Michol, to admonish him of the danger and to help him away in safety, though Saul thought she would have been a scandal unto him (or cause of ruin) by the hands of the Philistians. \+it 1. Reg. 18. v. 21.\+it* Upon which occasion David made this Psalm. As he also made others,\f* when Saul sent, and watched his house to kill him. \x - \xt 1. Reg. 19.\x*
\v 2 Deliver me from mine enemies, o my God: and from them that rise up against me defend me.
\v 3 Deliver me from them that work iniquity: and from bloody men save me.
\v 4 Because lo they \f b \fr 58.4 \ft They have so straitly besieged me, that it is now in their hands to take away my life.\f* have taken my soul: the strong have fallen violently upon me.
\v 5 Neither is it mine iniquity, nor my sin, o Lord: \f c \fr 58.5 \ft Of my part I have committed no fault against mine enemies, for which they can have any just cause to persecute me.\f* without iniquity have I run, and gone directly.
\v 6 Rise up to meet me, and see: and thou, o Lord, the God of powers, God of Israel, attend to visit \f d \fr 58.6 \ft The Prophet foreseeing in spirit, that the Catholic Church shall be unjustly persecuted, prayeth, and teacheth others to pray, that God will mercifully visit his faithful people of all nations:\f* all nations: have \f e \fr 58.6 \ft and not spare obstinate persecuters.\f* no mercy on all that work iniquity.
\v 7 They will return \f f \fr 58.7 \ft Persecuters labouring how much, or how long soever, shall at night, that his, in the end of all their wicked endeavours be unsatisfied in their desires,\f* at evening: and they shall suffer famine \f g \fr 58.7 \ft as hungry dogs that run hunting all the day, & night also, still seeking & not finding wherewith to fill their ravenous mouths and devouring bellies.\f* as dogs, and shall compass the city.
\v 8 Behold they \f h \fr 58.8 \ft They threaten and determine to use all cruelty,\f* will speak in their mouth, and a sword in their lips: \f i \fr 58.8 \ft as if there were no God, that heareth, and will punish it.\f* because who hath heard?
\v 9 And thou, o Lord wilt scorn them: thou wilt bring to nought all the nations.
\v 10 I will keep my strength to thee: \f k \fr 58.10 \ft Through God's grace the Church is still strong and the virtuous do persevere.\f* because thou art my receiver:
\v 11 my God, thy mercy shall prevent me.
\v 12 God will shew unto me concerning mine enemies, kill them not \f l \fr 58.12 \ft God suffereth afflictions to fall upon his servants, to keep them exercised, lest in prosperity they forget their duties to him.\f* lest sometime my peoples forget. Disperse them in thy strength: and \f m \fr 58.12 \ft Deprive them of power, that they may not do so much evil as they desire.\f* depose them my protector, o Lord.
\v 13 The sin of their mouth, the word of their lips: and let them be taken in their pride. And for \f n \fr 58.13 \ft After that their iniquity is complete\f* cursing and lying they shall be \f o \fr 58.13 \ft they shall be accused and punished for their blasphemies and lies.\f* talked of
\v 14 in consummation: in wrath of consummation and they shall not be. And they shall know that God will rule over Jacob: and over the ends of the earth.
\v 15 They \f p \fr 58.15 \ft As \+it v. 7.\+it*\f* shall be turned at evening, and shall suffer famine as dogs: & shall compass the city.
\v 16 They shall be \f q \fr 58.16 \ft They shall in vain seek oil for their lamps with the foolish virgins, repent with Judas, and finding no help,\f* dispersed to eat: and if they be not filled, they \f r \fr 58.16 \ft shall continually blaspheme in hell.\f* will murmur also.
\v 17 But I will sing thy strength: and will exalt thy mercy in \f s \fr 58.17 \ft In the resurrection.\f* the morning. Because thou art become my receiver, and my refuge in the day of my tribulation.
\v 18 My helper, I will sing to thee, because thou art God my receiver: my God, my mercy.
\c 59
\cd King David after his own and the people's many tribulations, 8. rendereth thanks for their renowned victories, 11. achieved by God's only power. \f - \ft King David's thanks for victories. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, for them \f a \fr 59.1 \ft The change of state from adversity to prosperity in the people of Israel, was a figure of the like change in the Church of Christ,\f* that shall be changed, in \f b \fr 59.1 \ft worthy to be remembered,\f* the inscription of the title \f c \fr 59.1 \ft for the instruction\f* to David himself, \f d \fr 59.1 \ft of God's beloved,\f* for doctrine,
\v 2 \f e \fr 59.2 \ft as the same are more largely recorded in the books of Kings.\f* when he set fire on Mesopotamia of Syria, and in Sobal, and Joab returned, and struck Idumæa in the valley of salt-pits twelve thousand. \x - \xt 2. Reg. 8. & 10. & 1. Paral. 18.\x*
\v 3 O God thou \f f \fr 59.3 \ft God suffereth his people to be afflicted, as well for their sins, as for exercise in virtue,\f* hast repelled us, & hast destroyed us: thou wast angry \f g \fr 59.3 \ft after sheweth his mercy in pardoning, and favour in advancing them.\f* and hast had mercy on us.
\v 4 Thou hast moved the earth, and hast troubled it: heal the breaches thereof, because it is moved.
\v 5 Thou hast shewed unto thy people \f h \fr 59.5 \ft Punishing sinners.\f* hard things: thou hast made us drink the wine of compunction.
\v 6 Thou hast \f i \fr 59.6 \ft Warning them to amend.\f* given a signification to them that fear thee: that they fly from the face of the bow.
\v 7 That thy beloved \f k \fr 59.7 \ft and then restoreth them to former good state.\f* may be delivered, save me with thy right hand, and hear me.
\v 8 God hath spoken in \f l \fr 59.8 \ft God also as he hath promised by his holy oracle,\f* his holy: I shall rejoice, and \f m \fr 59.8 \ft hath advanced King David in his temporal Kingdom, and doth much more advance him, and other elect in everlasting life.\f* shall divide Sichem: and shall measure the valley of tabernacles.
\v 9 Galaad is mine, and Manasses is mine: and Ephraim the strength of my head.
\v 10 Juda my king: Moab \f n \fr 59.10 \ft As a vessel for meaner uses.\f* the pot of my hope. Into Edom I will \f o \fr 59.10 \ft Bring it under my dominion.\f* stretch out my shoe: the foreigners are subject to me.
\v 11 Who shall conduct me into a fenced city? Who shall conduct me even into Idumea?
\v 12 Shall not thou, o God, \f p \fr 59.12 \ft As God doth sometimes punish\f* that hast expelled us: and \f q \fr 59.12 \ft so he also rewardeth.\f* wilt not thou o God, go forth in our hosts?
\v 13 Give us aid from tribulation: because man's salvation is vain.
\v 14 In God we shall do \f r \fr 59.14 \ft Strongly, with fortitude.\f* strength: and he shall bring to nothing them that afflict us.
\c 60
\cd Faithful people of the whole earth pray, and acknowledge that God mercifully heareth their prayer. 6. Expect the eternal kingdom of Christ, in which they shall praise him for evermore. \f - \ft A confident prayer for Christ's Incarnation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, \f a \fr 60.1 \ft In songs of praise and thanks to God.\f* in hymns to David.
\v 2 Hear, o God, my petition: attend to my prayer.
\v 3 From \f b \fr 60.3 \ft From all coasts of the earth faithful people pray to God.\f* the ends of the earth I have cried to thee: whilst my heart was in anguish, thou \f c \fr 60.3 \ft The Church builded upon an assured foundation, is exalted to great power and dignity.\f* didst exalt me on a rock.
\v 4 Thou hast \f d \fr 60.4 \ft God conducteth, defendeth, and delivereth those that confidently trust in him.\f* conducted me, because thou art made my hope: a tower of strength from the face of the enemy.
\v 5 I shall inhabit in \f e \fr 60.5 \ft In the Church, a place of assured protection.\f* thy tabernacle for ever: I shall be protected in the covert of thy wings.
\v 6 Because thou my God hast heard my prayer: thou hast given inheritance to those that fear thy name.
\v 7 Thou wilt add \f f \fr 60.7 \ft Christ's kingdom, the Church, perpetual to the end of this world, and eternal after the general Resurrection.\f* days upon the days of the king: his years even unto the day of generation and generation.
\v 8 He is permanent for ever in the sight of God: his \f g \fr 60.8 \ft Who is able to understand or explicate how great Christ's mercy is in redeeming us,\f* mercy and \f h \fr 60.8 \ft and his truth in performing his promised rewards?\f* truth who shall require?
\v 9 So \f i \fr 60.9 \ft For so inestimable benefits I will always praise thee with Psalms, Canticles, or other thanks in this life.\f* will I say a Psalm to thy name for ever and ever: that I may render my vows \f k \fr 60.9 \ft and eternally in the life to come.\f* from day to day.
\c 61
\cd A just man encourageth his own soul to serve God in sincere humility, 9. exhorteth also all others to trust in God, not in false and worldly policy, or wealth, because God's power & mercy will render to every one as they deserve. \f - \ft Exhortation to good life, in respect of reward or punishment. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, for \f a \fr 61.1 \ft Directed to Idithun, one of the masters of music, to sing it, or to make tune for it.\f* Idithun a Psalm of David.
\v 2 Shall \f b \fr 61.2 \ft The wicked treating to ruinate others, David, or any just man, feareth them not, because his soul is subject to God.\f* not my soul be subject to God? For of him is my salvation.
\v 3 For he is my God, and my saviour: my receiver, \f c \fr 61.3 \ft Therefore I firmly purpose never to be moved from God.\f* I shall be moved no more.
\v 4 \f d \fr 61.4 \ft In vain do you mine adversaries still assault me,\f* How long set you violently upon a man: \f e \fr 61.4 \ft though ye be all confederate to kill me,\f* you all do kill, as it were upon \f f \fr 61.4 \ft supposing me to be like a ruinous or shaken wall, that is easily thrown down.\f* a wall that is leaning, and a wall shaken.
\v 5 But \f g \fr 61.5 \ft They think still to deprive me of my reward, the price of my labours and merits,\f* yet they thought to repel my price. \f h \fr 61.5 \ft but I run so much more diligently, as thirsting after righteousness in this life, and glory in the next, to finish my course.\f* I ran in thirst: they \f i \fr 61.5 \ft Amost dangerous tentation, when after threats and cruelty, persecutors endeavour by sweet words and promises to persuade the just to fall into sin.\f* blessed with their mouth, and cursed with their heart.
\v 6 But yet my soul be thou subject to God: because my patience is from him.
\v 7 Because he is my God, and my saviour: my helper \f k \fr 61.7 \ft I resolutely purpose not to yield to any tentation.\f* I shall not remove.
\v 8 In God is my salvation, and my glory: the God of my help, and my hope is in God.
\v 9 Hope in him \f l \fr 61.9 \ft God's faithful servants are not only constant themselves, but also exhort and persuade all others, as much as in them lieth, to serve God and trust in him.\f* all ye the congregation of people: pour out your hearts before him: God is our helper for ever.
\v 10 But yet the children of men are vain, the children of men are \f m \fr 61.10 \ft Using false weights they defraud one another.\f* liars in balances: that they may deceive by vanity together.
\v 11 Hope not in iniquity, and covet not robberies: if riches abound set not your heart upon them.
\v 12 \f n \fr 61.12 \ft God having once spoken it is most assured.\f* Once hath God spoken, these \f o \fr 61.12 \ft Two especial attributes of God.\f* two things have I heard:
\v 13 That \f p \fr 61.13 \ft God is omnipotent, so that he can both reward and punish infinitely;\f* power is God's, and \f q \fr 61.13 \ft and Merciful, that he is ready to receive all sinners into his favour, if they will repent & turn to him.\f* mercy, o Lord, is to thee: because thou wilt render to every one according to his works. \x - \xt Mat. 16. Rom. 2. 1. Cor. 3. Gal. 6.\x*
\c 62
\cd David in banishment with great affection desireth to unite himself with God in meditation, 4. purposing and promising ever to praise him: 10. prophesieth the vain endeavours, and condemnation of his enemies and his own advancement. \f - \ft David's devotion in banishment. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 62.1 \ft Holy David made this devout meditation when he was in the forest of Haret, or desert of Ziph. 1. Reg. 22. & 23. and could not come to the tabernacle of God, nor to Jerusalem, where he especially desired to be, in the inheritance of our Lord, which was to him a great affliction. As the like is now to Catholics, when they are put in close prison for their faith, or otherwise hindered, that they cannot be present at the most holy and daily Sacrifice. In which case we must supply as we may, this great loss, and comfort ourselves with this, or like Psalm, or prayer, saying: O God my God, to thee I watch.\f* of David when he was in the desert of Juda. \x - \xt Reg. 22.\x*
\v 2 O God my God to thee I watch, \f b \fr 62.2 \ft Even from the first dawning of the morning,\f* from the morning light. \f c \fr 62.2 \ft my soul thirsteth after thee,\f* My soul hath thirsted to thee, \f d \fr 62.2 \ft yea also my very flesh, and whole body feeleth great pains, by this affliction of mind, and desireth relief and rest.\f* my flesh to thee very many ways.
\v 3 In a desert land, and inaccessible, and without water, \f e \fr 62.3 \ft Being now in case that I cannot serve thee, o God, as I would, yet I exhibit myself present in spirit, before thy holy place,\f* so in the holy have I appeared to thee, \f f \fr 62.3 \ft meditating thy power, and thy glory.\f* that I might see thy strength, and thy glory.
\v 4 Because \f g \fr 62.4 \ft This consolation in banishment from thy divine Service, is sweeter to me than many temporal lives, or any worldly prosperity.\f* thy mercy is better than lives, my lips shall praise thee.
\v 5 \f h \fr 62.5 \ft for as the Passions of Christ abound in us (saith S. Paul. \+it 2. Cor. 1.\+it* ) so also by Christ our comfort aboundeth.\f* So will I bless thee in my life: and in thy name I will lift up my hands.
\v 6 As with \f i \fr 62.6 \ft Replenish my soul, o God, with the aboundance of thy grace,\f* marrow and fatness let my soul be filled: \f k \fr 62.6 \ft so shall I be more able to praise thee.\f* and my mouth shall praise with lips of exultation.
\v 7 \f l \fr 62.7 \ft Seeing in the night, also in my bed I meditate on thee;\f* If I have been mindful of thee upon my bed, \f m \fr 62.7 \ft Iwill more diligently do the same in the morning.\f* in the morning I will meditate on thee:
\v 8 because thou hast been my helper.
\v 9 And in the covert of thy wings I will rejoice, my soul hath cleaved after thee: thy right hand hath received me.
\v 10 But \f n \fr 62.10 \ft My temporal and spiritual enemies:\f* they in vain have sought my soul, \f o \fr 62.10 \ft and they shall be damned for their sins.\f* they shall enter into the inferior parts of the earth.
\v 11 They \f p \fr 62.11 \ft It happened literally to Saul, that he was slain in battle; which he made against his enemies\f* shall be delivered into the hands of the sword, they shall be \f q \fr 62.11 \ft and his dead body was hung on a wall \+it (1. Reg. 31.)\+it* exposed to wild beasts, or birds, though it was afterwards burnt and buried.\f* the portions of foxes.
\v 12 But \f r \fr 62.12 \ft David was presently after Saul's death exalted to the Kingdom, in figure of Christ, whose name and glory was exalted, after the destruction of the Jews by Pagan Emperors.\f* the king shall rejoice in God, all shall be praised that swear by him: because the mouth is stopped of those that speak wicked things.
\c 63
\cd God is rightly praised in Sion and Jerusalem (in his Church only) for his benefits bestowed and promised, 8. unto which also in the time of grace all nations shall be called. \f - \ft Conversion of Gentiles. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, a Psalm of David.
\v 2 Hear o God my prayer when I make petition: from the fear of my enemy deliver my soul.
\v 3 Thou \f a \fr 63.3 \ft By example of thy former protection,\f* hast protected me from the \f b \fr 63.3 \ft from the conspiracy of wicked men, I trust most assuredly in thy help.\f* assembly of the malignant: from the multitude of them that work iniquity.
\v 4 Because they have sharpened their tongues as a sword: they have bent the bow a bitter thing,
\v 5 that they may shoot in secrets at the immaculate.
\v 6 Suddenly they will shoot at him, and will not fear: they \f c \fr 63.6 \ft They are resolved to entrap me.\f* have confirmed to themselves a wicked word. They have talked to hide snares: they have said, Who shall see them?
\v 7 They have searched iniquities: they \f d \fr 63.7 \ft But as they have failed, so they shall still fail, and be overreached in their bad counsels, as Achitophel. \+it 2. Reg. 17.\+it*\f* have failed searching with scrutiny.
\v 8 Man shall come to a deep heart: and God shall be exalted. \f e \fr 63.8 \ft God hath chosen the weak of this world to confound the strong.\f* Children's arrows are made their wounds:
\v 9 and their tongues are weakened against them. All that saw them \f f \fr 63.9 \ft Much marveled seeing the wicked so punished.\f* were troubled:
\v 10 and every man feared. And they shewed forth the works of God: and they understood his doings.
\v 11 The just shall rejoice in our Lord, and shall hope in him, & all the right of heart \f g \fr 63.11 \ft The just shall be praised, for rightly serving God.\f* shall be praised.
\c 64
\p
\v 1 To the end, a Psalm of David, \f a \fr 64.1 \ft The seventy Interpreters seeing David here prophesy of the people's return from Babylon, added the names of Jeremy and Ezechiel, who being in that captivity prophesied the same more largely. As likewise these and other Prophets, foresaw in spirit, and more especially prophesied the going forth of all nations from Babylon, that is, forsaking Idolatry, and embracing true Religion in the Church of Christ, so S. Augustin, Eutymius, and others.\f* the Canticle of Jeremy, and Ezechiel, to the people of the transmigration, when they began to go forth.
\v 2 An hymn, o God, becometh thee \f b \fr 64.2 \ft Not in Babylon, nor elsewhere, but only in the Church, praises and vows are grateful to God.\f* in Sion: & a vow shall be rendered to thee in Jerusalem.
\v 3 Hear my prayer: \f c \fr 64.3 \ft All nations shall know thee.\f* all flesh shall come to thee.
\v 4 The words of the wicked \f d \fr 64.4 \ft The wicked are insolent in threatening;\f* have prevailed upon us: and thou wilt be \f e \fr 64.4 \ft but thou mercifully pardoning our sins, they shall not hurt us.\f* propitious to our impieties.
\v 5 \f f \fr 64.5 \ft They are happy to whom thou hast prepared grace and glory.\f* Blessed is he whom thou hast chosen and taken: he shall dwell in thy courts.
\v 6 \f g \fr 64.6 \ft The voices of the faithful, rejoicing in the hope of\f* We shall be replenished in the goods of thy house: holy is thy temple, \f h \fr 64.6 \ft Nothing polluted shall enter into heaven. Apo. 21.\f* marvelous in equity. Hear us, o God our saviour, the hope of all the ends of the earth, and in the sea far.
\v 7 Preparing \f i \fr 64.7 \ft Thou which art all powerful, as appeareth by the huge mountains\f* mountains in thy strength, girded with might:
\v 8 which troublest \f k \fr 64.8 \ft seas, and other thy works.\f* the depths of the sea, the sound of the waves thereof.
\v 9 The Gentiles shall \f l \fr 64.9 \ft Thou wilt by thy omnipotent power move the hearts of obdurate men, and so convert innumerable of all nations to thee.\f* be troubled, and they that inhabit the borders shall be afraid of thy signs: \f m \fr 64.9 \ft Thou wilt draw many to thee, with joy and gladness, from the uttermost coasts of the east and west.\f* the out-goings of the morning and evening thou shalt delight.
\v 10 Thou hast visited the earth, and hast inebriated it: thou hast multiplied to enrich it. The \f n \fr 64.10 \ft God wrought diverse miracles in waters ( \+it Gen. 7. Exo. 7. 14. 15. Iosue. 3. 4. Reg. 5. 6\+it* &c.)\f* river of God is replenished with waters, thou hast prepared their \f o \fr 64.10 \ft likewise in providing meat for his people ( \+it Exo. 16, 3. Reg. 17, 4. Reg. 4, 7.\+it* &c.) which were figures of Baptism, Eucharist, and other Sacraments of Christ, washing from sins, and augmenting grace,\f* meat: because \f p \fr 64.10 \ft so replenishing the Church with most sacred Mysteries.\f* so is the preparation thereof.
\v 11 Inebriate \f q \fr 64.11 \ft Endowing the Apostles and other preachers with spiritual grace and learning,\f* her rivers, \f r \fr 64.11 \ft continuing the succession of pastors to water and seed the faithful people.\f* multiply her fruits: in her drops she shall rejoice springing.
\v 12 Thou wilt \f s \fr 64.12 \ft God blesseth the whole course, or circle of time, of the Church militant in this world,\f* bless the crown of the year of thy goodness: \f t \fr 64.12 \ft and the crown, or happy end of every just person's life.\f* and thy fields shall be replenished with plenty. \ef - \fr 64.12 \fq God will bless the crown. \ft Under the Allegory of the land of Jury ¹, whereunto the people of God were to be restored after their captivity in Babylon, the Psalmist here prophesieth greater things, than can be verified of the temporal state of the Jews ², that the militant Church, shall still be blessed from the beginning to the end, yielding expected fruit; and all the just, that persevere to the end of their lives, shall receive most happy and glorious rewards of their labours ³. As Saint Paul after his meritorious travails confidently expected his glorious reward, when he said (2. Tim. 4.) I have fought a good fight, I have consummate my course, I have kept my faith. Concerning the rest, there is laid up for me, a Crown of justice ⁴, which our Lord will render to me in that day, a just Judge. And not only to me, but to them also that love his coming. And this is called the crown of the year of God's benignity, because God of his own benignity, without man's former desert giveth grace, and in the end for merit following, giveth a crown of glory. So our Blessed Saviour according to his fullness of grace, which was in his soul, and infinite merit, received a crown of glory, in the consummation of his temporal life, after the space of thirty three years. And our Blessed Lady the mother of God, received an answerable crown to her excellent grace and merits, in the consummation of her life, at the end of sixty three years. In memory of which numbers of years, devout men have piously instituted certain forms of prayers, called the Crowns, or Corones of our Saviour ⁵, and of our Lady. \fq ¹ \ft Perpetuity of the Church. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Aug. Eutym.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Reward of the just. \fq ⁴ \ft Saints' crowns are of God's benignity. \fq ⁵ \ft The Corones of our Lord, and our Lady.\ef*
\v 13 The \f v \fr 64.13 \ft Even those which before had only a shew of beauty, but indeed were barren, shall yield aboundant fruit.\f* beautiful places of the desert shall be fat: and the \f w \fr 64.13 \ft Those which are more eminent shall particularly rejoice in their own and other's spiritual progress in virtue.\f* little hills shall be girded about with exultation.
\v 14 The \f x \fr 64.14 \ft The principal pastors shall in proportion rejoice above the rest for the grace and glory of all their flock.\f* rams of the sheep are clothed, and \f y \fr 64.14 \ft The subjects also and inferior people shall be satiate with their happy lot.\f* the valleys shall abound with corn: \f z \fr 64.14 \ft All together, prelates and people, higher and lower shall with uniform voice sing praises to God, and perpetual hymns.\f* they will cry, yea they will say an hymn.
\c 65
\cd The prophet inviteth all men to praise God for his marvelous works, and benefits done to the Jews. 7. Who being ungrateful, 8. Gentiles are called, 16. and bring forth better fruit. \f - \ft Gentiles succeed the Jews. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, a Canticle \f a \fr 65.1 \ft Mystical resurrection, Gentiles succeeding in place of the Jews.\f* of resurrection. Make ye \f b \fr 65.1 \ft Shew your internal joy by external words and deeds.\f* jubilation to God all the earth,
\v 2 say a Psalm to his name: give glory to his praise.
\v 3 Say ye to God: \f c \fr 65.3 \ft In drowning the world, in confounding the tongues in Babel, in burning Sodom and Gomorrha with brimstone, in plaguing the Ægyptians, in drowning Pharao and his whole army in the red sea; in destroying the Chananites and other infidels, in punishing the ten tribes, and afterwards the other two by captivity, & innumerable other punishments, all for sins,\f* How terrible are thy works, o Lord! In the multitude of thy strength thine enemies \f d \fr 65.3 \ft for which even the wicked, though not sincerely converted, yet of servile fear, feigned and falsely promised to amend, but performed it not: as Pharao afflicted with plagues, was forced to promise liberty to the children of Israel, which he afterwards denied.\f* shall lie to thee.
\v 4 Let all the earth adore thee, and sing to thee: let it sing a Psalm to thy name.
\v 5 Come ye, and see the works of God: terrible in counsels over the children of men.
\v 6 Who turneth \f e \fr 65.6 \ft The red sea,\f* the sea into dry land, in \f f \fr 65.6 \ft when Iosue brought the people over Iordan,\f* the river they shall pass on foot, \f g \fr 65.6 \ft in remembering and reciting these singular benefits.\f* there we shall rejoice in him.
\v 7 Who ruleth in his strength for ever his eyes look upon the gentiles: \f h \fr 65.7 \ft God's chosen people the Jews did often exasperate God by their ingratitude, murmuring, and other sins: whom the Prophet therefore admonished,\f* they that exasperate him let them \f i \fr 65.7 \ft not to be proud, lest they be subdued and brought low.\f* not be exalted in themselves.
\v 8 Ye \f k \fr 65.8 \ft By way of invitation the Psalmist prophesieth the conversion of Gentiles.\f* Gentiles bless our God: and make the voice of his praise heard.
\v 9 Who hath put \f l \fr 65.9 \ft The voice of the whole Church: confessing God's providence and protection, that she never faileth; for the Jews falling from Christ the Gentiles believed in him; and some nations or countries falling from Religion, others are converted.\f* my soul in life: and hath not given my feet to be moved.
\v 10 Because thou \f m \fr 65.10 \ft God suffereth his Church to be persecuted with all kinds of tribulation, as some are here recited.\f* hast proved us, o God: by fire thou hast tried us, as silver is tried.
\v 11 Thou hast brought us into a snare, thou hast laid tribulations on our back: thou hast set men upon our heads.
\v 12 \f n \fr 65.12 \ft But through God's assistance his servants pass through, and overcome all tentations.\f* We have passed through fire and water: and thou hast brought us out into refreshing.
\v 13 I will go into thy house with \f o \fr 65.13 \ft Sacrifice of thanks,\f* holocausts: I will render thee \f p \fr 65.13 \ft and voluntary vows,\f* my vows:
\v 14 which my lips have distinguished. And \f q \fr 65.14 \ft that which any promiseth to God in tribulation, they must perform accordingly.\f* my mouth hath spoken in my tribulation.
\v 15 \f r \fr 65.15 \ft These were the best external sacrifices of the old law:\f* Holocausts with marrow will I offer to thee, with incense of rams: I will offer to thee oxen with buck goats.
\v 16 \f s \fr 65.16 \ft But both then and now the internal sacrifices of contrite heart, of justice, and of divine praise best please God.\f* Come ye, hear, and I will tell all ye that fear God, what great things he hath done for my soul.
\v 17 To him have I cried with my mouth, and have exulted \f t \fr 65.17 \ft From the heart, which is under the tongue, and directeth the tongue what to speak.\f* under my tongue.
\v 18 \f v \fr 65.18 \ft Whosoever will be heard in prayer, must repent of his sins.\f* If I have beheld iniquity in my heart, our Lord will not hear.
\v 19 Therefore hath God heard, and hath attended to the voice of my petition.
\v 20 Blessed be God who hath not removed my prayer, and his mercy from me.
\c 66
\cd Notwithstanding great persecutions the Church prospereth. 10. Especially in the new testament, by Apostolical function, 18. ministry of Angels, Christ's Ascension, coming of the Holy Ghost, 31. confirming the faithful, repressing the insolent, and converting many. 35. For all which the prophet inviteth all men to praise God. \f - \ft The Church still conserved. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, in hymns, a Psalm \f a \fr 66.1 \ft This Psalm beginning to be sung by voices, instruments were adjoined.\f* of Canticle to David.
\v 2 God \f b \fr 66.2 \ft God first remit our sins:\f* have mercy upon us, and \f c \fr 66.2 \ft then give us thy manifold graces,\f* bless us: \f d \fr 66.2 \ft grant faith and repentance,\f* illuminate his countenance upon us, and \f e \fr 66.2 \ft and so forgiveness of sins.\f* have mercy on us.
\v 3 That we may know thy way upon earth: in all nations thy salvation.
\v 4 Let peoples, o God, confess to thee: \f f \fr 66.4 \ft All nations shall be converted.\f* let all peoples confess to thee.
\v 5 Let nations be glad & rejoice: because thou judgest peoples in equity, and the nations in earth thou doest direct.
\v 6 Let peoples, o God, confess to thee, let all peoples confess to thee:
\v 7 the earth hath yielded her fruit. \f g \fr 66.7 \ft God the Father,\f* God, \f h \fr 66.7 \ft God the Son,\f* our God bless us,
\v 8 \f i \fr 66.8 \ft God the Holy Ghost, save the people of all nations, by Evangelical preaching of thee, the most Blessed Trinity.\f* God bless us: and let all the ends of the earth fear him.
\c 67
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, a Psalm of Canticle, to David himself.
\v 2 Let \f a \fr 67.2 \ft In manner of praying that God will vouchsafe to defend the Church, the Psalmist prophesieth that God will arise,\f* God arise, and let his enemies be dispersed, and let them that hate him \f b \fr 67.2 \ft and the enemies shall fly away, not daring to abide the combat.\f* fly from his face.
\v 3 As smoke vanisheth, let them vanish away: as wax melteth at the presence of fire, so let sinners perish at the presence of God.
\v 4 And \f c \fr 67.4 \ft As God is terrible to the wicked: so he is comfortable to the just.\f* let the just make merry, and rejoice in the sight of God: and let them be delighted in mirth.
\v 5 Sing to God, say a Psalm to his name: \f d \fr 67.5 \ft Resist not God's inspiration, but receive it with joy and thanks.\f* make way to him, who \f e \fr 67.5 \ft Who triumpheth over death\f* mounteth upon the west, \f f \fr 67.5 \ft God is Lord, not only of these or those nations, countries or other creatures, but absolutely and universally of all.\f* Lord is his name. Rejoice ye in his sight, they shall be troubled at the presence of him:
\v 6 the father of orphans, and judge of widows. God in his \f g \fr 67.6 \ft That is the true holy Church, which hath\f* holy place:
\v 7 God that maketh men to inhabit \f h \fr 67.7 \ft unity in doctrine, touching faith and manners. S. Cypr. \+it ep. 76.\+it*\f* of one manner in a house. That bringeth forth them \f i \fr 67.7 \ft That be bound in sin,\f* that be bound, in strength, likewise them that \f k \fr 67.7 \ft even rebellious wills are altered by God's mercy, & freely embrace his law,\f* exasperate, that dwell \f l \fr 67.7 \ft also the dead and dry hearts that cared not for spiritual things are softened, and quickened with new grace.\f* in sepulchres.
\v 8 O God \f m \fr 67.8 \ft The benefits bestowed on the Israelites, are written in the books of Moyses, Iosue, and Judges.\f* when thou wentest forth in the sight of thy people, when thou didst pass through the desert.
\v 9 The earth was moved, and the heavens also distilled, at the presence of the God of Sina, at the face of the God of Israel.
\v 10 \f n \fr 67.10 \ft Not man's deserving, but God's mere good will, and free grace caused Christ to come, and by himself and his Apostles to preach the Evangelical doctrine, which watereth the whole world.\f* Voluntary rain shalt thou separate, o God, to thine inheritance: and it was \f o \fr 67.10 \ft God chose the weak, but made them strong.\f* weakened, but thou hath perfiteth it.
\v 11 Thy \f p \fr 67.11 \ft Those whom thou has chosen, and so made thine own peculiar people, shall enjoy this grace.\f* living creatures shall dwell in it: thou hast prepared \f q \fr 67.11 \ft Thou gavest Manna in the desert, the B. Sacrament in the new testament.\f* in thy sweetness for the poor, o God.
\v 12 Our Lord shall give \f r \fr 67.12 \ft God giveth to the Preacher what to speak,\f* the word to them that evangelize, \f s \fr 67.12 \ft and to some he giveth also power to work miracles, in confirmation of their doctrine. \+it Mar. 16.\+it*\f* with great power.
\v 13 The \f t \fr 67.13 \ft Some potent King, or (as it is in Hebrew) Kings being beloved, of the beloved of God, the only Son of God, shall yield themselves to the same beloved Son of God:\f* King of hosts the beloved of the beloved: and \f v \fr 67.13 \ft which shall redound to the glory and beauty of his Church, gaining such spiritual preys from the devil.\f* to the beauty of the house, to divide the spoils.
\v 14 If ye \f w \fr 67.14 \ft If you be in such danger, that the adversaries cast dice, or lots for your persons, and goods, yet you shall be delivered, as if a dove, with her glistering feathers, like silver and gold, fly away into a secure place, without loss or diminution, but rather with increase of virtues.\f* sleep among the midst of the lots, the wings of a dove laid over with silver, and the hinder parts of her back in the paleness of gold.
\v 15 Whilst \f x \fr 67.15 \ft When the heavenly King determineth thus of earthly Kings,\f* the heavenly discerneth kings over her, \f y \fr 67.15 \ft they shall be purged from their sins, and made white like snow, that falleth in mount Selmon. which is a shadowed hill, thick with trees, in mount Ephraim near to Jordan.\f* with snow they shall be made white in Selmon.
\v 16 The \f z \fr 67.16 \ft The Church of God is visible, and durable like to a mountain.\f* mountain of God a fat mountain. A mountain \f a \fr 67.16 \ft Combined, or joined together, as when milk is turned into curd, and so into cheese.\f* curded as cheese, a \f b \fr 67.16 \ft Fruitful, enriched by spiritual gifts of the Holy Ghost.\f* fat mountain: \ef - \fr 67.16 \fq The mountain of God. \ft For better discerning the true Church from other congregations, the Prophet here describeth certain properties thereof ¹, for he calleth it a mountain, because it is most visible to all men. secondly, a fat mountain, that is, replenished with all virtues, and gifts of the Holy Ghost; whereof it is called Holy ². Thirdly, it is curded, or consolidated in unity of faith and Religion ³, which conjoin the whole body, making it solid and firm, as the oven turneth liquid milk into curd, and so into cheese. Fourthly, it is the Congregation, wherein God always remaineth, even to the end, for ever ⁴; which sheweth two other properties; that the Church never faileth, nor erreth in doctrine ⁵: God still dwelling therein, and consequently conserveth it from error in doctrine. \fq ¹ \ft Marks of the Church: Visibility. \fq ² \ft Sanctity. \fq ³ \ft Unity. \fq ⁴ \ft Perpetuity. \fq ⁵ \ft Assured verity.\ef*
\v 17 why \f c \fr 67.17 \ft Ye that are not of this Church, do in vain and erroneously imagine, that any other mountains are united.\f* suppose you curded mountains? A mountain, in which it hath well pleased God to dwell therein: for indeed our Lord will dwell even to the end. \ef - \fr 67.17 \fq Why suppose you curded mountains. \ft As for other congregations ¹, it is certain and evident, that they are not the Church of God, because they are not curded, that is not united, in the same points of faith, but only in negative points, and in general opposition against the Catholic Church, and among themselves notoriously disagreeing and divided. As they also want the other marks of the true Church. \fq ¹ \ft No other pretended Church hath the mark of unity or the rest.\ef*
\v 18 The \f d \fr 67.18 \ft Innumerable Angels, ministers of God's will, do continually attend upon his Divine Majesty, as if he (who otherwise needeth no service) were carried by them, as in a\f* chariot of God is ten thousand fold, thousands of them that rejoice, our Lord in them, \f e \fr 67.18 \ft So God appeared in Majesty, when he gave his law in mount Sinai.\f* in Sina in the holy place.
\v 19 Thou art \f f \fr 67.19 \ft Christ ascended with innumerable Angels attending upon him,\f* ascended on high, thou \f g \fr 67.19 \ft carried with him the fathers of the old testament, that had been captive,\f* hast taken captivity: thou \f h \fr 67.19 \ft as man he received gifts of God, in and for men, his faithful servants,\f* hast received gifts in men: for even those \f i \fr 67.19 \ft yea also he received for his merit, that innumerable, which before were incredulous were converted, and God dwelt in their souls.\f* that do not believe, our Lord God to inhabit. \x - \xt Ephe. 4.\x*
\v 20 Blessed be our Lord day by day: the God of our salvations will make us a prosperous journey.
\v 21 Our God is the God of saving: and \f k \fr 67.21 \ft Our Lord, I say, our Lord, and none but he could overcome death by dying.\f* the issues of death are of our Lord, our Lord.
\v 22 But yet God \f l \fr 67.22 \ft Though Christ died to deliver all men from death, yet he will give capital sentence of eternal death to all that obstinately remain his enemies, and multiply sins upon sins, to the end of their temporal life.\f* still break the heads of his enemies: the hairy crown of them, that walk in their sins.
\v 23 Our Lord said: Out \f m \fr 67.23 \ft Even of the rudest barbarous nations, many shall be converted to Christianity\f* of Basan I will convert, I will convert \f n \fr 67.23 \ft namely God's grace is extended into the Islands of the Ocean, and other seas.\f* into the depth of the sea.
\v 24 That thy foot \f o \fr 67.24 \ft But such severe slaughter shall fall upon the obstinate contemners of this grace, that men's feet shall be defiled in their blood, and dogs shall lap it.\f* may be dipped in blood: the tongue of thy dogs \it (made red)\it* with the same \it (blood)\it* of the enemies. \f 1 \fr 67.24 \ft \+it Ex inimicis ab ipso.\+it*\f*
\v 25 They \f p \fr 67.25 \ft Many have seen, or known in general, but the faithful more exactly know how Christ came into this world, his conversation therein, and his going forth;\f* have seen thy enterings in, o God, the enterings of my God: of my King who is \f q \fr 67.25 \ft his reigning now in heaven our Mediator, by whom all other intercessors have access to God.\f* in the holy place.
\v 26 \f r \fr 67.26 \ft The Apostles sowing the first seed of Evangelical doctrine,\f* Princes came before \f s \fr 67.26 \ft with whom other Apostolical men,\f* joined with them that sang, in the midst \f t \fr 67.26 \ft and other souls of all nations converted by their preaching, most joyfully sing together in heart, voice, & instruments: especially in good works, shew their grateful affections to our Redeemer.\f* of young women playing on timbrels.
\v 27 In \f v \fr 67.27 \ft And all this in the particular Churches of diverse Kingdoms, and parts of the world:\f* Churches bless ye God our Lord, of \f w \fr 67.27 \ft beginning with the Israelites in Jerusalem, and so proceeding into all Jury, and Samaria, and to the utmost of the earth. Act. 1.\f* the fountains of Israel.
\v 28 There \f x \fr 67.28 \ft S. Paul of Jacob's youngest son Benjamin, last called to Apostleship, was chiefly sent to the Gentiles.\f* Benjamin a young man, in excess of mind. \f y \fr 67.28 \ft Other Apostles of diverse tribes sent first to the Jews, secondarily to Gentiles.\f* The Princes of Juda their leaders: the Princes of Zabulon, the Princes of Nephthali.
\v 29 Command thy strength, o God: \f z \fr 67.29 \ft As the Church began by the omnipotent power of God; so by the same only power it is conserved.\f* confirm this, o God, which thou hast wrought in us.
\v 30 From thy temple in Jerusalem, kings shall offer gifts to thee.
\v 31 Rebuke \f a \fr 67.31 \ft Chastise therefore, o God, all persecuters of thy Church, who are but as weak wavering reeds, in comparison of thy power,\f* the wild beasts of the reed, the congregation \f b \fr 67.31 \ft no better than bulls, with kine, that is, captains and popular people,\f* of bulls in the kine of thy peoples: \f c \fr 67.31 \ft endeavouring to alienate the constant proved Confessors from their faith.\f* that they may exclude them, which are tried with silver. Dissipate the nations that will wars:
\v 32 Legates shall come \f d \fr 67.32 \ft A prophecy that many should be converted to Christ in Ægypt, & Æthiopia: as appeareth by the innumerable multitude of religious Monks, & Nuns in those countries, shortly after the Apostles' days.\f* out of Ægypt: Æthiopia shall prevent his hands to God.
\v 33 Ye Kingdoms \f e \fr 67.33 \ft The like afterwards in all other nations, whom therefore the Prophet inviteth to praise God, for so inestimable benefits in the whole world.\f* of the earth sing to God: sing to our Lord:
\v 34 Sing ye to God, that mounteth upon the heaven of heaven, to the East. Behold he will give to his voice \f f \fr 67.34 \ft Christ will come to judge, in terror of voice, and with magnificence, accompanied with holy Angels and other Saints.\f* the voice of strength:
\v 35 give you glory to God upon Israel, his magnificence, and his power in the clouds.
\v 36 God is marvelous in his Saints, the God of Israel he will give power and strength to his people, God be blessed.
\c 68
\cd Christ in midst of afflictions (as one in dangerous waters) 5. describing the malice of persecutors, and his own true zeal, 14. prayeth his heavenly Father for help. 23. By way of just imprecation, foresheweth the severe punishment of his adversaries, 30. his own glorious Resurrection, and prosperous building of his Church. For which he inviteth all creatures to praise God. \f - \ft Christ's afflictions and victory. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 68.1 \ft Pertaining to the new Testament,\f* the end, \f b \fr 68.1 \ft for Gentiles converted to Christianity, and from vice to virtue,\f* for them that shall be changed, \f c \fr 68.1 \ft prefigured in David.\f* to David.
\v 2 Save me, o God: because \f d \fr 68.2 \ft Vehement afflictions environ my heart.\f* waters are entered into my soul.
\v 3 I \f e \fr 68.3 \ft Iam as one entangled with quicksand or quagmire in the bottom of a great water.\f* stick fast in the mire of the depth: and there is no sure standing. I am come into the depth of the sea: and a tempest hath overwhelmed me.
\v 4 I have \f f \fr 68.4 \ft Our Lord sweat blood for anguish in his prayer, and was not delivered from his passion, neither are his servants presently delivered from tribulations, but as is most to God's honour and their own good.\f* laboured crying, my jaws are made hoarse: my eyes have failed, whilst I hope in my God.
\v 5 They are multiplied above the hairs of my head, that hate me without cause. \x - \xt Ioan. 15.\x* Mine enemies are made strong, that have persecuted me unjustly: \f g \fr 68.5 \ft Our Saviour who had no sin, payed the ransom for all sins.\f* then did I pay the things that I took not.
\v 6 O God thou knowest my \f h \fr 68.6 \ft O God thou knowest, that this which seemeth folly to worldly men, is true wisdom\f* foolishness: and \f i \fr 68.6 \ft and though men charge men with offences, thou knowest that I am innocent.\f* mine offences are not hid from thee.
\v 7 \f k \fr 68.7 \ft Suffer not the weak to be scandalized in my passions.\f* Let them not be ashamed upon me, which expected thee, o Lord, Lord of hosts. Let them not be confounded upon me that seek thee, o God of Israel.
\v 8 Because for thee have I sustained reproach, confusion hath covered my face.
\v 9 I am become a foreigner to my brethren, and a stranger to the sons of my mother.
\v 10 Because \f l \fr 68.10 \ft The zeal of seeking God's honour, in propagating and advancing his Church, is the cause of persecution. As we see those are less persecuted, which have less godly zeal.\f* the zeal of thy house hath eaten me: and the reproaches of them that reproached thee, fell upon me. \x - \xt Ioan. 2. Rom. 15.\x*
\v 11 And I covered my soul in fasting: and it \f m \fr 68.11 \ft The wicked do reproach those that mortify themselves.\f* was made a reproach to me.
\v 12 And I put hair cloth my garment, & I became a parable to them.
\v 13 They spake against me \f n \fr 68.13 \ft The great men and judges, also the drunkards and rascality of the people.\f* that sat in the gate; and they sung against me that drank wine.
\v 14 But I, \f o \fr 68.14 \ft But I direct my prayer to thee.\f* my prayer to thee, o Lord: a time of thy good pleasure, o God. \f p \fr 68.14 \ft Expecting the time of thy good pleasure.\f* In the multitude of thy mercy hear me, in the truth of thy salvation:
\v 15 Deliver me out of the mire, that I stick not fast: deliver me from them that hate me, and from the depths of \f q \fr 68.15 \ft Tribulations.\f* waters.
\v 16 Let not the tempest of water drown me, nor the depth swallow me: \f r \fr 68.16 \ft Though Christ died and was buried, and in soul descended into hell, yet he could not be holden in his sepulchre, nor in limbo, but rose again.\f* neither let the pit shut his mouth upon me.
\v 17 Hear me, o Lord, because thy mercy is benign: according to the multitude of thy commiserations have respect to me.
\v 18 And turn not away thy face from thy servant: because I am in tribulation, hear me speedily.
\v 19 Attend to my soul, and deliver it: \f s \fr 68.19 \ft That they may either be converted, or confounded, and so do no more hurt.\f* because of mine enemies deliver me.
\v 20 Thou knowest my reproach & my confusion, & my shame.
\v 21 In thy sight are all they that afflict me, my heart hath looked for reproach and misery. And I expected somebody that would be sorry together with me, and there \f t \fr 68.21 \ft Not any that could mitigate our Saviour's affliction, would shew compassion towards him.\f* was none: and that would comfort me, and I found not.
\v 22 And they gave \f v \fr 68.22 \ft But contrarywise when he complained of thirst, they gave him gall and vinegar to drink.\f* gall for my meat: & in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink.
\v 23 \f w \fr 68.23 \ft A Prophecy of the destruction of the Jews, at the time of Pasch, when they should think to eat their paschal lamb with joy.\f* Let their table be made a snare before them, & for retributions, and for a scandal. \x - \xt Rom. 11.\x*
\v 24 Let \f x \fr 68.24 \ft They are also blind in heart, that they will not see the truth of Christ's doctrine:\f* their eyes be darkened that they see not: and make \f y \fr 68.24 \ft but bow themselves to earthly things, and worldly gains even to this day.\f* their back crooked always.
\v 25 Pour out thy wrath upon them: and let the fury of thy wrath overtake them.
\v 26 Let their habitation be made desert: and in their tabernacles let there be none to dwell. \x - \xt Act. 1.\x*
\v 27 Because \f z \fr 68.27 \ft Christ, whom God of his mercy designed to suffer death, for redemption of mankind, the Jews of mere envy and malice persecuted to death.\f* whom thou hast strucken, they have persecuted: and upon the sorrow of my wounds they have added.
\v 28 Add thou \f a \fr 68.28 \ft A prophecy that God would suffer them, to fall from one iniquity to another.\f* iniquity upon their iniquity: and let them not enter into thy justice.
\v 29 \f b \fr 68.29 \ft In the end of their lives, they shall not be found in the book of life, where they suppose themselves to be written.\f* Let them be put out of the book of the living: and with the just let them not be written.
\v 30 \f c \fr 68.30 \ft The voice of Christ, humbling himself to death, even to death of the Cross:\f* I am poor and sorrowful: \f d \fr 68.30 \ft from which he rose again.\f* thy salvation, o God, hath received me.
\v 31 I will praise the name of God with canticle: and will magnify him in praise.
\v 32 And \f e \fr 68.32 \ft Devout praise and thanks-giving please God more than sacrifices of the most tender calves, which were otherwise also grateful sacrifices.\f* it shall please God more than a young calf: that bringeth forth horns and hoofs.
\v 33 Let the poor see and rejoice: seek ye God, and your soul shall live.
\v 34 Because our Lord hath heard the poor: and he hath not despised \f f \fr 68.34 \ft God doth assuredly comfort all such, as are imprisoned for professing the truth.\f* his prisoners.
\v 35 Let \f g \fr 68.35 \ft All the creatures of God.\f* the heavens and earth praise him, the sea, and all the creeping beasts in them.
\v 36 \f h \fr 68.36 \ft God will always establish and protect the Catholic Church:\f* Because God will save Sion: \f i \fr 68.36 \ft and particular Churches, members of the universal, shall also prosper.\f* and the cities of Juda shall be built up. And they shall inhabit there, & by inheritance, they shall get it.
\v 37 And \f k \fr 68.37 \ft Perpetual succession of the Catholic Church.\f* the seed of his servants shall possess it, and they that love his name shall dwell in it.
\c 69
\cd Another prayer of David, when he was persecuted by Absalom: made in a Psalm after his delivery. \f - \ft David's prayer in persecution. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 69.1 \ft An apt prayer also for the afflicted in the new Testament,\f* the end, a Psalm of David, in remembrance, that our \f b \fr 69.1 \ft from the danger of Absalom \+it (2. Reg. 18.)\+it* or from any persecutor.\f* Lord saved him.
\v 2 O God \f c \fr 69.2 \ft All men at all times need God's help:\f* intend unto my help: Lord \f d \fr 69.2 \ft but most present need, in present dangers. The rest of this Psalm is contained in the \+it 39.\+it* Psalm from the \+it 15.\+it* verse. But there the whole Church prayeth for help, the world being almost drowned in sins; here David, or other particular persons, or peoples pray in their several distresses.\f* make haste to help me.
\v 3 Let them be confounded and be ashamed, that seek my soul. \x - \xt Psa. 39.\x*
\v 4 Let them be turned away backward, and be ashamed that will me evils. Let them be turned away forthwith ashamed, that say to me: Well, well.
\v 5 Let all that seek thee rejoice, and be glad in thee, and let them say always: Our Lord be magnified: which love thy salvation.
\v 6 But I am needy and poor: O God help me, thou art my helper, and deliverer: o Lord be not slack.
\c 70
\cd King David, or any other just person prayeth God to continue his grace and protection against the malice of the enemy. 5. Recounteth God's mercy in delivering him from falling into tentations: 6. prayeth for the same in old age, or weakness of body and spirit, 20. and promiseth perpetual gratitude and praises. \f - \ft A prayer for perseverance in virtue. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 70.1 \ft Though this Psalm (as also diverse others) is entitled to, or for David, it proveth not that some other was the author thereof, but the Seventy Interpreters insinuate hereby, that it pertaineth in more particular sort to David, growing old;\f* for David, \f b \fr 70.1 \ft they add also the sons of Jonadab a most holy family;\f* of the sons of Jonadab, and \f c \fr 70.1 \ft who for their singular piety, were suffered to remain in Jerusalem in the first captivity. \+it Jer. 35.\+it*\f* the former captives. In thee o Lord \f d \fr 70.1 \ft The words of David, or any faithful just person.\f* I have hoped, let me not be confounded for ever:
\v 2 in \f e \fr 70.2 \ft God of his justice revengeth the injuries done to his servants.\f* thy justice deliver me, and receive me. Incline thine ear to me and save me.
\v 3 Be unto me for a God protector, and for a fenced place: that thou mayst save me: Because thou art my firmament, and my refuge.
\v 4 My God deliver me out of the hand of the sinner, and out of the hand of him that doth against the law, and of the unjust.
\v 5 Because thou art \f f \fr 70.5 \ft Through thy grace I have patience to expect thy pleasure, when I shall be relieved.\f* my patience, o Lord; o Lord my hope from my youth.
\v 6 Upon thee have I been confirmed from the womb: from my mother's belly thou art my protector. In thee is my singing always:
\v 7 I was made to many as \f g \fr 70.7 \ft It seemeth strange to most men, why the just have more troubles than the wicked.\f* a wonder: & thou art a strong helper.
\v 8 Let my mouth be filled with praise, that I may sing thy glory: \f h \fr 70.8 \ft A necessary prayer for perseverance, which none can have without special and continual help of grace.\f* all the day thy greatness.
\v 9 Reject me not in the time of old age: \f i \fr 70.9 \ft Against this special danger, and last assaults of the enemy, Christ hath instituted the Sacrament of Extreme Unction.\f* when my strength shall fail, forsake me not.
\v 10 Because mine enemies have said to me: and they that watched my soul consulted together.
\v 11 saying: God hath forsaken him, pursue, and take him: because there is none to deliver.
\v 12 O God be not far from me: my God have respect to mine aid.
\v 13 Let them be confounded & fail that detract from my soul: let them be covered with confusion and shame, that seek evils to me.
\v 14 But I will always hope: and \f k \fr 70.14 \ft The best means to conserve virtue, is to exercise the same, adding good works towards our neighbours, and praises to God.\f* will add upon all thy praise.
\v 15 My mouth shall shew forth thy justice: all the day thy salvation. Because \f l \fr 70.15 \ft I profess not exact knowledge of natural things;\f* I have not known learning, \f m \fr 70.15 \ft but I consider and contemplate the infinite power of God, which appeareth in his works,\f* I will enter into the \f n \fr 70.15 \ft and his just judgements, as partly have appeared in punishing, and rewarding some in this life, but especially will be manifest in the end of this world.\f* powers of our Lord:
\v 16 O Lord I will be mindful of thy justice only.
\v 17 O God thou hast taught me from my youth: and until now I will pronounce thy marvelous works.
\v 18 And unto ancient age, and old age: o God, forsake me not? Until I shew forth thy arm to all the generation, that is to come. Thy might,
\v 19 and thy justice, o God, even to the highest, great marvels, which thou hast done: O God who may be like to thee?
\v 20 How great tribulations hast thou shewed me, many and evil: and turning thou \f o \fr 70.20 \ft After tribulations recreated me.\f* hast quickened me: and from the depths of the earth thou hast brought me back again.
\v 21 Thou hast multiplied thy magnificence: and being turned thou hast comforted me.
\v 22 For I also will confess to thee in the instruments of Psalm thy truth: O God, I will sing to thee on the harp, holy one of Israel.
\v 23 My \f p \fr 70.23 \ft Iwill praise thee with mouth and heart.\f* lips shall rejoice when I shall sing to thee; and my soul which thou hast redeemed.
\v 24 Yea and my tongue all the day shall meditate thy justice: when they shall be confounded and ashamed that seek evils to me.
\c 71
\cd By way of prayer the Psalmist prophesieth Christ's coming, our King and Judge: 4. the deliverer of mankind from the thraldom of the devil. 8. The greatness of his spiritual kingdom in the Gentiles, 16. and his continual glory and praise. \f - \ft Christ our King & Judge. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Concerning \f a \fr 71.1 \ft This Psalm is of Christ, prefigured by Salomon, whose Kingdom was most glorious of all the Kings of God's people; for of King Salomon himself many things in this Psalm cannot be truly understood. S. Aug.\f* Salomon.
\v 2 O God give thy judgment to \f b \fr 71.2 \ft O God most blessed Trinity, give power and authority, to the Son of Man, God incarnate, King of all kings,\f* the king: and thy justice to \f c \fr 71.2 \ft the Son of king David, to judge for mankind against the devil.\f* the son of the king. To judge thy people in \f d \fr 71.2 \ft Christ paying ransom for all mankind, and so man renouncing the devil and serving God, is justly, not injuriously delivered from captivity of sin, and of the devil.\f* justice, and thy poor in judgment.
\v 3 Let \f e \fr 71.3 \ft A prophecy of the Apostles receiving power to preach Christ's Gospel, of peace and reconciliation of men to God by penance,\f* the mountains receive peace for the people: and \f f \fr 71.3 \ft and of other Apostolical men that follow their steps.\f* the little hills justice.
\v 4 He \f g \fr 71.4 \ft Salomon in figure of Christ was for a time a just and good King.\f* shall judge the poor of the people, and shall save the children of the poor: and he shall humble the calumniator.
\v 5 And he shall \f h \fr 71.5 \ft But only Christ, not Salomon, nor any other King of that people, continueth or reigneth for ever.\f* continue with the sun, and before the moon in generation and generation.
\v 6 He \f i \fr 71.6 \ft The manner of Christ's Incarnation most silent, sweet, and grateful.\f* shall descend as rain upon a fleece: and as drops distilling upon the earth.
\v 7 There shall rise in his days justice, and aboundance of peace, \f k \fr 71.7 \ft Again the Prophet inculcateth, that Christ's kingdom, his Church, shall continue for ever.\f* until the moon be taken away.
\v 8 And he shall rule \f l \fr 71.8 \ft The Church is not only universal in all times, but also in places.\f* from sea unto sea: and from the river even to the ends of the round world.
\v 9 Before him shall the Æthiopians fall down: and his enemies shall lick the earth. \x - \xt Isa. 60.\x*
\v 10 The \f m \fr 71.10 \ft The three Sages or Kings, which adored our Saviour, and offered gold, frankincense, & myrrh, were the first that fulfilled this prophecy: & afterwards Constantin the Great, and other Emperors, Kings, and Princes.\f* kings of Tharsis, and \f n \fr 71.10 \ft Amongst other Islands Great Brittany (the greatest of Europe) was converted to Christ, according to this prophecy: first some few in the Apostles' time (Metaphrastes apud Surium, Theodoret, epist. ad Timoth. Sophronius Ser. de Nat. Apost, & alij.) More in the time of Eleutherius. Lastly, our English nation by S. Augustin and others sent by S. Gregory.\f* the Islands shall offer presents: the kings of the Arabians, and of Saba shall bring gifts. \x - \xt Mat. 2.\x*
\v 11 And all kings of the earth shall adore him: all nations shall serve him.
\v 12 Because he shall deliver the poor from the mighty: and the poor which had no helper.
\v 13 He shall spare the poor and needy: and he shall save the souls of the poor.
\v 14 From usuries and iniquity he shall redeem their souls: and their name shall be honourable before him.
\v 15 And \f 1 \fr 71.15 \ft How is this prophecy verified, except the Church be always visible?\f* he shall live, and there shall be given him of the gold of Arabia, and they shall adore it always: all the day they shall bless him.
\v 16 And there shall be a firmament in the earth in the tops of mountains, the fruit thereof shall be extolled far above Libanus: and they shall flourish of the city, as the grass of the earth.
\v 17 Be his name blessed for ever: before the sun his name is permanent. And all the tribes of the earth shall be blessed in him: all nations shall magnify him.
\v 18 Blessed be our Lord the God of Israel, \f o \fr 71.18 \ft No miracles can be done but by God's power.\f* who only doth marvelous things.
\v 19 And blessed be the name of his majesty for ever: and all the earth shall be filled with his majesty. \f p \fr 71.19 \ft Above all other desires, the holy Prophet wished God's glory, and praises in all the earth, as it is in heaven.\f* Be it, be it.
\v 20 \f q \fr 71.20 \ft It seemeth by this appendix added by Esdras, that this Psalm was last composed, though not put in the last place, but as it came to his hands.\f* The praises of David, \f r \fr 71.20 \ft David the natural son of Jesse, made no more Hymns, that is, no more musical praises of God after this Psalm, but: the prefigured David Christ our Saviour, the Son of God, in his body the Church, singeth still new praises. S. Aug. in enarrat. Psal. sequentis. 72.\f* the son of Jesse are ended.
\c 72
\cd The royal Prophet, first professing the unspeakable goodness of God, 2. in the person of the weak complaineth, that the wicked prosper and the just are afflicted: 15. reprehendeth the murmuration, for though we cannot comprehend the secret judgements of God, 18. yet they are most just. 25. So with desire to rest in God, he promiseth ever to praise him. \f - \ft Affliction in this world is recompensed in the end. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 72.1 \ft To be sung by Asaph the master of music, and his company.\f* to Asaph. How \f b \fr 72.1 \ft God is so excellent good, as no tongue can express, no heart can conceive.\f* good is God to Israel, to them that are of a right heart! \x - \xt Psa. 39.\x*
\v 2 But \f c \fr 72.2 \ft The peace and prosperity of the wicked tempted me to leave the right narrow way, and to go the broad easy way with them.\f* my feet were almost moved, my steps almost slipped.
\v 3 Because I have had zeal upon the wicked, seeing the peace of sinners.
\v 4 Because there is \f d \fr 72.4 \ft They never think of death;\f* no respect to their death: and \f e \fr 72.4 \ft and if they suffer some affliction, they quickly remove it from them.\f* stability in their plague.
\v 5 In \f f \fr 72.5 \ft They travail not so much as good men do, neither are they so often punished.\f* the labours of men they are not, and with men they shall not be scourged.
\v 6 Therefore \f g \fr 72.6 \ft Which maketh them proud, and so they run into all sorts of iniquity.\f* hath pride held them, they are covered with their iniquity and impiety.
\v 7 Their iniquity hath proceeded as it were of fat: they have passed into \f h \fr 72.7 \ft Whatsoever mischief came in their mind, they feared not to commit it in fact.\f* the affection of the heart.
\v 8 They have thought and have spoken wickedness: they have spoken iniquity \f i \fr 72.8 \ft Boldly extolling their own words and works, and despising others.\f* on high.
\v 9 They have set their mouth unto heaven: and their tongue hath passed in the earth.
\v 10 Therefore will my people \f k \fr 72.10 \ft For this prosperity of the wicked, some of God's people will fall from the right way,\f* return here: and \f l \fr 72.10 \ft and their days will abound with calamities.\f* full days shall be found in them.
\v 11 And they have said: \f m \fr 72.11 \ft The voice of the weak, inclining to diffidence, & doubt of God's knowledge and providence.\f* How doth God know, and is there knowledge in the highest?
\v 12 Lo the sinners themselves, and they that abound in the world, have obtained riches.
\v 13 And I said: Then have I justified my heart without cause, and have washed my hands amongst innocents:
\v 14 And have been scourged all the day, and my chastising in the mornings.
\v 15 If I said: I will speak this: behold \f n \fr 72.15 \ft The Prophet in his own person answereth, that if he should say such things, he should reprove God's children as faulty, unworthy of his protection, and to be neglected; whereas it is God's providence to chastise his children whom he loveth.\f* I reproved the nation of thy children.
\v 16 I \f o \fr 72.16 \ft In this life we cannot know the particular causes, why the just are afflicted, & many wicked prosper in this world.\f* thought to know this thing, it is labour before me:
\v 17 Until I may enter into the sanctuary of God: and may understand concerning their latter ends.
\v 18 But yet \f p \fr 72.18 \ft But only in general we are taught, that evils are prepared for the guiles, or deceits,\f* for guiles thou hast put it to them: thou hast cast them down whilst they \f q \fr 72.18 \ft pride, and other sins of the wicked.\f* were elevated.
\v 19 How are they brought into desolation, they have failed suddenly: they have perished for their iniquity.
\v 20 As the dream of them that rise, o Lord, in thy city thou shalt bring their \f r \fr 72.20 \ft Not real but imaginary felicity.\f* image to nothing.
\v 21 Because my heart is \f s \fr 72.21 \ft Almost burned up with afflictions.\f* inflamed, and my reins are changed:
\v 22 And I am brought to nothing, and knew not.
\v 23 As \f t \fr 72.23 \ft Charged with heavy burdens, without knowledge how long or to what end,\f* a beast am I become with thee: and I \f v \fr 72.23 \ft yet not destitute of thy protection.\f* always with thee.
\v 24 Thou hast held my right hand: and in thy will thou hast conducted me, and with glory thou hast received me.
\v 25 For what is to me \f w \fr 72.25 \ft Nothing to be desired in heaven nor in earth but God.\f* in heaven? And besides thee what would I upon the earth?
\v 26 My \f x \fr 72.26 \ft In body and mind I thirst after thee, my true inheritance.\f* flesh hath fainted, and my heart: God of my heart, and God my portion for ever.
\v 27 For behold, they \f y \fr 72.27 \ft Men by their freewill make themselves enemies to God.\f* that make themselves far from thee, shall perish: thou \f z \fr 72.27 \ft Determined their damnation, foreseeing their final sins.\f* hast destroyed all that fornicate from thee.
\v 28 But it is good for me to cleave to God: to put my hope in our Lord God. That I may shew forth all thy praises, in the gates of the daughter of Sion.
\c 73
\cd Faithful people pressed with persecution lamentably complaining, beseecheth God to respect his own inheritance, cruelly afflicted, 10. and left long without help, 12. whereas heretofore he relieved his people in like distresses. 18. And therefore confidently hopeth he will revenge the blasphemers of his name. \f - \ft A prayer in long affliction. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Understanding \f a \fr 73.1 \ft Instruction for the congregation of the faithful in distress, not to seek temporal rewards and prosperity, but to expect spiritual and eternal. S. Aug.\f* to Asaph. Why hast thou, o God, \f b \fr 73.1 \ft In long persecution the weak begin to fear or suspect, that God hath utterly abandoned them.\f* repelled for ever: is thy fury wrath upon the sheep of thy pasture?
\v 2 Be \f c \fr 73.2 \ft But the stronger pray with confidence,\f* mindful of thy congregation, \f d \fr 73.2 \ft being\f* which thou hast possessed from the beginning. Thou hast \f e \fr 73.2 \ft Another reason why God will conserve his Church is, because he hath delivered it often in a strong arm; as from bondage in Ægypt, from persecution and oppression in the time of Judges, and Kings, and lastly redeemed it from the tyranny of the devil by Christ's death.\f* redeemed the rod of thine inheritance: mount Sion, in which thou hast dwelt.
\v 3 Lift up thy hands upon \f f \fr 73.3 \ft Thirdly the pride of the enemies moveth God to revenge their wickedness, & so to deliver his own people.\f* their prides for ever: how great things hath the enemy done malignantly in the holy place?
\v 4 And they that hate thee have gloried: in the midst of \f g \fr 73.4 \ft They pretend to keep solemn feasts like to thine.\f* thy solemnity. They have set \f h \fr 73.4 \ft They set up altars against thine altars, or some new devised external shew, against the holy Rites of the Church:\f* their signs, for signs:
\v 5 and have not known, \f i \fr 73.5 \ft and that in public places, in the ends of high ways, or ports, and pinnacles.\f* as in the issue on high. As \f k \fr 73.5 \ft The Prophet describeth the manner how persecutors destroy temples and all sacred things.\f* in a wood of trees they have with axes
\v 6 cut out the gates thereof together: in hatchet and chip-axe they have cast it down.
\v 7 They have burnt thy sanctuary with fire: they have polluted the tabernacle of thy name in the earth.
\v 8 Their \f l \fr 73.8 \ft The whole crew or band of persecutors.\f* kindred together have said in their heart: Let us make all the festival days of God to cease from the earth.
\v 9 Our \f m \fr 73.9 \ft The weak complain that God sheweth not wonted signs, nor sendeth succourse to his people, by raising some Prophet, or other means to help them.\f* signs we have not seen, there is now no Prophet: and he will know us no more.
\v 10 How long, o God, shall the enemy upbraid: the adversary provoke thy name for ever?
\v 11 Why doest thou turn away thy hand, and thy right hand, out of the midst of thy bosom for ever?
\v 12 But \f n \fr 73.12 \ft The perfect do answer, that God both hath, and will relieve his people.\f* God our King before the worlds: he hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth. \x - \xt Luc. 1. v. 70.\x*
\v 13 Thou in thy strength hast confirmed \f o \fr 73.13 \ft Made the red sea like walls.\f* the sea: thou hast \f p \fr 73.13 \ft Drowned Pharao and his host.\f* crushed the head of Dragons in the waters.
\v 14 Thou hast broken the heads of the dragon: thou hast given him for meat to the peoples \f q \fr 73.14 \ft Of black devils.\f* of the Æthiopians.
\v 15 Thou hast \f r \fr 73.15 \ft Given water out of rocks,\f* broken up fountains, and torrents, thou hast dried the rivers \f s \fr 73.15 \ft made passage over Jordan.\f* of Ethan.
\v 16 The day is thine, and the night is thine: thou hast made the morning and the sun.
\v 17 Thou hast made all the coasts of the earth: the summer and the spring thou hast formed them.
\v 18 Be mindful of this, the enemy hath upbraided our Lord: and a foolish people hath provoked thy name.
\v 19 Deliver not to beasts the souls that confess to thee: and the souls of the poor forget not for ever.
\v 20 Have respect unto thy testament: because they that are \f t \fr 73.20 \ft Ignorant, blind, and barbarous infidels, lodge all Kinds of iniquity in their consciences.\f* obscure of the earth, are filled with houses of iniquities.
\v 21 \f v \fr 73.21 \ft And therefore it is unmeet, that faithful people should be in bondage under them.\f* Let not the humble be turned away being confounded: the poor and needy shall praise thy name.
\v 22 Arise God, judge thy cause: be mindful of those thy reproaches, that are \f w \fr 73.22 \ft These foolish audacious men do continually blaspheme all holy things.\f* from the foolish man all the day.
\v 23 \f x \fr 73.23 \ft Omit not to punish them,\f* Forget not the voices of thine enemies: the \f y \fr 73.23 \ft seeing they are obstinate & obdurate in pride and in hatred.\f* pride of them that hate thee, hath ascended always.
\c 74
\cd The Royal Prophet singeth God's praises, for his particular providence towards the Jews: 10. Further to be extended to all the meek of the whole earth. \f - \ft God's providence towards his people. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, \f a \fr 74.1 \ft Either this was the beginning of a song, to the tune whereof this Psalm was sung; as some Hebrew Rabbins testify; or, as S. Augustin and other fathers explicate, the Septuagint do admonish us by occasion of this Psalm, not to fail in our hope, for that God will in no case violate his promise nor purpose, but will render to every one in the day of Judgement, as they deserve.\f* Corrupt not, a Psalm of Canticle to Asaph.
\v 2 We \f b \fr 74.2 \ft Christ with his Apostles, and other assessory judges, will praise and thank God, for his admirable good providence, and government of this world.\f* will confess to thee, o God: \f c \fr 74.2 \ft This duplication of the same word, confirmeth us of the assured performance of that, which is here prophesied.\f* we will confess, and will invocate thy name. We will tell thy marvelous works:
\v 3 when \f d \fr 74.3 \ft Christ our singular, principal, and proper Judge only, and no mere creature, knoweth the determinate time of general judgement.\f* I shall take a time, I will judge justices.
\v 4 \f e \fr 74.4 \ft The earth at that time shall be purged with flaming fire:\f* The earth is melted, and all that dwell in it: \f f \fr 74.4 \ft yet shall not be destroyed, but changed in qualities.\f* I have confirmed the pillars thereof.
\v 5 I said to the wicked: \f g \fr 74.5 \ft An epitome, or brief sum of Christ's doctrine, to fly from sin.\f* Do not wickedly; and to them that offend: \f h \fr 74.5 \ft Be not proud, abuse not the power you have, to do what you please.\f* Exalt not the horn.
\v 6 Exalt not your horn on high: speak not iniquity against God.
\v 7 For neither from the East, nor from the West, nor from the desert mountains:
\v 8 \f i \fr 74.8 \ft When other Judges sit on tribunals, some may be absent, or escape from their sentences: but all without exception shall be brought in person, when Christ God and Man shall judge.\f* because God is Judge. \f k \fr 74.8 \ft Some shall have sentence of eternal pain in hell: some of eternal reward in glory,\f* This man he humbleth, and him he exalteth:
\v 9 \f l \fr 74.9 \ft though (partly in this world, partly in particular judgement of every soul) God hath already, and will before the last day punish sinners, yet there resteth more punishment, and no impenitent sinners shall escape, but all shall drink of the bitter cup of eternal damnation.\f* because there is a cup in the hand of our Lord \f m \fr 74.9 \ft Strong wine not delayed with water, but mingled with bitter sharp things, as gall, vinegar, brimstone, &c. and so poured into festered wounds, so they shall suffer untolerable endless pain, mixed with the worm of conscience, loss of glory, rancour of heart, and eternal desperation.\f* of mere wine full of mixture. And he hath poured it out \f n \fr 74.9 \ft Variety of hellish torments, from one extreme to another: as from snow-waters to exceeding heat. Job. 24.\f* of this into that, but yet the dregs thereof are not emptied: all the sinners of the earth shall drink.
\v 10 But I will shew forth for ever: I will sing to the God of Jacob.
\v 11 And I will break all the horns of sinners: and \f o \fr 74.11 \ft The just for well using their power of freewill, and of all power given them in this life, are rewarded in heaven.\f* the horns of the just shall be exalted.
\c 75
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, in praises, a Psalm \f a \fr 75.1 \ft For the congregation of faithful and godly\f* to Asaph, a Canticle to \f b \fr 75.1 \ft Amongst others, one notorious example of God's providence is recorded of an hundred fourscore & five thousand Assirians, slain in one night by an Angel. 4. Reg. 19.\f* the Assyrians.
\v 2 God is known \f c \fr 75.2 \ft God was not only known in general, as to pagan Philosophers, and some others, but more particularly to the Jews, the issue of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, by his special benefits towards them.\f* in Jewry: in Israel his name is great.
\v 3 And \f d \fr 75.3 \ft God suffering all other nations to err for their sins, in their fond fantasies of false gods, reserved the Israelites for his Church,\f* his place is made in peace; and \f e \fr 75.3 \ft establishing the principality thereof in Sion.\f* his habitation in Sion.
\v 4 \f f \fr 75.4 \ft For obtaining and conserving of Sion from wicked Infidels, God overthrew all sorts of contrary forces.\f* There he brake the powers of bows, the shield, the sword, and the battle.
\v 5 Thou \f g \fr 75.5 \ft God not only gave his people temporal victories over their enemies, but also illuminated their minds with knowledge of true religion:\f* doest illuminate marvelously from the eternal mountains:
\v 6 all the foolish of heart \f h \fr 75.6 \ft others are often troubled in mind, hearing the truth, but are not converted through their obstinate folly.\f* were troubled. They \f i \fr 75.6 \ft Worldly men in supine carelessness, as in a sleep pass over this life, and afterwards find themselves excluded from heaven, for lack of merits, and good works, with the foolish virgins.\f* slept their sleep: and all the men of riches found nothing in their hands.
\v 7 At \f k \fr 75.7 \ft God undertaking the defence of his people, and threatening the adverse part,\f* thy reprehension, o God of Jacob, they \f l \fr 75.7 \ft they failed in courage, as men overcome with drowsiness of sleep.\f* have all slumbered that mounted on horses.
\v 8 Thou art terrible, and who shall resist thee? \f m \fr 75.8 \ft Even from the first notice of thy will, the adversaries were dejected, fearing thy potent wrath.\f* From that time thy wrath.
\v 9 From \f n \fr 75.9 \ft Terrible signs from the firmament appearing before the day of judgement,\f* heaven thou \f 1 \fr 75.9 \ft The Prophets do often speak in the pretertense, for the assurance of the things to come.\f* hast made thy judgment heard: \f o \fr 75.9 \ft persecutors and others being terrified shall be astonished and silent.\f* the earth trembled and was quiet.
\v 10 When God arose unto judgment, \f p \fr 75.10 \ft God will come to judge the world, more especially for the justs' sake.\f* that he might save all the meek of the earth.
\v 11 Because \f q \fr 75.11 \ft Men that shall seriously think and meditate upon these things, will praise and thank God for them:\f* the cogitation of man shall confess to thee: and \f r \fr 75.11 \ft and the effect and sweet repast of such meditation, shall make as it were a great festival day, in the devout soul spiritually joined with God.\f* the remains of the cogitation shall keep festival day to thee.
\v 12 \f s \fr 75.12 \ft The soul thus inflamed with God's love, is then apt of gratitude, for his goodness towards man, to make vows of things whereto we are not obliged,\f* Vow ye and \f t \fr 75.12 \ft but most necessary it is to be maturely advised, and not rashly nor lightly to vow; for being once vowed, we are strictly bound to whatsoever we have lawfully promised. And it is great sin to vow undiscreetly.\f* render to our Lord your God: all ye that round about him bring gifts. To \f v \fr 75.12 \ft Remembering that for vows, and all other works we must answer to God, who is a terrible judge, ready to punish in body and soul, sparing none for their greatness, nor Princes nor Kings, nor vulgar sort, for all are to him alike.\f* the terrible,
\v 13 and him that taketh away the spirit of Princes, terrible to the kings of the earth.
\c 76
\cd Any faithful devout person meditating God's benefits, 7. examineth his conscience, that nothing be in his soul, that may offend God. Who is always ready to forgive: 11. and therefore he still reneweth his purpose to serve God \it sincerely, 15. particularly remembering the delivery of Israel from Ægypt.\it* \f - \ft God's special protection of the Jews. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, for \f a \fr 76.1 \ft For Idithun to sing, or to make tune for it.\f* Idithun, a psalm \f b \fr 76.1 \ft For the faithful congregation to consider God's benefits.\f* to Asaph.
\v 2 With \f c \fr 76.2 \ft Having heretofore prayed,\f* my voice I have cried to our Lord: with my voice to God \f d \fr 76.2 \ft Ihave obtained.\f* and he attended to me. \x - \xt Psal. 141.\x*
\v 3 In the day of my tribulation I sought God, with my hands \f e \fr 76.3 \ft Especially being in tribulation, and praying with heart and hands lifted up, as well in the night as day:\f* in the night before him: and I \f f \fr 76.3 \ft Iwas not frustrate of my prayer.\f* was not deceived. My \f g \fr 76.3 \ft Iwas sometimes in such anguish that nothing seemed comfortable,\f* soul refused to be comforted,
\v 4 I \f h \fr 76.4 \ft but I relied upon God so firmly,\f* was mindful of God and was delighted, and was exercised: and \f i \fr 76.4 \ft that my spirit came into an ecstasy or trance.\f* my spirit fainted.
\v 5 Mine eyes \f k \fr 76.5 \ft I arose early before the ordinary time of awaking,\f* prevented the watches: I was \f l \fr 76.5 \ft my heart being attentive inwardly, I uttered nothing with my tongue.\f* troubled & spake not.
\v 6 I thought upon old days: and the eternal years I had in mind.
\v 7 And I meditated in the night with my heart, and I was exercised, and I \f m \fr 76.7 \ft I diligently examined my conscience.\f* swept my spirit.
\v 8 Why, \f n \fr 76.8 \ft Assuredly God will not reject for ever, but he will be pleased with his Church.\f* will God reject for ever: or will he not add to be better pleased as yet?
\v 9 Or will he cut off his mercy for ever, from generation unto generation?
\v 10 Or will God forget to have mercy? Or will he in his wrath keep in his mercies?
\v 11 And I said: \f o \fr 76.11 \ft Whilst I thus thought, I erred greatly, now I see and confess that God suffereth all calamities for the good of his servants:\f* Now have I begun; \f p \fr 76.11 \ft and this I knew not by myself, but by the inspiration of God, making this change in me by his gracious hand.\f* this is the change of the right hand of the Highest.
\v 12 I have been mindful of the works of our Lord: because I will be mindful from the beginning of thy marvelous works.
\v 13 And I will meditate in all thy works: and in thy inventions I will be exercised.
\v 14 O God in the holy is thy way: what god is great as our God?
\v 15 thou art the God that doest marvelous things. Thou hast made thy power known among peoples:
\v 16 thou hast with thine arm redeemed thy people, the children of \f q \fr 76.16 \ft The progeny of Jacob, received and nourished in Ægypt for Joseph's sake, as his adopted children.\f* Jacob, and Joseph.
\v 17 The waters \f r \fr 76.17 \ft The red sea, and Jordan felt thy divine power, and obeyed thy will.\f* saw thee, o God, the waters saw thee: and they were afraid, and the depths were troubled.
\v 18 A \f s \fr 76.18 \ft Noise of waters meeting after the Israelites were passed, thunders and lightnings also happened, to the terror of the persecutors, though not mentioned in Exodus.\f* multitude of the sounding of waters: the clouds gave a voice. For indeed arrows do pass:
\v 19 the voice of thy thunder in a wheel. Thy lightnings shined to the round world: the earth was moved and troubled.
\v 20 Thy way in the sea, and thy paths in many waters: and thy steps shall not be known. \x - \xt Exo. 14.\x*
\v 21 Thou hast conducted thy people as sheep: \f t \fr 76.21 \ft By the ministry of Moyses and Aaron.\f* in the hand of Moyses and Aaron.
\c 77
\cd The Royal Prophet exhorting the people to attend, 5. reciteth many great benefits of God towards their forefathers (whose ingratitude, often rebellion, and chastisement he still noteth) 9. as in their entrance into the land of Chanaan: 12. also before the same in Ægypt, and in the desert. 42. How God plagued the Ægyptians: 52. protected, and conducted his people into the promised land. 56. Where likewise they often offended, were punished: 65. yet were still conserved: 69. and the tribe of Juda exalted in King David. \f - \ft God's great Benefits bestowed upon the Jews, and their ingratitude. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Understanding \f a \fr 77.1 \ft Commended to Asaph a chief musician, that the people might understand and consider God's benefits.\f* to Asaph. My people attend ye to \f b \fr 77.1 \ft Neither the law, nor the people was David's, but presenting God's person, he speaketh in his name or authority, with whose inspiration he was replenished. S. Greg. Prefat. in Job c. 2.\f* my law: incline your ear unto the words of my mouth.
\v 2 I will open my mouth in \f c \fr 77.2 \ft Albeit the Prophet reciteth historically things done, yet the same were parables, similitudes, and figures of other things:\f* parables: I will speak \f d \fr 77.2 \ft yea of secret hidden Mysteries, obscurely signified in the old Testament, and revealed in the new.\f* propositions from the beginning.
\v 3 How great things have we heard and \f e \fr 77.3 \ft Which partly we know by written holy Scriptures\f* have known them, and \f f \fr 77.3 \ft partly by Traditions.\f* our fathers have told us.
\v 4 They were not hid from their children, in another generation. Telling the praises of our Lord, and his powers, and his marvelous works which he hath done.
\v 5 And he \f g \fr 77.5 \ft God of his mercy without precedent merit, raised up a peculiar people of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob,\f* raised up a testimony in Jacob: and \f h \fr 77.5 \ft and gave them a particular law, first of Circumcision, & more largely by Moyses.\f* made a law in Israel. How great things he commanded our fathers, \f i \fr 77.5 \ft So Abraham instructed his children & his house after him, Gen. 18.\f* to make the same known to their children:
\v 6 that \f k \fr 77.6 \ft in like sort others taught their children.\f* another generation may know. The children that shall be born, and shall rise up, and shall tell their children.
\v 7 That they may \f l \fr 77.7 \ft For three causes God gave his law, that his people may have confidence in him, he shewing his care to instruct and govern them:\f* put their hope in God, and may \f m \fr 77.7 \ft that they remember his benefits:\f* not forget the works of God: and may \f n \fr 77.7 \ft and keep his commandments.\f* seek after his commandments.
\v 8 That they become not as their fathers: \f o \fr 77.8 \ft The Jewish nation very often, and in great numbers murmured, rebelled, and committed other great sins, and therefore David exhorted the people of his time, not to do the like. And this exhortation pertaineth more especially to Christians, as S. Paul teacheth. 1. Cor. 10.\f* a perverse generation and exasperating. A generation, that hath not directed their heart, their spirit hath not been faithful towards God. \x - \xt 1. Par. 7. v. 21.\x*
\v 9 The \f p \fr 77.9 \ft They first (trusting in their own strength) without God's commandment (Num. 14.) went forth to battle and were overthrown. \+it 1. Par. 7. v. 21.\+it*\f* children of Ephrem bending, and shooting with bow: were turned in the day of battle.
\v 10 They kept not the testament of God: and in his law they would not walk.
\v 11 And they forgat his benefits, and his marvelous works, which he shewed them.
\v 12 Before their fathers he did marvelous things in the land of Ægypt, in the field \f q \fr 77.12 \ft Tanis the principal city in Ægypt, near the river Nilus, where Moyses wrought his great miracles.\f* of Tanis.
\v 13 He divided the sea & brought them through: and he made the waters to stand as in a bottle. \x - \xt Exo. 14.\x*
\v 14 And he \f r \fr 77.14 \ft This cloud shadowed them from the heat of the sun in the day, and the fire shined in the night, all the time that\f* conducted them in a cloud by day: and all the night by light of fire.
\v 15 He struck the rock in \f s \fr 77.15 \ft In mount Horeb: and there was continual water in all the camp, which occupied near four miles in length and breadth.\f* the desert: and gave them water to drink as in a great depth.
\v 16 And he brought forth water out of the rock: and made waters run down as rivers.
\v 17 And they added as yet to sin unto him: they provoked the Highest to wrath in the place \f t \fr 77.17 \ft Which naturally wanted water: but by miracle had aboundance.\f* without water.
\v 18 And they tempted God in their hearts: so that they asked \f v \fr 77.18 \ft Not content with Manna, they demanded to have flesh.\f* meats for their lives.
\v 19 And they spake evil of God, they said: Can God prepare a table in the desert?
\v 20 Because he struck the rock and waters ran, & torrents flowed: \f w \fr 77.20 \ft Still incredulous, not believing God's omnipotency, they thought that, albeit he had given them manna and water, yet he could not give them flesh.\f* Can he also give \f x \fr 77.20 \ft By bread in general is understood all competent meat usual for a table.\f* bread, or prepare a table for his people?
\v 21 Therefore our Lord heard, and \f y \fr 77.21 \ft For this incredulity, murmuring, and other sins, God kept the children of Israel forty years in the desert, till all that were of age when they came from Ægypt, were dead, except only Iosue and Caleb.\f* made delay: and \f z \fr 77.21 \ft In the mean time amongst other punishments, many murmurers were burnt to death with strange fire. Num. 11.\f* fire was kindled in Jacob, and wrath ascended upon Israel.
\v 22 Because they believed not in God, nor hoped in his salvation.
\v 23 And he commanded the clouds from above, and opened the gates of heaven.
\v 24 And he rained them Manna to eat, and bread of heaven he gave to them.
\v 25 Bread \f a \fr 77.25 \ft Manna made by Angels.\f* of Angels did man eat: he sent them victuals in aboundance.
\v 26 He \f b \fr 77.26 \ft God so changed the wind, that it brought aboundance of quails and other birds into their camp. \+it Exo. 16. Nu. 11.\+it*\f* transported the South-wind from heaven: and in his power he brought in the South-west-wind.
\v 27 And he rained upon them flesh as dust: and as the sand of the sea feathered fowls.
\v 28 And they fell in the midst of their camp: about their tabernacles.
\v 29 And they did eat and were filled exceedingly, and their desire he brought to them:
\v 30 They were not defrauded of their desire. \f c \fr 77.30 \ft Immediately after a month (for so long they had aboundance of these birds, ibid. v. 20.) they were strucken with a plague, & many died for their concupiscence.\f* As yet their meats were in their mouth:
\v 31 And the wrath of God ascended upon them. And he killed their fat ones, and \f d \fr 77.31 \ft The most fresh strong men died, and so were hindered from possessing the promised land of Chanaan.\f* the chosen of Israel he hindered.
\v 32 In all these things they sinned as yet: and they believed not in his marvelous works.
\v 33 And their days failed in vanity: and their years \f e \fr 77.33 \ft In forty years above six hundred thousand died.\f* in haste.
\v 34 When he slew them, they sought him: and they returned, and \f f \fr 77.34 \ft They offered morning sacrifice.\f* early they came to him.
\v 35 And they remembered that God is their helper: and the high God is their redeemer.
\v 36 And they loved him \f g \fr 77.36 \ft But were not sincere in their hearts.\f* with their mouth, and with their tongue they did lie to him.
\v 37 But their heart was not right with him: neither were they counted faithful in his testament.
\v 38 \f h \fr 77.38 \ft Howsoever multitudes of people commit great sins, and are severely punished, yet God's mercy preserveth some by his effectual grace, and never suffereth the whole Church to fail, nor to be destroyed.\f* But he is merciful, and will be propitious to their sins: and he will not destroy them. And he abounded to turn away his wrath: and he kindled not all his wrath.
\v 39 And he remembered that they are flesh: \f i \fr 77.39 \ft Man's life is like the wind, that still passeth, and the same returneth not. As Aristotle teacheth. Here the Hebrews note the midst of the Psalter, in 1263. verses, and so many in the rest.\f* spirit going, and not returning.
\v 40 How \f k \fr 77.40 \ft The people of Israel murmured so often in the desert, that it was not easy to tell how often. See the Annotation.\f* often have they exasperated him in the desert: provoked him to wrath in the place without water. \ef - \fr 77.40 \fq How often have they exasperated? \ft Moyses (Deu. 9. v. 7.) repeating what had passed in the desert, chargeth the people that they had still provoked our Lord to wrath, ¹ from the day, that they came out of Ægypt, and always contended against him. And our Lord himself expostulating their ingratitude & often murmuring saith (Num. 14. v. 22.) in the beginning of the second year, that they had then tempted him ten times ²; either by this certain number signifying an uncertain, or else chiefly ten times; for so often we find recorded that they tempted him, and murmured within that small time more notoriously. First ³, near unto the red sea (Exod. 14. v. 11.) where seeing the Ægyptians pursuing them, they murmured against Moyses; for bringing them out of Ægypt, saying: It had been much better to have served the Ægyptians, than to die in the wilderness. Secondly ⁴, for want of sweet water, Exod. 15. v. 24. Thirdly ⁵, for lack of meat, Exo. 16. v. 3. Fourthly ⁶, keeping Manna for the next day, contrary to God's commandment. ibid. v. 20. Fifthly ⁷, going on the Sabbath day, also contrary to God's commandment, to gather Manna. Ibid. v. 27. Sixthly ⁸, for want of water in Raphidim. Exod. 17. v. 2. Num. 26. v. 2. Seventhly ⁹ in Horeb, adoring a calf & the image thereof. Exo. 32. Eighthly, ¹⁰ repining for their travails in the wilderness. Num. 11. v. 1: Ninthly, ¹¹ loathing Manna, and burning with desire to eat flesh. Ibid. v. 4. 5. 6. Tenthly, ¹² despairing to possess the promised land of Chanaan, after that the discoverers had reported the difficulties, with the force of the people, and of the cities against which they must fight. Num. 14. v. 1. All which and the rest, saith S. Paul, happened to them in figure of us: and are written for our correption (or admonition) that we murmur not as they did. 1. Cor. 10. \fq ¹ \ft The people of Israel often murmured in the desert. \fq ² \ft Ten times more notoriously. \fq ³ \ft 1. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. \fq ⁵ \ft 3. \fq ⁶ \ft 4. \fq ⁷ \ft 5. \fq ⁸ \ft 6. \fq ⁹ \ft 7. \fq ¹⁰ \ft 8. \fq ¹¹ \ft 9. \fq ¹² \ft 10.\ef*
\v 41 And \f l \fr 77.41 \ft For eftsoons repenting they offended God again and again.\f* they returned, and tempted God: and the holy one of Israel they exasperated.
\v 42 They did not remember his hand: in the day that he redeemed them from the hand of the afflicter.
\v 43 As he put \f m \fr 77.43 \ft The first sign was in turning a rod into a serpent, which was a miracle, but no plague, the other signs were also plagues to the Ægyptians.\f* his signs in Ægypt, and his wonders in the field of Tanis.
\v 44 And he turned \f n \fr 77.44 \ft The first plague.\f* their rivers into blood, & their \f o \fr 77.44 \ft Pools, lakes, and all sorts of water, yea showers, or rain water which seldom happeneth in Ægypt.\f* showers that they might not drink.
\v 45 He sent upon them \f p \fr 77.45 \ft The fourth plague, in order as they are recited in Exodus.\f* a swarm of flies, and it eat them: and \f q \fr 77.45 \ft The second plague.\f* the frog, and it destroyed them. \f 1 \fr 77.45 \ft \+it * Coenomyiam.\+it*\f*
\v 46 And he gave their fruits to \f r \fr 77.46 \ft This was a less plague, not mentioned in Exodus, with the greater.\f* the blast, and their labours to \f s \fr 77.46 \ft The eighth plague.\f* the locust.
\v 47 And he killed their vineyards with \f t \fr 77.47 \ft The seventh plague.\f* hail: and their mulberry trees with \f v \fr 77.47 \ft This also is omitted in Exodus.\f* hoar-frost.
\v 48 And he delivered \f w \fr 77.48 \ft Not only all trees, and plants, but also beasts were subject to the hail,\f* their beast to hail: and their possession \f x \fr 77.48 \ft and to fiery lightnings.\f* to fire.
\v 49 He sent upon them \f y \fr 77.49 \ft In these general terms, of wrath, indignation, and tribulation, the Prophet comprehendeth all the other plagues, to wit, the third of sciniphs, the fifth of pestilence, the sixth of boyles in men and beasts, the ninth of darkness three days together.\f* the wrath of his indignation: indignation, & wrath, and tribulation: immissions \f z \fr 77.49 \ft All which God sent by the ministry of devils, evil Angels.\f* by evil angels.
\v 50 He made a way to the path of his wrath, he spared not their lives from death: and their cattle he shut up in death.
\v 51 And \f a \fr 77.51 \ft The tenth and greatest plague, Exod. \+it 11. v. 5. & c. 12. v. 29.\+it*\f* he struck all the first-born in the land of Ægypt: the first fruits of all their labours in the tabernacles \f b \fr 77.51 \ft Egyptians also descended from Cham, by his son Mesraim. \+it Gen. 10. v. 6.\+it*\f* of Cham.
\v 52 And he \f c \fr 77.52 \ft After that Ægypt was thus plagued, God brought Israel out of their servitude, as a shepherd leadeth his sheep, and defendeth them.\f* took away his people as sheep: and led them as a flock in the desert.
\v 53 And he brought them forth in hope, and they feared not: and the sea covered their enemies.
\v 54 And he brought them into \f d \fr 77.54 \ft Judea a hilly country.\f* the mount of his \f e \fr 77.54 \ft Into that country which God chose, and endowed with many blessings.\f* sanctification, the mount, which his right hand purchased. And he cast \f f \fr 77.54 \ft As is written in Josue.\f* out the gentiles from their face: and by lot he divided the land of them in a cord of distribution:
\v 55 And he made the tribes of Israel to dwell in their tabernacles.
\v 56 And \f g \fr 77.56 \ft After the conquest and quiet possession, the Israelites often fell into gross sins, especially in the time of Judges.\f* they tempted, and exasperated God the highest, and they kept not his testimonies.
\v 57 And they turned away themselves, & kept not the covenant: even as their fathers, they were turned as a \f h \fr 77.57 \ft A crooked bow deceiveth the archer, so this people failed to serve God, and deceived themselves.\f* crooked bow.
\v 58 They incensed him to wrath in their \f i \fr 77.58 \ft In their altars erected in hills to Idols.\f* hills: and in their \f 1 \fr 77.58 \ft \+it * Sculptilibus.\+it*\f* gravens they provoked him \f k \fr 77.58 \ft By graven images of Idols, they provoked God to\f* to emulation.
\v 59 God heard, and contemned: and he brought Israel to nothing \f l \fr 77.59 \ft Not absolutely to nothing, but punished them exceedingly, till they repented and then spared and delivered them from tribulation, as appeareth in the book of Judges.\f* exceedingly.
\v 60 And he rejected the tabernacle \f m \fr 77.60 \ft The Ark of God sometime kept in Silo, Iosue \+it 18.\+it* in the tribe of Ephraim, was taken by the Philistiims. \+it 1. Reg. 4.\+it* and never returned thither again.\f* of Silo, his tabernacle, \f n \fr 77.60 \ft But wheresoever the Ark was, there God more especially heard their petitions, and gave answers.\f* where he dwelt among men.
\v 61 And he delivered \f o \fr 77.61 \ft For their sins God suffered the Ark to be taken,\f* their force into captivity: and their beauty into the hands of the enemy.
\v 62 And he \f p \fr 77.62 \ft And the Israelites to be sore afflicted by their enemies.\f* shut up his people in the sword: and he despised his inheritance.
\v 63 \f q \fr 77.63 \ft The zeal and just wrath of God suffered these calamities to happen.\f* Fire devoured their young men: and their virgins were not lamented.
\v 64 Their \f r \fr 77.64 \ft Ophni and Phinees the sons of Heli slain: and Heli himself hearing that the Ark was taken, fell from his stool and broke his neck. \+it 1. Reg. 4.\+it*\f* Priests fell by the sword: and their widows were not wept for.
\v 65 And \f s \fr 77.65 \ft Nevertheless God plagued the infidels, and conserved his Church. \+it 1. Reg. 5.\+it*\f* our Lord was raised up as one that sleepeth: as a mighty man having surfeited of wine.
\v 66 And he struck his enemies on the hinder parts: an everlasting reproach he gave to them.
\v 67 And he rejected the tabernacle \f t \fr 77.67 \ft As before. \+it v. 60.\+it*\f* of Joseph: and the tribe of Ephraim he chose not.
\v 68 But he chose the tribe \f v \fr 77.68 \ft After a time the Ark was brought into the tribe of Juda.\f* of Juda, mount Sion which he loved.
\v 69 And he built \f w \fr 77.69 \ft The Church was firm, and ever preserved in the old testament till Christ, and from Christ's time to the end of the world.\f* his sanctuary as of unicorns in the land, which he hath founded for ever.
\v 70 And \f x \fr 77.70 \ft God's particular grace in choosing and exalting David, was a special benefit to the Israelites.\f* he chose David his servant, and took him from the flocks of sheep: from after the ewes with young he took him.
\v 71 To \f y \fr 77.71 \ft To rule and govern the people of Israel.\f* feed Jacob his servant, and Israel his inheritance.
\v 72 And he fed them in the innocency of his heart: and in the understandings of \f z \fr 77.72 \ft Prudently using his power and authority.\f* his hands he conducted them.
\c 78
\cd The Prophet, in person of the Church, lamenteth the cruelty of persecutors (both in the old and new testament) 5. prayeth for release, with just revenge against God's enemies, that blaspheme his name, 3. and promiseth to be grateful in divine praises. \f - \ft The Church suffereth very great persecutions. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 78.1 \ft To be sung by Asaph and his company, in the behalf of people united in faith.\f* to Asaph. O God \f b \fr 78.1 \ft Cruel infidels have invaded those things, which pertain to thy Church:\f* the Gentiles are come into thine inheritance, they \f c \fr 78.1 \ft even possessed & profaned the holy temple of the Jews, & Churches of Catholic Christians. Fulfilled by Antiochus in Jerusalem, by the Vandals in Afrike, by Protestants, and Puritans, in diverse parts of Europe, and will be more universally by Antichrist in all Christendom.\f* have polluted thy holy temple: they have made Jerusalem \f d \fr 78.1 \ft Instead of great and fair Churches, God's servants are fain to use mean houses, yea poor cottages.\f* as a watch tower of fruits.
\v 2 They have made \f e \fr 78.2 \ft Some persecutors suffer not Martyrs' bodies to be buried, but hang them on poles and pinnacles, where birds may eat them.\f* the carcasses of thy servants, meats for the fowls of the air: the flesh of thy Saints for the beasts of the land.
\v 3 They have poured out their blood as water round about Jerusalem: and there was none to bury them.
\v 4 We are \f f \fr 78.4 \ft Christians Galileans, Catholics Homousians, and now Papists, in spite and reproach.\f* become a reproach to our neighbours: a scorn and mock to them that are round about us.
\v 5 How long, o Lord, wilt thou \f g \fr 78.5 \ft Sins provoking God's wrath are one cause why he suffereth his people to be persecuted.\f* be angry for ever: shall thy zeal be kindled as a fire?
\v 6 Pour out thy wrath upon the Gentiles, \f h \fr 78.6 \ft Though the faithful commit some sins, yet in respect that they believe rightly, have zeal in God's cause, and deny not God, they are near to grace and favour.\f* that have not known thee: & upon the Kingdoms, that have not invocated thy name.
\v 7 Because they have devoured Jacob: and his place they have made desolate.
\v 8 \f i \fr 78.8 \ft Especially when they repent, and confess their sins; than those that neither know nor will know God, but obstinately impugn and resist the truth.\f* Remember not our old iniquities, let thy mercies quickly prevent us: because we are become exceeding \f k \fr 78.8 \ft So much afflicted, and despised, that none will regard the truth which we profess.\f* poor.
\v 9 Help us, o God our Saviour: and for the glory of thy name o Lord deliver us: and be propitious to our sins for thy name's sake.
\v 10 Lest they say perhaps amongst the Gentiles: \f l \fr 78.10 \ft Albeit, o Lord, thou suffer us to be punished, yet suffer\f* where is their God? And \f m \fr 78.10 \ft Therefore we pray, that the revenge of thy servants' blood may be so notified, that it cannot be denied, nor doubted of. Which is also here prophesied, that it will so come to pass in the end.\f* be notified in the nations before our eyes. The revenge of the blood of thy servants, which hath been shed:
\v 11 let the groaning of the fettered enter in thy sight. According to the greatness of thine arm possess thou \f n \fr 78.11 \ft Preserve also, o God, the relics of thy servants, the successors of thy Martyrs.\f* the children of them that are put to death.
\v 12 And \f o \fr 78.12 \ft This also is a prophecy, that God will most severely revenge the blasphemy of persecutors.\f* render to our neighbours sevenfold in their bosom: their reproach wherewith they have reproached thee, o Lord.
\v 13 But \f p \fr 78.13 \ft God's people delivered from persecution, and perpetually conserved, will always praise God for the same.\f* we thy people, and the sheep of thy pasture, will confess to thee for ever. Unto generation and generation we will shew forth thy praise.
\c 79
\cd The Prophet prayeth for the release of Israel in great tribulation. 15. Foresheweth the coming of Christ to redeem man from sin, and from thraldom of the devil. \f - \ft Christ our Redeemer from sin and captivity. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 79.1 \ft Pertaining to the new Testament,\f* the end, for \f b \fr 79.1 \ft especially to Gentiles that shall be converted to Christ.\f* them that shall be changed, testimony to \f c \fr 79.1 \ft For perpetual memory to the congregation of faithful.\f* Asaph, a Psalm.
\v 2 Thou that rulest Israel, attend: that \f d \fr 79.2 \ft By Joseph the Prophet understandeth all Israel; because the first-birth-right being taken from Ruben was given to him, to wit double portion of inheritance, two tribes of twelve.\f* conductest Joseph as a sheep. Which sittest upon the Cherubs, \f e \fr 79.2 \ft Appear and shew thy mighty hand before thy people:\f* be manifest \f f \fr 79.2 \ft whereof Ephraim, Benjamin, and Manasses following the Ark, when they marched or camped, should most directly see what appeared therein, the other nine tribes being places before it, and on both sides. Num. 2.\f*
\v 3 before Ephraim, Benjamin, and Manasses. Raise \f g \fr 79.3 \ft Thou which always can help us, now use thy power in delivering us from this temporal misery.\f* up thy might, and come to save us.
\v 4 O God \f h \fr 79.4 \ft Set us in a better state.\f* convert us: and shew \f i \fr 79.4 \ft Shew thy benign countenance and favour. Mystically, send the promised Messias, Christ, the essential Image of God. \+it 2. Cor. 4. v. 4. Colos. 1. v. 15.\+it*\f* thy face, & we shall be saved.
\v 5 O Lord the God of hosts, how long wilt thou be angry \f k \fr 79.5 \ft How long wilt thou defer to hear our prayer?\f* upon the prayer of thy servant?
\v 6 Thou wilt feed us with the bread of tears: and give us drink with tears \f l \fr 79.6 \ft Thou dost justly punish us, but thou temperest the same with measure, that it exceed not to our ruin.\f* in measure.
\v 7 Thou hast made us to be a contradiction to our neighbours: and our enemies have scorned us.
\v 8 O God of hosts \f m \fr 79.8 \ft God first preventing with his grace,\f* convert us: and shew thy face, and we \f n \fr 79.8 \ft man may cooperate therewith to his justification and salvation.\f* shall be saved.
\v 9 Thou didst transport \f o \fr 79.9 \ft Thy Church and people,\f* the vineyard out of Ægypt: thou didst cast out \f p \fr 79.9 \ft The Chananites.\f* the Gentiles, and plantedst it.
\v 10 Thou wast the guide of the way, \f q \fr 79.10 \ft The cloud and pillar of fire were visible guides.\f* in the sight thereof: thou didst plant the roots thereof, and it \f r \fr 79.10 \ft And it multiplied mightily.\f* filled the earth.
\v 11 \f s \fr 79.11 \ft Hyperbolical speech, to signify the great multiplication of the Israelites.\f* The shadow of it covered the mountains: and the boughs of it the cedars of God.
\v 12 It extended her branches even to \f t \fr 79.12 \ft From the Mediterranean sea of Palestine,\f* the sea; and her boughs unto \f v \fr 79.12 \ft to the river Euphrates. Exod. \+it 23. v. 31.\+it* Deut. \+it 11. v. 24.\+it*\f* the river.
\v 13 Why hast thou destroyed the hedge thereof: and all that pass by the way do pluck it.
\v 14 The \f w \fr 79.14 \ft Cruel enemies more like to most cruel beasts than to men.\f* boar of the wood hath destroyed it: and \f x \fr 79.14 \ft Yea so cruel, as none else in the world are like unto them.\f* the singular wild beast hath eaten it.
\v 15 O God of hosts return: regard \f y \fr 79.15 \ft The Prophet now prayeth for the coming of Christ, which he saw in spirit.\f* from heaven, and see, and visit this vineyard.
\v 16 And \f z \fr 79.16 \ft The Church of the old testament in her best state wanted the perfection which the Church of Christ hath.\f* perfit it, which thy right hand hath planted: and upon \f a \fr 79.16 \ft Christ our Messias most commonly called by this title: The Son of man.\f* the son of man whom thou hast confirmed to thee.
\v 17 \f b \fr 79.17 \ft So thy vineyard cannot endure if it be still afflicted, and trodden down.\f* Things burnt with fire, and digged down at the increpation of thy countenance shall perish.
\v 18 Let \f c \fr 79.18 \ft Christ working by God's power redresseth all miseries.\f* thy hand be upon the man of thy right hand: and upon the son of man, whom thou hast confirmed to thee.
\v 19 And we depart not from thee, thou wilt quicken us: and we will invocate thy name.
\v 20 \f d \fr 79.20 \ft The same is the \+it 8.\+it* and the \+it 15.\+it* verse (with little alteration) and here repeated the third time. In which we also pray for three things: First to be purged and converted from sin: secondly to be illuminated by Christ, the Image of God: Thirdly, to be sanctified, and saved in eternal glory, to have the fruition of the most Blessed Trinity.\f* O Lord the God of hosts convert us: and shew thy face, and we shall be saved.
\c 80
\cd All men are invited to celebrate festival days, 6. which were instituted in memory of benefits received. 9. The devout shall be protected, and the negligent left in distress. \f - \ft Invitation to celebrate festival days devoutly. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 80.1 \ft This Psalm pertaineth not only to the old testament, but also to the new.\f* the end, for \f b \fr 80.1 \ft God's servants oppressed with tribulations.\f* wine presses, a Psalm to \f c \fr 80.1 \ft To be sung by Asaph a chief master of music.\f* Asaph himself.
\v 2 Rejoice to God our helper: make Jubilation to the God of Jacob.
\v 3 Take ye Psalm, and \f d \fr 80.3 \ft Make ready all these musical instruments.\f* give timbrel: pleasant Psalter with the harp.
\v 4 Sound ye with trumpet in \f e \fr 80.4 \ft In the Calends, or first day of every month, in remembrance of God's providence, and perpetual government of all creatures:\f* the \f 1 \fr 80.4 \ft The feast of Noemenia.\f* new moon, in \f f \fr 80.4 \ft and most especially solemnity in the first day of the seventh month, in memory of Isaac conserved from death, in whom God promised Abraham to multiply his seed, and to bless all nations. Gen. \+it 17. v. 21. c. 21. v. 12. c. 22. v. 18.\+it*\f* the \f 2 \fr 80.4 \ft Feast of trumpets.\f* notable day of your solemnity.
\v 5 Because it is a precept in Israel, and judgment to the God of Jacob.
\v 6 He put it for a testimony in \f g \fr 80.6 \ft The people of Israel signified by Joseph, as Psal. \+it 79. v. 2.\+it*\f* Joseph, when he came out of the Land of Ægypt: he heard a tongue which he knew not.
\v 7 He \f 1 \fr 80.7 \ft In memory of this benefit Pasch was instituted.\f* turned away his back \f h \fr 80.7 \ft God delivered the same people from their untolerable bondage of carrying burdens in baskets, from gathering straw, making bricks, and other servitude. Exo. \+it 1. v. 14. c. 5. v. 7.\+it*\f* from burdens: his hands served in baskets.
\v 8 In \f i \fr 80.8 \ft The admonition of God to the people:\f* tribulation thou didst invocate me, and I heard thee: I heard thee in the secret of the tempest: I \f k \fr 80.8 \ft yet after so great benefits thou didst murmur, and contradict me. Exo. \+it 17.\+it* Num. \+it 20.\+it*\f* proved thee at the water of contradiction.
\v 9 Hear, o my people, and I \f l \fr 80.9 \ft Seriously admonish thee.\f* will contest thee: Israel \f m \fr 80.9 \ft Man by free will may choose whether he will obey or no.\f* if thou wilt hear me.
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 80.10 \ft The feast of Pentecost in memory of the law.\f* There shall be \f n \fr 80.10 \ft This was another great benefit, to give an express law, for their instruction.\f* no new god in thee, neither shalt thou adore a strange god.
\v 11 For I am the Lord thy God, which brought thee out of the land of Ægypt: \f o \fr 80.11 \ft Observing my commandments, ask what thou wilt, and I will give it thee.\f* dilate thy mouth and I will fill it.
\v 12 And my People heard not my voice: and Israel attended not to me.
\v 13 And I let them alone, according to the desires of their heart, they shall go in their own inventions. \x - \xt Rom. 1. v. 24.\x*
\v 14 If my people had heard me, if Israel had walked in my ways:
\v 15 I had \f p \fr 80.15 \ft Very easily,\f* for nothing \f q \fr 80.15 \ft of my freewill and liberality, without necessity or obligation.\f* perhaps humbled their enemies: and had laid my hand upon those that afflict them.
\v 16 The enemies of our Lord have lied to him: and \f r \fr 80.16 \ft The wicked that promise to serve God, and do it not, shall be in eternal torments.\f* their time shall be for ever.
\v 17 And \f s \fr 80.17 \ft In the mean time God bestowed these benefits upon them, for the justs' sake.\f* he fed them of the fat of corn: and out of the rock with honey he filled them.
\c 81
\cd The Prophet declareth, 2. that God reprehendeth unjust Judges and Magistrates: premonisheth them of his severe & eternal punishment: 8. whereto the prophet conforming his will prayeth for the same. \f - \ft Admonition to Magistrates. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm to Asaph. God stood in the assembly \f a \fr 81.1 \ft The words of the Prophet, admonishing all Magistrates, that when they sit in judgement, or determine any cause, God who is there and every-where present,\f* of gods, and in the midst he \f b \fr 81.1 \ft attendeth their processes and therefore it behoveth them to be advised what they do: even as if they heard God speaking as here followeth:\f* judgeth gods.
\v 2 \f c \fr 81.2 \ft The words of God, though not uttered sensibly, yet in effect intimated by his law, according whereto he will proceed in judgement against unjust judges.\f* How long judge ye iniquity: and accept ye the persons of sinners.
\v 3 Judge ye for the needy and the pupil: justify ye the humble, and the poor.
\v 4 Take away the poor: and deliver the needy from the hand of the sinner.
\v 5 They \f d \fr 81.5 \ft Such judges proceed in gross ignorance, not caring to understand, but content to walk in darkness.\f* knew not, neither did they understand, they walk in darkness: \f e \fr 81.5 \ft Ye are so evil disposed, that you would turn all upside down, justifying the wicked, and condemning the just.\f* all the foundations of the earth shall be moved.
\v 6 I said: You are \f f \fr 81.6 \ft For your office which you participate of me, you are certain gods upon earth.\f* gods, and the sons of the highest all.
\v 7 \f g \fr 81.7 \ft But when you die you shall find that you are men, subject to God's judgement:\f* But you shall die as men: and fall \f h \fr 81.7 \ft yea your punishment will be grievous, and importable: for the mighty shall mightily suffer torments.\f* as one of the Princes. \x - \xt Sap. 6.\x*
\v 8 \f i \fr 81.8 \ft Again the words of the Prophet praying God,\f* Arise, o God, judge the earth: \f k \fr 81.8 \ft that seeing he is Lord of all, he will judge all.\f* because thou shalt inherit in all the Gentiles.
\c 82
\cd The Church impugned by all sorts of enemies 10. prayeth God to confound them, as he hath done diverse the like: 17. whereby some shall be converted. \f - \ft Persecuters of the Church confounded or converted. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Canticle of Psalm to Asaph.
\v 2 O God \f a \fr 82.2 \ft Seeing none is like to thee, o God,\f* who shall be like to thee? \f b \fr 82.2 \ft shew thy power and majesty,\f* Hold not thy peace, neither \f c \fr 82.2 \ft be not silent.\f* be thou appeased, o God:
\v 3 for behold \f d \fr 82.3 \ft The cruel persecuters are most insolent\f* thine enemies have made a sound: they that hate thee \f e \fr 82.3 \ft and proud.\f* have lifted up the head.
\v 4 They have taken malignant counsel upon thy people: and they have devised against thy Saints.
\v 5 They have said: Come, and let us destroy them out of \f f \fr 82.5 \ft That there be no more any faithful people,\f* the nation: and let \f g \fr 82.5 \ft any Catholic left alive.\f* the name of Israel be remembered no more.
\v 6 Because they have devised with one consent: they have together made a covenant against thee.
\v 7 The tabernacles of \f h \fr 82.7 \ft The progeny of Esau,\f* the Idumeians, and \f i \fr 82.7 \ft the seed of Ismael\f* the Ismahelites, \f k \fr 82.7 \ft descending from the elder son of Lot.\f* Moab, and \f l \fr 82.7 \ft The issue of Abraham by Agar, who falsely call themselves Saracens, as if they were of Sara.\f* the Agarens.
\v 8 \f m \fr 82.8 \ft People of Gebal a city of Syria,\f* Gebal, and \f n \fr 82.8 \ft of the other son of Lot.\f* Ammon, \f o \fr 82.8 \ft Those that first oppugned the Israelites, after they were parted from Ægypt. \+it Exo. 17.\+it*\f* Amalec: \f p \fr 82.8 \ft The Philistians,\f* the foreigners, with \f q \fr 82.8 \ft and Tyrians, all near neighbours, and some of them near akin to the Israelites, were their great enemies.\f* the inhabitants of Tyre.
\v 9 Yea and \f r \fr 82.9 \ft Others also coming further off, joined against God's people, in figure that all heretics, and other infidels conspire together against Catholics.\f* Assur also is come with them: they are made an aid to the children of Lot.
\v 10 Do to them as \f s \fr 82.10 \ft The Psalmist therefore prayeth (and withal prophesieth) that God will at last destroy them, as he did Madian. Num. \+it 31.\+it* Iudic. \+it 6.\+it* & \+it 7.\+it*\f* to Madian, and \f t \fr 82.10 \ft Sisara, Captain general,\f* Sisara: as to \f v \fr 82.10 \ft for Jabin King of Asor near Cisson. Iudic. \+it 4. v. 7.\+it* & \+it 23.\+it*\f* Jabin in the torrent Cisson.
\v 11 They perished \f w \fr 82.11 \ft Within the territory of Manasses \+it (Ios. 17.)\+it* which they invaded.\f* in Endor: they were made as \f x \fr 82.11 \ft Slain and not buried.\f* the dung of the earth.
\v 12 Put their Princes as \f y \fr 82.12 \ft These four Princes of the Madianites were slain by Gedeon's forces. Iudic. \+it 7. & 8.\+it*\f* Oreb, and Zeb, and Zebee, and Salmana. All their Princes:
\v 13 which have said: Let us possess the Sanctuary of God for an inheritance.
\v 14 My God put them \f z \fr 82.14 \ft By four similitudes the Prophet describeth the punishment that shall fall upon persecuters.\f* as a wheel: and as stubble before the face of the wind.
\v 15 Even as fire that burneth a wood: & as a flame that burneth the mountains.
\v 16 So shalt thou pursue them in thy tempest: and in thy wrath thou shalt trouble them.
\v 17 Fill their faces with ignominy: and \f a \fr 82.17 \ft God by punishing seeketh the conversion of sinners, not their eternal death.\f* they will seek thy name, o Lord.
\v 18 Let \f b \fr 82.18 \ft But such as be still obstinate, and finally impenitent do perish for ever.\f* them be ashamed, and troubled for ever and ever: and let them be confounded, and perish.
\v 19 And let them know that \f c \fr 82.19 \ft God only the Creator of heaven and earth, is properly called LORD: Whose essential and incommunicable name\f* Lord is thy name, thou only the Highest in all the earth.
\c 83
\cd Devout persons fervently desire eternal glory, 6. accounting it in the mean time, a happy state to be in the militant Church, 12. where God first giving grace, will give glory in the triumphant. \f - \ft Eternal glory. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, \f a \fr 83.1 \ft For men afflicted in this vale of misery.\f* for wine presses, to \f b \fr 83.1 \ft By the children of Core not being musicians (but porters in the temple, \+it 1. Par. 26.\+it* ) S. Augustin here and in other titles of Psalms, understandeth the faithful Children of Christ.\f* the children of Core, a Psalm.
\v 2 How beloved are \f c \fr 83.2 \ft The glorious mansions in heaven, which God hath prepared for the just.\f* thy tabernacles, o Lord of hosts!
\v 3 my soul coveteth and \f d \fr 83.3 \ft Vehement desires do sometimes deprive us of external sense.\f* fainteth unto the courts of our Lord. My heart and \f e \fr 83.3 \ft The mind rejoicing in hope, the body is also recreated, relieved, and revived, which before was dull and heavy.\f* my flesh have rejoiced toward the living God.
\v 4 For \f f \fr 83.4 \ft As sparrows by natural instinct seeking habitations, find houses to dwell in,\f* the sparrow also hath found her an house: and \f g \fr 83.4 \ft and turtles have nests, wherein to lay their young ones: so faithful souls seek to dwell in heaven, and in the mean time to lay up good works within the Catholic Church; out of which (saith Saint Augustin in this place) how good soever works do seem (as when pagans and heretics feed the hungry, clothe the naked, receive strangers into their houses, visit the sick, comfort prisoners) being not laid in the nest, conculcabuntur, & conterentur: non servabuntur, non custodientur: they shall be trod under-foot, they shall be bruised in pieces: they shall not be conserved, they shall not be kept: but (that such works may be profitable and be conserved) they must be done in true faith, in the Catholic faith, in society of the unity of the Church.\f* the turtle-dove a nest for herself, where she may lay her young ones. Thine \f h \fr 83.4 \ft Works are good, and rightly laid up when they are done in unity, and participation of God's Altars, the most proper places of Divine service, of external Sacrifice in this life, and spiritual sacrifice of perfect praises in eternal glory; where all Saints without ceasing sing: Holy, holy, holy, Lord God of hosts Isaiæ \+it 6.\+it* Apoc. \+it 4.\+it*\f* altars, o Lord of hosts: my King, and my God.
\v 5 Blessed are they that dwell in \f i \fr 83.5 \ft The Catholic Church.\f* thy house, o Lord: for ever and ever they shall praise thee.
\v 6 Blessed is the man, whose help is from thee: he \f k \fr 83.6 \ft The just by God's grace and help, may resolve to ascend by steps and degrees, from virtue to virtue \+it (v. 8.)\+it* even to heaven,\f* hath disposed ascensions in his heart,
\v 7 in \f l \fr 83.7 \ft though he be now in this vale of tears, by reason of man's sin, who otherwise was before sin in Paradise, a place of delight.\f* the vale of tears, in the place which he hath appointed.
\v 8 For \f m \fr 83.8 \ft Christ our Law-giver,\f* the law-giver shall give \f n \fr 83.8 \ft giveth aboundance of graces,\f* blessing, they shall go \f o \fr 83.8 \ft with continual increase.\f* from virtue into virtue: \f p \fr 83.8 \ft But our only omnipotent God, is to be seen by this effect of his grace, in the Church and not elsewhere.\f* the God of gods shall be seen in Sion.
\v 9 Lord God of hosts hear my prayer: receive with thine ear, o God of Jacob.
\v 10 Behold, o God our protector: and \f q \fr 83.10 \ft Agreeably to this the Church maketh all her petitions, concluding all prayers, By Christ our Lord.\f* look upon the face of thy Christ.
\v 11 Because \f r \fr 83.11 \ft In respect of the future retribution, which every one shall receive according to their deserts, one day in God's Church is better than thousands out of it.\f* better is one day in thy courts, above thousands. I have chosen to be \f s \fr 83.11 \ft And better to\f* an abject in the house of my God: rather than to dwell \f t \fr 83.11 \ft than in greatest palaces or highest dignities amongst sinners.\f* in the tabernacles of sinners.
\v 12 Because God \f v \fr 83.12 \ft The Divine wisdom so useth mercy, and verity, that neither may prejudice the other:\f* loveth mercy and truth: our Lord will give \f w \fr 83.12 \ft and so giveth grace in this life,\f* grace \f x \fr 83.12 \ft and glory in the next.\f* and glory.
\v 13 He will not deprive them of good things, that \f y \fr 83.13 \ft Besides innocency conserved without sin, there is also innocency after remission of sin, of which the Prophet here speaketh.\f* walk in innocency: o Lord of hosts, blessed is the man that hopeth in thee.
\c 84
\cd With commemoration of God's former benefits, 5. Christ's Incarnation is prophesied, 9. bringing peace and salvation, 11. mercy and justice concurring together. \f - \ft Incarnation of Christ. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, to the children of Core, a Psalm.
\v 2 O Lord thou \f a \fr 84.2 \ft God bestowed many great benefits upon the people of Israel:\f* hast blessed thy land: thou \f b \fr 84.2 \ft he brought them out of the bondage of Ægypt.\f* hast turned away the captivity of Jacob.
\v 3 Thou \f c \fr 84.3 \ft Remitted their manifold sins:\f* hast forgiven the iniquity of thy people: thou \f d \fr 84.3 \ft pardoned also a great part of due punishment.\f* hast covered all their sins.
\v 4 Thou hast mitigated all thy wrath: thou hast turned away from the wrath of thine indignation.
\v 5 \f e \fr 84.5 \ft As thou hast spared thy peculiar people, so we beseech thee, o God Creator and general Saviour of all mankind,\f* Convert us, o God our Saviour: and \f f \fr 84.5 \ft mitigate thy wrath towards us all.\f* avert thy wrath from us.
\v 6 Wilt thou be wrath with us for ever? Or wilt thou extend thy wrath from generation unto generation?
\v 7 O God thou \f g \fr 84.7 \ft Till God first shew his mercy, sinners lie dead in guilt of sin, but by his grace they are stirred up, and quickened:\f* being turned shalt quicken us: and thy people \f h \fr 84.7 \ft and joyfully return to God.\f* shall rejoice in thee.
\v 8 Shew us, o Lord, thy mercy and give us thy salvation.
\v 9 I will \f i \fr 84.9 \ft The words of the Prophet,\f* hear what our Lord God will speak in me: \f k \fr 84.9 \ft signifying that God had revealed unto him the redemption of mankind.\f* because he will speak peace upon his people. And upon \f l \fr 84.9 \ft Not all men are justified, and saved, but those that are heartily and sincerely converted.\f* his saints: and upon them, that are converted to the heart.
\v 10 But yet his salvation is nigh to them that \f m \fr 84.10 \ft Though all be not saved (because many will not cooperate to God's grace) yet very many having the fear of God, which is the beginning of godly wisdom, freely accept of God's mercy, and so the Church is gloriously propagated.\f* fear him: that glory may inhabit in our land.
\v 11 \f n \fr 84.11 \ft Whereas God's mercy would save all, and his truth or justice requireth that sins be duly punished, by Christ's Passion and death, sufficient satisfaction is offered for all sins, and those that will be partakers by penance, and conformity to God's law, may have remission,\f* Mercy and truth have met each other: \f o \fr 84.11 \ft and so justice is observed, and peace made between God and his subjects.\f* justice and peace have kissed.
\v 12 \f p \fr 84.12 \ft Integrity of conscience reigneth in good men,\f* Truth is risen out of the earth: \f q \fr 84.12 \ft God sending just means from heaven to save them.\f* and justice hath looked down from heaven.
\v 13 For \f r \fr 84.13 \ft God giveth grace,\f* our Lord certes will give benignity: and \f s \fr 84.13 \ft and so men yield fruit.\f* our land shall give her fruit.
\v 14 \f t \fr 84.14 \ft Yea they walk in justice, and right path of God's law.\f* Justice shall walk before him: and shall set his steps in the way.
\c 85
\cd The Church of Christ beginning in Jerusalem, 3. is extended to all Nations, 5. glorious, 9. and permanent 7. in holy joy. \f - \ft A prayer for continual grace. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A prayer \f a \fr 85.1 \ft Aform of prayer for King David, and for any faithful person.\f* to David himself. Incline thine ear, o Lord, & \f b \fr 85.1 \ft There be sundry just causes which move God to hear our prayers:\f* hear me: \f c \fr 85.1 \ft first our necessity requireth God's help.\f* because I am needy and poor.
\v 2 Keep my soul, because \f d \fr 85.2 \ft Secondly, because we profess, and promise to lead a holy life.\f* I am holy: save thy servant my God, that \f e \fr 85.2 \ft Thirdly, because we trust and hope in God.\f* hopeth in thee.
\v 3 Have mercy on me, o Lord, because I have cried to thee \f f \fr 85.3 \ft Fourthly, because we persevere in prayer.\f* all the day:
\v 4 make joyful the soul of thy servant, because to thee, o Lord, have I \f g \fr 85.4 \ft Fifthly, if we pray with attention of mind.\f* lifted up my soul.
\v 5 Because thou, o Lord, art \f h \fr 85.5 \ft Sixthly, because God of his own nature is benign, ready to bestow benefits.\f* sweet, and \f i \fr 85.5 \ft Seventhly, he is meek to remit offences.\f* mild: and \f k \fr 85.5 \ft Eighthly he is merciful to mitigate the punishment, to those that make recourse unto him.\f* of much mercy to all that invocate thee. \f l \fr 85.5 \ft For these causes we pray as followeth.\f*
\v 6 Receive, o Lord, my prayer with thine ears: and attend to the voice of my petition.
\v 7 In the day of my tribulation I have called to thee: because thou hast heard me.
\v 8 There is not the like to thee amongst gods, o Lord: and there is not according to thy works.
\v 9 \f m \fr 85.9 \ft Vocation of Gentiles.\f* All nations whatsoever thou hast made, shall \f n \fr 85.9 \ft They shall come by faith,\f* come, and shall adore before thee, o Lord: and they shall \f o \fr 85.9 \ft and glorify God by good works. Mat. \+it 5. v. 17.\+it*\f* glorify thy name.
\v 10 Because thou art great and doing marvelous things: thou only art God.
\v 11 Conduct me, o Lord, in thy way, and I will walk in thy truth: let my heart rejoice that it may fear thy name.
\v 12 I will \f p \fr 85.12 \ft Confession of praise.\f* confess to thee, o Lord my God, with all my heart, and will glorify thy name for ever.
\v 13 Because thy mercy is great upon me: & thou hast delivered my soul out of \f q \fr 85.13 \ft From the state of eternal damnation.\f* the lower hell.
\v 14 O God the wicked are risen up upon me, and the synagogue of the mighty have sought my soul: and they have not set thee in their sight.
\v 15 And thou, o Lord, the God of compassion and merciful, patient, and of much mercy, and \f r \fr 85.15 \ft In performing all promises.\f* true.
\v 16 Have respect to me and have mercy on me, \f s \fr 85.16 \ft A digression (usual to Prophets) of Christ's Empire & Kingdom the Church, given to him, being the son of an Immaculate Virgin, the handmaid of God.\f* give thine empire to thy servant: and save the son of thy handmaid.
\v 17 Make with me \f t \fr 85.17 \ft The chief and principal sign of Christ's, and his Church's glory, is his Resurrection, prefigured in Ionas.\f* a sign unto God, that they may see which hate me, and may be confounded: \f v \fr 85.17 \ft Whereby all enemies are confounded, either to their conversion, or to eternal damnation. See S. Augustin.\f* because thou o Lord hast holpen me.
\c 86
\p
\v 1 To the children of Core, a Psalm of Canticle. The foundations thereof in the holy \f a \fr 86.1 \ft Christ's Church was first founded in Jerusalem on Whitsunday, \+it Act. 2.\+it* in mount Sion, which hath two tops, in one of which the Temple stood, in the other David's tower or palace.\f* mountains:
\v 2 our Lord loveth the gates of Sion above all the Tabernacles of Jacob.
\v 3 Glorious things are said of thee, o city of God.
\v 4 \f b \fr 86.4 \ft The Prophet in the person of Christ saith he will commend unto his Apostles, and other Apostolical men, that they convert all nations (as our Saviour gave express commission and commandment. Math. \+it 28.\+it* Luc. \+it 24. v. 47.\+it* Act. \+it 1. v. 8.\+it* )\f* I will be mindful of \f c \fr 86.4 \ft naming here Rahab (which is Ægypt) and Babylon:\f* Rahab, and Babylon knowing me. Behold \f d \fr 86.4 \ft The Philistiims, Tyrians, Æthiopians,\f* the foreigners, and Tyre, & the people of the Æthiopians, these \f e \fr 86.4 \ft & the rest, shall be regenerate in this Church, which for the assured certainty thereof (after the Prophet's manner of speaking) is affirmed in the pretertense, as if it were then done.\f* were there.
\v 5 Shall it not be said of Sion: \f f \fr 86.5 \ft It shall be reported, or one shall say to another: Lo this and that man, all these and all these men are regenerate by Baptism in the Church of Christ.\f* Man and man, is born in her; and \f g \fr 86.5 \ft God himself; Christ God and man, founded this Church.\f* the Highest himself founded her?
\v 6 Our Lord will declare \f h \fr 86.6 \ft The multitude of the elect is so great, that only God knoweth the number,\f* in scriptures of peoples, and \f i \fr 86.6 \ft and the qualities of all sorts, of Princes, Prelates, and Peoples.\f* of Princes: of those that have been in her.
\v 7 The \f k \fr 86.7 \ft Great spiritual joy with peace of conscience, is in true Christian Catholics in the militant Church; but the blessed have the most absolute & secure joy of all, in the Church triumphant.\f* habitation in thee, is as it were of all rejoicing.
\c 87
\cd A faithful person sore and long afflicted, lamentably complaineth, praying God, 15. not still to repel him, being left desolate 19. without all consolation of friends. \f - \ft A prayer in long affliction. The 7 key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Canticle of a Psalm, to the children of Core, unto the end, for \f a \fr 87.1 \ft An instrument of music apt for lamentable songs. Not expressed in the title of any other Psalm. Perhaps because this Psalm mixeth not any consolation with mourning, as other Psalms do, which are also prayers in affliction. As the \+it 30. 53. 63. 73.\+it*\f* Maheleth \f b \fr 87.1 \ft Neither is this word in any other title. It is added here to admonish us, that as this Psalm and some others were sung by two choirs, one answering the other, so we must in answer and imitation of Christ, suffer long and great afflictions with patience, seeing he in his passion was left without ordinary consolation.\f* to answer, \f c \fr 87.1 \ft By some interpreted (his brethren) For Christ (saith S. Augustin) vouchsafeth to make them his brethren, which understand the mystery of his Cross, and not only are not ashamed thereof, but also faithfully glory therein.\f* of understanding to Eman the Ezrahite.
\v 2 O Lord the God of my salvation: \f d \fr 87.2 \ft Ihave cried to thee very often both by day and by night.\f* in the day have I cried, and in the night before thee.
\v 3 Let my prayer enter in thy sight: incline thine ear to my petition.
\v 4 Because my soul is replenished with evils: & \f e \fr 87.4 \ft Iam almost dead.\f* my life hath approached to hell.
\v 5 I \f f \fr 87.5 \ft Accounted as dead, and ready to be buried.\f* am accounted with them that descend into the lake. I am become as a man without help,
\v 6 \f g \fr 87.6 \ft If I were dead, I should be free from these afflictions. Especially it agreeth to Christ, who was free, yea of infinite power amongst the dead.\f* free among the dead, as the wounded sleeping in the sepulchres, of whom thou art mindful no more: and they are cast off from thy hand.
\v 7 They have put me in \f h \fr 87.7 \ft Mine enemies have endeavoured not only to bereave me of temporal life, whereby I should go into limbus, but also to kill my soul spiritually, whereby I should descend into the lower hell of the damned.\f* the lower lake: in the dark places, and in the shadow of death.
\v 8 Thy \f i \fr 87.8 \ft Thy just wrath also, o God, hath exceedingly afflicted me.\f* fury is confirmed upon me: and all thy waves thou hast brought in upon me.
\v 9 Thou hast made my familiars far from me: they have put me abomination to themselves. I was delivered and came not forth:
\v 10 mine eyes languished for poverty. I cried to thee o Lord all the day: I stretched out my hands to thee.
\v 11 Wilt thou \f k \fr 87.11 \ft O God deliver me whilst I am yet living, for I may not look for extraordinary and miraculous help, as to be raised again after death.\f* do marvels to the dead: or shall physicians raise to life, and they confess to thee?
\v 12 Shall any \f l \fr 87.12 \ft When I shall be dead & buried, I cannot denounce thy praise, as now I can, to mortal men.\f* in the sepulchre declare thy mercy, and thy truth in perdition?
\v 13 Shall thy marvelous works be known \f m \fr 87.13 \ft Much less shall the damned praise thee in eternal perdition.\f* in darkness: and thy justice in the land of oblivion.
\v 14 And I o Lord have cried to thee: and in the morning shall my prayer prevent thee.
\v 15 Why doest thou, o Lord, reject my prayer: turnest away thy face from me?
\v 16 I am poor, and in labours \f n \fr 87.16 \ft As well young\f* from my youth: and being \f o \fr 87.16 \ft as waxing elder I have been still afflicted.\f* exalted, humbled, and troubled.
\v 17 Thy wraths have passed upon me: and thy terrors have troubled me.
\v 18 They have compassed me as water all the day: they compassed me together.
\v 19 Thou \f p \fr 87.19 \ft My miserable estate hath alienated all friends, neighbours, & acquaintances from me.\f* hast made friend, and neighbour far from me and my familiars because of misery.
\c 88
\cd God's mercy and truth, with his great promises to David, 6. his power in the whole world, and just judgements, are the true joy of his servants. 20. Christ's kingdom shall remain for ever: 31. yea many offending yet all shall not perish, 39. but after great affliction, 47. God will respect man's infirmity. 50. his own promise, and the enemies reproaching his servants and himself: 53. who is blessed for ever. \f - \ft The Church of Christ never faileth. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Of understanding \f a \fr 88.1 \ft Otherwise called Idithun. \+it 1. Paral. 25.\+it* or rather Ethan, who was very wise, mentioned with others, to whom Salomon is preferred for wisdom. \+it 3. Reg. 4. v. 31.\+it* and signifieth strong: applied here to those that are strong in assured hope of Christ's promises, notwithstanding it seemeth sometimes to the weak, that his promises are not performed.\f* to Ethan the Ezrahite.
\v 2 The mercies of our Lord I will sing for ever. In generation \f b \fr 88.2 \ft In all generations.\f* and generation I will shew forth thy truth in my mouth.
\v 3 Because thou saidst: Mercy shall be built up for ever \f c \fr 88.3 \ft The heavens shall rather fall, than God's truth fail. Mystically, in the Apostles, and by their preaching, the Church of Christ is built for ever.\f* in the heavens: thy truth shall be prepared in them.
\v 4 I have ordained a testament with mine elect, I have sworn to David my servant:
\v 5 \f d \fr 88.5 \ft David's seed conserved till Christ was born of his Virgin Mother: and in his spiritual seed, his kingdom the\f* for ever will I prepare thy seed. And I will build thy seat unto generation and generation.
\v 6 The heavens shall confess thy marvelous works o Lord: yea and thy truth in the Church of Saints.
\v 7 For who in the clouds shall be equal to our Lord: shall be like to God among \f e \fr 88.7 \ft The Angels.\f* the sons of God?
\v 8 God who is glorified in the counsel of Saints, great and terrible over all that are round about him.
\v 9 O Lord God of hosts, who is like to thee? Thou art mighty, o Lord, and thy truth round about thee.
\v 10 Thou rulest over the power of the sea: and the moving of the waves thereof thou doest mitigate.
\v 11 Thou \f f \fr 88.11 \ft The Prophet alludeth to the plagues and miracles in Ægypt, and in other enemies.\f* humbledst the proud one, as one wounded: in the arm of thy strength thou hast dispersed thine enemies.
\v 12 The heavens are thine, and the earth is thine, the round earth, and the fullness thereof thou hast founded:
\v 13 the north and the sea thou hast created. \f g \fr 88.13 \ft Conversion of Gentiles.\f* Thabor and Hermon shall rejoice in thy name:
\v 14 thy arm is with might. Let \f h \fr 88.14 \ft Whether God punish, as with the left hand,\f* thy hand be confirmed, and thy \f i \fr 88.14 \ft or bestow benefits, as with the right hand, all is to his glory, and according to mercy and truth.\f* right hand exalted:
\v 15 justice and judgment is the preparation of thy seat. Mercy and truth shall go before thy face:
\v 16 \f k \fr 88.16 \ft They are spiritually happy, that do thus consider of God's marvelous proceedings, praise the same, and rejoice therein.\f* blessed is the people that knoweth jubilation. Lord they shall walk in the light of thy countenance,
\v 17 and in thy name they shall rejoice all the day: and in thy justice they shall be exalted.
\v 18 Because thou art the glory of their strength: and in thy good pleasure shall our \f l \fr 88.18 \ft Power, and kingdom.\f* horn be exalted.
\v 19 Because our protection is of our Lord: and of the holy one of Israel our King.
\v 20 Then didst thou speak in vision to thy Saints, and saidst: \f 1 \fr 88.20 \ft Thus God promised to establish the Kingdom of the Jews in David and his family. \+it 1. Reg. 16. 1. Reg. 5.\+it* and other places. Which was performed as in a figure, but more fully in Christ. \+it Act. 13. v. 22.\+it*\f* I have put help on the mighty one: & have exalted an elect one of my people.
\v 21 I have found David my servant: with mine holy oil have I anointed him.
\v 22 For mine hand shall help him: and mine arm shall strengthen him.
\v 23 The enemy shall nothing prevail in him: and the son of iniquity shall not add to hurt him.
\v 24 And I will cut down his enemies before his face: and them that hate him I will put to flight.
\v 25 And my truth, and my mercy with him: and in my name shall his horn be exalted.
\v 26 And I will put his hand in the sea: and his right hand in the rivers.
\v 27 He shall invocate me: Thou art my Father: my God, and the protector of my Salvation.
\v 28 And I will put him the first-begotten, high above the kings of the earth.
\v 29 I will keep my mercy unto him for ever: and my testament faithful to him.
\v 30 I will put his seed for ever and ever: and his throne as the days of heaven.
\v 31 But if his children shall forsake my law: and will not walk in my judgments.
\v 32 If they shall profane my justices: and not keep my commandments:
\v 33 I will visit their iniquities with a rod: and their sins with stripes.
\v 34 But my mercy I will not take away from him: neither will I hurt in my truth. \ef - \fr 88.34 \fq My mercy I will not take away from him. \ft Although Christians, signified by the children or successors of David, sin most grievously ¹, yea suppose they will sin with desperation (saith Saint Augustine) and obstinately persist in sin, that they offend the eyes of their Father, & deserve to be disinherited &c. Yet for these Christ shall not remain without inheritance, the corn shall not also perish for the chaff, some fishes shall be gathered out of the net into vessels, notwithstanding the evil fishes are cast away. ² And a little after the same Doctor discoursing of eternal glory both in body and soul, of those that die in God's favour, saith: These things are promised concerning Christ ³, very certain, very firm, very plain, and undoubted. For albeit some things are covered in mysteries, yet some things are so manifest, that by them the obscure things may most easily be cleared. \fq ¹ \ft Though Christians do sin, yet Christ loseth not his Church. \fq ² \ft Hard places explicated by the clear. \fq ³ \ft God's promises to David were not fulfilled in Salomon, but in Christ.\ef*
\v 35 Neither will I profane my testament: and the words that proceed from my mouth I will not make frustrate.
\v 36 Once I have sworn in my holy, if I lie to David:
\v 37 his seed shall continue for ever. \f 1 \fr 88.37 \ft \+it I will not lie.\+it*\f*
\v 38 And \f m \fr 88.38 \ft Christian just souls as the sun,\f* his throne as the Sun in my sight, and \f n \fr 88.38 \ft and as the perfect, or full moon, See the first Tome, page. \+it 716.\+it* S. Augustin also expoundeth this verse in the Anagogical sense, of the just after the Resurrection in glory, where the soul shall be like the sun, and the body, which now is mutable, shall be like the moon, not as now aways changing, but as the full moon, always perfect.\f* as the Moon perfect for ever: and a faithful witness in heaven.
\v 39 But \f 1 \fr 88.39 \ft God having promised all the aforesaid, the Prophet in the person of the weak, lamenteth that the contrary shall happen as well in the temporal kingdom oppressed by the Assirians, Babylonians, Persians, Grecians, and Romans: as in the Church impugned by innumerable sorts of Heretics and other Infidels.\f* thou hast repelled and despised: thou \f o \fr 88.39 \ft Amongst many pensive things, this one word doth comfort us, thy promise remaineth, thou hast not denied to send Christ, but deferred him.\f* hast deferred thy Christ. \ef - \fr 88.39 \fq But thou hast repelled &c. \ft Again Saint Augustine addeth upon the next verses following: God performed not these promises in David, that when thou seest they were not fulfilled in David, which necessarily must be fulfilled, thou mayst seek another, in whom it may be shewed that they were fulfilled. God promised something (a Kingdom) for ever of David's seed: and Salomon was born: and became of so great wisdom, and so great prudence, that God's promise concerning David's seed seemed to be fulfilled in him. But Salomon fell, and gave place of expecting Christ; that because God neither can be deceived, nor deceive, he put not his promise in him, whom he knew would fall; but thou shouldest rely upon God, and exact his promise. ¹ A little after: Thou seekest the Kingdom of the Jews, it is not: thou seekest the altar of the Jews, it is not: thou seekest the sacrifice of the Jews, it is not: thou seekest the Priesthood of the Jews, it is not. Whereupon he concludeth: All these defects came to the Jews: yet was not Christ taken from them, but differed. Some Jews believed in him, and many Gentiles. As the Psalmist prophesieth from the 47. verse to the end of this Psalm. \fq ¹ \ft Defects in the Jews supplied in the Gentiles.\ef*
\v 40 Thou hast overthrown the testament of thy servant: thou hast profaned his sanctuary on the earth.
\v 41 Thou hast destroyed all the hedges thereof: thou hast made the firmament thereof fear.
\v 42 All that pass by the way have spoiled him: he is become a reproach to his neighbours.
\v 43 Thou hast exalted the right hand of them that oppress him: thou hast made all his enemies joyful.
\v 44 Thou hast turned away the help of his sword: and hast not holpen him in battle.
\v 45 Thou hast destroyed him from \f p \fr 88.45 \ft From the use of Sacrifice and Sacraments, whereby sinners were wont to be cleansed.\f* emundation: and his seat thou hast broken down to the ground.
\v 46 Thou hast lessened the days of his time: thou hast overwhelmed him with confusion.
\v 47 \f 1 \fr 88.47 \ft The Psalmist prayeth and prophesieth that God will respect the weakness of man, maintain his Church in many nations, & save many souls.\f* How long, o Lord, doest thou turn away for ever: shall thy wrath burn as a fire?
\v 48 Remember what my substance is: for hast thou made all the children of men in vain?
\v 49 Who is the man that shall live, and shall not see death: shall deliver his soul from the hand of hell?
\v 50 Where are thine old mercies, o Lord, as thou swarest to David in thy truth?
\v 51 Be mindful, o Lord, of the reproach of thy servants (which I have held in my bosom) of many nations.
\v 52 Which thine enemies have reproached, o Lord, which they have reproached \f q \fr 88.52 \ft As though Christ were changed and turned from us.\f* the commutation of thy Christ.
\v 53 Blessed be our Lord for ever: \f r \fr 88.53 \ft So we wish and pray that all may bless and praise thee. Amen.\f* Be it, be it.
\c 89
\cd Under the form of prayer, the Psalmist describeth the shortness of man's life & other calamities: God's strict judgement, 13. but first his comfortable mercy, 16. and perpetual regard of his own work. \f - \ft Man rightly created fell by sin into miseries. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A prayer \f a \fr 89.1 \ft Some Expositors think Moyses was the author of this Psalm, and of the ten next following. But others hold that David was Author of all, and that Moyses his name is here put in the title by Esdras, because his Psalm is like to the prayer of Moyses, when the people provoked God's wrath by their sins in the desert. And because man's creation, fall, punishment, and God's mercy towards him, are here described: which Moyses first writ, as going before the written law. And that Moyses made not this Psalm is probably gathered by the \+it 10.\+it* verse, where the ordinary age of men is described to be (in strength and vigor) seventy years, or of fourscore: and the greater part (of the one or the other) is in labour and sorrow. And it is evident (Deut. \+it 34.\+it* ) that Moyses lived in all an hundred and twenty years, and his eye was not dim, neither were his teeth moved. So Aaron, Josue, and others commonly lived longer than is here mentioned. But David was old and impotent at seventy years. \+it 3. Reg. 1.\+it* Saint Hilarion, living near seventy years in his hermitage, S. Remigius governing the Church of Rhemes seventy years, and the like are accounted to have been full of days, and such as lived longer are reputed extraordinary. Again it is more evidently proved that Moyses was not author of the \+it 94.\+it* and \+it 95.\+it* Psalms.\f* of Moyses the man of God. Lord, thou art made a refuge for us: \f b \fr 89.1 \ft Always from the beginning of the world to the end.\f* from generation unto generation.
\v 2 Before \f c \fr 89.2 \ft The Prophet sheweth that the world was created in and with time, not eternal.\f* the mountains were made, or the earth and the world formed, \f d \fr 89.2 \ft And that only God is eternal.\f* from everlasting even unto everlasting thou art God.
\v 3 \f e \fr 89.3 \ft God hath often said, that he would not the death of sinners, but rather that they be converted and live for ever.\f* Turn not away man into humiliation: thou saidst: Be converted ye children of men.
\v 4 Because \f f \fr 89.4 \ft Though some lived long (none for all that did reach to a thousand years) yet it is nothing before God, and in respect of eternity.\f* a thousand years before thine eyes, are as yesterday that is past. And as a watch in the night,
\v 5 things that are counted nothing shall their years be.
\v 6 In \f g \fr 89.6 \ft The youth of man quickly passeth:\f* the morning as an herb he shall pass, in \f h \fr 89.6 \ft old age cannot last long: whereof cometh our English proverb: A young man may die soon, an old man cannot live long.\f* the morning he shall flourish, and pass: in the evening he shall fall, be hardened, and withered.
\v 7 Because we have fainted in thy wrath, and in thy fury we are troubled.
\v 8 Thou hast put \f i \fr 89.8 \ft Sin the cause of shortness of man's life.\f* our iniquities in thy sight: our age in the light of thy countenance. \f 1 \fr 89.8 \ft \+it Sæculum.\+it*\f*
\v 9 Because all our days have failed, and in thy wrath we have failed. Our years shall be considered \f k \fr 89.9 \ft Man's life as brickle as a spider's web: or man's life wasteth continually, as a spider wasteth herself by spinning, and consuming her own substance.\f* as a spider:
\v 10 the days of our years in them are \f l \fr 89.10 \ft These numbers literally shew the shortness of the longer sort of men's lives. Mystically, seven signify the rest after labours of this world, and pertain to the old testament: eight signify the reward in the resurrection, pertaining to the new testament. Which multiplied by ten, a perfect number, make seventy and eighty. Which joined together make a hundred and fifty. The number of all these Psalms.\f* seventy years. And if in strong ones \f m \fr 89.10 \ft See previous note\f* eighty years: and the more of them, labour & sorrow. Because \f n \fr 89.10 \ft It is of God's mild providence, that man's life is short, for that many if they were sure, or had probability to live long, would presume to sin more.\f* mildness is come upon us: and we shall be chastised.
\v 11 \f o \fr 89.11 \ft Seeing God of his justice punished all mankind for one sin of our first parent, his wrath must needs be very great to every sinner, for his own proper sins.\f* Who knoweth the power of thy wrath: and for fear
\v 12 to number thy wrath? So make thy right hand known: and men learned in heart, in wisdom.
\v 13 Turn, o Lord, how long? And be entreated for thy servants.
\v 14 \f p \fr 89.14 \ft The hope of glorious resurrection turneth our calamities into spiritual joy.\f* We are replenished in the morning with thy mercy: and we have rejoiced, and art delighted all our days.
\v 15 \f q \fr 89.15 \ft Yes the more we suffer in this life for the truth, the greater is our comfort in hope of reward.\f* We have rejoiced for the days wherein thou hast humbled us: the years, wherein we have seen evils.
\v 16 Look upon \f r \fr 89.16 \ft Not only in that we are thy creatures, but also in that we are thy servants, we are thy proper work: therefore in both these respects, o God, look upon us with clemency.\f* thy servants, and upon thy works: and \f s \fr 89.16 \ft Lead also our posterity into the right way, and make them thy servants.\f* direct their children.
\v 17 And \f t \fr 89.17 \ft O God illuminate our understanding,\f* let the brightness of our Lord God be upon us, and \f v \fr 89.17 \ft make our actions by thy grace profitable to us:\f* direct thou the works of our hands over us: and \f w \fr 89.17 \ft & make perfect in us the work of charity. In which one work all good works are included, and to which all other are directed. For then works are right (saith S. Augustin) when they are directed to this one end.\f* the work of our hands do thou direct.
\c 90
\cd Whosoever faithfully and firmly trusteth in God's providence, is secure from all dangers of secret, subtle, and open enemies. 7. His adversaries shall come to ruin. 11. Angels shall defend him: 13. no kind of serpent, nor beast shall hurt him. God himself assureth him of his protection, and of eternal salvation. \f - \ft God's providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Praise \f a \fr 90.1 \ft Praise of God's providence, with thanks,\f* of a \f b \fr 90.1 \ft which David sung with voice.\f* Canticle to David. He \f c \fr 90.1 \ft He that firmly relieth and resteth upon God's providence, is assuredly protected by him.\f* that dwelleth in the help of the Highest, shall abide in the protection of the God of heaven.
\v 2 He shall say to our Lord: Thou art my protector, and my refuge: my God I will hope in him.
\v 3 Because he hath delivered me from \f d \fr 90.3 \ft All secret and subtle machinations:\f* the snare of the hunters, and from \f e \fr 90.3 \ft and from all cruelty of tyrants.\f* the sharp word.
\v 4 With his shoulders shall he overshadow thee: and under his wings thou shalt hope.
\v 5 With shield shall his truth compass thee: thou shalt not be afraid \f f \fr 90.5 \ft Terrors obscurely suggested by evil men or spirits, with erroneous conceit that men are not bound in time of temporal dangers to confess the truth.\f* of the fear in the night. \ef - \fr 90.5 \fq Thou shalt not be afraid. \ft S. Augustine here observeth four manners of tempting the faithful to fall from true Religion ¹. Sometimes with tentation that is but light and obscure ², which the Prophet here calleth fear in the night: when ignorant men are tempted by suggestion, or apprehension or temporal afflictions, not knowing that they fall into eternal damnation, by flying from worldly, or bodily calamities. Sometimes the tentation threateneth present death to them that are well instructed in the truth ³, and know that they must confess it even to death, which the Prophet calleth an arrow flying in the day: when the faithful clearly seeth what danger hangeth over him, to wit present death, if he stand constant, and damnation if he deny his faith. Sometimes the tentation is more vehement, but yet obscure, which he called business walking in darkness: ⁴ when by subtle endeavours, framing arguments in excuse of sin, men are persuaded that they may lawfully take some oath, or do some other thing, which indeed is not lawful: and so by earnest and subtle persuasions they ignorantly decline from Catholic Religion, or commit other grievous sins. But the greatest and manifest tentation is called invasion & midday devil ⁵: when persecuters seeing neither more easy persuasions can deceive God's servants, nor present death force them to deny the truth, they then assault them more vehemently, and more dangerously, with long and continual afflictions, not remitting their cruelty till the afflicted either yield to their will, or die in long torments. And by these two latter kinds of persecution many are overthrown, which were constant in the former. For whilst tyrants proposed dangers to simple people, and deceived some, yet threatening present death to others, that were better instructed, and confirmed in Religion, innumerable persevered, & gloriously died in confession of Christian Catholic faith. But by subtle arguing of hard points of Christian doctrine or practice; and by long torments many have been seduced, blindly falling into errors, and many wittingly have denied the truth, which they clearly believed in their hearts, to avoid this midday devil, the extremity of long manifest, and grievous afflictions. Nevertheless in all these tentations God protecteth them that firmly trust in him. Those (saith this holy Father) have failed, which presumed of themselves, which dwelt not in the help of the Highest, and in protection of the God of heaven: which said not to our Lord: Thou art my Protector, and my refuge, which trusted not under the shadow of his wings, but relied, or attributed much to their own strength. \fq ¹ \ft Four sorts of persecution for the Catholic faith. \fq ² \ft 1. \fq ³ \ft 2. \fq ⁴ \ft 3. \fq ⁵ \ft 4.\ef*
\v 6 Of \f g \fr 90.6 \ft Open persecution threatening present death, except men deny the truth which they know,\f* the arrow flying in the day, \f h \fr 90.6 \ft circumvention of crafty enemies by subtle arguing, and drawing men into error, and so to decline from Catholic Religion,\f* of business walking in darkness: \f i \fr 90.6 \ft long\f* of invasion, and the midday devil.
\v 7 A \f k \fr 90.7 \ft On thy left side, in adversity many fall from God,\f* thousand shall fall on thy side, & \f l \fr 90.7 \ft & on thy right side, in prosperity many more forget and forsake God.\f* ten thousand on thy right hand: but to thee it shall not approach.
\v 8 But thou shalt consider with thine eyes: and shalt see the retribution of sinners.
\v 9 Because \f m \fr 90.9 \ft In sincerely saying Thou art my hope thou makest God thy refuge.\f* thou o Lord art my hope: thou hast made the Highest thy refuge.
\v 10 There shall no evil come to thee: and scourge shall not approach to thy tabernacle.
\v 11 Because he hath given \f n \fr 90.11 \ft Angels have protection of men by God's ordinance.\f* his Angels charge of thee: \f o \fr 90.11 \ft The devil corruptly allege this scripture \+it (Mat. 4.)\+it* omitting\f* that they keep thee in all thy ways.
\v 12 In their hands they shall bear thee: lest perhaps thou knock thy foot against a stone.
\v 13 Upon the asp and the basiliscus thou shall walk: & thou shalt tread upon the lion, and the dragon.
\v 14 \f p \fr 90.14 \ft God speaketh the rest that followeth in this Psalm.\f* Because he hath hoped in me, I will deliver him: I will protect him, because he hath known my name.
\v 15 He shall cry to me, and I will hear him: with him I am in tribulation: I will deliver him, and \f q \fr 90.15 \ft In eternal salvation.\f* will glorify him.
\v 16 With length of days I will replenish him: and I will shew him my salvation.
\c 91
\cd God is by all manner of voices and instruments to be praised in his admirable works. 7. which the foolish not doing are punished; 11. and the wise are rewarded. \f - \ft The works of God admirable. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 91.1 \ft Voices beginning instruments prosecute this song:\f* of Canticle, \f b \fr 91.1 \ft when we rest from work, then especially we ought to think upon God's works, praise and thank him for the same.\f* in the sabbath day.
\v 2 It is good \f c \fr 91.2 \ft To give thanks.\f* to confess to our Lord: and to sing to thy name, o Highest.
\v 3 To shew forth thy mercy in \f d \fr 91.3 \ft In prosperity.\f* the morning: and thy truth in \f e \fr 91.3 \ft in adversity.\f* the night.
\v 4 In \f f \fr 91.4 \ft On every instrument of ten strings, signifying the observation of the ten commandments:\f* the instrument of ten strings, in \f g \fr 91.4 \ft namely on the Psalter\f* Psalter: with Canticle, on \f h \fr 91.4 \ft also on the harp, which signifieth mortification.\f* the Harp.
\v 5 Because thou hast delighted me, o Lord, in thy workmanship: and in the works of thy hands I will rejoice.
\v 6 How are thy works magnified, o Lord! Thy cogitations are made very profound.
\v 7 The \f i \fr 91.7 \ft Carnal and sensual man,\f* unwise man will not know; and the \f k \fr 91.7 \ft he that thinketh only of present things, not of future.\f* fool will not understand these things.
\v 8 When sinners shall spring up as grass: and all that work iniquity shall appear. That they may perish for ever:
\v 9 but thou the Highest for ever, o Lord.
\v 10 Because lo thine enemies, o Lord, because lo thine enemies shall perish: and all that work iniquity shall be dispersed.
\v 11 And \f l \fr 91.11 \ft The just in confidence of a good conscience expect exaltation of their power:\f* my horn shall be exalted, as the unicorn's: \f m \fr 91.11 \ft and great consolation in the end of their life.\f* and my old age in plentiful mercy.
\v 12 And \f n \fr 91.12 \ft Then shall the justice their enemies depressed: and themselves flourish, like the palm, and cedar trees as followeth:\f* mine eye hath looked upon mine enemies: and the malignant rising up against me, mine ears shall hear.
\v 13 The just shall flourish as a palm tree: as the cedar of Libanus shall he be multiplied.
\v 14 They that are planted in \f o \fr 91.14 \ft Militant Church:\f* the house of our Lord, shall flourish in \f p \fr 91.14 \ft triumphant.\f* the courts of the house of our God.
\v 15 As yet shall they be multiplied in plentiful old age: and they shall be well affected,
\v 16 that they \f q \fr 91.16 \ft Publicly profess God's praise, as in the words following:\f* may shew forth: That the Lord our God is righteous, and there is no iniquity in him.
\c 92
\cd The faithful servant of God assuredly professeth, that all the pride, 5. cruelty, 7. foolish imaginations, and secret thoughts of the wicked, are manifest to God. 12. Acknowledgeth himself happy, that he is better instructed of God: whereas he had otherwise been damned. 20. Sharply reprehendeth those that consider not of God's judgements: concluding that the just shall be glorified, and the wicked damned. \f - \ft Eternal salvation and damnation. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Praise \f a \fr 92.1 \ft Praise to be sung with voice:\f* of Canticle \f b \fr 92.1 \ft composed by David:\f* to David himself in \f c \fr 92.1 \ft the sixth day of the week, which is our Friday,\f* the day before the sabbath, \f d \fr 92.1 \ft in which day the Church of Christ was founded by his blood shed on the cross.\f* when the earth was founded. Our Lord \f e \fr 92.1 \ft Our Saviour founding his Church by his death began then to reign therein\f* hath reigned, he \f f \fr 92.1 \ft gloriously descending in soul into limbus, and in body to his grave:\f* hath put on beauty: our Lord hath \f g \fr 92.1 \ft he then put on all armour of strength to reform the world, and to enlarge his Kingdom, according to his own prediction where he said: If I be exalted from the earth, I will draw all things unto myself. Ioan. \+it 12. v. 31.\+it*\f* put on strength, and hath girded himself. For he hath established \f h \fr 92.1 \ft Not only in Jury and Samaria, but the whole earth:\f* the round world, which \f i \fr 92.1 \ft and the same Church shall not be destroyed.\f* shall not be moved.
\v 2 Thy seat is prepared from that time: thou \f k \fr 92.2 \ft Christ being eternal, hath an everlasting Church.\f* art from everlasting.
\v 3 The \f l \fr 92.3 \ft All sorts of persecuters, the High priests (who sometimes watered the spiritual land, like rivers) which Scribes, Pharisees, and other incredulous Jews, also Pagans, Turks, and Heretics have oppugned the Church.\f* rivers o Lord have lifted up: the rivers have lifted up their voice. The rivers have lifted up their waves,
\v 4 \f m \fr 92.4 \ft With more force than any persecutions in the old Testament.\f* above the voices of many waters. The surges of the sea are marvelous, \f n \fr 92.4 \ft But though all these assaults be great and marvelous, yet Christ in protecting his Church, is more marvelous.\f* marvelous is our Lord on high.
\v 5 Thy testimonies are made \f o \fr 92.5 \ft Articles of faith are not evidently apparent to knowledge, but evident to credibility, to those that are disposed by God's grace, illuminating their understanding, and moving their free will, to give consent of belief if they will.\f* credible exceedingly: \f p \fr 92.5 \ft It behoveth therefore all members of the Church to converse piously, and religiously in this life, seeing she hath so excellent a spouse, protector, and instructor,\f* holiness becometh thy house, o Lord \f q \fr 92.5 \ft even to the end of the world.\f* for length of days.
\c 93
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 93.1 \ft The Hebrew letter Lamed, which ordinarily is prefixed to the dative case, or signifieth to, being set before proper names, is a sign of the genitive case. Yet the Septuagint express it by the dative, and so doth the Latin, \+it ipsi\+it* David, and consequently our English hath, to David himself, to shew a difference between sacred and profane writers. For in human books the writer and author is all one; but in divine, the Holy Ghost is the proper author, and a man is the writer. To signify therefore the principal author, David is sometimes named as the instrumental cause, to whom the Holy Ghost inspired this and other Psalms, and by whom they were written. And when the titles express otherwise:\f* David himself, in \f b \fr 93.1 \ft Made and ordinarily sung in the fourth day of the week, our Wednesday, in which day Judas the traitor sold our Saviour Christ to his enemies. The revenge of which wickedness, and of all other sins, is here prophesied.\f* the fourth of the sabbath. Our Lord God of revenges: the God \f c \fr 93.1 \ft God more commonly called the God of mercy (which virtue in him is above all his works, Psal. \+it 144.\+it* ) is also the God of revenges, according to his justice.\f* of revenges hath done \f d \fr 93.1 \ft He proceedeth in judgement resolutely, not depending, nor fearing, nor respecting any person, power, dignity, wisdom or other like quality, but their just merits.\f* freely.
\v 2 Be \f 1 \fr 93.2 \ft A prayer of just zeal.\f* exalted thou that judgest the earth: render retribution to the proud.
\v 3 How long shall sinners, o Lord: how long shall sinners glory?
\v 4 Shall they utter, and speak iniquity? Shall all they speak \f e \fr 93.4 \ft Shall most wicked men still be suffered to speak so insolently?\f* that work injustice?
\v 5 Thy \f 1 \fr 93.5 \ft A description of heathenish and heretical cruelty.\f* people, o Lord, they have humbled: and thine inheritance they have vexed.
\v 6 The widow, and the stranger they have slain: and the pupils they have killed.
\v 7 And \f 1 \fr 93.7 \ft Scarce any Atheists are so blind as thus to think, but many sinners so behave themselves, as if God saw not, knew not, or at least cared not what they do.\f* they have said: The Lord shall not see, neither shall the God of Jacob understand.
\v 8 Understand ye foolish in the people: and ye fools be wise at sometime.
\v 9 He that planted the ear, shall he not hear? Or he that made the eye doth he not consider?
\v 10 He that chastiseth nations, shall he not rebuke: he that teacheth man knowledge?
\v 11 Our Lord knoweth \f f \fr 93.11 \ft So unpossible is it that God should be ignorant, or careless what men do, that he also knoweth and observeth most secret thoughts.\f* the cogitations of men: that they be vain.
\v 12 Blessed is the man whom thou shalt instruct, o Lord, and shalt teach out of thy law.
\v 13 That thou mayst \f g \fr 93.13 \ft Mitigate and temper his afflictions, that by patience and fortitude the just may persevere, and not be overwhelmed.\f* give him quietness from the evil days: till a pit be digged for the sinner.
\v 14 Because our Lord \f h \fr 93.14 \ft The whole Church shall never be rejected, nor forsaken.\f* will not reject his people: and his inheritance he will not forsake.
\v 15 Until justice \f i \fr 93.15 \ft Justice is converted into judgement, when just meaning is put in work and practice, that it may appear in judgement. Also God who doth & suffereth all justly, will conserve his inheritance the Church, even unto the day of judgement.\f* be turned into judgment: and \f k \fr 93.15 \ft The sense is easy by transposing the words: all that are right of heart, are near it, that is, shall like and approve God's justice, when the wicked shall repine, and blaspheme it.\f* they who are near it, are all that are right of heart.
\v 16 Who shall rise for me against the malignant? Or who shall stand with me against them that work iniquity?
\v 17 But that our Lord hath holpen me: within very little my soul had dwelt in hell.
\v 18 If I said: \f l \fr 93.18 \ft When I felt and complained that I was in danger, thou didst assist me.\f* My foot is moved: thy mercy, o Lord, did help me.
\v 19 According to the multitude of my sorrows in my heart: thy consolations have made my soul joyful.
\v 20 Doth the seat of iniquity cleave to thee: which makest \f m \fr 93.20 \ft Only faith sufficeth not, but careful labour, in keeping God's commandments is required.\f* labour in precept?
\v 21 They will hunt after the soul of the just: and will condemn innocent blood.
\v 22 And our Lord became my refuge: and my God the help \f n \fr 93.22 \ft The just do hope for eternal salvation, to which God will bring them.\f* of my hope.
\v 23 And he \f o \fr 93.23 \ft And God, the revenger of wrongs, will at last cast the wicked into eternal torments.\f* will repay them their iniquity: and in their malice he will destroy them: the Lord our God will destroy them.
\c 94
\cd An invitation to serve and adore Christ our Lord and Messias, 3. as well for the benefits of creating all things, 7. as for his Incarnation, and not to harden our hearts as the Jews did. \f - \ft Christ our Lord and King. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Praise \f a \fr 94.1 \ft Praise sung with voices:\f* of Canticle, \f b \fr 94.1 \ft inspired to David, & written by him.\f* to David himself. Come, \f 1 \fr 94.1 \ft This invitation is most fitly ordained by the Church for the proem or beginning of Matins.\f* let us \f c \fr 94.1 \ft With great and solemn exultation:\f* rejoice to our Lord: let us make jubilation to God \f d \fr 94.1 \ft God our Creator, is also our Protector & Saviour.\f* our saviour.
\v 2 Let us \f e \fr 94.2 \ft Let us be more diligent, and prevent our accustomed time. For no man can prevent God's grace with any good work, who first preventeth us, else we can neither do, nor think any good thing.\f* prevent his face in confession: and \f f \fr 94.2 \ft Not only in singing his praise with voice, but also with musical instruments.\f* in Psalms let us make jubilation to him.
\v 3 Because our Lord is a great God: and a great King above all gods.
\v 4 Because in his hand are the ends of the earth: and the heights of the mountains be his.
\v 5 Because the sea is his, and he made it: and his hands formed the dry land.
\v 6 Come let us adore, \f g \fr 94.6 \ft So also Isaias \+it (c. 45. v. 23.)\+it* and S. Paul \+it (Philip. 2.)\+it* teach that kneeling or bowing the knees, as an external religious ceremony, is acceptable to God.\f* and fall down: and weep before our Lord, that made us.
\v 7 Because he is the Lord \f h \fr 94.7 \ft It is most just and necessary that we adore God, because he made us, and all this world for us, hath also redeemed us, and made us his people, as sheep of his pasture, and as a Pastor feedeth and governeth us.\f* our God, and we the people of his pasture, and the sheep \f i \fr 94.7 \ft Of his making.\f* of his hand.
\v 8 \f k \fr 94.8 \ft Though some have often repelled, and resisted God's grace, yet if they receive it being offered again, it will avail them to remission of sins.\f* Today if ye shall hear his voice, harden not your hearts. \ef - \fr 94.8 \fq Harden not your hearts. \ft Whatsoever God proposeth by preaching or inspiration to a sinner, it resteth still in the power of his freewill ¹, to harden his heart, and to reject all such good motions, and so he doth not only frustrate God's grace, and hinder his own justification, but also increaseth his former sins. ² But by not resisting, when deliberating thereupon he could resist, he disposeth himself and cooperateth to first justification. And therefore the royal Prophet here admonisheth, and earnestly exhorteth all men, to do this which God hath put in our power, not to harden our own hearts, when we hear his voice, by resisting and rejecting his grace freely offered, without all merit of our part. \fq ¹ \ft It is in man's freewill to resist good motions. \fq ² \ft \+it Concil. Triden. Sess. 6. c. 5.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 As in the provocation according to the day \f l \fr 94.9 \ft The Israelites in the desert tempted God, by desiring water, and flesh, of voluptuous concupiscence without necessity. For Manna did both extinguish their thirst, and tasted unto them whatsoever they desired: Exod. \+it 16.\+it* That also which was left ungathered when the sun waxed hot, melted \+it (v. 21.)\+it* and served their cattle for drink. So this tentation was a figure of those, which require to communicate under both kinds, as if one did not contain as much as both.\f* of the tentation in the desert: where your fathers tempted me, proved me, and saw my works.
\v 10 \f m \fr 94.10 \ft By this mention of the offence of forty years, as long before passed, is convinced that Moyses writ not this Psalm, who died in the very fortieth year of their abode in the desert. And Saint Paul citing the words of this Psalm (Heb. \+it 4.\+it* ) manifestly acknowledgeth David the writer thereof, and that it was written long after Moyses' time in these words: \+it (v. 7.)\+it* Again he limiteth a certain day: Today, in David saying after so long time, as is above said. Today if you shall hear his voice, do not obdurate your hearts. For if Jesus (that is Iosue) had given them rest, he would never speak of another day afterward.\f* Forty years was I \f n \fr 94.10 \ft Being greatly offended, I approached near unto them, in punishing the offenders.\f* offended with that generation, and said: These always err in heart.
\v 11 And these have not known my ways: as I sware in my wrath: \f o \fr 94.11 \ft Those that murmured died in the desert, and entered not into the promised land, even so those that finally offend Christ, shall not enter into everlasting rest. Heb. \+it 3.\+it* & \+it 4.\+it*\f* if they shall enter into my rest.
\c 95
\cd All peoples & nations are invited to praise the blessed Trinity, 3. for Christ's Incarnation, and spiritual kingdom in all the world. 10. even senseless creatures acknowledging his majesty, 13. and judicial power. \f - \ft Christ's divine power. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Canticle \f a \fr 95.1 \ft Inspired to David, and written by him:\f* to David \x - \xt 1. Par. 16. v. 23.\x* himself, \f b \fr 95.1 \ft prophesying the restoration of the temple, after the future captivity. And that in figure of the universal redemption of mankind by Christ, from the captivity of the devil.\f* when the house was built after the captivity. Sing ye to our Lord \f c \fr 95.1 \ft For a new benefit, far greater than the delivery of Israel from Ægypt.\f* a new song: sing to our Lord all the earth.
\v 2 \f d \fr 95.2 \ft The same words (Sing to our Lord) thrice repeated, signify the Blessed Trinity, as some Fathers note. Likewise \+it v. 7.\+it* & \+it 8.\+it* Bring ye to our Lord &c. in both places concluding in the singular number, bless his name, bring to his name, importing one God.\f* Sing ye to our Lord, and bless his name: shew forth his salvation from day to day.
\v 3 Shew forth his glory among the Gentiles, his marvelous works in all peoples.
\v 4 Because our Lord is great, and exceeding laudable: he is terrible above all gods.
\v 5 Because all the gods of the Gentiles are \f e \fr 95.5 \ft What creatures soever spiritual or corporal, visible or invisible the Pagans serve for gods, still they be devils that deceive them, and diverse ways usurp divine honour, making such idolaters to think, that there is divine power, where none is.\f* devils, but our Lord \f f \fr 95.5 \ft He only is true God, who is Creator of heaven, and of all creatures. For no creature can create any thing at all, that is, make any thing of nothing, but only God.\f* made the heavens.
\v 6 Confession and beauty in his sight: holiness and magnificence in his sanctification.
\v 7 Bring to our Lord ye families of Gentiles, bring ye to our Lord glory and honour:
\v 8 bring to our Lord glory unto his name. Take up hosts, and enter into his courts:
\v 9 adore ye our Lord in his holy court. Let all the earth be moved before his face:
\v 10 Say ye among the Gentiles that our Lord \f g \fr 95.10 \ft Diverse ancient Doctors read more in this place: Our Lord hath reigned from the wood, to wit, Christ by his death on the cross conquered the devil, sin, and death, and thence began to reign. S. Justinus Martyr, dialogo advers. Triphonem, Tertullian li. advers. Iudæos. c. 9. & 13. & advers. Marcionem li. 3. c. 19. & 21. S. Augustin in this place, according to the old Roman Psalter. Before him Arnobius, and after him Cassiodorus and others, whereby it is probable, that it was sometimes in the Hebrew text, and blotted out by the Jews.\f* hath reigned. For he hath corrected the round world, which shall not be moved: he will judge peoples in equity.
\v 11 \f h \fr 95.11 \ft The Psalmist in aboundance of spirit inviteth all creatures to praise God, as Daniel in his Canticle. \+it c. 3.\+it*\f* Let the heavens be glad, and the earth rejoice, the sea be moved, and the fullness thereof:
\v 12 the fields shall be glad, and all things that are in them. Then shall the trees of the woods rejoice
\v 13 before the face of our Lord, because he cometh: because he cometh to judge the earth. He \f i \fr 95.13 \ft Christ judgeth now in the world by his ministers, discerning and deciding causes, rewarding and punishing, but especially he will judge all in the last day.\f* will judge the round world in equity, and peoples in his truth.
\c 96
\cd All men are again invited joyfully to celebrate the marvelous conquest of Christ in all nations, 4. with harp, voice, and instruments: 8. all creatures acknowledging
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 96.1 \ft In figure of Christ,\f* this David, \f b \fr 96.1 \ft whose body rose the third day after his death: to whom many returned believing in him after his resurrection, which fell from him in his passion: and to whom all things shall be subdued, as to their true Lord, in the day of judgement.\f* when his land was restored again to him. Our Lord hath reigned, let the earth rejoice: let \f c \fr 96.1 \ft Holy David and other Prophets having great joy to see long before in spirit only, Christ's Kingdom extended in the whole earth, yea to the Islands, we Islanders have great cause to be glad, that God hath not only so blessed us long since, but as yet conserveth seed, whereby we trust the whole Island shall be again restored unto him.\f* many Islands be glad.
\v 2 \f d \fr 96.2 \ft As in a cloud with terror God gave his law to the Jews: so in a cloud with greater terror and majesty, he will judge the world;\f* Cloud and mist round about him: justice and judgment \f e \fr 96.2 \ft not as many corrupted seats of judgement, in this world, but as a corrected tribunal, where justice and right judgement shall be practised.\f* the correction of his seat.
\v 3 Fire shall go before him, and shall inflame his enemies round about.
\v 4 His lightnings \f 1 \fr 96.4 \ft These things are denounced as if they were already done, for the assured certainty thereof.\f* shined to the round world, the earth saw: and was moved.
\v 5 The mountains melted as wax, before the face of our Lord: before the face of our Lord all the earth.
\v 6 The heavens have shewed forth his justice: and all peoples have seen his glory.
\v 7 Let them all be confounded, that adore \f f \fr 96.7 \ft As well the worshippers of graven or painted images of Jupiter, Mars, Bacchus & the like, as the worshippers of the same imagined false gods shall be confounded.\f* sculptiles: and that glory in their idols. Adore him all ye his Angels:
\v 8 \f g \fr 96.8 \ft The Catholic Church.\f* Sion heard, and was glad. And \f h \fr 96.8 \ft And all particular Churches, members of the universal.\f* the daughters of Juda rejoiced, because of thy judgments, o Lord.
\v 9 Because thou Lord most high over all the earth: thou art exalted exceedingly above all gods.
\v 10 You that love our Lord, hate ye evil, our Lord keepeth the souls of his saints, out of the hand of the sinner he will deliver them.
\v 11 Light is risen to the just, and joy to the right of heart.
\v 12 Be glad ye just in our Lord: and confess ye to the memory \f i \fr 96.12 \ft Praise our Lord Christ, who is sanctity it-self, and sanctifieth others.\f* of his sanctification.
\c 97
\cd his coming to judge the world. \f - \ft The Church in all nations. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 97.1 \ft Prefiguring Christ, who hath made his salvation known in all nations.\f* to David himself. Sing ye to our Lord \f b \fr 97.1 \ft Anew benefit of grace, making men new in spirit, requireth a new song of gratitude.\f* a new song: because he hath done marvelous things. His right hand hath wrought salvation \f c \fr 97.1 \ft Raised up himself from death.\f* to himself: and his arm is holy.
\v 2 Our Lord hath \f d \fr 97.2 \ft Made his grace effectually known by raising men from sin, and delivering them from the power of the devil.\f* made known his salvation: in the sight of the Gentiles he hath revealed his justice.
\v 3 He hath remembered his mercy, and his truth to \f e \fr 97.3 \ft some of the Jews converted to Christianity. Rom. \+it 11.\+it*\f* the house of Israel. All the ends of the earth have seen the salvation of our God.
\v 4 Make ye jubilation to God all the earth: \f f \fr 97.4 \ft In voice.\f* chant, and \f g \fr 97.4 \ft In heart.\f* rejoice, and \f h \fr 97.4 \ft In instruments.\f* sing. \f 1 \fr 97.4 \ft \+it Cantate, exultate, psallite.\+it*\f*
\v 5 Sing to our Lord on harp, on harp and voice of Psalm:
\v 6 on long drawn trumpets, and voice of cornet of horn. Make jubilation in the sight of the King our Lord:
\v 7 let the sea be moved, and the fullness thereof: the round world, and they that dwell therein.
\v 8 The rivers shall clap with hand: the mountains together shall rejoice
\v 9 at the sight of our Lord: \f i \fr 97.9 \ft Christ directeth and disposeth all things rightly in this world.\f* because he cometh to judge the earth. \f k \fr 97.9 \ft And will accordingly give just sentence in the end.\f* He will judge the round earth in justice, and the peoples in equity.
\c 98
\cd Christ reigneth, notwithstanding his enemies' repine, is adored (5. also his foot-stool) 6. whom ancient Prophets did invocate. \f - \ft Christ our Messias. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A psalm to David himself. Our Lord hath reigned, \f a \fr 98.1 \ft Though many enemies do rage and impugn Christ:\f* let peoples be angry: he that sitteth upon the Cherubs, let \f b \fr 98.1 \ft though the whole earth be troubled therewith, yet Christ who sitteth Lord over the highest Angels, Cherubins and Seraphins, obtaineth the victories, reigneth, and doth his will in the earth.\f* the earth be moved.
\v 2 Our Lord great in Sion: and high above all peoples.
\v 3 Let them confess to thy great name: because it is terrible and holy.
\v 4 And the honour of the King \f c \fr 98.4 \ft Requireth discretion.\f* loveth judgment. Thou hast prepared directions: thou hast done judgment and justice \f d \fr 98.4 \ft in favour of thine elect people.\f* in Jacob.
\v 5 Exalt ye the Lord our God, and adore \f e \fr 98.5 \ft Hebrew Doctors expound this of the Ark in the old testament, but the Doctors of the Church understand Christ's humanity, in the holy Eucharist.\f* his foot-stool: because it is holy. \ef - \fr 98.5 \fq Adore his foot-stool. \ft For so much as all Expositors, also the Hebrew Rabbins, affirm that the Psalmist here prophesieth of Christ the promised Messias ¹, that should redeem mankind, and seeing the Ark of covenant pertaineth not to the service of Christ, but was only a figure of him, the foot-stool of the Messias here mentioned must needs be something pertaining to him: and therefore most ancient Fathers expound it of Christ's humanity. And because the Prophet speaketh of perpetual adoration, not only of the short time he conversed with men in this life, when very few adored him, the same fathers understand here the adoration of Christ in the Blessed Sacrament of the Eucharist. Which S. Ambrose ² teacheth (lib. 3. de Spiritu Sancto c. 12.) in these plain words: By the foot-stool must be understood the earth, by the earth the flesh of Christ. Which we also at this day adore in the Mysteries, and which the Apostles adored in our Lord Jesus. S. Augustine ³ more largely upon this Psalm: I am made doubtful (saith he) I fear to adore the earth, lest he condemn me that made heaven & earth. Again, I fear not to adore the foot stool of my Lord, because the Psalm saith to me: Adore his foot-stool: I seek what is his foot-stool, and the Scripture (Isaiæ 66.) telleth me, the earth is his foot-stool. Doubtful I turn myself unto Christ, because I seek him here, & I find how without impiety the earth may be adored, without impiety his foot-stool may be adored. For he took earth of earth, because flesh is of earth, and he took flesh of the flesh of (the B. Virgin) Mary. And because he walked here in the same flesh, and gave the very flesh to us to eat, unto salvation, & no man eateth that flesh, unless he first adore it ⁴: it is found how such a foot-stool of our Lord may be adored: and not only we do not sin in adoring, but we should sin in not adoring. Thus far S. Augustine. Further instructing, not to conceive of Christ's flesh, as the Capharnaites did, that he would cut it in pieces from his body, & give them portions thereof. His very flesh is given and eaten, not in fleshly manner, but in sacramental. See Annotations, Ioan. 6. \fq ¹ \ft Christ's humanity is his foot-stool, adored in the Eucharist. \fq ² \ft S. Ambrose. \fq ³ \ft S. Augustin. \fq ⁴ \ft The receivers of the B. Sacrament do sin if they do not adore it.\ef*
\v 6 Moyses and Aaron in his \f f \fr 98.6 \ft Here it is evident (and S. Augustin saith this place taketh away all doubt) that Moyses was a Priest, against those that for maintaining the heresy of Lay-headship, deny it.\f* Priests: and Samuel among them that invocate his name: They invocated our Lord \f g \fr 98.6 \ft By example of their praying and obtaining; the Psalmist confirmeth his prophecy, that Priests of the new Testament shall pray, and obtain mercy of Christ for the Church.\f* and he heard them:
\v 7 in a pillar of a cloud he spake to them. They kept his testimonies, & the precept which he gave them.
\v 8 O Lord our God thou heardest them: God thou wast propitious to them, and taking vengeance upon all their \f h \fr 98.8 \ft God revenged the machinations made against them,\f* inventions.
\v 9 Exalt ye the Lord our God, and adore ye in his holy mount: because the Lord our God is holy.
\c 99
\cd All are invited to rejoice in God, Creator of all. \f - \ft One Creator of all things. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm \f a \fr 99.1 \ft of praise.\f* in confession.
\v 2 Make ye jubilation to God \f b \fr 99.2 \ft Not only Jews, but also all Gentiles.\f* all the earth: serve ye our Lord in gladness. Enter ye in \f c \fr 99.2 \ft God every-where present, yet more peculiarly, heareth his suppliants, praying in the temple, or places dedicated to his service.\f* before his sight, in exultation.
\v 3 Know ye that our Lord \f d \fr 99.3 \ft He only whom we serve as our Lord, is the only God, and there is no other.\f* he is God: he made us, & not we ourselves. His people, and the sheep of his pasture:
\v 4 enter ye into \f e \fr 99.4 \ft Peculiar dedicated place, as \+it v. 2.\+it*\f* his gates in confession, his courts in hymns, confess ye to him. Praise ye his name:
\v 5 because our Lord is sweet, his \f f \fr 99.5 \ft As God is always merciful in giving and promising:\f* mercy for ever, and \f g \fr 99.5 \ft so he is ever faithful in performing.\f* his truth even unto generation and generation.
\c 100
\cd A sinner in affliction of mind prayeth God to deliver him, 10. desolate of all other help. 13. Conceiveth comfort in God's eternal goodness, and singular mercy, in redeeming mankind, and propagating the Church. 24. Prayeth to be made mature in virtue before he die, that he may live with God: 26. Who only and wholly being immutable, establisheth his servants for ever. \f - \ft The fifth penitential Psalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm to David himself. Mercy \f a \fr 100.1 \ft These two capital divine virtues are ever joined in all God's works, for both which experienced towards himself, the Psalmists rendereth thanks and praises.\f* and judgment I will sing to thee, o Lord: I will sing,
\v 2 and I \f b \fr 100.2 \ft Iwill do mine endeavour to know the immaculate way,\f* shall understand in the immaculate way, \f c \fr 100.2 \ft which I cannot do, but by thy grace coming unto me. For by help thereof I did as followeth:\f* when thou shalt come to me. I walked through in the innocency of my heart, in the midst of my house.
\v 3 I did not propose before mine eyes any unjust thing: I hated them that do prevarication.
\v 4 A perverse heart hath not cleaved to me: the malignant declining from me I knew not.
\v 5 \f d \fr 100.5 \ft That is, all and every one thus wickedly disposed I abhorred.\f* One secretly detracting from his neighbour, him did I persecute. One of a proud eye, and unsatiable heart, with him I did not eat.
\v 6 Mine eyes are towards the faithful of the earth that they may sit with me. A man that walketh in the immaculate way \f e \fr 100.6 \ft Ikept such under, as a servant or slave.\f* he did minister to me.
\v 7 He that doth proudly shall not dwell in the midst of my house: he that speaketh unjust things, hath \f f \fr 100.7 \ft Prospered not, got no benefit by me.\f* not directed in the sight of mine eyes.
\v 8 In \f g \fr 100.8 \ft Speedily and without delay I cut off all disordered people:\f* the morning did I kill all the sinners of the earth: that I might destroy \f h \fr 100.8 \ft that others might not be corrupted by them.\f* out of the city of our Lord all those that work iniquity.
\c 101
\p
\v 1 The prayer of the poor, when he shall be anxious, and shall make his petition before our Lord.
\v 2 Lord hear my \f a \fr 101.2 \ft Every petition is a prayer,\f* prayer: and let my \f b \fr 101.2 \ft and that which proceedeth from more fervent affection, is called a cry, though it burst not out into clamour, nor perhaps into any voice at all. For God said to Moyses, praying in mere silence, but with vehemency of spirit \+it (Exod. 14.)\+it* Why cryest thou to me?\f* cry come to thee.
\v 3 Turn \f c \fr 101.3 \ft Though sin provoke God's wrath, because we by sinning turn from him, and not he first from us: yet we pray God not so to leave us, but to give us new grace, that by humility, and penance we may return to him, and not die in sin.\f* not away thy face from me: in what day soever I am in tribulation, incline thine ear to me. In what day soever I shall invocate thee, hear me speedily.
\v 4 Because my days have vanished as \f d \fr 101.4 \ft Man's days, and all his works are nothing worth, but vanish like smoke, so long as he is in mortal sin:\f* smoke: and my \f e \fr 101.4 \ft yea his best works, as if he give alms, fast, pray, and die for the truth, yet all those avail nothing. \+it 1. Cor. 13.\+it* but are\f* bones are withered as \f f \fr 101.4 \ft like dried sticks, or chips, fit to kindle the fire.\f* a dry burnt firebrand.
\v 5 I \f g \fr 101.5 \ft My soul separated by sin from God, withereth as grass that is cut from the root:\f* am strucken as grass, and my heart is withered: because \f h \fr 101.5 \ft because I have lost all favour and appetite to spiritual meat.\f* I have forgotten to eat my bread.
\v 6 For \f i \fr 101.6 \ft In this miserable state\f* the voice of my groaning, my \f k \fr 101.6 \ft Iam as bones and flesh cleaving together without moisture, or radical humour.\f* bone hath cleaved to my flesh.
\v 7 I am become like \f l \fr 101.7 \ft Ifled from conversation of men for sorrow and shame of my sins:\f* a pelican of the wilderness: I am become as \f m \fr 101.7 \ft as a crow that only flieth by night: or as an owl or bat.\f* a night crow in the house.
\v 8 I have watched, and am become as \f n \fr 101.8 \ft Also as a sparrow having lost her mate, remaineth mourning and solitary in the accustomed nest, or near unto it.\f* a sparrow solitary in the house-top.
\v 9 All the day did mine enemies upbraid me: and they \f o \fr 101.9 \ft Those that were wont to praise or flatter me, now are as sworn enemies against me.\f* that praised me sware against me.
\v 10 Because I did \f p \fr 101.10 \ft Bread savoured to me no better than ashes.\f* eat ashes as bread, & mingled \f q \fr 101.10 \ft And drink gave me no comfort, but still I wept.\f* my drink with weeping.
\v 11 At \f r \fr 101.11 \ft Iam most especially afflicted, because thou art angry.\f* the face of thy wrath and indignation: because \f s \fr 101.11 \ft In that thou didst sometime advance me in prosperity,\f* lifting me up thou hast thrown me \f t \fr 101.11 \ft my fall is so much greater, and more grievous.\f* down.
\v 12 My days have declined \f v \fr 101.12 \ft As a shadow declineth to nothing, & all becometh darkness, when the sun, and other light departeth, even so I, that am but a shadow, decline to mere darkness when thy favour parteth from me:\f* as a shadow: and I am withered \f w \fr 101.12 \ft and I lose my beauty, as grass cut from the ground withereth.\f* as grass.
\v 13 \f x \fr 101.13 \ft But I am marvelously comforted, considering that thou our Messias, the Son of God, art immutable for ever:\f* But thou o Lord endurest for ever: and \f y \fr 101.13 \ft and thy memorable promise of redeeming mankind, will have effect in all generations.\f* thy memorial in generation and generation.
\v 14 Thou \f z \fr 101.14 \ft Thou rising to help, who seemedst to have forgot, wilt protect the Church, and every faithful soul.\f* rising up shalt have mercy on Sion: because it is \f a \fr 101.14 \ft Because thou hast deferred long:\f* time to have mercy on it, because \f b \fr 101.14 \ft and because the time by thee designed seemeth to approach.\f* the time cometh.
\v 15 Because \f c \fr 101.15 \ft Men that shall hear thine Apostles preach, shall prove good and fit matter, for the building of thy Church:\f* the stones thereof have pleased thy servants: and they shall have pity on \f d \fr 101.15 \ft and the simplest poor people, as it were the earth or dust,\f* the earth \f e \fr 101.15 \ft shall participate of this mercy.\f* thereof.
\v 16 And \f f \fr 101.16 \ft Besides those Jews that shall believe in Christ, much more the Gentiles shall fear and serve him.\f* the Gentiles shall fear thy name o Lord, and all \f g \fr 101.16 \ft His glory is so evident that all kings know it, though all be not converted.\f* the kings of the earth thy glory.
\v 17 Because our Lord hath built Sion: and he shall be seen in his glory.
\v 18 He hath had respect to the prayer \f h \fr 101.18 \ft Of holy Patriarchs, Priests, Prophets, and of all true penitents.\f* of the humble: and he hath not despised their petition.
\v 19 Let these things be written unto another generation: and the people that shall \f i \fr 101.19 \ft That shall be made a new creature in Christ.\f* be created shall praise our Lord.
\v 20 Because he hath looked forth from his high holy place: our Lord from heaven hath looked upon the earth.
\v 21 That he might hear the groanings of the fettered: that he might loose the children of them that are slain:
\v 22 That they may \f k \fr 101.22 \ft The faithful people of the Church, according to their hability endeavour to serve Christ.\f* shew forth the name of our Lord in Sion: and his praise in Jerusalem.
\v 23 In the assembling of the people together in one, and kings to serve our Lord.
\v 24 He answered him in the way of his strength: Shew me the fewness of my days.
\v 25 Call me not back \f l \fr 101.25 \ft Grant me time and means to be mature in virtue in this life.\f* in the half of my days: thy years are unto generation and generation.
\v 26 In the beginning, o Lord, thou didst found the earth: and the heavens are the works of thy hands.
\v 27 They \f m \fr 101.27 \ft Be changed in quality.\f* shall perish, but thou art permanent: and they shall all wax old as a garment. \x - \xt Heb. 1.\x* And as a vesture thou shalt change them, and they shall be changed:
\v 28 but thou art the self-same, and thy years shall not fail.
\v 29 The children of thy servants shall inhabit: and \f n \fr 101.29 \ft The Church of Christ perpetual.\f* their seed shall be directed for ever.
\c 102
\cd Thanks to God for private 6. and public benefits. 17. His mercy, justice, and other properties are immutable. 20. Angels and all other creatures are invited to praise him. \f - \ft Gratitude for God's benefits. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 102.1 \ft Inspired to David, and written by him.\f* David himself. My soul \f b \fr 102.1 \ft Shew forth praises and thanks:\f* bless thou our Lord: and \f c \fr 102.1 \ft all my cogitations, affections, senses, and powers.\f* all things, that are within me, his holy name.
\v 2 My soul bless thou our Lord: and forget not all his retributions.
\v 3 Who is \f d \fr 102.3 \ft The first benefit of grace is remission of sins:\f* propitious to all thine iniquities: who \f e \fr 102.3 \ft the second, is curing evil habits, or dispositions.\f* healeth all thine infirmities.
\v 4 Who \f f \fr 102.4 \ft The third, to conserve from falling again:\f* redeemeth thy life from deadly falling: who \f g \fr 102.4 \ft the fourth, to give victory and reward in aboundant measure.\f* crowneth thee in mercy and commiserations.
\v 5 Who \f h \fr 102.5 \ft The fifth, to grant all lawful petitions temporal and spiritual, which are good for the soul,\f* replenisheth thy desire in good things: thy youth \f i \fr 102.5 \ft the sixth, resurrection of flesh in glory.\f* shall be renewed as the eagle's. \ef - \fr 102.5 \fq Thy youth shall be renewed, as the Eagle's. \ft Aristotle and Pliny write, that an Eagle decayeth not ¹, nor ever dieth by old age, but the upper part of her beak still growing, at last hindereth her from eating, and so she dieth of famine. Saadias, and other Hebrew Rabbins report, that an Eagle Every ten years washeth herself in the sea as in a bath, & then flying very high burneth her feathers in the elemental fire, & new feathers growing she becometh fresh, as in her first youth, till at last about an hundred years old, she is not able to rise from the water, and so is drowned. Saint Augustine more probably affirmeth, that in long time her beak growing long, and stopping her mouth that she cannot eat, she breaketh the upper hooked part thereof against a stone, and so receiveth meat and recovereth strength, as in her youth. But whatsoever is the natural property of this kingly bird, the Royal Prophet here instructeth us by the similitude of her long life, or by the renovation of her strength, that just men, God's servants, are spiritually renovated in Christ ², the principal rock, on whom the Church, & all the faithful are built, either by receiving new strength by his grace in their souls, after they are weakened by sin, as Saint Jerome and Euthymius expound this place: or by restoration of their bodies glorified in the resurrection, as Saint Augustine teacheth ³: or by both, as most Catholic Doctors understand it. For one sense of holy Scripture excludeth not another. Especially when one is subordinate to the other. As here these two senses do very well concur, seeing the state of the body after the resurrection, dependeth upon the state of the soul, at the time of death. \fq ¹ \ft An Eagle fresh in old age as in youth. \fq ² \ft Renovation of a sinner by grace. \fq ³ \ft Diverse senses of the same Scripture.\ef*
\v 6 Our Lord \f k \fr 102.6 \ft Our Lord, whose special property is to shew mercy,\f* doth mercies: and \f l \fr 102.6 \ft when the same is neglected; he revengeth the wrongs, delivering the oppressed, and punishing the oppressors.\f* judgment to all that suffer wrong.
\v 7 He made his ways \f m \fr 102.7 \ft By giving them as written law.\f* known to Moyses, his wills to the children of Israel.
\v 8 Our Lord is \f n \fr 102.8 \ft God is naturally pitiful to relieve the afflicted.\f* pitiful, and \f o \fr 102.8 \ft Merciful towards sinners.\f* merciful: \f p \fr 102.8 \ft Loath to be angry, or to punish.\f* long suffering, and very merciful.
\v 9 He will \f q \fr 102.9 \ft God punisheth not penitents with eternal pain, but with temporal: for though (as in the next verse) our sins of their own nature deserve eternal punishment, yet Christ paying our ransom, true penitents are only punished temporally.\f* not be angry always: neither will he threaten for ever. \ef - \fr 102.9 \fq He will not be angry always. \ft ¹ Origen misunderstood this place, and some other like, holding an erroneous opinion, that all, even the most wicked sinners, both men and devils, shall at last be saved, and not eternally damned: which is a condemned heresy, contrary to evident places of holy Scriptures. Psal. 9. v. 6. The impious hath perished: their name thou hast destroyed for ever, & for ever & ever. Mat. 25. The wicked shall go into fire everlasting, into everlasting punishment. Apoc. 20. The beast and the false Prophet (and the same reason is for all the wicked) shall be tormented day and night, for ever and ever. Neither are these words (God will not be angry always) spoken universally, touching all sinners whosoever, but are limited, v. 13. & 17. to those that fear him, and keep his testament: whereas all those that die in mortal sin ², are still obstinate in malice, and can never rightly repent, nor rightly fear God, nor keep his commandments. \fq ¹ \ft Origen's heresy, that all shall be saved. \fq ² \ft The damned can never repent.\ef*
\v 10 He hath not done to us according to our sins: neither according to our iniquities hath he rewarded us.
\v 11 For according to the height of heaven from the earth: hath he strengthened his mercy upon them that fear him.
\v 12 As far \f r \fr 102.12 \ft See here the perfect washing away, and separation of sins.\f* as the East is distant from the West: hath he made our iniquities far from us.
\v 13 As a father hath compassion of his children, so hath our Lord compassion on them that fear him:
\v 14 because he hath known \f s \fr 102.14 \ft In regard of our frailty he hath compassion: yet his mercy only availeth to them that fear him: as in the next words before, and \+it v. 17.\+it*\f* our making. He remembered that we are dust:
\v 15 man, his days are as grass, as the flower of the field, so shall he flourish.
\v 16 Because the spirit shall pass in him, and he shall not stand: and he shall know his place no more.
\v 17 But the mercy of our Lord from everlasting, and unto everlasting upon them that fear him. And his justice is upon the children's children, to them that keep his testament.
\v 18 And are mindful of his commandments to \f t \fr 102.18 \ft Ajust man not only knoweth, & remembereth, but also doth the commandments.\f* do them.
\v 19 Our Lord hath prepared his seat in heaven: and his Kingdom shall have dominion over all.
\v 20 Bless our Lord all ye his Angels: mighty in power, doing his word, that fear the voice of his words.
\v 21 Bless our Lord all ye his hosts: you his ministers, that do his will.
\v 22 Bless ye our Lord \f v \fr 102.22 \ft All creatures, though naturally senseless, yet praise God, because they are his work,\f* all his works: in every place \f w \fr 102.22 \ft and because they are in his dominion.\f* of his dominion, my soul bless thou our Lord.
\c 103
\cd The Psalmist inviteth himself and others to praise God, for his marvelous works in the heavens, 5. the earth, and water, 9. limiting their bonds, producing all things necessary for all living creatures, in convenient seasons. 27. With continual providence of all. \f - \ft God's works marvelous. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To David himself. My soul bless thou our Lord: o Lord my God thou art magnified exceedingly.
\v 2 Thou hast put on \f a \fr 103.2 \ft Thou possessest all majesty, and matter of praise.\f* confession and beauty: being clothed with light as with a garment. Stretching out the heaven as a skin:
\v 3 which coverest the higher parts thereof \f b \fr 103.3 \ft Thou hast compassed the sphere of the fixed stars with a sphere of crystalline substance; which is as water congealed.\f* with waters. Which \f c \fr 103.3 \ft According to our capacity the Prophet describeth the speedy coming, or working of God, as if he came in a swift cloud, or with wings of the wind, to signify that he worketh what and when he pleaseth without delay. He said, and things were made: he commanded and they were created, \+it Psal. 32.\+it*\f* makest the cloud for thee to ascend on: which walkest upon the wings of winds.
\v 4 Which makest spirits thine \f d \fr 103.4 \ft Thy messengers to execute thy will:\f* Angels: and thy \f e \fr 103.4 \ft & the same Angels are as a burning fire in operation, yea they dispatch more easily, and more speedily than we can conceive.\f* ministers a burning fire.
\v 5 Which hast founded the earth upon \f f \fr 103.5 \ft Most firmly established by natural weight, in the center of the world.\f* the stability thereof: it shall not be inclined for ever and ever.
\v 6 The \f g \fr 103.6 \ft The water\f* depth, \f h \fr 103.6 \ft should naturally cover all the earth.\f* as a garment, is his clothing: upon the mountains shall waters stand.
\v 7 At \f i \fr 103.7 \ft But by thy commandment the waters are contained in their limited places.\f* thy reprehension they shall fly: at the voice of thy thunder they shall fear.
\v 8 The \f k \fr 103.8 \ft The waters being contained in their appointed channels, both hills and fields appear, which otherwise would be covered.\f* mountains ascend: and the plain fields descend into the place, which thou hast founded for them.
\v 9 Thou hast set a bound, which \f l \fr 103.9 \ft The waters.\f* they shall not pass over: neither shall they return to cover the earth.
\v 10 Which sendest forth fountains in the valleys: between the midst of mountains shall waters pass.
\v 11 All the beasts of the field shall drink: the wild asses shall \f m \fr 103.11 \ft Hope for and receive.\f* expect in their thirst.
\v 12 Over them shall the fowls of the air inhabit: out of the midst of rocks they shall give forth voices.
\v 13 Watering the mountains from higher places: of the fruit of thy work shall the earth be filled.
\v 14 Bringing forth grass for beasts, and herb for the service of men. That thou mayst bring forth \f n \fr 103.14 \ft By these three principal kinds of food, bread, wine, and oil, all sorts of nutriment are understood.\f* bread out of the earth:
\v 15 & \f o \fr 103.15 \ft See previous note\f* wine may make the heart of man joyful. That he may make the face cheerful with \f p \fr 103.15 \ft See previous note\f* oil: and bread may confirm the heart of man.
\v 16 The trees of the field shall be filled, and the cedars of Libanus, which he hath planted:
\v 17 there sparrows shall make their nest. The house of the heron is the leader of them:
\v 18 the high mountains for harts: the rock a refuge for the Irchins.
\v 19 He made the moon for seasons: the sun knoweth his going down.
\v 20 Thou didst appoint darkness, and night was made: in it shall all the beasts of the wood pass.
\v 21 The whelps of lions roaring, to raven, and to seek of God meat for themselves.
\v 22 The sun is risen, & they are gathered together: and in their couches they shall be placed.
\v 23 Man shall go forth to his work: and to his working until evening.
\v 24 How magnified are thy works, o Lord! Thou hast made all things in wisdom: the earth is filled with \f q \fr 103.24 \ft With thy creatures.\f* thy possession.
\v 25 This great sea, and very large, there are \f r \fr 103.25 \ft Fishes, serpents, worms, and all living creatures that lack feet.\f* creeping beasts, whereof \f s \fr 103.25 \ft No kind of living creatures multiplieth so much as fishes. Aristotel \+it li. 9.\+it* Animal. \+it c. 17.\+it*\f* there is no number. Little beasts with great:
\v 26 there ships shall pass. This \f t \fr 103.26 \ft Amost huge fish called Leviathan. Job. \+it 40. v. 20.\+it*\f* dragon, whom thou madest to \f v \fr 103.26 \ft Albeit in the water he passeth man's strength, yet deprived of water he is not able to defend himself. \+it v. 29.\+it*\f* delude:
\v 27 all expect of thee that thou give them meat in season.
\v 28 Thou giving unto them, they shall gather it: thou opening thy hand, all shall be filled with bounty.
\v 29 But thou turning away the face, they shall be troubled: thou shalt take away their spirit, and they shall fail, and shall return into their dust.
\v 30 Thou shalt send forth thy spirit, and they shall be created: and thou shalt renew the face of the earth.
\v 31 Be the glory of our Lord for ever: our Lord will rejoice in his works.
\v 32 Who looketh upon the earth, & maketh it to tremble: who toucheth the mountains, and they smoke.
\v 33 I will chant to our Lord in my life: I will sing to my God as long as I am.
\v 34 Let my speech be acceptable to him: but I will take delight in our Lord.
\v 35 \f w \fr 103.35 \ft A prediction that impenitent sinners shall be damned, wherein the Prophet conforming his will to God's, uttereth it in form of a prayer.\f* Let sinners fail from the earth, and the unjust, so that they be not: my soul bless thou our Lord.
\c 104
\cd The Israelites are exhorted to sing praises to God, 5. for his marvelous benefits towards Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. 11. Whose particular family, being then small, went from Chanaan into Ægypt. (17. Whither Joseph by God's providence was carried before) there increased in number, was persecuted, 26. delivered by Moyses and Aaron, working many great miracles, 36. protected, and fed in the desert, 44. and finally possessed Chanaan. \f - \ft God's special benefits towards the Jews. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. \f a \fr 104.1 \ft Alleluia signifieth more than Laudate Dominum, Praise ye our Lord. For by these two Hebrew words, Alleluia, the Prophet inviteth all men to praise God, with gladness, and jubilation, with heart, voice, and gesture, with instruments, and howsoever we are able. And therefore S. Jerome, S. Augustin, and all Catholic writers keep the same words, and translate it not, neither in the titles of Psalms, nor ordinarily in any place or holy Scripture. This is the first Psalm thus titled, and is the same Psalm in sense, and in good part of the words, which the Royal Prophet made, and caused to be sung, when he brought the Ark of God from the house of Obededom into his own house. 1. Par. 16. v. 8.\f* Confess ye to our Lord, and invocate his name: \f b \fr 104.1 \ft How much grateful is it now to God, that we celebrate the greater mysteries of the new Testament.\f* shew forth his works among the Gentiles. \f 1 \fr 104.1 \ft For an exposition of this Psalm read the places quoted in the inner margin.\f*
\v 2 Chant to him, and sing to him: tell ye all his marvelous works.
\v 3 Praise ye him in his holy name: let the heart of them rejoice that seek our Lord.
\v 4 Seek ye our Lord, and be confirmed: seek \f c \fr 104.4 \ft His present help.\f* his face always.
\v 5 Remember ye his marvelous works, which he hath done: his wonders, and the judgments of his mouth.
\v 6 The seed of Abraham his servants: the children of Jacob his elect.
\v 7 He is the Lord our God: in \f d \fr 104.7 \ft Not only in Israel, but in all the world.\f* all the earth are his judgments.
\v 8 He hath been mindful for ever of his testament; of the word which he commanded \f e \fr 104.8 \ft For ever, to the end of the world.\f* unto a thousand generations. \x - \xt Gen. 12. v. 7. Gen. 17. v. 4. Gen. 26. v. 3. Gen. 28. v. 13. Gen. 46. v. 26. 27.\x*
\v 9 Which he disposed to Abraham: and of his oath to Isaac.
\v 10 And he appointed it to Jacob for a precept: and to Israel for an eternal testament,
\v 11 saying: To thee will I give the land of Chanaan, the cord of your inheritance.
\v 12 When they were \f f \fr 104.12 \ft But 70. persons.\f* of small number, very few and sojourners thereof:
\v 13 And they passed from nation into nation, & from Kingdom to another people.
\v 14 He left not a man to hurt them: and he rebuked kings for their sake:
\v 15 Touch not my anointed, and toward my Prophets be not malignant.
\v 16 And \f g \fr 104.16 \ft By his providence suffered.\f* he called a famine upon the land: and he destroyed all the strength of bread. \x - \xt Gen. 41. v. 54.\x*
\v 17 He sent a man before them: Joseph was sold to be a servant.
\v 18 They humbled his feet in fetters, iron passed through his soul, \x - \xt Gen. 37. v. 28. Gen. 39. & seq.\x*
\v 19 until his word came. The word of our Lord inflamed him:
\v 20 the king sent, and loosed him; the Prince of the people, and released him.
\v 21 He appointed him lord of his house: and Prince of all his possession.
\v 22 That he might instruct his Princes as himself: and might teach his ancients wisdom.
\v 23 And Israel entered into Ægypt, and Jacob was a sojourner in the land \f h \fr 104.23 \ft Ægypt, possessed by Mesraim Cham's second son. Gen. \+it 10. v. 13.\+it*\f* of Cham. \f 1 \fr 104.23 \ft Gen 46.\f*
\v 24 And he increased his people exceedingly: and strengthened them over their enemies. \x - \xt Exo. 1. v. 7.\x*
\v 25 He \f i \fr 104.25 \ft May it be understood, or believed (saith S. Augustin) that God turneth the heart of man to commit sins? Or is it no sin, or is it a small sin, to hate the people of God? Or to work guile towards his servants? Who will say this? What then, is God author of these so grievous sins, who is not to be supposed the author of a most small sin? This learned Father therefore answereth, that God perverted not a right heart, but turned that was of it-self perverse, to the hatred of his people, where he might use that evil well, not by making them evil, but by bestowing upon his own people good things, which the evil might easily envy. Which hatred & to the glory of his own name, the things that follow do teach us; which are here remembered to his praise.\f* turned their heart that they hated his people: and to work guile toward his servants.
\v 26 He sent Moyses his servant: Aaron \f k \fr 104.26 \ft In whom God established the Priesthood of Moyses' law.\f* himself whom he chose. \x - \xt Exo. 3. 4. 7. 8. 9. 10. 12.\x*
\v 27 He did put in them the words of his signs, and of his wonders in the Land of Cham.
\v 28 He sent \f l \fr 104.28 \ft The ninth plague of the Ægyptians.\f* darkness, and obscured: and did \f m \fr 104.28 \ft God willingly, not as one loath or unwilling, performed all that he threatened.\f* not exasperate his words.
\v 29 He turned their \f n \fr 104.29 \ft The first plague.\f* waters into blood: and killed their fishes.
\v 30 Their land brought forth \f o \fr 104.30 \ft The second plague.\f* frogs in \f 1 \fr 104.30 \ft David knew this by revelation, or by tradition, for it is not in Exodus.\f* the inner chambers of their kings.
\v 31 He said, and \f p \fr 104.31 \ft The fourth plague.\f* the \f 1 \fr 104.31 \ft \+it a swarm of flies.\+it*\f* cænomyia came: and the \f q \fr 104.31 \ft The third plague.\f* sciniphs in all their coasts.
\v 32 He made their rains \f r \fr 104.32 \ft The seventh plague.\f* hail: fire burning in their land.
\v 33 And he struck their vines, and their fig-trees: and he destroyed the wood of their coasts.
\v 34 He said, & \f s \fr 104.34 \ft The eighth plague.\f* the locust came, & the \f t \fr 104.34 \ft Aworm that spoileth corn,\f* bruchus whereof there was no number.
\v 35 And it did eat all the grass in their land: and it did eat all the fruit of their land.
\v 36 And he struck every \f v \fr 104.36 \ft The tenth plague. The fifth & sixth of pestilence and boyles are omitted.\f* first begotten in their land: the first fruits of all their labour.
\v 37 And he brought them forth with gold and silver, and there was not in their tribes a feeble person.
\v 38 Ægypt was glad at their departure: because the fear of them lay upon them.
\v 39 He spread a cloud for their protection, and fire to shine unto them by night. \x - \xt Exod. 13. v. 21. Exod. 16. v. 13. Exod. 17. v. 6. Gen. 12.\x*
\v 40 They made petition, and the quail came: and he filled them with the bread of heaven.
\v 41 He divided the rock, and waters flowed: rivers ran in the dry ground.
\v 42 Because he was mindful of his holy word, which he had uttered to Abraham his servant.
\v 43 And he brought forth his people in exultation, and his elect in joy.
\v 44 And he gave them the countries of the Nations: and they possessed the labours of peoples: \x - \xt Iosue 6. & seq.\x*
\v 45 That they might keep his justifications, and seek after his law.
\c 105
\cd The Prophet exhorteth the people to render thanks and praise to God, 6. for remitting their manifold sins, in the desert. 34. and in the conquered land: 38. foreshewing like sins to come, God's wrath and punishment for the same. 44. & that he will give grace of repentance to some 47. for which he prayeth, and praiseth God. \f - \ft The Israelites often sinned, and were mercifully punished. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Confess ye to our Lord \f a \fr 105.1 \ft God is of himself and essentially good. All other goodness is participated of him.\f* because he is good: because his mercy is for ever. \f 1 \fr 105.1 \ft Judith 13. v. 21.\f*
\v 2 \f b \fr 105.2 \ft No creature can fully express God's perfections.\f* Who shalt speak the powers of our Lord, shall make all his praises to be heard?
\v 3 \f c \fr 105.3 \ft It is a happy state in this life, either to keep God's law:\f* Blessed are they, that keep judgment, & \f d \fr 105.3 \ft or to repent and do worthy penance for transgressing.\f* do justice at all time.
\v 4 Remember us, o Lord \f e \fr 105.4 \ft According to thy wonted benevolence, promised to thy people. \+it Exod. 16. v. 13. Exod. 17. v. 6. Gen. 12.\+it*\f* in the good pleasure of thy people: visit us in thy salvation:
\v 5 To \f f \fr 105.5 \ft That we may see and enjoy the benefits promised to thine elect.\f* see in the goodness of thine elect, to rejoice in the joy of thy nation: that thou mayst be praised with thine inheritance.
\v 6 We have \f g \fr 105.6 \ft We have erred and hurt ourselves:\f* sinned with our fathers: we have \f h \fr 105.6 \ft we have wronged our neighbours:\f* dealt unjustly, we have \f i \fr 105.6 \ft we have offended against God.\f* done iniquity.
\v 7 Our fathers in Ægypt did not understand thy marvelous works: they were not mindful of the multitude of thy mercy. And they provoked thee to wrath going up unto the sea, the Red sea.
\v 8 And he saved them \f k \fr 105.8 \ft Albeit the people by their murmuring deserved more punishment, yet God for the glory of his own name saved them from utter destruction.\f* for his name sake; that he might make his power known. \x - \xt Exod. 14. v. 11.\x*
\v 9 And he rebuked the Red sea, and it was made dry: and he led them in the depths as in a desert.
\v 10 And he saved them from the hand of them that hated them: and he redeemed them out of the hand of the enemy. \ef - \fr 105.10 \fq He redeemed them. \ft What price (or ransom) saith S. Augustine, was given in this redemption? Or is it a prophecy, that this was done in figure of Baptism ¹, where we are redeemed from the hand of the devil by a great price, which is the blood of Christ? Whereupon it was more conveniently figured, not by what sea soever, but by the red sea: For blood hath red colour. And touching the effect of Baptism destroying all former sins, he teacheth in the exposition of the next Psalm (and either the same holy father, or some other good author, Ser. 42. de temp.) that as the Israelites passed safely through the red sea, and all the Ægyptians going in with them were drowned: so the baptised are saved in the water of Baptism, and all their sins are destroyed. ² \fq ¹ \ft The red sea a figure of Baptism. \fq ² \ft All former sins destroyed in Baptism.\ef*
\v 11 And water overwhelmed those that afflicted them: there did not one of them remain.
\v 12 And they believed his words: and they sang his praises.
\v 13 They had \f l \fr 105.13 \ft They persevered not long in their duty towards God, seeing his omnipotent power by his marvelous works:\f* quickly done, they forgot his works: and they \f m \fr 105.13 \ft nor were content with his providence, but carnally coveted things not necessary.\f* expected not his counsel.
\v 14 And they coveted concupiscence in the desert: and tempted God in the place without water. \x - \xt Exo. 16. & 17.\x*
\v 15 He gave them their petition: and sent saturity into their \f n \fr 105.15 \ft According to their carnal desires.\f* souls. \x - \xt Nu. 10.\x*
\v 16 And they provoked Moyses in the camp: Aaron the \f o \fr 105.16 \ft Holy by his function.\f* holy of our Lord.
\v 17 The earth was opened, and swallowed Dathan, and overwhelmed the congregation of Abiron.
\v 18 And a fire flamed up in their synagogue: the flame burnt the sinners.
\v 19 And they made a calf in Horeb: and they adored \f p \fr 105.19 \ft They adored the image that represented a calf, not God.\f* the sculptile. \x - \xt Exo. 23.\x*
\v 20 And they \f q \fr 105.20 \ft God being their true glory, they changed him for a false god of the Ægyptians (who especially honoured a calf called Apis) making an image thereof, and attributed their delivery from Ægypt to this imagined god. Exo. \+it 32. v. 4. 8.\+it* Of which and the like foolish and abominable idolatry S. Paul writeth, Rom. \+it 1. v. 23.\+it* They changed the glory of the incorruptible God into a similitude of the image of a corruptible man, and of fowls, and of four footed beasts, and of them that creep. Where we see what manner of images holy Scriptures condemn, and not the images of Christ and his Saints.\f* changed their glory into the similitude of a calf that eateth grass.
\v 21 They forgat God, which saved them, which did great things in Ægypt,
\v 22 marvelous things in the land of Cham, terrible things in the Red sea.
\v 23 And \f r \fr 105.23 \ft He said, he would destroy them, but for Moyses' prayer spared them.\f* he said to destroy them: if Moyses his elect had not stood in the way before him \x - \xt Exo. 32.\x*. To turn away his wrath that he should not destroy them:
\v 24 and they esteemed for naught the land that was to be desired. \x - \xt Nu. 14.\x* They did not believe his word.
\v 25 and they murmured in their tabernacles: they heard not the voice of our Lord.
\v 26 And he lifted up his hand over them: to overthrow them in the desert: \x - \xt Num. 14. v. 21. 22.\x*
\v 27 And to cast down their seed among the Nations: & to disperse them in the countries.
\v 28 And they were professed to \f s \fr 105.28 \ft The Idol of Moabites, and Madianites.\f* Beelphegor: and they did eat the sacrifices \f t \fr 105.28 \ft As God is indeed the living God, that liveth of himself, and giveth life to others: so false gods are called dead gods, that cannot give life to any, but do kill all that serve them; at least spiritually, and often corporally.\f* of the dead. \x - \xt Num. 25. v. 3.\x*
\v 29 And they provoked him in their inventions: & ruin was multiplied on them.
\v 30 And Phinees stood, and \f v \fr 105.30 \ft Phinees moved by the zeal of God (as the holy text witnesseth, Num. \+it 25. v. 11.\+it* )\f* pacified: and the slaughter ceased.
\v 31 And it was reputed to him unto justice, in generation and generation even for ever.
\v 32 And they provoked him at the waters of contradiction: and \f w \fr 105.32 \ft Moyses afflicted in spirit, by the enormous murmuring of the people, doubted whether God would give them water out of the rock or no: not doubting of his power, but of his will: and so when he should have spoken to the rock, Num. \+it 20. v. 8.\+it* he spoke to the incredulous people. \+it v. 10.\+it* and therein offended God: for which he was temporally punished. \+it v. 12.\+it* Deut. \+it 1. v. 37.\+it* &c. \+it c. 3. v. 26. c. 4. v. 21.\+it*\f* Moyses was vexed for them: \x - \xt Nu. 10. v. 2. 12.\x*
\v 33 because they exasperated his spirit. And he plainly affirmed in his lips:
\v 34 they destroyed not the nations, of which our Lord spake to them.
\v 35 And they were mingled among the nations, and learned their works: \x - \xt Deut. 2. v. 2. & 12. v. 2. & 3. Iudic. 2. v. 11. 12. Iudic. 3. v. 5. 6. &c. Iere. 19. v. 5.\x*
\v 36 and they served their sculptiles: and it became a scandal to them.
\v 37 And they immolated their sons, and their daughters to devils.
\v 38 And \f x \fr 105.38 \ft Some Jews offered these most cruel, unnatural, and abominable sacrifices; perhaps in the times of Judges, when they were mingled with idolatrous people, and served their gods. Iud. \+it 2. v. 12. c. 3. v. 6.\+it* But it is more express after David's time, whereof he here prophesieth, and was verified by Achaz. \+it 4. Reg. 16. v. 3.\+it* and by Manasses. \+it 4. Reg. 21. v. 6.\+it* Which with other idolatry King Iosias destroyed. \+it 4. Reg. 23. v. 1.\+it*\f* they shed innocent blood: the blood of their sons and of their daughters, which they sacrificed to the sculptiles of Chanaan. And the land was infected with blood,
\v 39 & was contaminated in their works: and they did fornicate in their inventions.
\v 40 And our Lord was wrath with fury upon his people: and he abhorred his inheritance.
\v 41 And he delivered them into the hands of the nations: and they that hated them had the dominion of them.
\v 42 And their enemies afflicted them, and they were humbled under their hands:
\v 43 he did often deliver them. But they exasperated him in their counsel: and they were humbled in their iniquities.
\v 44 And he \f y \fr 105.44 \ft God respected them with his merciful eye, and gave them grace to repent.\f* saw when they were afflicted: and he heard their prayer.
\v 45 And he was mindful of his testament: and it repented him according to the multitude of his mercy.
\v 46 And he gave them into mercies in the sight of all, that had taken them. \f z \fr 105.46 \ft Here the Psalmist concludeth both the history and prophecy of this Psalm, with prayer and praise, as followeth.\f*
\v 47 \f 1 \fr 105.47 \ft Avery fit prayer in time of schism.\f* Save us, o Lord our God: and gather us out of the Nations. That we may confess to thy holy name: & may glory in thy praise.
\v 48 Blessed be our Lord the God of Israel from everlasting unto everlasting: and all the people shall say: Be it, be it.
\c 106
\cd Again the Psalmist inviteth all men to render thanks to God for their delivery from dangers, or evils in general: 4. particularly from dangers in journey, 10. in prison or captivity, 17. in sickness spiritual and corporal, 23. in navigation, 33. describing the changeable course of things in this world, 38. especially of men's states; 42. for all which the just will praise God. \f - \ft God's perpetual providence towards all men.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Confess \f a \fr 106.1 \ft Praise God by confessing his mercy, providence, and goodness.\f* ye to our Lord, because he is good: because his mercy is for ever. \x - \xt 1. Par. 16. v. 34.\x*
\v 2 Let them say that are \f b \fr 106.2 \ft God of his mercy promised the Redeemer of mankind straight after Adam's fall:\f* redeemed of our Lord, whom he redeemed out of the hand of the enemy: and out of the countries he gathered them:
\v 3 From \f c \fr 106.3 \ft which redemption was intended for all, & faileth not of God's part in any, but of men's own willful refusing to be duly penitent, and to keep God's precepts.\f* the rising of the sun, and the going down: from the north, and the sea.
\v 4 They \f d \fr 106.4 \ft Literally of such as wander in this world, having no settled place to dwell in: spiritually of all mankind after his fall.\f* wandered in the wilderness, in a place without water: the way of city for habitation they found not.
\v 5 Hungry and thirsty: their soul fainted in them.
\v 6 And \f e \fr 106.6 \ft Whensoever they call upon God, he helpeth them, as is best for their spiritual health.\f* they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation: and he delivered them out of their necessities.
\v 7 And he conducted them into the right way: to go into a city of habitation.
\v 8 Let \f f \fr 106.8 \ft All God's benefits, which are of his mercy, not of man's desert, are just matter of praising God.\f* the mercies of our Lord confess to him: and his marvelous works to the children of men.
\v 9 Because he hath filled the empty soul: and the hungry soul he hath filled with good things.
\v 10 Them that sat in darkness, and in the shadow of death: bound in neediness, and iron.
\v 11 Because they \f g \fr 106.11 \ft Calamities in this world are commonly inflicted for sins.\f* exasperated the words of God: and they provoked the counsel of the Highest.
\v 12 And their heart was humbled in labours: they were weakened, neither was there any to help.
\v 13 And they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation: and he delivered them out of their necessities.
\v 14 And he brought them out of darkness, and the shadow of death: and brake their bonds asunder.
\v 15 Let the mercies of our Lord confess to him: & his marvelous works to the children of men.
\v 16 Because he hath destroyed the gates of brass; and the bars of iron he hath broken.
\v 17 He hath received them out of the way of their iniquity: for they were humbled for their injustices.
\v 18 Their soul did abhor all meat: & they approached even to the gates of death.
\v 19 And they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation: and he delivered them out of their necessities.
\v 20 He sent his word, and healed them: and delivered them out of their destructions.
\v 21 Let the mercies of our Lord confess to him: & his marvelous works to the children of men.
\v 22 And let them sacrifice the sacrifice of praise: & shew forth his works in exultation.
\v 23 They that go down into the sea in ships, making traffic in the great waters.
\v 24 They have seen the works of our Lord, and his marvelous things in the depth.
\v 25 He said, and the blast of the storm stood: and the waves thereof were exalted.
\v 26 They ascend even to the heavens, and they descend even to the depths: their soul pined away in evils.
\v 27 They were troubled, and were moved as a drunken man: and all their wisdom was devoured.
\v 28 And \f h \fr 106.28 \ft As before in the \+it 6. 13.\+it* and \+it 19.\+it* verses.\f* they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation, and he brought them out of their necessities.
\v 29 And he turned his storm into calm: & the waves thereof were quiet.
\v 30 And they rejoiced because they were quiet: and he conducted them into the haven of their will.
\v 31 Let \f i \fr 106.31 \ft This verse also is four times in this Psalm. \+it v. 8. 15. 21.\+it* and \+it 31.\+it* to admonish us, that as there is one means to escape from all dangers by crying to God, as \+it v. 6. 13. 19.\+it* and \+it 28.\+it* with mourning and penance: so there is one cause of praise and thanks for our delivery, which is God's mercy and grace.\f* the mercies of our Lord confess to him: and his marvelous works to the children of men.
\v 32 And let them exalt him in the Church of the people: and the chair of the ancients let them praise him.
\v 33 \f k \fr 106.33 \ft God to shew sometimes his power, also to benefit some, and to punish others, changeth the accustomed course of things, and states of men, at his divine pleasure, as here the Royal Prophet reciteth some examples. And some others are recorded in diverse times and places.\f* He turned \f l \fr 106.33 \ft No doubt much change was made in the earth by Noe's flood. And many think that the land of Chanaan was made more fruitful in the time of the Jews' inhabiting, and now is more barren again.\f* the rivers into a desert: and the issues of waters into dryness.
\v 34 The \f m \fr 106.34 \ft He alludeth to the country about Sodome and Gomorre, which was most fruitful, and most pleasant, Gen. \+it 13. v. 10.\+it* but shortly after was burnt with fire and brimstone, Gen. \+it 19. v. 14.\+it* subverted, and turned into a dead and salt sea.\f* fruitful land into a salt ground, for the malice of them that inhabit it.
\v 35 He turned the desert into pools of waters: and the land without water into issues of waters.
\v 36 And he placed the hungry there, and they built a city of habitation.
\v 37 And they sowed fields and planted vineyards: and they made fruit \f n \fr 106.37 \ft Made aboundance of fruit to grow.\f* of nativity.
\v 38 And he blessed them, and they were multiplied exceedingly: and their beasts he lessened not.
\v 39 And \f o \fr 106.39 \ft Again some countries punished for sins.\f* they were made few: and were vexed by the tribulation of evils, and with sorrow.
\v 40 Contempt was poured out upon Princes: and he made them wander where was no way, and not in the way.
\v 41 And he \f p \fr 106.41 \ft Another change in relieving the poor being humbled.\f* did help the poor out of poverty: and made families as sheep.
\v 42 The just shall see, and shall rejoice: & all iniquity shall stop her mouth.
\v 43 Who is wise and will keep these things? And will understand the mercies of our Lord?
\c 107
\cd The royal Prophet promiseth, 5. and rendereth praises to God, 7. for his delivery from troubles, and advancement in the kingdom, 13. praying God still to help man's infirmity. \f - \ft David singeth praises for benefits received. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A canticle \f a \fr 107.1 \ft This Psalm was sung with instruments beginning the music, and voices following.\f* of Psalm, to David himself.
\v 2 My heart is ready, o God, my heart is ready \x - \xt Psa. 56.\x* : I will chant and will sing in my glory. \f 2 \fr 107.2 \ft The former part of this Psalm to the 7. verse, is the same in sense, and almost in words, with the later part of the 56. from the 8. verse.\f*
\v 3 Arise my glory, arise psalter and harp: I will arise early.
\v 4 I \f b \fr 107.4 \ft King David subdued not only some parts of Chanaan, not subject to the Jews before \+it (2. Reg. 5. 1. Par. 11.)\+it* but also brought the Philistiims, Moabites, Ammonites, Idumeans, Amalechites, the Kings of Soba, Syria, & Emath, to pay tribute. \+it 2. Reg. 8. 1. Par. 18.\+it*\f* will confess to thee in peoples, o Lord: and I will sing to thee \f c \fr 107.4 \ft Yet all these victories & conquests were but a figure of Christ's power and dominion in all nations. And therefore the rest of this Psalm, by S. Augustin & other fathers' judgement, was rather prophetically uttered by David, in the person of Christ, & more perfectly performed by Christ in his Church, than historically averred of David himself.\f* in the Nations.
\v 5 Because thy mercy is great above the heavens: & thy truth even to the clouds.
\v 6 Be exalted above the heavens o God, and thy glory over all the earth: \x - \xt Psa. 59. v. 7.\x*
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 107.7 \ft The rest of this Psalm is the same with the later part of the 59. from the 7. verse.\f* that thy beloved may be delivered. Save with thy right hand, and hear me:
\v 8 God spake in his holy: I will rejoice, and will divide Sichem, and I will measure the vale of tabernacles.
\v 9 Galaad is mine, and Manasses is mine: and Ephraim the protection of my head. Juda is my king:
\v 10 Moab the pot of my hope. Upon Idumea I will extend my shoe: the strangers are made my friends.
\v 11 Who will conduct me into a fenced city? Who will conduct me into Idumea?
\v 12 Wilt not thou, o God, which hast repelled us, and wilt not thou go forth, o God, in our hosts?
\v 13 Give us help out of tribulation: because man's salvation is vain.
\v 14 In God we shall do strength: and he will bring our enemies to nothing.
\c 108
\cd Christ (by the mouth of David) requesteth of God to be justly declared innocent, and his enemies punished, 6. particularly describing Judas the traitor's malice, 21. and his own temporal afflictions: 26. prayeth, 30. and praiseth God for his delivery. \f - \ft Christ persecuted & his enemies punished. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, a Psalm of David.
\v 2 O \f a \fr 108.2 \ft The words of Christ.\f* God conceal not my praise: because the mouth of the sinner, and the mouth of \f b \fr 108.2 \ft The Pharisees and Herodians \+it (Mat. 22.)\+it* with their mouth acknowledged Christ a true speaker, & a teacher of the way of God in truth, thereby to draw him into danger, and to shed his blood.\f* the deceitful man is open upon me.
\v 3 They \f c \fr 108.3 \ft At other times they accused him of great crimes, lastly of treason against Cæsar.\f* have spoken against me with deceitful tongue, and with words of hatred they have compassed me: and they have impugned me without cause.
\v 4 For that they should love me, they backbited me: but I prayed.
\v 5 And they set against me evil things for good: and hatred for my love.
\v 6 Appoint \f d \fr 108.6 \ft A prediction that Judas would not make recourse to any good counseller, but complain of his miserable tormented conscience to the wicked, who gave him no comfort at all,\f* a sinner over him: and \f e \fr 108.6 \ft and so despairing, the devil persuaded him to hang himself.\f* let the devil stand on his right hand.
\v 7 When he is judged, let him come forth condemned: and let his prayer be turned into sin.
\v 8 Let his days be made few: and let another take his \f f \fr 108.8 \ft The office of Apostleship.\f* bishoprick. \x - \xt Act. 1. v. 16.\x*
\v 9 Let \f g \fr 108.9 \ft The posterity or successors of wicked persecuters prosper not long in this world.\f* his children be made orphans: and his wife a widow.
\v 10 Let his children be transported wandering, and let them beg: and let them be cast out of their habitations.
\v 11 Let the usurer search all his substance: and let strangers spoil his labours.
\v 12 Let there be none to help him: neither let there be any to have pity on his pupils.
\v 13 Let his children come to destruction: in \f h \fr 108.13 \ft Arch-heretics that devise new opinions, are shortly forsaken, their followers still coining new heresies of their own, differing from their false master.\f* one generation let his name be clean put out.
\v 14 Let the iniquity of his fathers return to memory in the sight of our Lord: and let not the sin of his mother be blotted out.
\v 15 Let them be before our Lord always, and let the memory of them perish out of the earth:
\v 16 For that he remembered not to do mercy.
\v 17 And he persecuted the poor, and needy man, and the compunct in heart to kill him.
\v 18 And he \f i \fr 108.18 \ft Let them observe this, that use more swearing and blaspheming, than praying or meditating.\f* loved cursing, and it shall come to him: and he would not blessing, and it shall be far from him. And he put on cursing as a garment, and it entered as water into his inner parts, and as oil in his bones.
\v 19 Be it to him as a garment, wherewith he is covered: and as a girdle, wherewith he is always girded.
\v 20 This is the work of them that detract from me before our Lord: and that speak evils against my soul.
\v 21 And thou Lord, Lord, do with me for thy name's sake: because thy mercy is sweet. Deliver me
\v 22 because I am needy and poor: and \f k \fr 108.22 \ft Christ's soul was pensive when he prayed in the garden, and he did works of penance for our sins all his life.\f* my heart is troubled within me.
\v 23 As a shadow when it declineth, am I taken away: and I am shaken as locusts.
\v 24 My knees are weakened with fasting: and my flesh is changed by reason of oil.
\v 25 And I am made a reproach to them: they saw me, and wagged their heads.
\v 26 Help me, o Lord my God: \f l \fr 108.26 \ft He prayed also for his resurrection, and glorification.\f* save me according to thy mercy.
\v 27 And let them know that this is thy hand: & thou, o Lord, hast done it.
\v 28 They will curse, and thou shalt bless: let them that rise up against me be confounded: but thy servant shall rejoice.
\v 29 Let them that detract from me, be clothed with shame: and let them be covered with their confusion, as with a \f m \fr 108.29 \ft With all possible confusion.\f* double patched cloak.
\v 30 I will confess to our Lord exceedingly with my mouth: and in the midst of many I will praise him.
\v 31 Because he hath stood on the right hand of the poor, that he might save my soul from the persecutors.
\c 109
\cd Christ rising and ascending into heaven sitteth on the right hand of God: 2. beginning in Jerusalem reigneth in the Church of the whole earth: 4. useth the Priesthood of Melchisedech's order to the end of the world. 6. And shall judge the world. \f - \ft Christ's exaltation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm of David. Our \f a \fr 109.1 \ft God the Father.\f* Lord said \f b \fr 109.1 \ft To God the Son, the Lord of David, and of all mankind, yet the son of David, according to his humanity.\f* to my Lord: Sit on my right hand: \f c \fr 109.1 \ft He limiteth not the time, but excludeth all time, wherein the enemy might imagine, that Christ's kingdom should cease: signifying that Christ shall reign, till all his enemies be subdued, much more afterwards in all eternity.\f* till I make thine enemies, the foot-stool of thy feet. \x - \xt Matt. 22. Act. 2. 1. Cor. 15. Heb. 1. & 10.\x*
\v 2 Our Lord will send forth the rod of thy strength \f d \fr 109.2 \ft The Church of Christ beginning in Jerusalem on Whitsunday, the fiftieth day from his Resurrection, continueth evermore.\f* from Sion: rule thou in the midst of thine enemies.
\v 3 With thee \f e \fr 109.3 \ft Thou shall have principality,\f* the beginning in the day \f f \fr 109.3 \ft in the day of thy powerful conquest, and rising from death.\f* of thy strength: in \f g \fr 109.3 \ft In excellency of holy spiritual mysteries and graces:\f* the brightness of holy things: from the womb \f h \fr 109.3 \ft because I God the Father, of my substance begat thee God the Son in eternity. The same which Micheas saith (c. 5. v. 2.) His coming forth from the beginning, from the days of eternity.\f* before the day star I begat thee.
\v 4 Our Lord \f i \fr 109.4 \ft God most firmly, and unchangeably affirmed: that thou (Christ our Messias) art not only a King, but also a Priest:\f* sware, and it shall not repent him: Thou art a Priest \f k \fr 109.4 \ft not for a time, as Aaron was, but for ever,\f* for ever according to \f l \fr 109.4 \ft neither of Aaron's order, but according to the order of Melchisedech.\f* the order of Melchisedech. \x - \xt Heb. 5. v. 7.\x* \ef - \fr 109.4 \fq A Priest for ever. \ft In two respects Christ is a Priest for ever: ¹ in that from the first instant of his Incarnation he was, and remaineth a Priest, now also in heaven: and all other Priests are his ministerial Vicars, not successors. So that all Priestly functions, which they do, he by them doth the same, as the principal Priest. Whereupon saith S. Paul \+it (1. Cor. 4.)\+it* So let a man think of us as of the ministers of Christ, and dispensers of the mysteries of God. Secondly, Christ daily offering Sacrifice by the hands of his Priests, doth continually pacify God's wrath, in behalf of those sinners, for whom it is duly applied, even to the end of the world. Whereas the Priesthood of Aaron, and of all others in the old Testament, ceased by their deaths, both in the office, and in the effect. \fq ¹ \ft Christ's Priesthood for ever both in function and in effect.\ef* \ef - \fr 109.4 \fq According to the order of Melchisedech. \ft ¹ As Melchisedech, King of peace and justice, without father, mother, or genealogy expressed in holy Scriptures, or otherwise known to the world, was Priest of the Highest, offered bread & wine, an unbloody sacrifice, communicating with both Chanaaites and Hebrews, blessed Abraham, and took tithes of him & his subjects: so Christ the true King of peace & justice, without father of his humanity, without mother of his Divinity, the Son of God, of ineffable genealogy, born of a Virgin in his humanity, the Priest of God, offereth Sacrifice (not only bloody on the Cross, but also) unbloody in the forms of bread and wine, continueth the same by the ministry of other Priests, maketh all nations partakers thereof, blesseth them and receiveth of them all dutiful & religious service, as of his subjects. \fq ¹ \ft The resemblance of Christ's and Melchisedech's Priesthood.\ef*
\v 5 Our Lord on thy right hand \f m \fr 109.5 \ft Kings that sometimes persecute Christians, are subdued with other people to Christ.\f* hath broken kings in the day of his wrath.
\v 6 He \f n \fr 109.6 \ft He shall judge and punish the incredulous people,\f* shall judge in nations, he \f o \fr 109.6 \ft make great slaughters amongst those that resist:\f* shall fill ruins, he \f p \fr 109.6 \ft and bring Princes with their populous kingdoms to nothing.\f* shall crush the heads in the land of many.
\v 7 \f q \fr 109.7 \ft He shall in the mean time (and also his best servants) suffer much tribulation in this life\f* Of the torrent in the way he shall drink: \f r \fr 109.7 \ft and for the same be highly exalted in life everlasting.\f* therefore shall he exalt the head.
\c 110
\cd Praise of God for benefits, 4. especially for the B. Sacrament of the Eucharist, 6. with other graces imparted to the Catholic Church. \f - \ft Graces given to the Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. I will confess to thee, o Lord, with all my heart: in \f a \fr 110.1 \ft Iwill praise God both in secret for discharge of mine own conscience;\f* the counsel of the just, and \f b \fr 110.1 \ft and in public for edification of others.\f* the congregation. \f 1 \fr 110.1 \ft This Psalm in the Hebrew is composed with every verse and middle of verse, beginning with a distinct letter, in order of the Alphabet.\f*
\v 2 The works of our Lord are great: exquisite according \f c \fr 110.2 \ft God's will is the whole cause of all his works.\f* to all his wills.
\v 3 \f d \fr 110.3 \ft Every work of his is praise-worthy and magnifical.\f* Confession and magnificence his work: and his justice continueth for ever and ever.
\v 4 He hath made \f e \fr 110.4 \ft God hath left one most special and beneficial memory of all other benefits, his own body and blood, in memory of his Passion, and our redemption,\f* a memory of his marvelous works; a merciful & pitiful Lord:
\v 5 he hath given \f f \fr 110.5 \ft the spiritual food and sustenance of all the souls that rightly fear him.\f* meat to them that fear him. He will be mindful for ever \f g \fr 110.5 \ft Of his promise to conserve his Church perpetually.\f* of his testament:
\v 6 \f h \fr 110.6 \ft The powerable operation of his death, and of all his mysteries.\f* the force of his works he will shew forth to his people:
\v 7 To give them the inheritance of the gentiles: the works of his hands truth and judgment.
\v 8 All his commandments \f i \fr 110.8 \ft God's commandments do justify all that keep them.\f* are faithful: confirmed for ever and ever, made in truth and equity.
\v 9 He sent \f k \fr 110.9 \ft He also of his mercy redeemed man, that he might be able to keep his precepts.\f* redemption to his people: he commanded his testament for ever. Holy and terrible is his name:
\v 10 \f l \fr 110.10 \ft Beginning with fear of God, bringeth at last by other degrees to true wisdom, which two are the first and last of the seven gifts of the Holy Ghost.\f* the fear of our Lord is the beginning of wisdom. Understanding is good to all that do it: his praise remaineth for ever and ever.
\c 111
\cd God is to be praised, who being high, regardeth and provideth for the needy in this world. \f - \ft God's providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. \f a \fr 111.1 \ft The Septuagint Interpreters added this mention of Aggeus and Zacharie, returning from captivity, to signify that this Psalm was very proper, & meet to be commended to the people at that time, whereby they might learn, that their sins were the cause of their captivity, and of all their miseries: and if they desired temporal or spiritual prosperity, they must observe the means here prescribed to obtain the same.\f* Of the return of Aggeus and Zachary. \x - \xt Psal. 1.\x* Blessed is the man that feareth our Lord: \f b \fr 111.1 \ft He that sincerely feareth God, will take great delight in keeping his commandments.\f* he shall have great delight in his commandments. \f 2 \fr 111.1 \ft This Psalm is also composed by the Alphabet, as the next before.\f*
\v 2 \f c \fr 111.2 \ft So doing he and his shall prosper.\f* His seed shall be mighty in the earth: the generation of the righteous shall be blessed.
\v 3 Glory and riches in his house: and \f d \fr 111.3 \ft The just shall not only prosper in this world but also in the next.\f* his justice abideth for ever and ever.
\v 4 \f e \fr 111.4 \ft God will also comfort the just in tribulations.\f* Light is risen up in darkness to the righteous: he is merciful, and pitiful, and just.
\v 5 Acceptable is the man, that is merciful and lendeth, that \f f \fr 111.5 \ft That shall give discreet and wholesome counsel to the afflicted.\f* shall dispose his words in judgment:
\v 6 Because he shall not be moved for ever.
\v 7 The just shall be in eternal memory: he shall not fear at the hearing of evil. His heart is ready to hope in our Lord,
\v 8 his heart is confirmed: he shall not be moved till he look over his enemies.
\v 9 He distributed, he gave to the poor: \f g \fr 111.9 \ft Works of mercy are also called justice, because they concur to man's justification,\f* his justice remaineth for ever and ever, \f h \fr 111.9 \ft and to his salvation.\f* his horn shall be exalted in glory. \x - \xt 2. Cor. 9.\x*
\v 10 The sinner shall see, and will be angry, he shall gnash his teeth and pine away: the desire of sinners shall perish.
\c 112
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Praise our Lord ye \f a \fr 112.1 \ft All God's servants.\f* children: praise ye the name of our Lord.
\v 2 Be the name of our Lord blessed, from henceforth now and for ever,
\v 3 From the rising of the sun unto the going down, the name of our Lord is laudable.
\v 4 Our Lord is high above all nations, and his glory above the heavens.
\v 5 Who is as the Lord our God, that dwelleth on high,
\v 6 and beholdeth the low things \f b \fr 112.6 \ft In respect of God all creatures are low, though they be in heaven.\f* in heaven and in earth?
\v 7 Raising up the needy from the earth, and lifting up the poor out of the dung:
\v 8 \f c \fr 112.8 \ft See the example of Ioseph so advanced.\f* To place him with Princes, with the Princes of his people.
\v 9 Who maketh the \f d \fr 112.9 \ft of Sara, Rebecca, Rachæl, and other women made fruitful.\f* barren woman to dwell in a house, a joyful mother of children.
\c 113
\cd For the marvelous passage of Israel out of Ægypt, 3. the red sea, the river of Jordan, 7. and the hills giving them place, 8. the rocks yielding them water, 9. God, not themselves, is to be praised. 12. Idols and Idolaters are vain, and shall be confounded. 17. The faithful trust in God, 20. are blessed, and for ever praise God. \f - \ft The marvelous passage of Israel from Ægypt. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. In the coming forth of Israel out of Ægypt: of the house of Jacob from \f a \fr 113.1 \ft People of false religion counted barbarous, especially such as also persecute the true Religion; for otherwise the Ægyptians were both civil in manners, and learned in many sciences.\f* the barbarous people.
\v 2 \f b \fr 113.2 \ft The people of Jews were more notoriously renowned in the world from the time of their delivery out of Ægypt, for the peculiar people, whom God sanctified, & in whom, as in his elected inheritance or dominion, he dwelled and reigned.\f* Jewry was made his sanctification, Israel his dominion.
\v 3 The \f c \fr 113.3 \ft The Psalmist writing in verse doth often describe things in poetical manner,\f* sea saw, and \f d \fr 113.3 \ft when the Israelites went forth of Ægypt,\f* fled: Jordan \f e \fr 113.3 \ft when they entered into Chanaan, but more truly than profane poets: for that in very deed all creatures, otherwise senseless, as the sea, do in a sort feel the power of their Creator, & obey his will.\f* was turned backward. \x - \xt Exo. 14. Iosue 3.\x*
\v 4 \f f \fr 113.4 \ft Either there was an earthquake, or some other moving of hills, not mentioned by Moyses, or else the Psalmist speaketh of the rocks of the torrents, which bowed, that the Israelites might rest in Ar, and lie in the borders of the Moabites, Num. \+it 21. v. 15.\+it*\f* The mountains leaped as rams: and the little hills as the lambs of sheep.
\v 5 \f g \fr 113.5 \ft By the figure Apostrophe he speaketh to the sea, river, and hills: using also Prosopopoeia, as if senseless things understood, and should answer.\f* What aileth thee o sea that thou didst fly: and thou o Jordan, that thou was turned backward?
\v 6 Ye mountains leaped as rams, and ye little hills as the lambs of sheep.
\v 7 At the face of our Lord was the earth moved, at the face of the God of Jacob.
\v 8 Who turned \f h \fr 113.8 \ft Another miraculous benefit, that rock yielded them water in their necessity.\f* the rock into pools of waters, and stony hill into fountains of waters.
\v 9 \f 1 \fr 113.9 \ft Here some Hebrew Rabbins begin another Psalm, but by the coherence of the matter Saint Augustine proveth that it is but one Psalm, where is shewed that the true invisible God is known by such works as are here recited: and contrarywise, that the gentiles' idols are not Gods, because they are made of silver, gold, or other matter, by men's hands; having resemblance of living things are altogether senseless.\f* \sc Not to us O Lord, Not\sc* TO US: but to thy name give the glory.
\v 10 For \f i \fr 113.10 \ft Thou didst all this, o God, of mere mercy towards thy people:\f* thy mercy, and \f k \fr 113.10 \ft for thy truth's sake, seeing thou didst promise to protect them:\f* thy truth: lest at any time \f l \fr 113.10 \ft that the Gentiles should not take occasion to blaspheme.\f* the Gentiles say: Where is their God?
\v 11 But our God is in heaven: he hath done all things what soever he would.
\v 12 The idols of the gentiles are silver, and gold, the works of men's hands. \ef - \fr 113.12 \fq The Idols of the Gentiles are silver and gold. \ft All Catholic Divines agree in this authentical definition of Idolatry, ¹ that is divine honour given to any creature, as to God. Of the diverse sorts also of Idolatry the ancient learned Doctors have written much. ² Namely Justinus Martyr in his Orations against the Gentiles, Tertullian in Apologetico, Arnobius Orat. ad Gentes. Lactantius, lib. 2. c. 17. Divinar. Instit. and many others. But mostly copiously and profoundly S. Augustine, especially in his ten first books de Civitate Dei. Into which error & crime the Platonists fell, holding that spiritual invisible created substances, to wit, Angels good and evil, ³ whom they called Intelligentias separatas, had divine power, & so gave to them divine honour. Others honour dead men, and some before their death, as gods, for their notable acts achieved in this life, as Saturn, Jupiter, Hercules, and the like. Some yield divine honour to mere corporal creatures, ⁴ living or without life, as to beasts, and serpents, the sun, the moon, fire, water, earth, the whole machine of the world, as if it were animate, and that with divine spirit or soul. Again all these have been worshipped as gods, ⁵ not only in themselves but also in their images. But to omit other diversities, the most gross sort of all, is the Idolatry which the Psalmist here describeth, when Images made by men's hands are immediately honoured as gods in themselves. ⁶ For such gods the Gentiles had, and of them the Prophet here speaketh. Comparing these visible senseless imagined gods ⁷, with the one eternal invisible God; who is made notorious by his divine conspicuous works, whereas these idols, by how much they are more visible, so much more they are contemptible: because being fashioned with mouth, eyes, ears, nose, hands, feet, throat, and other members, they are altogether senseless, not able to speak with their mouth, and therein more base in nature than the men that made them; nor able to see, hear, smell, taste, feel, to walk, to move, nor to cry, all which beasts can do. And therefore those that make them, or have confidence in them (as the Prophet here signifieth that some have, v. 16.) are most absurd: becoming like to the same idols, in their understanding and internal cogitations, as these idols are void of external sense. ⁸ And all Idolaters are most wicked, giving divine honour to any creature, & are therein servants of devils: ⁹ Whether they immediately honour devils, as when sorcerers and witches, making pact with the devil, adore him, and he for the same doth something which they demand: or that they honour some other creature, wherein by the devil's false persuasion, they think there is divine power. Both which ways devils usurp God's honour: and therefore the same Prophet said in another Psalm: ¹⁰ All the gods of Gentiles are devils. \fq ¹ \ft The definition of Idolatry. \fq ² \ft Diverse sorts of Idolatry. \fq ³ \ft Angels honoured as gods. \fq ⁴ \ft Corporal creatures sensible and without sense. \fq ⁵ \ft Men living or dead. \fq ⁶ \ft Images of false gods. \fq ⁷ \ft Images themselves reputed gods. \fq ⁸ \ft Idolaters are void of reason. \fq ⁹ \ft And servants of devils. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it Psa. 95. v. 5.\+it*\ef*
\v 13 They have mouth, and shall not speak: they have eyes, an shall not see.
\v 14 They have ears, and shall not hear: they have nostrils and shall not smell.
\v 15 They have hands, and shall not handle: they have feet, and shall not walk: they shall not cry in their throat.
\v 16 \f m \fr 113.16 \ft This is a just prayer of the zealous, conforming their desires to God's will. But if God give idolaters grace to amend, then all the just will also rejoice in their conversion.\f* Let them that make them become like to them: and all that have confidence in them.
\v 17 \f n \fr 113.17 \ft Though many Jews fell to idolatry, yet there always remained so many in God's true service, that it might still be truly said: The house of Israel hath hoped in our Lord, as is here averred.\f* The house of Israel hath hoped in our Lord: he is their helper and their protector.
\v 18 The house of Aaron hath hoped in our Lord: he is their helper and their protector.
\v 19 They that fear our Lord have hoped in our Lord; he is their helper and their protector.
\v 20 Our Lord hath been mindful of us: and hath blessed us. He hath blessed the house of Israel: he hath blessed the house of Aaron.
\v 21 He hath blessed all that fear our Lord, the little with the great.
\v 22 Our Lord add upon you: upon you, & upon your children.
\v 23 Blessed be you of our Lord, which made heaven and earth.
\v 24 \f o \fr 113.24 \ft This in effect all worldly politics say in their hearts: as\f* The heaven of heaven is to our Lord: but \f p \fr 113.24 \ft and contenting themselves with earthly possessions.\f* the earth he hath given to the children of men.
\v 25 \f q \fr 113.25 \ft But when such profane men are dead, they make no shew at all of praising God:\f* The dead shall not praise thee, o Lord: nor all they \f r \fr 113.25 \ft for parting from the earth, they descend into hell, and there eternally blaspheme God.\f* that go down into hell:
\v 26 but we \f s \fr 113.26 \ft Contrarywise the just, aspiring to heaven, which is the proper Kingdom of God, & using this world as they ought to do, for a means to ascend into heaven, shall bless and praise God for evermore.\f* that live do bless our Lord, from this time, and for ever.
\c 114
\cd A just man acknowledging that spiritual life beginneth by faith, and by public profession thereof, 4. gratefully accepteth of Christ's Redemption, 5. dedicateth his life and all he hath to God's service. \f - \ft Thanks-giving for our Redeemer. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. I \f a \fr 114.1 \ft Iam induced to love God,\f* have loved, because our Lord \f b \fr 114.1 \ft because he always heareth my prayers.\f* will hear the voice of my prayer.
\v 2 Because he hath inclined his ear to me: and \f c \fr 114.2 \ft So long as I shall live.\f* in my days I will invocate.
\v 3 The sorrows \f d \fr 114.3 \ft When I erred like a stray sheep from thee, the pains of death,\f* of death have compassed me: and \f e \fr 114.3 \ft and the danger of hell-torments, both due for sin, environed me, and I was not ware thereof.\f* the perils of hell have found me. \f f \fr 114.3 \ft But by tribulation falling upon me, I came to know my dangerous estate:\f* I have found tribulation and sorrow:
\v 4 and I \f g \fr 114.4 \ft turned to thee, and prayed as followeth.\f* invocated the name of our Lord. O Lord deliver my soul:
\v 5 our Lord is merciful, and just, and our God doth mercy.
\v 6 Our Lord keepeth little ones: \f h \fr 114.6 \ft Afflicted with tribulations.\f* I was humbled, and he hath delivered me.
\v 7 Turn o my soul into thy rest: because our Lord hath done good to thee.
\v 8 Because he hath delivered my soul from death: mine eyes from tears, my feet from sliding.
\v 9 I \f i \fr 114.9 \ft Iwill endeavour to please God, in the congregation of those that live here in grace, and in heaven in glory.\f* will please our Lord in the country of the living.
\c 115
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. I \f a \fr 115.1 \ft Ibelieved that God would help me:\f* believed, \f b \fr 115.1 \ft therefore I freely professed that I trusted in him. For then indeed faith is perfect, when we confess with mouth, that which we believe in heart.\f* for which cause I spake: but I \f c \fr 115.1 \ft Iwas vehemently afflicted in tribulations.\f* was humbled exceedingly. \f 1 \fr 115.1 \ft This in the Hebrew is joined to the next Psalm before.\f*
\v 2 I said \f d \fr 115.2 \ft In the midst of my great affliction I professed, that all man's help is vain, false, deceitful, and defective, and therefore our trust must be in God only.\f* in mine excess: Every man is a liar.
\v 3 What \f e \fr 115.3 \ft Considering that God hath not only given, and bestowed many great benefits upon me, and all mankind, but also hath rendered good for evil, mercy for our sins, we have rendered evil for good: what now shall I render, saith a true penitent, for all that he hath thus rendered to me, deserving so evil?\f* shall I render to our Lord, for all things that he \f f \fr 115.3 \ft See previous note\f* hath rendered to me?
\v 4 I \f g \fr 115.4 \ft Seeing I am not able to render any thing worthy of God's favour to me, yet I will do that I can: I will gratefully accept his great benefit, the cup of Christ's passion, which\f* will take the chalice of salvation: and \f h \fr 115.4 \ft and will praise, and call upon his name.\f* I will invocate the name of our Lord.
\v 5 I will \f i \fr 115.5 \ft Iwill pay voluntary vows,\f* render my vows to our Lord \f k \fr 115.5 \ft for God's glory, and edification of others:\f* before all his people:
\v 6 \f l \fr 115.6 \ft yea I will offer my life, and suffer death, when God's glory shall require it, in whose sight the death of Saints is precious, and most highly esteemed.\f* precious in the sight of our Lord is the death of his Saints.
\v 7 O Lord because \f m \fr 115.7 \ft Always understood, that such as suffer persecution, be in good state of their souls, the true servants of God,\f* I am thy servant: I am thy servant, and \f n \fr 115.7 \ft the children of the Church his handmaid.\f* the son of thy handmaid. Thou hast \f o \fr 115.7 \ft Delivered me from captivity of sin.\f* broken my bonds:
\v 8 I will sacrifice to thee the host of praise, and I will invocate the name of our Lord.
\v 9 I will render my vows to our Lord in the sight of all his people:
\v 10 in the courts of the house of our Lord, in the midst of \f p \fr 115.10 \ft In the Church of the faithful.\f* thee, o Jerusalem.
\c 116
\cd Faithful people collected in the Church of Christ, exhort each other to render thanks to God, for their delivery from spiritual and temporal tribulations. 16. The Laity demand participation of Christ's Mysteries, promising to serve him duly: 25. Which the Pastors freely impart, and together with the people, solemnly celebrate God's praise. \f - \ft Christ beneficial Mysteries are celebrated by his Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Praise our Lord \f a \fr 116.1 \ft Not only some, but all nations of the Gentiles:\f* all ye Gentiles: praise him \f b \fr 116.1 \ft and all Jews (Christ's Redemption being aboundantly sufficient for all) are invited to praise God.\f* all ye peoples. \x - \xt Rom. 15. v. 11.\x*
\v 2 Because \f c \fr 116.2 \ft Because he hath multiplied his mercy to us Gentiles, to whom he made no promise:\f* his mercy is confirmed, upon us: and his \f d \fr 116.2 \ft and most truly performed his promise made to the Jews.\f* truth remaineth for ever.
\c 117
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Confess ye to our Lord because he is \f a \fr 117.1 \ft Let us praise God, for his goodness, in making us of nothing, giving us many benefits,\f* good: because \f b \fr 117.1 \ft and remitting our sins.\f* his mercy is for ever. \x - \xt Psal. 105. 106. 135.\x*
\v 2 Let Israel \f c \fr 117.2 \ft Let the Church of the new testament especially confess his goodness, which hath received more mercy and grace.\f* now say that he is good: that his mercy is for ever.
\v 3 Let the house of Aaron \f d \fr 117.3 \ft Let all the Clergy praise God now in the time of more grace, and of greater spiritual functions.\f* now say: that his mercy is for ever.
\v 4 Let them now say \f e \fr 117.4 \ft Yea let the whole body of the Church, all that fear and serve God, praise his mercy.\f* which fear our Lord: that his mercy is for ever.
\v 5 From \f f \fr 117.5 \ft As well spiritual as temporal.\f* tribulation I invocated our Lord: and our Lord heard me in largeness.
\v 6 Our Lord is my helper: I will not fear what man can do to me.
\v 7 Our Lord is my helper: and I will look over mine enemies.
\v 8 It is good to hope in our Lord, rather than to hope in man.
\v 9 It is good to hope in our Lord, rather than to hope in Princes.
\v 10 \f g \fr 117.10 \ft Though innumerable oppose, and endeavour to hurt me (saith the Church, or any just person)\f* All nations have compassed me: and in the name of our Lord am I \f h \fr 117.10 \ft yet by God's power, not by mine own, I am defended, and they punished, and so the just hath the victory, and triumpheth.\f* revenged on them.
\v 11 Compassing they have compassed me: and in the name of our Lord I was revenged on them.
\v 12 They compassed me as \f i \fr 117.12 \ft In great troops, and fury,\f* bees, and were inflamed as \f k \fr 117.12 \ft with sharp though short force, and with special noise, to terrify me, but in God I overcame all.\f* fire in thorns: and in the name of our Lord I was revenged on them.
\v 13 Being thrust \f l \fr 117.13 \ft Iwas sometimes by vehemency of tentation declining to sin:\f* I was overturned to fall: and our Lord \f m \fr 117.13 \ft but God's grace assisted and strengthened me.\f* received me.
\v 14 Our Lord is my strength, and my praise: and he is made my salvation.
\v 15 The voice of exultation, and of salvation in the tabernacles of the just.
\v 16 The right hand of our Lord hath wrought strength: the right hand of our Lord hath exalted me, \f n \fr 117.16 \ft The same word (right hand) thrice mentioned, signifieth the B. Trinity. Also (Our Lord) signifying Christ in his humanity, the chief instrument of God, is here often repeated, to signify the singular efficacy, thereof.\f* the right hand of our Lord hath wrought strength.
\v 17 I shall not die, but shall live: and I will tell the works of our Lord.
\v 18 Our Lord \f o \fr 117.18 \ft God chastiseth his children,\f* chastising hath chastised me: and \f p \fr 117.18 \ft because he would not that they should die eternally. So he punisheth as a father, not as an enemy.\f* to death he hath not delivered me.
\v 19 \f q \fr 117.19 \ft The Prophet now speaketh in the person of just souls, requiring spiritual doctrine, and food,\f* Open ye the gates of justice to me, being entered into them I will confess to our Lord:
\v 20 \f r \fr 117.20 \ft and promising to serve God.\f* this is the gate of our Lord, the just shall enter into it.
\v 21 I will confess to thee because thou hast heard me: and art become my salvation.
\v 22 \f s \fr 117.22 \ft An evident Prophecy of Christ uttered by the Royal Psalmist, and now confessed by every Christian, that our Saviour rejected by the Jews, is nevertheless the builder of his Church, by joining the two peoples of Jews and Gentiles, as two walls into one house.\f* The stone, which the builders rejected: the same is made into the head of the corner.
\v 23 This was done by our Lord: and it is marvelous in our eyes.
\v 24 This \f t \fr 117.24 \ft God ordained this acceptable time of grace.\f* is the day, which our Lord made: let us rejoice, and be glad therein.
\v 25 \f v \fr 117.25 \ft The song of the Hebrew children, when Christ entered Jerusalem with palms of triumph, and acclamations.\f* O Lord save me, o Lord give good success: Blessed be he that cometh in the name of our Lord. \x - \xt Matt. 21. Lu. 20. Act. 4. Rom. 9. 1. Pet. 2.\x*
\v 26 \f w \fr 117.26 \ft The voice of Christ, and his Apostles, and other Clergy blessing the people as they desire.\f* We have blessed you of the house of our Lord:
\v 27 our Lord is God, and he hath given light to us. Appoint a solemn day with \f x \fr 117.27 \ft This was fulfilled when Christ was brought with bows of palm, and other signs of triumph, from Bethania,\f* thick boughs, even to \f y \fr 117.27 \ft through the whole city, even into the Temple and unto the Altar. Mat. \+it 21.\+it*\f* the horn of the altar.
\v 28 Thou art my God, and I will confess to thee: thou art my God, and I will exalt thee. I will confess to thee because thou hast heard me: and art become my salvation.
\v 29 \f z \fr 117.29 \ft Our first, chief, and final duty is to praise God. \+it v. 1.\+it* & ult.\f* Confess ye to our Lord because he is good: because his mercy is for ever.
\c 118
\cd A perpetual recommendation of the singular excellency, absolute necessity, and eternal heavenly profit of God's law: with frequent aspirations to perfection, hatred of sin, love of virtue, and fervent desire to rest in God. \f - \ft Perfect justice is in keeping God's law. The 7. key.\f*
\s1 GENERAL ANNOTATIONS UPON THIS CXVIII. PSALM.
\ip As this Psalm is the longest in the whole Psalter, so it seemeth to the ancient Fathers most profound in sense. And so much the harder to be understood, because also the very hardness \f - \ft The obscurity of this profound Psalm appeareth not to the vulgar reader.\f* thereof lieth hidden, which in diverse other Psalms and parts of holy Scripture, easily appeareth to the reader. But here the words being clear, and the sense also plain and easy in some points of doctrine, yet the more diligence is employed, the more difficulty is found in searching the whole sense and meaning of every word and sentence, with the manner observed in composing it, and the frequent repetition of the same or like words. All which maturely considered caused that great Clerk, and light of the Church Saint Augustin, to omit this Psalm, when he explicated all the \f - \ft S. Augustin deferred the explication of this Psalm. Omitted to discuss one difficulty.\f* rest. And when at last he added also this, he wittingly omitted one special difficulty, which he doubted not to be contained in the manner of composing it, not only by order of the Hebrew Alphabet, as diverse more Psalms, and some other parts of holy Scripture, but more artificially than any other, the first eight verses all beginning with the first letter Aleph; the next eight, with the second letter Beth: and so to the last of the two and twenty letters. Of which omission he yieldeth this only reason, because he found nothing (as he humbly affirmeth) that might properly pertain thereunto. Confessing also expressly, that whensoever he applied his cogitations to expound the text it-self, it always exceeded his hability. But finally to satisfy the often and earnest request of his brethren and friends, trusting (as always) in God's special help, he largely expoundeth it, in thirty two distinct Sermons. \f - \ft At last made 32. sermons in explication thereof.\f*
\ip S. Ambrose, also moved with like piety, made two and twenty Sermons in exposition of this \f - \ft S. Ambrose writ 22. sermons upon this Psalm.\f* Psalm. Affirming in his Prologue, that amongst other Psalms, especially this sheweth how great a master King David was of moral good life. For all Moral doctrine, being of his own nature sweet, yet most delighteth the ears, and gently toucheth the mind, being uttered, as here it is, with pleasantness of verse, and sweetness of song. Again whereas this Royal Prophet in many places of this book poureth out sentences of moral Psalms or songs, as bright stars, that shine and \f - \ft King David a great master of moral doctrine.\f* glister to all the world, here most excellently he produceth a more singular mirror, as the sun, of full light, burning with meridian heat. And for the profit of all, the better to draw our \f - \ft Why this Psalm was composed in order of the Alphabet.\f* attentions, to learn that we may, though we cannot attain to all that we would, he disposed this Psalm through all the Alphabet: that as children beginning with the first letters, make entrance to further knowledge; so by the same beginnings we should lay the first foundation, and thereupon proceed in our spiritual building, towards perfection in good life, the true service of God. Which is yet further insinuated (as the same Doctor teacheth) by the eight verses continually beginning with the same letter, and so other eight in order through the whole Alphabet, to signify that \f - \ft Why eight verses are begun with every letter.\f* after seven days travail in this temporal life, we may come to that unity, which we expected in the eighth day of resurrection, when we hope to rise revived in our Lord Jesus, in newness of eternal life.
\ip Likewise Saint Basil in the Argument of this Psalm admonisheth, that whereas holy David, \f - \ft Saint Basil's judgement that this Psalm containeth the argument of many Psalms.\f* according to diverse states, which he passed, writ diverse Psalms: as when he fled from his enemies, when he lamented his distresses, mourned in pensiveness, enjoyed peace and comfort, ran a right course of virtue, fell from God by sin, & again returning observed God's laws; in this one Psalm he comprehendeth all his prayers made to God at sundry times, & here proposeth the same, a certain profitable moral doctrine, to all sorts and states of men. Neither doth he pretermit doctrinal points of faith, but interposeth them also with moral documents, in such sort, that this one Psalm may suffice to teach the well disposed, how to attain to perfection in virtue, to stir up the slothful unto diligent care of their souls, to recreate the desolate with spiritual consolations, & briefly it administereth all kind of medicine to the diverse passions of mortal men.
\ip For the like judgements of other Fathers we remit the learned reader, to Saint Hilary, \f - \ft Other expositors of this Psalm.\f* Theodoret, Prosper, Arnobius, Cassiodorus, Beda, Euthymius, and others, but cannot well omit a brief instruction of S. Jerome. Who in his Epistle to Paula Urbica: not only sheweth the interpretation of the two and twenty letters, but also explicateth their sense in this place, by connecting them into certain short sentences, in this manner: \f - \ft S. Jerome's interpretation, and explication of the Hebrew Alphabet.\f*
\ip Which is the first connection, signifying that the doctrine of the house, that is the Church of God, is found in the fullness of divine books.
\ip The second connection is:
\ip For what other life can there be without knowledge of Scriptures? Whereby also Christ is known, who is the life of them that believe in him.
\ip The third connection is: \f - \ft \+it idem prooem. lamen.\+it*\f*
\ip Albeit we now could know all things which are written, yet we know but in part, and in part we prophesy: for we see now by a glass, in a dark sort, but when we shall be worthy to be with \f - \ft \+it 1. Cor. 13.\+it*\f* Christ, and shall be like to Angels, then doctrine of books shall cease, & then we shall see face to face the Good Beginning, even as he is. \f - \ft \+it God in himself.\+it*\f*
\ip The fourth connection is: \f - \ft Most of these letters have also other significations. And are diversely explicated by S. Ambrose, S. Beda, and others. Whereby we may learn (though we understand no more) that holy Scriptures are full of mysteries (as S. Jerome calleth this) & hard to be understood.\f*
\ip The hands are understood in work, heart and discipline are understood in sense or meaning, because we cannot rightly do any thing, unless we first know what things are to be done. The fifth connection is:
\ip This needeth not explication, for it is manifest as the light, that from Scriptures are eternal helps. The sixth connection is:
\ip According to that which we have expounded in the fourth connection: that deeds & intention must concur.
\ip The seventh connection which is last, in which number of seven is also mystical understanding:
\ip Distinct voice is produced by the teeth, & in these signs we come to the Head of all which is Christ, by whom we have access to the everlasting Kingdom.
\ip Or thus (not transposing the words) By vocation of Christ the Head, through distinct voice of signs (for words are signs shewing the mind) we are conducted to the eternal Kingdom, the happiness which all men desire.
\ip What I pray thee (saith this holy Doctor) is more sacred than this mystery, what more pleasant than this delight? What meat, & what honey are sweeter, than to know God's wisdom; to enter into his secret closet; to behold the sense of our Creator; and to teach the words of thy Lord God, full of spiritual wisdom, which are derided by the wise of this world.
\ip We must also advertise the reader of the like discourses of ancient Fathers (over long to be here recited) concerning the manifold high praises of God's Law, contained in this Psalm, with \f - \ft God's law escpecially commended in this Psalm.\f* frequent repetition of certain Synonyma words signifying the same thing, in all fourteen, to wit: The Law of God, his Ways, Testimonies, Commandments, Precepts, Statutes, Justifications, \f - \ft 14. Synonyma signifying the law of God.\f* Judgements, Justice, Equity, Verity, Words, Speeches, & Sermons: of which there is commonly one, in every verse, & sometimes two or three in the same verse. But our English tongue hardly sufficing rightly, to distinguish the three last, which in Latin are Verba, Eloquia, Sermones, we translate WORDS only, adding in the margin, Eloquia, and Sermones, when they occur.
\ip Leaving therefore larger commentaries to others; we shall prosecute our wonted manner of brief glosses. Only here premonishing the diligent readers, especially Clergyman (ourselves and our brethren) who every day sing or read this whole Psalm in the Canonical hours, to observe two particular points of Christian doctrine, evidently proved by many places of this Psalm. The one against the Pelagians' heresy, denying the necessity of God's special grace in meritorious works. For the Psalmist often here inculcateth man's insufficiency, that of himself, and by natural \f - \ft God's grace necessary in every good work.\f* forces, he cannot keep the commandments of God, but needeth always the particular grace of God, as well to believe in him, to repent for sins, and to begin good works; as to proceed, and persevere in good state to the end. The other against the heresy of our time, denying merit by grace & freewill. For here it is also manifest, that God's grace maketh man able, to keep his \f - \ft It enableth freewill to merit.\f* commandments, and by keeping them to become just in this life, and so to merit eternal glory. Sundry other principal Articles of Christian Catholic Religion are likewise comprised in this one Psalm: but especially Moral doctrine.
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. \f 1 \fr 118.1 \ft This title was added by the Septuagint, to admonish us that this Psalm containeth that singular manner of praising God, signified by the two Hebrew words Alleluia, as before Psal. 104.\f* Aleph. Doctrine. Blessed \f a \fr 118.1 \ft Whereas all without exception, desire to be happy and blessed:\f* are \f b \fr 118.1 \ft they are indeed happy (according to the perfectest happiness of this life) that are immaculate:\f* the immaculate in the way: which \f c \fr 118.1 \ft and they are immaculate, that walk in the law of God. Where the holy Psalmist presupposeth, that some can and do keep the law of God, and so are immaculate, and blessed in the way of this life.\f* walk in the law of our Lord.
\v 2 Blessed are they \f d \fr 118.2 \ft Those that are immaculate, are again blessed, by searching God's testimonies, that is, his law, testifying that the good shall be rewarded, and the wicked punished: but searching these testimonies, whilst one is contaminate with sins against God's law, maketh not blessed:\f* that search his testimonies: that \f e \fr 118.2 \ft neither doth every superficial careless search bring this blessing, but searching with true affection of the heart.\f* seek after him with all their heart.
\v 3 For \f f \fr 118.3 \ft contrarywise they that work iniquity are not blessed;\f* they that work iniquity, have not walked in his \f g \fr 118.3 \ft because they have not walked in the ways of God, to wit, not kept his commandments and law, which are the way to happiness.\f* ways.
\v 4 Thou hast \f h \fr 118.4 \ft For man's own good, that he may come to true happiness, God hath most seriously commanded us to keep his commandments, that is, to observe his Law commanded by most sovereign divine authority.\f* very much commanded thy commandments to be kept.
\v 5 Would God my ways \f i \fr 118.5 \ft Therefore the faithful servant of God, knowing his own insufficiency, desireth that God by his grace will direct and strengthen him,\f* might be directed, to keep thy \f k \fr 118.5 \ft to keep his law, called Justifications, because thereby man is made just.\f* justifications.
\v 6 Then shall I \f l \fr 118.6 \ft They shall be safe from eternal confusion, when they shall keep not only part, but all thy commandments: because breach of any bringeth confusion.\f* not be confounded, when I shall look throughly in all thy commandments.
\v 7 \f m \fr 118.7 \ft So shall I praise thee, and render thanks,\f* I will confess to thee \f n \fr 118.7 \ft with sincere not feigned affection,\f* in direction of heart: in that I have learned the \f o \fr 118.7 \ft for this great benefit, that I have learned, that thy law is according to most just judgement.\f* judgments of thy justice.
\v 8 \f p \fr 118.8 \ft Ihave therefore a firm purpose, & do faithfully promise to keep thy law, which maketh the keeper thereof just.\f* I will keep thy justifications: \f q \fr 118.8 \ft Albeit thou suffer me sometimes to be in tribulation, or in tentation, yet forsake me not wholly. The Psalmist knew well (saith S. Gregory) that he might be profitably left a while, who prayed, that he should not be wholly forsaken.\f* forsake me not wholly.
\v 9 Beth. House. Wherein \f a \fr 118.9 \ft In this second octonary, as also in all the rest, the Holy Ghost by the Prophet's pen teacheth the means how to come to perfection & happiness. Here by way of interrogation, as it were demanding how a young man, that is every man prone to worldly pleasure, & slow in God's service, shall begin to correct his course?\f* doth a young man correct his way? \f b \fr 118.9 \ft Whereto the same Holy Ghost answereth, that he must keep God's law, called here his words. For all the words which God uttereth, are laws to his servants.\f* In keeping thy \f 1 \fr 118.9 \ft \+it * sermones.\+it*\f* words.
\v 10 \f c \fr 118.10 \ft The Psalmist now speaketh in the person of perfect just men, or of the whole Church in general. Whose common spirit seeketh God entirely.\f* With my whole heart I have sought after thee: \f d \fr 118.10 \ft And considering that this perfect good will is the gift of God, prayeth that he will conserve the same, and not suffer it to be altered, or to err from his commandments.\f* repel me not from thy commandments.
\v 11 \f e \fr 118.11 \ft Another, sincere profession of a resolute good purpose not to sin.\f* In my heart I have hid thy \f 1 \fr 118.11 \ft \+it * eloquia.\+it*\f* words: that I may not sin to thee.
\v 12 \f f \fr 118.12 \ft A grateful aspiration praising God.\f* Blessed art thou, o Lord: \f g \fr 118.12 \ft Again the just prayeth to be more and more instructed in justifications: that which S. John exhorteth unto: He that is just, let him yet be justified. Apoc. \+it 22.\+it*\f* teach me thy justifications.
\v 13 In my lips I have pronounced all the \f h \fr 118.13 \ft God's law is also called his judgements, because sitting in judgement he giveth sentence according to his Law.\f* judgments of thy mouth.
\v 14 I am \f i \fr 118.14 \ft As the just professeth by mouth, so he delighteth in heart.\f* delighted in the way of thy testimonies, as in all riches.
\v 15 I \f k \fr 118.15 \ft Practiseth in work.\f* will be exercised in thy commandments: and I will consider thy ways.
\v 16 I \f l \fr 118.16 \ft And diligently meditateth God's law.\f* will meditate in thy justifications: I will not forget thy \f 1 \fr 118.16 \ft \+it * sermones.\+it*\f* words.
\v 17 Gimel. Fullness. Render \f a \fr 118.17 \ft O Lord liberally give me that which I here crave,\f* to thy servant, \f b \fr 118.17 \ft quicken me with spiritual life, thy grace.\f* quicken me: \f c \fr 118.17 \ft so I shall keep thy law, which otherwise I cannot.\f* and I shall keep thy \f 1 \fr 118.17 \ft \+it * sermones.\+it*\f* words.
\v 18 \f d \fr 118.18 \ft Illuminate mine understanding by thy grace,\f* Reveal mine eyes: \f e \fr 118.18 \ft that I may be able to see the marvelous great and just reasons of thy law, instructing all, threatening the perverse, encouraging the well disposed, punishing the wicked, rewarding the good, doing right to all.\f* and I shall consider the marvelous things of thy law.
\v 19 \f f \fr 118.19 \ft Ithat have but a small time in this world,\f* I am a sojourner in the land, \f g \fr 118.19 \ft desire to be instructed in thy law, what is therein commanded.\f* hide not thy commandments from me.
\v 20 My soul hath coveted to desire thy justifications, at all time.
\v 21 Thou hast \f h \fr 118.21 \ft I consider that thou, o God, dost sharply reprove the proud contemners of thy commandments:\f* rebuked the proud: \f i \fr 118.21 \ft laying curses upon them for declining from thine obedience.\f* cursed are they that decline from thy commandments.
\v 22 Take from me reproach, and contempt: because I have sought after thy testimonies.
\v 23 For \f k \fr 118.23 \ft Though persecutors were very potent,\f* Princes sat, and they spake against me: but \f l \fr 118.23 \ft yet the faithful servant of God persevered in his service.\f* thy servant was exercised in thy justifications.
\v 24 For both \f m \fr 118.24 \ft In time of persecution and tentation we must think and meditate, that God's law testifieth eternal reward, or punishment,\f* thy testimonies are my meditation: and \f n \fr 118.24 \ft and in our deliberation or consultation, we must consider that keeping God's law maketh just: & consequently meriteth reward.\f* thy justifications my counsel.
\v 25 Daleth. Of Tables. My soul \f a \fr 118.25 \ft This also is uttered in the person of the just, who is often brought to great distress: as it were, even near to death,\f* hath cleaved to the pavement: \f b \fr 118.25 \ft in which case he confidently prayeth to be relieved, according to God's word, law, and promise.\f* quicken me according to thy word.
\v 26 I have uttered my ways, and thou hast heard me: teach me thy justifications.
\v 27 Instruct me the way of thy justifications: and I shall be exercised in thy marvelous works.
\v 28 My soul \f c \fr 118.28 \ft Being in so great anxiety that my mind is almost distracted or overcome.\f* hath slumbered for tediousness: \f d \fr 118.28 \ft Icall to thee God, that thou wilt conserve me, that I still keep thy law, uttered by thy words.\f* confirm me in thy words.
\v 29 \f e \fr 118.29 \ft Protect me that I fall not to iniquity.\f* Remove from me the way of iniquity: and according to thy law, \f f \fr 118.29 \ft And of thy mercy conserve me in state of grace.\f* have mercy on me.
\v 30 I have chosen the way of truth: I have not forgotten thy judgments.
\v 31 I have cleaved to thy testimonies, o Lord: \f g \fr 118.31 \ft Suffer me not to be confounded.\f* do not confound me.
\v 32 \f h \fr 118.32 \ft Man is able, and doth run in the right way of God's commandments,\f* I ran the way of thy commandments: \f i \fr 118.32 \ft yet not of himself, but when God replenisheth his heart with grace.\f* when thou didst dilate my heart.
\v 33 He. This thing. \f a \fr 118.33 \ft Impress, o God, thy law in mine affection, make me to love it, and to desire to be justified,\f* Set me a law, o Lord, the way of thy justifications: \f b \fr 118.33 \ft so shall I heartily and always seek it.\f* and I will seek after it always.
\v 34 \f c \fr 118.34 \ft After thou hast given me a desire to keep thy law, give me also understanding,\f* Give me understanding, \f d \fr 118.34 \ft then shall I fruitfully search it. For this is the right order (as before in the first, and second verses) first to love God's law, to be justified, and to become immaculate, and then to search to know the law, and so it is more easily learned.\f* and I will search thy law: and I will keep it with my whole heart.
\v 35 \f e \fr 118.35 \ft God's grace first draweth and leadeth,\f* Conduct me into the path of thy commandments: \f f \fr 118.35 \ft then freewill inflamed with desire effectually concurreth.\f* because I would it.
\v 36 \f g \fr 118.36 \ft Still the Prophet inculcateth the necessity of God's grace, as well to make us desire that is good;\f* Incline my heart into thy testimonies: and \f h \fr 118.36 \ft as to fly from evil.\f* not into avarice.
\v 37 \f i \fr 118.37 \ft It is necessary also to pray that God will take away occasions, which might move to sin:\f* Turn away mine eyes that they see not vanity: \f k \fr 118.37 \ft and still to grant his helping grace in progress of virtue.\f* in thy way quicken me.
\v 38 \f l \fr 118.38 \ft Again the just prayeth for confirmation in grace, to be established in the fear of God.\f* Establish thy \f 1 \fr 118.38 \ft \+it * eloquium.\+it*\f* word to thy servant, in thy fear.
\v 39 \f m \fr 118.39 \ft To be delivered also from all the effects of former sins,\f* Take away my reproach, which I have feared: \f n \fr 118.39 \ft for sin is therefore reproachful and odious, because it is contrary to God's law, & true judgements, which are most pleasant.\f* because thy judgments are pleasant.
\v 40 Behold I have coveted thy commandments: in thine equity \f o \fr 118.40 \ft Being thus affected with desire to keep the commandments, the soul prayeth to be still quickened, more and more with good spirit, and so to persevere to the end.\f* quicken me.
\v 41 Vau. And. And \f a \fr 118.41 \ft Again considering that without God's grace preventing, man cannot do any good thing, the Prophet reneweth his prayer, requesting God's mercy,\f* let thy mercy come upon me, o Lord: \f b \fr 118.41 \ft and his help freely promised to all that ask it.\f* thy salvation according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.41 \ft \+it * eloquium.\+it*\f* word.
\v 42 And \f c \fr 118.42 \ft Wherewith being assisted and strengthened, he that before was weak will boldly answer all calumniators, that reproachfully say: God will not help him:\f* I shall answer a word to them that upbraid me: \f d \fr 118.42 \ft that indeed he hath not in vain trusted in God's promised help.\f* because I have hoped in thy \f 1 \fr 118.42 \ft \+it * sermonibus.\+it*\f* words.
\v 43 And \f e \fr 118.43 \ft He also prayeth, though he be sometimes fearful, that God will not suffer him wholly to omit manifest profession of faith and true religion,\f* take not away out of my mouth the word of truth utterly: \f f \fr 118.43 \ft seeing by thy former grace I have already reposed my trust in thy promises, made to them that are resolved to keep thy law.\f* because I have much hoped in thy judgments.
\v 44 And \f g \fr 118.44 \ft For I do firmly purpose ever and always to keep thy law.\f* I will keep thy law always: for ever, and for ever and ever.
\v 45 And \f h \fr 118.45 \ft In this I have had great joy and comfort of mind:\f* I walked in largeness: \f i \fr 118.45 \ft because I did indeed seek after thy commandments, which is specially uttered (as also the three next verses) in the person of those, that are in trial of persecution for their faith:\f* because I have sought after thy commandments.
\v 46 And \f k \fr 118.46 \ft Who boldly in time of persecution, even before persecuting Kings and Emperors, profess Christ's true Religion. Verified in innumerable glorious Martyrs, yea also of the frail sex, in S. Catharine, S. Cecily, S. Lucy, S. Margaret, S. Wenefride, S. Ursula, and her fellows, and many more, most constantly answering all words of reproach objected, as if it were a base or contemptible thing to be Christians, to be Catholics, to be Papists. No, all these & the like, are honourable & glorious titles; importing the true service of Christ, in unity of the Catholic Church: and spiritual participation with the visible head thereof, Christ's Vicar in earth.\f* I spake of thy testimonies in the sight of kings: and was not confounded.
\v 47 And \f l \fr 118.47 \ft Such confessors as yet mortal, rejoice in that they have meditated in God's commandments, which they have fervently loved.\f* I meditated in thy commandments, which I loved.
\v 48 And \f m \fr 118.48 \ft Also shewed the same in external work, not dissembling by silence, by word, nor fact.\f* I have lifted up my hands to thy commandments, which I loved: and \f n \fr 118.48 \ft every way exercising God's law, which maketh the observers just.\f* I was exercised in thy justifications.
\v 49 Zain. This. Be \f a \fr 118.49 \ft That which God hath decreed and promised, being in it-self most certain and assured, yet includeth the means, whereby it shall be put in execution: and therefore the just, his elect, do pray for the performance of his will.\f* mindful of thy word to thy servant, wherein thou hast given me hope.
\v 50 This hath comforted me in my humiliation: because \f b \fr 118.50 \ft Expectation of thy promise hath given me courage.\f* thy \f 1 \fr 118.50 \ft \+it eloquium.\+it*\f* word hath quickened me.
\v 51 The \f c \fr 118.51 \ft Proud contemners of God's law, have every way molested me, by detracting, deriding, calumniating, and violently persecuting me.\f* proud did unjustly exceedingly: \f d \fr 118.51 \ft All which I have borne patiently, and not declined from thy law.\f* but I declined not from thy law.
\v 52 I \f e \fr 118.52 \ft Iremembered and considered thy just punishments inflicted upon the impious,\f* have been mindful of thy judgments from \f f \fr 118.52 \ft even from the beginning of the world (both upon the devils and wicked men) and that thou wilt exercise the like hereafter,\f* everlasting, o Lord: \f g \fr 118.52 \ft which consideration of thy justice comforted me.\f* and was comforted.
\v 53 \f h \fr 118.53 \ft Otherwise if I had not seen thy justice, my zeal against contemners of thy law would have killed me.\f* Fainting possessed me, because of sinners forsaking thy Law.
\v 54 \f i \fr 118.54 \ft In this place of my peregrination from heaven, I am comforted by remembering, celebrating, and singing thy just commandments and laws, which make thy servants just.\f* Thy justifications were song by me, in the place of my peregrination.
\v 55 I have been mindful in \f k \fr 118.55 \ft In persecution, and in all tribulation, I kept thy law, because I would not dishonour thy name:\f* the night of thy name, o Lord: and have kept thy law.
\v 56 This was done to me: \f l \fr 118.56 \ft And my tribulation especially fell upon me, because I sought to be justified by keeping thy law.\f* because I sought after thy justifications.
\v 57 Heth. Life. My \f a \fr 118.57 \ft The Prophet proceedeth speaking in the person of the just tending to perfection, and saying: This is my happy choice, that I desire no other inheritance, nor possession, but to keep God's Law.\f* portion, o Lord, I said to keep thy law.
\v 58 \f b \fr 118.58 \ft And seeing this exceedeth my proper strength, I prayed God of his mercy to make able to keep it.\f* I besought thy face, with all my heart: have mercy on me according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.58 \ft \+it eloquium.\+it*\f* word.
\v 59 I \f c \fr 118.59 \ft Pondering my former actions, I turned my paths to observe more perfectly the Law, which God hath testified to be the right way.\f* thought upon my ways: and converted my feet unto thy testimonies.
\v 60 I \f d \fr 118.60 \ft With promptness of mind, and without hesitation I resolved to keep the commandments.\f* am prepared, and am not troubled: to keep thy commandments.
\v 61 The \f e \fr 118.61 \ft The wicked laid cords, nets, or snares, to entrap and hinder me,\f* cords of sinners have wrapped me round about: and \f f \fr 118.61 \ft but I kept thy law fresh in memory.\f* I have not forgotten thy law.
\v 62 At \f g \fr 118.62 \ft That this is not understood only mystically in time of affliction, but also literally and prophetically, that some special servants of God should observe a godly profession of praying at mid-night, the word (I rose) maketh it probable. Saint Paul & Silas,\f* mid-night I rose to confess to thee, for the judgments of thy justification.
\v 63 \f h \fr 118.63 \ft A great benefit, and a singular consolation, that all true living members of Christ, are partakers of all the prayers, good works, and merits, of the whole Church militant and triumphant. Which in our Creed is called, The Communion of Saints.\f* I am partaker of all that fear thee: and that keep thy commandments.
\v 64 The \f i \fr 118.64 \ft so great is the mercy of God, extended, communicated, and multiplied in the whole earth.\f* earth, o Lord, is full of thy mercy: \f k \fr 118.64 \ft Instruct me, and direct me therefore, o God, that I may learn and observe thy law, and so be justified, and made participant of so great mercy.\f* teach me thy justifications.
\v 65 Teth. Good. Thou hast \f a \fr 118.65 \ft Dealt very bountifully\f* done bounty with thy servant, o Lord: \f b \fr 118.65 \ft as thou didst promise.\f* according to thy word.
\v 66 \f c \fr 118.66 \ft He that hath bountifully received grace at God's hand, prayeth for more grace, that he may be beneficial to others in relieving the needy,\f* Teach me goodness, \f d \fr 118.66 \ft in instructing the ignorant,\f* and discipline, and \f e \fr 118.66 \ft in persuading to keep the law of God:\f* knowledge: \f f \fr 118.66 \ft because he hath learned and believeth the commandments, by which he is bound to love, and have care of his neighbour.\f* because I have believed thy commandments.
\v 67 Before I \f g \fr 118.67 \ft Before I was afflicted, I often fell into sin:\f* was humbled I offended: \f h \fr 118.67 \ft but vexation gave me understanding, therefore now I keep thy law.\f* therefore have I kept thy \f 1 \fr 118.67 \ft \+it eloquium.\+it*\f* word.
\v 68 Thou art good: and in thy goodness teach me thy justifications.
\v 69 The iniquity of \f i \fr 118.69 \ft Contemners of thy law have endeavoured to entangle me,\f* the proud is multiplied upon me: \f k \fr 118.69 \ft but I persist in keeping thy commandments. As before \+it v. 51. 61.\+it*\f* but I in all my heart will search thy commandments.
\v 70 Their heart is \f l \fr 118.70 \ft Though the wicked combine themselves together against me,\f* curded together as milk: \f m \fr 118.70 \ft yet I consider, that it is necessary to persevere in thy law.\f* but I have meditated thy law.
\v 71 It is good for me that thou hast humbled me: that I may learn thy justifications.
\v 72 The law of thy mouth is \f n \fr 118.72 \ft A clear comparison, that it is better to keep God's law, which bringeth life everlasting, than to have all the riches & Kingdoms of this world.\f* good unto me, above thousands of gold and silver.
\v 73 Iod. Beginning. Thy hands have made me, and formed me: \f a \fr 118.73 \ft God being our Creator, we may with confidence pray him to illuminate our minds, that we may learn what is his pleasure, and so endeavour to fulfill it.\f* give me understanding, and I will learn thy commandments.
\v 74 They that fear thee \f b \fr 118.74 \ft Others that love God will be glad to see me also serve him.\f* shall see me, & shall rejoice: because I have much hoped in thy words.
\v 75 \f c \fr 118.75 \ft The just being afflicted, and not seeing the particular cause thereof, yet knoweth and confesseth, that God doth it for most just cause.\f* I know, o Lord, that thy judgments are equity: and in thy truth thou hast humbled me. \f d \fr 118.75 \ft And therefore with patience prayeth for comfort, as followeth:\f*
\v 76 Let thy mercy be done to comfort me, according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.76 \ft \+it * eloquium.\+it*\f* word unto thy servant.
\v 77 Let thy commiserations come to me, and I \f e \fr 118.77 \ft Who am almost dead in tribulation.\f* shall live: because thy law is my meditation.
\v 78 Let the proud \f f \fr 118.78 \ft The just also prayeth that the wicked may be ashamed, and converted, for so the Hebrew word here signifieth, though it is also lawful to desire the just punishment of obstinate sinners.\f* be confounded, because they have done unjustly toward me: but I will be exercised in thy commandments.
\v 79 Let them \f g \fr 118.79 \ft He prayeth again for conversion of the wicked, and to have peace with them.\f* be converted to me that fear thee: and that know thy testimonies.
\v 80 Let my heart be made immaculate in thy justifications, that I be not confounded.
\v 81 Caph. Hand, or Palm of the hand. My soul hath fainted for \f a \fr 118.81 \ft Many just of the old testament most fervently desired the coming of Christ our Saviour, as our Lord himself testifieth, Mat. \+it 13. v. 17.\+it* And now the just desire his coming in glory. \+it 2. Tim. 4. v. 8.\+it*\f* thy salvation: and I have much hoped in thy word.
\v 82 Mine eyes have failed for thy \f 1 \fr 118.82 \ft \+it * eloquium.\+it*\f* word, saying: \f b \fr 118.82 \ft Delayed hope afflicteth.\f* When wilt thou comfort me?
\v 83 Because I am made \f c \fr 118.83 \ft As a leather bottle made of a beast's skin, congealed with the frost, and after parched in smoke, so is the body of the just mortified by diverse sorts of afflictions, made a new bottle fit to receive new wine, that is perfect doctrine of Christian life, as of fasting, and other austerity, whereof our Saviour speaketh. \+it Mat. 9. v. 17.\+it*\f* as a bottle in the hoar frost: I have not forgotten thy justifications.
\v 84 How many are \f d \fr 118.84 \ft Such is man's infirmity, yea of the just, that he apprehendeth tribulations to be very long, and therefore desireth consummation; and that without sin, so he still submit his will to God's will.\f* the days of thy servant: when wilt thou do judgment on them that persecute me?
\v 85 The unjust have told me \f e \fr 118.85 \ft Frivolous idle tails, which are not according to God's law.\f* fables: but not as thy law.
\v 86 All thy commandments are truth: they have unjustly persecuted me, help me.
\v 87 They have well near \f f \fr 118.87 \ft Iwas in great danger; but am not overthrown.\f* made end of me in the earth: but I have not forsaken thy commandments.
\v 88 According to \f g \fr 118.88 \ft And by thy merciful grace shall persist.\f* thy mercy quicken me: and I shall keep the testimonies of thy mouth.
\v 89 Lamed. Discipline. For ever Lord \f a \fr 118.89 \ft The praise of God's works, which are firm and permanent in the order, wherein he set them.\f* thy word is permanent in heaven.
\v 90 Thy truth in generation and generation: thou hast founded the earth, and it is permanent.
\v 91 By thy ordinance the day continueth: because \f b \fr 118.91 \ft All things of this world, man excepted, do God's will.\f* all things serve thee.
\v 92 \f c \fr 118.92 \ft Man, except he meditate God's law, and thereby be holden up, is in danger,\f* But that thy law is my meditation: I had then \f d \fr 118.92 \ft perhaps in every tentation to perish eternally. For he can never rise out of mortal sin, by his own power, and all should perish if God's mercy did not spare some, and give them new effectual grace to repent.\f* perhaps perished in my humiliation.
\v 93 I will not forget thy justifications for ever: because in them thou hast quickened me.
\v 94 I am thine, save me: because \f e \fr 118.94 \ft Always understood, that God's grace prevented, else no man can seek to observe the commandments.\f* I have sought out thy justifications.
\v 95 Sinners have expected me to destroy me: I understood thy testimonies.
\v 96 \f f \fr 118.96 \ft All worldly things have their consummation and end:\f* Of all consummation I have seen the end: \f g \fr 118.96 \ft God's commandment continueth ever. For we are perpetually bound to love and serve God: to love our neighbours, yea and enemies. The reward also for keeping God's commandments, & punishment for breaking them, are eternal without end.\f* thy commandment is exceeding large.
\v 97 Mem. Of them. \f a \fr 118.97 \ft It is marvel to a perfect just man, that he hath so much loved and observed God's law. By acknowledging whereof, he yieldeth praise and thanks to God, whose gift it is.\f* How have I loved thy law, o Lord! All the day it is my meditation.
\v 98 \f b \fr 118.98 \ft The fruits of observing God's law are many and great. Amongst others, it maketh the observer, wiser than his enemies.\f* Above mine enemies thou hast made me wise by thy commandment: because it is to me for ever.
\v 99 Above \f c \fr 118.99 \ft It maketh the observer wiser than his temporal masters, that taught him, to wit, than those that teach well, and do not perform the same.\f* all that taught me have I understood: because thy testimonies are my meditation.
\v 100 Above \f d \fr 118.100 \ft Younger in years that keep God's commandments, are wiser than the more ancient that keep them not.\f* ancients have I understood: because I have sought thy commandments.
\v 101 I have stayed my feet from all evil way: that I may keep thy words.
\v 102 I have not declined from thy judgments: because thou hast set me a law.
\v 103 \f e \fr 118.103 \ft Another fruit is the sweetness, which the just feeleth in his own soul.\f* How sweet are thy \f 1 \fr 118.103 \ft \+it eloquia.\+it*\f* words to my jaws. More than honey to my mouth!
\v 104 By thy commandments I have understood: therefore have I \f f \fr 118.104 \ft It brideth also just hatred to sin.\f* hated all the way of iniquity.
\v 105 Nun. Everlasting. \f a \fr 118.105 \ft The word or law of god declared by Prophets, Pastors, or other Preachers, is the ordinary means for others to learn, how to direct their ways and actions.\f* Thy word is a lamp to my feet, and a light to my paths.
\v 106 I \f b \fr 118.106 \ft Such profession God's people made in the old law, in\f* sware, and have determined to keep the judgments of thy justice.
\v 107 I am \f c \fr 118.107 \ft All that will live godly in Christ Jesus, shall suffer persecution. \+it 2. Tim. 3. v. 12.\+it*\f* humbled exceedingly, o Lord: quicken me according to thy word.
\v 108 The \f d \fr 118.108 \ft Besides the commandments, the just also offer voluntary works of supererogation, acceptable to God.\f* voluntaries of my mouth make acceptable, o Lord: and teach me thy judgments.
\v 109 My \f e \fr 118.109 \ft By this Hebrew proverb is signified, that a just man's temporal life is in continual danger, as the thing that is in one's hand, is ready to be laid by, put out of his hand, or to be by and by disposed of, or may forthwith fall from his hand.\f* soul is in my hands always: and I have not forgotten thy law.
\v 110 Sinners laid a snare for me: and I have not erred from thy commandments.
\v 111 For inheritance I have purchased thy testimonies for ever: because they are the joy of my heart.
\v 112 I have inclined my heart to do thy justifications for ever, \f f \fr 118.112 \ft Amost evident place, that the keeping of God's commandments merit reward, and are rightly observed in respect of reward.\f* for reward.
\v 113 Samech. Help. I have \f a \fr 118.113 \ft Not any mortal man is to be hated, in his person, but his iniquity, by which he is an enemy to God's law,\f* hated the unjust: and I have \f b \fr 118.113 \ft which every just man loveth.\f* loved thy law.
\v 114 Thou art my helper, and protector: and upon thy word I have much hoped.
\v 115 \f c \fr 118.115 \ft Whosoever will seriously and securely search the law of God, must avoid the conversation of evil men.\f* Depart from me ye malignant: and I will search the commandments of my God.
\v 116 \f d \fr 118.116 \ft A general and very fit prayer, when we addict ourselves by a firm resolution to serve God, beseeching him to receive us into his protection:\f* Receive me according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.116 \ft \+it eloquium.\+it*\f* word, \f e \fr 118.116 \ft whereby spiritual life is conserved:\f* and I shall live: and \f f \fr 118.116 \ft which we pray with great confidence, because he hath promised to hear those that seek, ask, and knock at the door of his grace.\f* confound me not of mine expectation.
\v 117 \f g \fr 118.117 \ft We must pray also that he suffer us not to be confounded, or made frustrate of the reward which we expect; for hope confoundeth not, if charity be poured in our hearts, by the Holy Ghost which is given us. \+it Rom. 5. v. 5.\+it*\f* Help me, and I shall be saved: and I will meditate in thy justifications always.
\v 118 Thou hast \f h \fr 118.118 \ft God rejecteth sinners:\f* despised all that revolt from thy judgments: \f i \fr 118.118 \ft so long as they think perversely: that is, until by his grace, some return to a better mind, which of themselves they can never do.\f* because their cogitation is unjust.
\v 119 All the sinners of the earth I have \f k \fr 118.119 \ft as God accounteth of sinners, so the just also esteemeth them, conforming his judgement to God's.\f* reputed prevaricators: therefore have I loved thy testimonies.
\v 120 \f l \fr 118.120 \ft Servile fear is profitable as this place maketh evident: though perfect charity afterwards succeeding, expelleth that fear, and moveth to do well for the love of God, not for fear of punishment. \+it 1. Ioan. 4.\+it*\f* Pierce my flesh with thy fear: for I am afraid of thy judgments.
\v 121 Ain. A fountain, or an eye. I \f a \fr 118.121 \ft The just again in fervent zeal, not arrogantly, but confidently professing his innocency,\f* have done judgment and justice: \f b \fr 118.121 \ft prayeth to be defended from calumniators.\f* deliver me not to them that calumniate me.
\v 122 Receive thy servant \f c \fr 118.122 \ft Grant therefore, o God, the good and lawful request which I demand.\f* unto good: let not the proud calumniate me.
\v 123 Mine eyes have failed \f d \fr 118.123 \ft By long expecting to be delivered and saved from tribulation.\f* after thy salvation: and for the \f 1 \fr 118.123 \ft \+it eloquium.\+it*\f* word of thy justice.
\v 124 Do with thy servant according to thy mercy: and teach me thy justifications.
\v 125 I am thy servant: give me understanding, that I may know thy testimonies.
\v 126 \f e \fr 118.126 \ft It is time, and high time, saith fervent zeal of the just man, that God deliver the innocent:\f* It is time to do, o Lord: \f f \fr 118.126 \ft when the wicked have not only persecuted the good, but have also contemptuously made havoc of God's law and true religion.\f* they have dissipated thy law.
\v 127 \f g \fr 118.127 \ft For this zeal of God's law so despised and dissipated, the just more and more loveth, that which the wicked so deadly hate.\f* Therefore have I loved thy commandments, above gold and topazius.
\v 128 \f h \fr 118.128 \ft Even by the mortal hate of the wicked I saw that God's law is most excellent, and therefore addicted myself so much the more to love it,\f* Therefore was I directed to all thy commandments: \f i \fr 118.128 \ft and to hate all wicked ways.\f* all wicked way I have hated.
\v 129 Phe. Mouth. Thy \f a \fr 118.129 \ft God's marvelous power and wisdom, testified by his works and commandments,\f* testimonies are marvelous: \f b \fr 118.129 \ft worthily invite just souls to meditate and contemplate the same.\f* therefore hath my soul searched them.
\v 130 The \f c \fr 118.130 \ft First entrance into knowledge of holy Scripture, illuminateth the understanding of the humble, whereby they proceed to know more.\f* declaration of thy \f 1 \fr 118.130 \ft \+it Sermonum.\+it*\f* words doth illuminate: and giveth understanding to little ones.
\v 131 I \f d \fr 118.131 \ft By this Metaphor, of gaping or wide opening the mouth, and drawing breath, the Prophet describeth the great desire of the just, to know and keep God's commandments.\f* opened my mouth, and drew breath: because I desired thy commandments.
\v 132 Look upon me, and have mercy on me, according to \f e \fr 118.132 \ft According to thy accustomed equity, in shewing mercy to them that love thy name.\f* the judgment of them that love thy name.
\v 133 Direct my steps according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.133 \ft \+it eloquium.\+it*\f* word: and let not any injustice have domination over me.
\v 134 Redeem me from the calumnies of men: that I may keep thy commandments.
\v 135 Illuminate \f f \fr 118.135 \ft Let thy divine Majesty look upon me with favourable countenance.\f* thy face upon thy servant: and teach me thy justifications.
\v 136 \f g \fr 118.136 \ft True repentance consisteth not only in purpose to avoid sin hereafter, which indeed is first required, but also in sorrow and lamentation for sins past.\f* Mine eyes have gushed forth issues of waters: because they have not kept thy law.
\v 137 Sade. Justice. Thou art just, o Lord: and \f a \fr 118.137 \ft God being essentially just of himself, maketh men just according to right judgement, by giving them grace of mercy, wherewith they cooperating are just by justice indeed inherent in their souls, not by imputation only: for it were not right judgement to impute, or account man just, who is not so indeed.\f* thy judgment is right.
\v 138 Thou hast commanded \f b \fr 118.138 \ft The same is more confirmed by these three synonyma, Justice, Testimonies, Verity, signifying the law of God, most earnestly commanded.\f* justice thy testimonies: and thy verity exceedingly.
\v 139 My zeal hath made me to pine away: because mine enemies have forgotten thy words.
\v 140 Thy \f 1 \fr 118.140 \ft \+it * eloquia.\+it*\f* word is \f c \fr 118.140 \ft God's law is as pure as any thing purged by fire.\f* fired exceedingly: and thy servant hath loved it.
\v 141 I am \f d \fr 118.141 \ft Ajust man is often judged ignorant, immature, unexperienced, by the worldly wise;\f* a youngman, and contemned: I \f e \fr 118.141 \ft but indeed is wise, in that he forgetteth not to keep the law, which maketh him just.\f* have not forgotten thy justifications.
\v 142 Thy justice is justice for ever: and thy law is verity.
\v 143 Tribulation and distress have found me: thy commandments are my meditation.
\v 144 Thy testimonies are equity for ever \f f \fr 118.144 \ft Having professed the necessity of perfect justice, he concludeth this Octonary, praying to be illuminated in his understanding, that so he may attain justice and live thereby.\f* give me understanding, and I shall live.
\v 145 Coph. Vocation. I \f a \fr 118.145 \ft Most serious and fervent invocation of God for his grace is necessary to the fulfilling of his law.\f* have cried in my whole heart, hear me, o Lord: I will seek after thy justifications.
\v 146 I have cried to thee, save me: that I may keep thy commandments.
\v 147 I have prevented in \f b \fr 118.147 \ft Ihave prevented the nature and ordinary time of the night, and have prayed\f* maturity, and \f c \fr 118.147 \ft very attentively.\f* have cried: because I hoped much in thy words.
\v 148 Mine eyes \f d \fr 118.148 \ft Again in the morning I have prevented the accustomed time of prayer.\f* have prevented early unto thee: that I might meditate thy \f 1 \fr 118.148 \ft \+it eloquia.\+it*\f* words.
\v 149 Hear my voice according to thy mercy, o Lord: and according to \f e \fr 118.149 \ft According to thy accustomed manner of shewing mercy, shew it me, that thereby I may live.\f* thy judgment quicken me.
\v 150 They that persecute me have approached to iniquity: but from thy law they are made far off.
\v 151 Thou art \f f \fr 118.151 \ft God is always ready to hear all that sincerely invocate him.\f* nigh, o Lord: and all thy ways are truth.
\v 152 \f g \fr 118.152 \ft God's law is the same in substance from the beginning of the world, and will be for ever.\f* From the beginning I knew of thy testimonies: that thou hast founded them for ever.
\v 153 Res. Head. See \f a \fr 118.153 \ft Another prayer of the just in affliction.\f* my humiliation, and deliver me: because I have not forgotten thy law.
\v 154 Judge my judgment, & redeem me: for thy \f 1 \fr 118.154 \ft \+it eloquium.\+it*\f* word \f b \fr 118.154 \ft Conserve me in thy grace.\f* quicken thou me.
\v 155 Salvation is far from sinners: because they have not sought after thy justifications.
\v 156 Thy mercies are many, o Lord: \f c \fr 118.156 \ft As before \+it v. 149.\+it*\f* according to thy judgment quicken me.
\v 157 There are many that persecute me, and afflict me: I have not declined from thy testimonies.
\v 158 Isaw the prevaricators, and I pined away: because they kept not thy \f 1 \fr 118.158 \ft \+it eloquia.\+it*\f* words.
\v 159 \f d \fr 118.159 \ft Sincere profession of innocency is no arrogancy.\f* See that I have loved thy commandments, o Lord: in thy mercy quicken me.
\v 160 The \f e \fr 118.160 \ft God's essential verity is the beginning from whence, as from the fountain, all other truths are derived:\f* beginning of thy words is truth: \f f \fr 118.160 \ft And all commandments proceeding from this first truth, are for ever immutable.\f* all the judgments of thy justice are for ever.
\v 161 Sin. Tooth. Princes have persecuted me \f a \fr 118.161 \ft Potent wicked men persecute the godly without cause, that is, without any just reason moving them; & without the effect intended by them which is to draw God's servants from truth and equity;\f* without cause: and my \f b \fr 118.161 \ft whose heart being possessed with the true fear of God, they persist in keeping God's commandments.\f* heart hath been afraid of thy words.
\v 162 I \f c \fr 118.162 \ft Yea they also rejoice in keeping the commandments, with such difficulty, as those do, that gaining the victory over their enemies, carry away great and rich spoils.\f* will rejoice at thy \f 1 \fr 118.162 \ft \+it eloquia.\+it*\f* words: as he that findeth many spoils.
\v 163 I have heard iniquity, and abhorred it: but thy law I have loved.
\v 164 \f d \fr 118.164 \ft Every day the just praise God often, signified by the number of seven. From hence also the Church of Christ took example to institute the seven Canonical Hours,\f* Seven times in the day I have said praise to thee, for the judgments of thy justice.
\v 165 There is \f e \fr 118.165 \ft Amongst other benefits, it is a special commodity, that those which perfectly love God's law have always peace in their own conscience:\f* much peace to them that love thy law: & \f f \fr 118.165 \ft and are never scandalized, that is, do not fall nor commit sin, by any occasion whatsoever given them by others. For it is a general assured doctrine, that the perfect are not scandalized, because they are constant in virtue, and not moved by any example, persuasion, provocation, or other means to offend God: but only the weak and unperfect are moved and drawn to sin by occasions given them, who otherwise would not have sinned. Neither is he excused that falleth by such occasions, because he ought to be constant.\f* there is no scandal to them.
\v 166 I expected thy salvation, o Lord: and have loved thy commandments.
\v 167 My soul hath kept thy testimonies: & \f g \fr 118.167 \ft Not of servile fear, but of true charity & filial love.\f* hath loved them exceedingly.
\v 168 \f h \fr 118.168 \ft Because whatsoever I do is in thy sight, whom I will in no case offend.\f* I have kept thy commandments, and thy testimonies: because all my ways are in thy sight.
\v 169 Tau. Sign. \f a \fr 118.169 \ft In this last Octonary, and conclusion of this Psalm, the faithful servant of God prayeth,\f* Let my petition approach in thy sight, o Lord: according to thy word give me \f b \fr 118.169 \ft not for human knowledge, or other temporal things, but to understand God's law.\f* understanding.
\v 170 Let my request enter in thy sight: \f c \fr 118.170 \ft Thou that hast promised to hear all that invocate thee,\f* according to thy word \f d \fr 118.170 \ft vouchsafe to deliver me in time of tribulations and tentations.\f* deliver me.
\v 171 My lips shall utter an hymn, when thou shalt teach me thy justifications.
\v 172 My tongue shall pronounce thy \f 1 \fr 118.172 \ft Why the 15 Psalms following are called Gradual Canticles. Here follow in order fifteen Psalms entitle Gradual Canticles.\f* word: \f e \fr 118.172 \ft God giving grace, it behoveth his servants thankfully to serve and praise him.\f* because all thy commandments are equity.
\v 173 \f f \fr 118.173 \ft Though man be endued with grace, yet he needeth more grace, that by God's hand and power, not by his own, he may resist tentations.\f* Let thy hand be to save me: because I have chosen thy commandments.
\v 174 I have coveted thy salvation, o Lord: and thy law is my meditation.
\v 175 \f g \fr 118.175 \ft By this assistance of grace, the soul continueth in spiritual life, and praiseth God.\f* My soul shall live, and shall praise thee: and thy judgments shall help me.
\v 176 \f h \fr 118.176 \ft All mankind, and universally every one, hath been as a lost sheep,\f* I have strayed as a sheep that is lost: \f i \fr 118.176 \ft & Christ came into this world to seek and save all:\f* seek thy servant, \f k \fr 118.176 \ft but effectually findeth and saveth those only, that forget not to keep his commandments.\f* because I have not forgotten thy commandments.
\c 119
\cd The just rejoice that their prayer is heard, and that God continually protecteth them. \f - \ft God's protection. The 3. key.\f*
\s1 A BRIEF NOTE CONCERNING the Gradual Psalms.
\ip Here follow in order fifteen Psalms entitle Gradual Canticles. The Hebrew word Mahaloth \f - \ft Why the 15 Psalms following are called Gradual Canticles.\f* signifieth Steps, or Ascensions. The reason whereof Aadias, and some other Rabbins yield, for that they were sung with highest elevated notes, that can be in Music. The Talmud saith they are so called, because they were sung in the fifteen steps going up into the Temple. But S. Augustin, S. Basil, and other Christian Fathers expound them according to the history, and immediate prophetical sense, of the delivery of the Jews from captivity of Babylon, ascending into \f - \ft The historical sense.\f* Jerusalem: which is so situated on mountains, that the way from all parts was by ascending unto it. According to the Mystical sense, of ascending spiritually by virtues to perfection, and to \f - \ft The mystical sense.\f* eternal felicity. For the way tending to virtue (saith S. Basil) is like to certain steps (or degrees) by little and little bringing the man that loveth wisdom unto heaven. These Canticles therefore are prayers, mixed with consolations, for the joyful delivery of God's people, from that great captivity in Babylon, which the Psalmist King David saw in prophetical spirit, and which his posterity felt, and sometime endured. Which again as a figure signifieth the return and ascending of mankind from sin to grace, and from the miserable state of this world into heaven. Whereupon S. Augustine interpreteth this prophecy of the ascension, or elevation of the heart, from the vale of tears. In \f - \ft These Psalms are consolatory prayers and prophecies.\f* the mean time, whiles we are in this world, these Psalms are consolatory prayers, and prophetical assurance, that God's people, Catholic Christians, shall be delivered from the thraldom and persecution of Pagans, Turks, and Heretics. As partly we see by the delivery from the Roman persecuting Emperors, from the Vandals, Goths, and Huns: & therefore with assured confidence we hope and expect the like delivery from Turks, and all Heretics of Luther's brood.
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. When I was in tribulation I cried to our Lord: and \f a \fr 119.1 \ft Former experience of God's mercy in hearing the prayers of those that invocated him, giveth hope that he will hear in like case.\f* he heard me.
\v 2 O Lord deliver my soul from \f b \fr 119.2 \ft Nothing is more dangerous than untrue and deceitful tongues, nothing more damage than to diminish, and detract from the good fame of the just: and therefore this is a most necessary prayer, that God will deliver us from the wicked tongues of Turks, Heretics, and other wicked men.\f* unjust lips, and from a deceitful tongue.
\v 3 \f c \fr 119.3 \ft What punishment is great enough for wicked tongues?\f* What may be given thee, or what may be added unto thee to a deceitful tongue?
\v 4 \f d \fr 119.4 \ft Surely the malice of wicked tongues deserveth sharpest punishments to be afflicted by strong hands.\f* The sharp arrows of the mighty, with coals of desolation.
\v 5 \f e \fr 119.5 \ft Jews lamented their long absence from the holy land, especially from Jerusalem and the Temple. Christians mourn for their restraint from God's service, and long peregrination from heaven.\f* Woe is to me, that my sojourning is prolonged: I have dwelt with the \f f \fr 119.5 \ft Of Cedar the son of Ismael, came Mahomet, the Turks' false Prophet, whose tyranny is great and long. The name Cedar is interpreted, blackness, and obscurity, which signifieth darkness of error and sin.\f* inhabitants of Cedar:
\v 6 My soul hath been long a sojourner.
\v 7 With them that hated peace I was peaceable: when I spake to them, they impugned me \f g \fr 119.7 \ft The wicked afflict those that give no cause of offence.\f* without cause.
\c 120
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. I have lifted up mine eyes unto \f a \fr 120.1 \ft Towards Jerusalem, and towards heaven.\f* the mountains, \f b \fr 120.1 \ft All help cometh from heaven, that is, from God, who of his divine ordinance especially heareth prayers made in holy places. \+it 3. Reg. 7.\+it*\f* from whence help shall come to me.
\v 2 My help is from our Lord, who made heaven and earth.
\v 3 Give he not \f c \fr 120.3 \ft The just speaketh and wisheth well to his own soul. Especially the whole Church rejoiceth in God's assured protection.\f* thy foot to be moved: neither do he slumber that keepeth thee.
\v 4 Lo he shall not slumber nor sleep, that keepeth \f d \fr 120.4 \ft The militant Church.\f* Israel.
\v 5 Our Lord keepeth thee, our Lord is thy protection, upon thy right hand.
\v 6 By day \f e \fr 120.6 \ft Prosperity,\f* the sun shall not burn thee: nor \f f \fr 120.6 \ft nor adversity can overthrow the Church.\f* the moon by night.
\v 7 Our Lord doth keep thee from all evil: our Lord keep \f g \fr 120.7 \ft Spiritual life.\f* thy soul.
\v 8 Our Lord keep thy coming in, and thy going out: from hence forth now, and for ever.
\c 121
\cd A fervent and attentive prayer, to be delivered from captivity, or any other affliction. \f - \ft A prayer in affliction. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. I rejoiced in these things, which \f a \fr 121.1 \ft Diverse Prophets told the Jews in captivity, that they should return to Jerusalem. All Prophets, Christ also, and his Apostles, and Priests, preach the entrance, and joys of heaven to the just. All which the Psalmist saw in prophetical spirit, and rejoiced.\f* were said to me: We shall go into the house of our Lord.
\v 2 \f b \fr 121.2 \ft The Jews consider that sometimes they were joyful in the Temple of Jerusalem: Christians rejoice in the comfort they have in the militant Church.\f* Our feet were standing, in thy courts, o Jerusalem.
\v 3 Jerusalem, which is built as a city: whose \f c \fr 121.3 \ft Community and participation of spiritual graces, is a great joy to Catholics, whereof the same Prophet speaketh, \+it Psal. 118. v. 63.\+it* and often elsewhere.\f* participation is together in it-self.
\v 4 For \f d \fr 121.4 \ft All the twelve Tribes frequented Jerusalem,\f* thither did the tribes ascend, \f e \fr 121.4 \ft and all nations of the world do come to the Catholic Church.\f* the tribes of our Lord: the testimony of Israel to confess unto the name of our Lord.
\v 5 Because seats \f f \fr 121.5 \ft Seats of Judgement were placed in Jerusalem,\f* sat there in judgment, \f g \fr 121.5 \ft and seats of Judgement in the Catholic Church\f* seats upon the house \f h \fr 121.5 \ft of Christ.\f* of David.
\v 6 \f i \fr 121.6 \ft Christ exhorteth to ask,\f* Ask ye the things that are for the peace of Jerusalem: and \f k \fr 121.6 \ft and promiseth to give that is rightly asked, Christians also invite each other to pray for the Church.\f* aboundance to them that love thee.
\v 7 Peace be made in thy strength: and aboundance in thy towers.
\v 8 \f l \fr 121.8 \ft Christ prayeth for his Church,\f* For my brethren, and my neighbours' sakes, \f m \fr 121.8 \ft and gave his peace to the Apostles, and in them to their successors.\f* I spake peace of thee:
\v 9 For the house of our Lord God, I have sought \f n \fr 121.9 \ft For in heavenly Jerusalem, all good things are prepared, & are given to Saints reigning there for ever.\f* good things to thee.
\c 122
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. To thee have I lifted up mine eyes, which dwellest in the heavens.
\v 2 Behold as the eyes \f a \fr 122.2 \ft Servants expecting necessaries at their masters hands are commonly very attentive to receive that which they hope for: so must the faithful praying God, be very attentive, and not distracted in their prayers.\f* of servants, are on the hands of their masters, As the eyes \f b \fr 122.2 \ft Another example of handmaids, who generally are more diligent than men.\f* of the handmaid on the hands of her mistress: so are our eyes to our Lord God until he have mercy on us.
\v 3 Have mercy on us, o Lord, have mercy on us: because we are \f c \fr 122.3 \ft Though God suffer his servants to sustain some affliction and reproach, yet he heareth and delivereth them before they be oppressed.\f* much replenished with contempt.
\v 4 Because our soul is \f d \fr 122.4 \ft Seeing persecution still increase, or continue long, the faithful are then specially to conceive hope of speedy relief.\f* much replenished: reproach to them that abound, and contempt to the proud.
\c 123
\cd The Church, and holy members thereof, are always protected by God: 5. The wicked being suffered for a while, are at last punished. \f - \ft God's protection. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. But that our Lord \f a \fr 123.1 \ft If God had not sent his help and defence to us.\f* was in us, let Israel now say,
\v 2 but that our Lord was in us. \f b \fr 123.2 \ft We could not have escaped the force of our enemies.\f* When men rose up against us,
\v 3 \f c \fr 123.3 \ft The word (perhaps) doth not here import a doubt, or uncertainty, but in modest manner of asseveration, leaveth he judgement of the event that should happen (if God did not protect his servants) to their own consideration: which is an usual phrase in most languages.\f* perhaps they had swallowed us \f d \fr 123.3 \ft So suddenly should the weak, without God's protection be destroyed, as men are devoured & swallowed up by ravening wild beasts, even before they be throughly dead. So was Jonas swallowed into the whale's belly.\f* alive: When their fury was angry against us,
\v 4 perhaps \f e \fr 123.4 \ft Furious persecution.\f* water had swallowed us.
\v 5 Our soul hath passed through \f f \fr 123.5 \ft Sudden great troubles.\f* a torrent: perhaps our soul had passed through an intolerable water.
\v 6 Blessed be our Lord which hath not given us for a prey to their teeth.
\v 7 Our soul as a sparrow is delivered from the snare of the fowlers: The snare is \f g \fr 123.7 \ft Man's subtlety often deceiveth another man, but there is no counsel able to circumvent God.\f* broken, and we are delivered.
\v 8 Our help is in the name of our Lord, who made heaven and earth.
\c 124
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. They \f a \fr 124.1 \ft Those that confidently trust in God, are in such security as is mount Sion, which is a hill, defended also with other hills round about.\f* that trust in our Lord, as mount Sion: he shall not be moved for ever \f b \fr 124.1 \ft Another thing required to this security, is to dwell within Jerusalem, not the terrestrial city, for (as S. Augustin observeth) the Jews that dwelt therein are destroyed or made captives, and hitherto rejected of God, but in the Catholic Church.\f* that dwelleth
\v 2 in Jerusalem. Mountains round about it: and our Lord round about his people, from hence forth now and for ever.
\v 3 Because our Lord \f c \fr 124.3 \ft God will not always, nor finally leave his servants in tribulation, but only a while for their good.\f* will not leave the rod of sinners upon the lot of the just: that the just reach not their hands to iniquity.
\v 4 \f d \fr 124.4 \ft This prayer is also an asseveration, for it is certain that God will defend, and reward the good, and right of heart:\f* Do well, o Lord, to the good, and right of heart.
\v 5 But \f e \fr 124.5 \ft And no less assuredly God will punish not only the notorious wicked, and principal Authors of wickedness, but also all those that for fear or for commodity, or for any other cause, decline into obligations, bonds, covenants, or any way consent in external shew with the wicked against God: as in outward profession of Heresy, or Schism, though such temporizers do not think in their heart, that the pretended religion is true, whereto they are drawn to yield external conformity. For, as the Psalmist here teacheth, our Lord will bring all such accessory offenders, to the same judgement and punishment, with the principal workers of iniquity.\f* those that decline into obligations, our Lord will bring with them that work iniquity: \f f \fr 124.5 \ft All which being punished, then Jerusalem, the Catholic Church, shall have peace.\f* peace upon Israel.
\c 125
\cd Neither house nor city can be built, or kept without God's special providence and help, 3. those that trust in him shall prosper in their endeavours. \f - \ft God's help in all good works. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. When our Lord turned the captivity of Sion: we were made as \f a \fr 125.1 \ft The Jews released from captivity, were exceedingly, and almost incredibly comforted, as men for great, and unexpected sudden joy, think it rather a dream than a truth that they are delivered from miseries. So S. Peter when he was delivered out of prison by an Angel, thought it rather a vision, than a true delivery.\f* men comforted.
\v 2 Then was our mouth replenished with joy: and our tongue with exultation. Then \f b \fr 125.2 \ft Whereas in the two former verses (and very commonly) the Prophet speaketh in the pretertense, for the assurance of that he foresheweth, as if it were already done, yet here he uttereth his Prophecy in the future tense, that the Gentiles will confess that God dealeth magnifically with his people.\f* shall they say among the Gentiles: Our Lord hath done magnifically with them.
\v 3 \f c \fr 125.3 \ft The people also themselves gratefully confess that God dealeth magnifically with them.\f* Our Lord hath done magnifically with us: we are made joyful.
\v 4 \f d \fr 125.4 \ft The Prophet foreseeing all this in spirit, prayeth for the performance hereof.\f* Turn our captivity, o Lord, \f e \fr 125.4 \ft And that it may speedily be done, as a torrent that runneth in the south part of the world, is commonly very great, much desired, but scarce expected.\f* as a torrent in the South.
\v 5 \f f \fr 125.5 \ft This is the ordinary disposition of God, that his servants shall make their seeding, which is, do good works (saith S. Augustin) with tears, in tribulation upon earth:\f* They that sow in tears, \f g \fr 125.5 \ft and reap a plentiful harvest, the reward of their suffering and well working, in the next life. In assured hope whereof the Psalmist, and the whole Church joyfully conclude this Psalm with the two verses following.\f* shall reap in joyfulness.
\v 6 Going they went and wept, casting their seeds. But coming they shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves.
\c 126
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle \f a \fr 126.1 \ft God not permitting David to build the Temple, promised that his son should build it, and therefore besides other good admonitions, given to his son Salomon, he\f* of Salomon. Unless \f b \fr 126.1 \ft Unless God be the principal Angel, no work can prosper.\f* our Lord build the house, they have laboured in vain that build it. Unless our Lord keep the city, he watcheth in vain that keepeth it.
\v 2 It is vain for you to rise \f c \fr 126.2 \ft It is vain to attempt any thing without God's grace assisting:\f* before light: \f d \fr 126.2 \ft they that so doing, & thinking they have done something, rest\f* rise ye after ye have sitten, which eat \f e \fr 126.2 \ft after their painful travail, must rise again & begin anew, because that which they seem to have done well, is nothing worth, nor shall have reward.\f* the bread of sorrow.
\v 3 \f f \fr 126.3 \ft Contrarywise where God giving grace, those that truly love him, do good works,\f* When he shall give \f g \fr 126.3 \ft with great ease, and delight, as they take their sleep,\f* sleep to his beloved: behold \f h \fr 126.3 \ft they merit inheritance in heaven,\f* the inheritance of our Lord \f i \fr 126.3 \ft for their good works.\f* are children: \f k \fr 126.3 \ft The reward is promised to the true children of God, born to him in the womb of the Catholic Church.\f* the reward, the fruit of the womb.
\v 4 As \f l \fr 126.4 \ft Moreover as a strong archer striketh deep with his arrows; so they that patiently suffer much in this world,\f* arrows in the hand of the mighty: so are the \f m \fr 126.4 \ft do multiply good works.\f* children of them that are shaken.
\v 5 \f n \fr 126.5 \ft Such shall be very happy,\f* Blessed is the man that hath filled his desire of them: he shall \f o \fr 126.5 \ft & very easily answer all that can be objected against them\f* not be confounded when he shall speak to his enemies \f p \fr 126.5 \ft in the day of Judgement.\f* in the gate.
\c 127
\cd The Church often (3. and much) impugned, is not overcome. 4. Her persecuters are destroyed, 6. contemned, 8. and cursed. \f - \ft The Church still firm in persecution. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. Blessed are all that fear our Lord, that walk in his ways.
\v 2 Because thou shalt eat the labours of thy hands: blessed art thou, and it shall be well with thee.
\v 3 Thy \f a \fr 127.3 \ft If such servants of God be married, they shall ordinarily have issue, & succession in their family, but especially the souls of such shall bring forth many meritorious works,\f* wife as a fruitful vine, in \f b \fr 127.3 \ft in the bosom of the Catholic Church, which was founded in Christ's side.\f* the sides of thy house. \f c \fr 127.3 \ft Children also signify good works.\f* Thy children as young plants of olive-trees, round about thy table.
\v 4 Behold so shall the man be blessed, that feareth our Lord.
\v 5 Our Lord out of Sion bless thee: and that thou mayst see the good things of Jerusalem all the days of thy life.
\v 6 And that thou mayst see thy \f d \fr 127.6 \ft Reward in heaven for good works in earth.\f* children's children, peace upon Israel.
\c 128
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. \f a \fr 128.1 \ft Israel which is the Church of God, rejoicing saith: that enemies have often,\f* Often have they impugned me \f b \fr 128.1 \ft even from the beginning of the world, persecuted me, as when Cain persecuted Abel, other wicked persecuted Seth, Enoch, Noe: the Chaldeas persecuted Abraham: the Ægyptians persecuted the Israelites: and so in other generations.\f* from my youth, let Israel now say:
\v 2 Often have they impugned me from my youth: \f c \fr 128.2 \ft But they have never overcome me. So the Psalmist testifieth for all times past, & prophesieth the same for times to come.\f* but they have not prevailed against me.
\v 3 Sinners \f d \fr 128.3 \ft Persecuters not being able to overthrow or suppress the Church, have laid great weighty burdens of tribulations upon her back, which she hath patiently and strongly borne,\f* have builded upon my back: they have \f e \fr 128.3 \ft they have still persisted, one sort after another, but with longanimity the Church hath stood fast, and constantly passed through all distresses. In moral sense sinners build iniquity upon the back of the Church, yea and upon God's back, when they presume to sin, trusting in the end to be absolved by virtue of holy Sacraments left in the Church. Likewise when they excuse their sins, imputing the cause to other creatures of God, whereby they are allured. Which is in effect (saith S. Augustin) to accuse God, and to build iniquities on God's back, who made those creatures.\f* prolonged their iniquity.
\v 4 \f f \fr 128.4 \ft God therefore who is just, will at last cast such presumptuous sinners from his back, and break their stiff necks:\f* Our just Lord will cut the necks of sinners:
\v 5 let them all \f g \fr 128.5 \ft then shall they be confounded,\f* be confounded and \f h \fr 128.5 \ft separated, eternally from God, become like fruitless and withered grass, cast away, despised, yea cursed of all, and blessed by none, as the Prophet denounceth in the next verses.\f* turned backward, that hate Sion.
\v 6 Let them be made as grass in the tops of houses: which is withered before it be plucked up.
\v 7 Whereof the reaper hath not filled his hand, and he that gathereth the sheaves his bosom.
\v 8 And they said not that passed by: The blessing of our Lord be upon you: we have blessed you in the name of our Lord.
\c 129
\cd The Jews, or other people in tribulation for sin, or temporal captivity, cry to God to be delivered: 4. trusting and encouraging each other in God's accustomed mercy, assuredly hoping that he will redeem & deliver them. \f - \ft The sixth penitential Psalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. From \f a \fr 129.1 \ft This prayer agreeth to all true penitents, crying to God for help, being either in depth of sorrow for sin, and so it is one of the Penitential Psalms; or the depth of fervent desire to ascend towards perfection in virtue, and from this vale of misery into heaven, and so it is a Gradual Psalm; or in the depth of temporal pains, and so it is a special prayer for souls in Purgatory, offered by the Church in their behalf.\f* the depths I have cried to thee, o Lord:
\v 2 Lord hear my voice: Let thine ears be intent to the voice of my petition.
\v 3 If thou shalt observe iniquities, o Lord: Lord \f b \fr 129.3 \ft None is able to abide the rigour of God's justice.\f* who shall sustain it?
\v 4 Because with thee there is \f c \fr 129.4 \ft But all must rely upon his mercy.\f* propitiation: and for \f d \fr 129.4 \ft For thy promises made in the law, that thou wilt remit sins to the penitent, give more grace to them that seek it, and mitigate also the pains due for sins.\f* thy law I have expected thee, o Lord. My soul hath expected in his word:
\v 5 my soul hath hoped in our Lord.
\v 6 From \f e \fr 129.6 \ft The hope of penitents is like to the watches of the day time, from morning until night, which are more comfortable than watches of the night:\f* the morning watch even until night: let Israel hope in our Lord.
\v 7 Because with our Lord there is mercy: and with him \f f \fr 129.7 \ft The greatest comfort is in Christ our Redeemer, whose plentiful Redemption bringeth more aboundance of grace.\f* plenteous redemption.
\v 8 And he shall redeem \f g \fr 129.8 \ft Christ's Redemption being sufficient for all the world, is effectual only to true living members of the Catholic Church.\f* Israel, from all his iniquities.
\c 130
\cd The Psalmist earnestly prayeth God to give him leave, & to shew him where to build a Temple. But more especially prayeth for, and prophesieth the coming of Christ, the promised Son of David: 14. signifying God's promise thereof, and of establishing his Church. \f - \ft Christ's coming to restore man. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle of \f a \fr 130.1 \ft David by God's special grace having a sincere mind towards all men, even towards his enemies, and an humble heart, not desiring any thing ambitiously, but all to the honour of God, proposeth his own example, for others to imitate: that they may with him offer the sacrifice of humility and innocency unto God, from whom all good things proceed.\f* David. Lord my heart is not exalted: \f b \fr 130.1 \ft All this with a thankful mind to God who gave this grace.\f* neither are mine eyes lofty. Neither have I walked in great matters: nor in marvelous things above me.
\v 2 If I was not humbly minded: but exalted my soul: As \f c \fr 130.2 \ft As children after they are weaned come still willingly to their mother, so doth the child of God rely upon God's help, though he always feel not the same sweetness:\f* the weaned child is toward his mother, so \f d \fr 130.2 \ft & according to his demeanor herein, he expecteth reward.\f* retribution in my soul.
\v 3 \f e \fr 130.3 \ft King David, or any other, being for his virtue advanced, & rewarded by God, is a good example to move others to do the like, so shall they receive like reward.\f* Let Israel hope in our Lord, from henceforth now and for ever.
\c 131
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. Remember \f a \fr 131.1 \ft It is an usual thing that David, Moyses, & other Prophets speak of themselves in the third person.\f* David, o Lord, and all his meekness:
\v 2 As he sware to our Lord, \f b \fr 131.2 \ft King David desiring, and so far as lay in him, promising to build a Temple to God, with great instance prayeth that he might perform the same. But God disposing otherwise, that not he, but his son should build it, he nevertheless prepared the matter, workmen, and money, shewed the form, and disposed the Levites how to serve therein.\f* vowed a vow to the God of Jacob.
\v 3 \f c \fr 131.3 \ft Moreover by vow deprived himself of entering into his own house, or taking his ordinary rest, till he might (if it so pleased God) know the place where it should be built.\f* If I shall enter into the tabernacle of my house, if I shall ascend into the bed of my couch.
\v 4 If I shall give sleep to mine eyes, and slumbering to mine eye lids:
\v 5 And rest to my temples: until I find a place for our Lord, a tabernacle for the God of Jacob.
\v 6 Behold we have heard of it \f d \fr 131.6 \ft It was revealed to David, that the Temple should be built in that part of Jerusalem, which looketh towards Bethleem (otherwise called Ephrata) where our Saviour was born.\f* in Ephrata: we have found it in \f e \fr 131.6 \ft Within Jerusalem, which is compassed with woods. In this vision also the whole form of the Temple was revealed unto him, as he testifieth. \+it 1. Par. 28. v. 19.\+it* All things, quoth he, came written with the hand of our Lord unto me: that I might understand all the works of the pattern.\f* the fields of the wood.
\v 7 \f f \fr 131.7 \ft Holy David moved with exceeding devotion, repaired to the place, where God's Temple should be built,\f* We will enter into his tabernacle: we will \f g \fr 131.7 \ft & adored God, where the Propitiatory, as a footstool representing God's presence, should stand. What marvel then if devotion move Christians to visit the holy places, where our Saviour, God and Man was Incarnate, was born, suffered death, was buried, ascended into heaven, or any\f* adore in the place where his feet stood.
\v 8 \f h \fr 131.8 \ft O God, leaving Silo, Gabaon, and the like places, come into thy holy Temple,\f* Arise Lord into thy rest, thou, and \f i \fr 131.8 \ft with the ark of covenant, where thou sanctifiest thy people. And here again the Prophet illuminated with a higher Mystery, and inflamed with more devotion, prayeth for Christ's coming into the world, and prophesieth that after his Passion he will rise, not only in glory of soul, but also of body, prefigured by the Ark of testimony, which was in the tabernacle, and after in the Temple.\f* the ark of thy sanctification.
\v 9 Let thy Priests be clothed with \f k \fr 131.9 \ft Grant therefore that thy Priests, which must offer sacrifice in this sacred place, be endued with virtues, and good life worthy of their degree:\f* justice: & let thy \f l \fr 131.9 \ft and the Levites who are ordained to serve there, be likewise made fit for their diverse functions, both in the old and new Testament.\f* saints rejoice.
\v 10 \f m \fr 131.10 \ft And seeing thou hast given such meekness, devotion, zeal, sincerity, and other virtues, making him a man according to thine own heart, and thereupon promised to establish his see,\f* For David thy servant's sake, \f n \fr 131.10 \ft defer not to send thy promised Messias, Christ our Redeemer.\f* turn not away the face of thy Christ.
\v 11 Our Lord \f o \fr 131.11 \ft Hence forth to the end of this Psalm the Prophet relateth God's revelation to him: That he hath truly promised with an oath,\f* hath sworn truth to David, and \f p \fr 131.11 \ft and will perform,\f* he will not disappoint it: \f q \fr 131.11 \ft to set one of David's sons upon his Throne: which was not only fulfilled in Salomon, who reigned in great peace, and builded the Temple, but especially in Christ the Son of David, to whom our Lord God gave the seat of David his father, & he shall reign in the house of Jacob for ever, and of his Kingdom there shall be no end. Luc. 1. v. 32. 33. By this promise Saint Peter also proveth Christ's Resurrection. Act. 2. v. 30.\f* Of the fruit of thy womb I will set upon thy seat.
\v 12 \f r \fr 131.12 \ft Concerning the children of Christ, members of his Kingdom the Church, the promise is conditional, if they observe God's commandments, they shall sit upon his seat: be coheirs of his Kingdom. \+it Rom. 8. v. 17.\+it*\f* If thy children shall keep my testament, and these my testimonies which I will teach them: Their children also even for ever, shall sit upon thy seat.
\v 13 Because our Lord hath chosen Sion: he hath chosen it for an habitation to himself.
\v 14 This is my \f s \fr 131.14 \ft Sion, the Catholic Church, is a perpetual place where God dwelleth.\f* rest for ever and ever: here will I dwell because I have chosen it.
\v 15 Blessing I will bless her \f t \fr 131.15 \ft The Church wanting Christ's visible presence, is replenished with many blessings,\f* widow: her \f v \fr 131.15 \ft and all her humble children are relieved with holy Sacraments.\f* poor I will fill with breads.
\v 16 Her \f w \fr 131.16 \ft The Church hath always some holy Priests\f* Priests I will clothe with salvation: and her \f x \fr 131.16 \ft and some holy people.\f* saints shall rejoice with joyfulness.
\v 17 \f y \fr 131.17 \ft Of this Church, elected by God, Christ is the Protector, and strong defence.\f* Thither will I bring forth a horn to David, I have prepared \f z \fr 131.17 \ft And the same Church shall be a perpetual lamp, whereby others may come to him.\f* a lamp to my Christ.
\v 18 His enemies I will clothe with confusion: but upon him shall my sanctification flourish.
\c 132
\cd Fraternal concord is commended to all in the Church, as necessary and delectable in itself, and blessed of God. \f - \ft Fraternal concord. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle \f a \fr 132.1 \ft David being a most peaceable man, even toward his enemies, is added by Esdras in this title, as an example for others to imitate.\f* of David. Behold \f b \fr 132.1 \ft That concord is both good and pleasant, needeth no other proof, but only to consider & to behold the fruit thereof in every community, and especially\f* how good and how pleasant a thing it is, for brethren to dwell in one.
\v 2 \f c \fr 132.2 \ft The ointment, wherewith Aaron and other High Priests were consecrated, was precious, and most odoriferous, signifying grace bought by Christ's blood, and producing virtues, which make sweet savour:\f* As ointment on \f d \fr 132.2 \ft was poured upon the High Priest's head, who being head of the Church,\f* the head, which ran down upon \f e \fr 132.2 \ft unity and concord descended from him to other Priests,\f* the beard, the beard of Aaron, which ran down unto \f f \fr 132.2 \ft and so to the people, even to the lowest and meanest in God's Church.\f* the hem of his garment.
\v 3 \f g \fr 132.3 \ft Another similitude to declare the excellency of concord: As the dew of mount Hermon, which is perpetually covered with snow, that falleth from the heaven, thence descending refresheth & fructifieth the hill of Sion: so mutual concord, and fraternal charity amongst the faithful, nourisheth each other, making them fruitful in all good works.\f* As the dew of Hermon, which runneth down upon mount Sion. Because \f h \fr 132.3 \ft Where there is unity God giveth aboundance of grace, as where the Apostles with other faithful were gathered, and continued in prayer, the Holy Ghost came upon them all. And the multitude of believers (then increasing) had one heart and one soul. \+it Act. 2.\+it* & \+it 4. v. 32.\+it*\f* there hath our Lord commanded blessing, and life even for ever.
\c 133
\cd God who only is omnipotent, and fountain of all goodness, is of all to be praised. 8. He is the special protector of his elected people. 15. Contrarywise false gods are impotent, vain, and cannot help those that serve them. 19. Only the Church doth rightly praise him. \f - \ft God's perfection and goodness. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. Lo \f a \fr 133.1 \ft This last Gradual Psalm, as a conclusion exhorteth all to praise God.\f* now bless our Lord, all ye the servants of our Lord: Which stand in the house of our Lord, in the courts of the house of our God.
\v 2 In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy places, and bless ye our Lord.
\v 3 Our Lord out of Sion bless thee, who \f b \fr 133.3 \ft For to this end he made all creatures in heaven & in earth.\f* made heaven and earth.
\c 134
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Praise ye the name of our Lord, \f a \fr 134.1 \ft God's servants.\f* ye servants praise our Lord.
\v 2 Ye \f b \fr 134.2 \ft Clergy that serve in the Temple,\f* that stand in the house of our Lord, in \f c \fr 134.2 \ft and ye Laity that attend to his service, must praise him for these causes.\f* the courts of the house of our God.
\v 3 Praise ye our Lord, because our Lord is \f d \fr 134.3 \ft his goodness.\f* good: sing ye to his name, because it is \f e \fr 134.3 \ft his benignity.\f* sweet.
\v 4 Because our Lord \f f \fr 134.4 \ft He hath adopted us his children.\f* hath chosen Jacob to himself, Israel for his own possession.
\v 5 Because I have known that our Lord is \f g \fr 134.5 \ft He is omnipotent, as appeareth by his works, here and elsewhere recited: and many ways known to the world.\f* great, and our God above all gods.
\v 6 All things whatsoever our Lord would he hath done, in heaven, in earth, in the sea, and in all the depths.
\v 7 Bring forth clouds from the uttermost of the earth: lightnings he hath turned into rain. Who bringeth forth the winds out of his treasures:
\v 8 who struck the first begotten of Ægypt from man even to beast.
\v 9 He sent forth signs and wonders in the midst of thee, o Ægypt: upon Pharao, and upon all his servants. \x - \xt Exo. 11.\x*
\v 10 Who struck many nations: and slew strong kings:
\v 11 Sehon the king of the Amorrheites, & Og the king of Basan, and all the Kingdoms of Chanaan. \x - \xt Ios. 12. Num. 21. Ios. 13.\x*
\v 12 And he gave their land for inheritance, for an inheritance to Israel his people.
\v 13 Lord thy name is for ever: Lord thy memorial is unto generation and generation.
\v 14 Because our Lord shall judge his people: and will be entreated toward his servants.
\v 15 The idols of the Gentiles are silver, and gold, the works of men's hands.
\v 16 They have mouth, & shall not speak: they have eyes, and shall not see.
\v 17 They have ears, and shall not hear: for neither is there breath in their mouth.
\v 18 Let them that make them become like to them: and all that have confidence in them.
\v 19 Ye house of Israel bless our Lord: ye house of Aaron bless our Lord.
\v 20 Ye house of Levi bless our Lord: you that fear our Lord, bless ye our Lord.
\v 21 Blessed be our Lord out of Sion, who dwelleth in Jerusalem.
\c 135
\cd God being marvelous in himself, 4. hath shewed his power and goodness in his works, as well in general to all the world, 10. as in particular towards his elected people. \f - \ft God's marvelous works. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Confess ye to our Lord \f a \fr 135.1 \ft Whose goodness is incomparable.\f* because he is good: \f b \fr 135.1 \ft This second part of every verse, first pronounced by the Priests, confessing & praising God's mercy above all his works, was still repeated by musicians, or other assistants, in manner as now in Litanies (o Lord deliver us) and (We beseech thee to hear us.) And as Gloria Patri, &c. is added in the end of Psalms, and our Lord's prayer, and the Angelical salutation are often repeated in diverse Offices, and in the Rosary.\f* because his mercy is for ever.
\v 2 Confess ye to \f c \fr 135.2 \ft Whose divine power created all other things.\f* the God of gods: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 3 \f d \fr 135.3 \ft By this invitation to praise our Lord God, thrice repeated, we profess the Blessed Trinity, who is one God in substance, and three Divine Persons.\f* Confess ye to \f e \fr 135.3 \ft Whose dominion is over all the world.\f* the Lord of lords: because his mercy is for ever
\v 4 Who \f f \fr 135.4 \ft Only God can do true miracles; to wit, works above the course of all nature.\f* only doth great marvels: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 5 Who made the heaven \f g \fr 135.5 \ft In admirable wisdom.\f* in understanding: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 6 Who established \f h \fr 135.6 \ft Made the waters above nature to remain within their bonds, & not to cover the earth.\f* the earth over the waters: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 7 Who made the great lights: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 8 The sun to rule the day: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 9 The moon and stars to rule the night: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 10 Who struck Ægypt with their first-begotten: because his mercy is for ever. \x - \xt Exo. 11.\x*
\v 11 Who brought forth Israel out of the midst of them: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 12 In a mighty hand, and lofty arm: because his mercy is for ever. \x - \xt Exo. 13.\x*
\v 13 Who divided the Red sea into divisions: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 14 And brought forth Israel through the midst thereof: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 15 And he overthrew Pharao, and his host in the Red sea: because his mercy is for ever. \x - \xt Exo. 14.\x*
\v 16 Who led his people through the desert: because his mercy is for ever. \x - \xt Num. 21.\x*
\v 17 Who struck great kings: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 18 And slew strong kings: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 19 Sehon the king of the Amorrheites: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 20 And Og the king of Basan: because his mercy is for ever:
\v 21 And he gave their land for an inheritance: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 22 For an inheritance to Israel his servant: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 23 For in our humiliation he was mindful of us: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 24 And he redeemed us from our enemies: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 25 Who giveth food to \f i \fr 135.25 \ft All God's benignity, as well of particular benefits towards his people, as general to them and all others, proceedeth from his mercy.\f* all flesh: because his mercy is for ever.
\v 26 Confess ye to the God of heaven: because his mercy is for ever. Confess ye to the Lord of lords: because his mercy is for ever.
\c 136
\cd The Prophet describeth how lamentably the people in captivity of Babylon will bewail the want of means to serve God, and of their native soil: 7. with just desire of their enemies' punishment. \f - \ft The Jews' lamentation in captivity. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm of David \f a \fr 136.1 \ft By adding to this title (for Jeremy) the Septuagint Interpreters signify that this Psalm treateth of the same captivity, in which Jeremias writ his Lamentations.\f* for Jeremy. Upon the rivers \f b \fr 136.1 \ft Near the rivers in Chaldea, whereof Babylon was the head city,\f* of Babylon, there we \f c \fr 136.1 \ft the Jews remained mourning,\f* sat and wept: whilst we \f d \fr 136.1 \ft remembering the holy rites and service of God, which had been in Sion, whereof they were deprived in the captivity.\f* remembered Sion.
\v 2 On the willows in the midst thereof, we hanged up \f e \fr 136.2 \ft All their musical instruments, as having no use of them.\f* our instruments.
\v 3 Because there they that led us captive, \f f \fr 136.3 \ft Either in earnest or in scorn the Chaldees willed them to sing, as they were accustomed in their country.\f* demanded of us words of songs. And they that led us away: Sing ye an hymn to us of the songs of Sion.
\v 4 \f g \fr 136.4 \ft They excused themselves, and refused to sing sacred Psalms before profane people, neither had they mind to sing in that mourning state of captivity.\f* How shall we sing the song of our Lord in a strange land?
\v 5 \f h \fr 136.5 \ft The people shew not only their fervent present desire to serve God in Jerusalem, but also their firm purpose still to desire the same, wishing that if they forget it, or lose this affection, their right hands, or what soever is most dear, or necessary for them, may be forgotten, not conserved, but suffered to perish.\f* If I shall forget thee, o Jerusalem, let my right hand be forgotten.
\v 6 \f i \fr 136.6 \ft If I lose this affection, let me also lose the use of my tongue.\f* Let my tongue cleave to my jaws, if I do not remember thee: If I shall not set Jerusalem in the beginning of my joy.
\v 7 Be mindful, o Lord, of \f k \fr 136.7 \ft The Idumeans incensed the Chaldees to be cruel against the Jews, whereof they pray for just revenge, and withal the Psalmist prophesieth that it will be revenged, which Isaias also prophesieth, \+it c. 21. v. 11.\+it*\f* the children of Edom, in \f l \fr 136.7 \ft for their rejoicing in Jerusalem's misery.\f* the day of Jerusalem: \f m \fr 136.7 \ft The voice of the Idumeans, inciting the Babylonians utterly to destroy Jerusalem.\f* That say: Rase it, rase it, even unto the foundation thereof.
\v 8 \f n \fr 136.8 \ft A Prophecy that the people of Babylon should also be punished, for their cruelty against the Jews, whereof Isaias\f* Daughter of Babylon miserable: blessed is he, that shall repay thee thy payment, which thou hast payed us.
\v 9 Blessed is he \f o \fr 136.9 \ft God will bless, or reward them that shall severely afflict the Babylonians,\f* that shall hold, and \f p \fr 136.9 \ft not sparing their children. Morally he is blessed, that mortifieth his own passions, cutteth off first ill motions, or punisheth venial sins, that they grow not strong within his soul, and so draw it to commit mortal sin. S. Aug. hic, & S. Greg. in fine expos. Psal. 4. pænit.\f* shall dash thy little ones against the rock.
\c 137
\cd God's knowledge, 7. and presence (10. without the help, or hinderance of any thing) extendeth to all things, times, and places. 17. He giveth exceeding great honour to his saints, 20. the wicked, as enemies of God, are justly hated, 23. the just pray for God's perpetual direction. \f - \ft God's special providence of his servants. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 137.1 \ft This Psalm is a fit form of thanks for David, or any other servant of God.\f* David himself. I will \f b \fr 137.1 \ft Iwill render thanks and praise.\f* confess to thee, o Lord, in my whole heart: \f c \fr 137.1 \ft God ever heareth the prayer that is rightly made, for which the suppliant is therefore to give thanks.\f* because thou hast heard the words of my mouth. In \f d \fr 137.1 \ft Angels are present where the faithful pray, observe our prayers, and offer them to God, if they be sincere, as the prayers of Tobias, and Cornelius.\f* the sight of Angels I will sing to thee:
\v 2 I will adore \f e \fr 137.2 \ft When there was not access to the Temple, yet the Jews praying in captivity, turned themselves towards the Temple.\f* toward thy holy temple, and will confess to thy name: \f 1 \fr 137.2 \ft \+it 1. Cor. v. 10. Tob. 12. Act. 10. Psal. 24. v. 10.\+it*\f* For thy mercy, and thy truth: because thou hast magnified above every thing \f f \fr 137.2 \ft The Name and Majesty of GOD, the WORD (which is the Son of God) also the name of JESUS, is magnified above all names, or temporal things.\f* thy holy name.
\v 3 In what day soever I shall invocate thee, hear me: thou wilt multiply strength in my soul.
\v 4 Let \f g \fr 137.4 \ft Sooner or later Kings and Princes of all Kingdoms and nations, have been or shall be connected to Christ.\f* all the kings of the earth, o Lord, confess to thee: because they have heard all the words of thy mouth:
\v 5 And let them sing in the ways of our Lord: because great is the glory of our Lord.
\v 6 Because our Lord is high, and he beholdeth low things: and high things he knoweth \f h \fr 137.6 \ft God knoweth proud men, not as his friends or servants, but far off, as strangers and enemies.\f* far off.
\v 7 If I shall walk in the midst of tribulation, thou wilt quicken me: and upon the wrath of mine enemies thou hast extended thy hand, and thy right hand hath saved me.
\v 8 Our Lord will repay for me: o Lord thy mercy is for ever: despise not the works of thy hands.
\c 138
\p
\v 1 Unto \f a \fr 138.1 \ft By this part of the title (to the end) is signified (as is noted Psal. 4.) that the matter contained in the Psalm, pertaineth to the new Testament.\f* the end, a Psalm of David. Lord thou \f b \fr 138.1 \ft God who knoweth all things most absolutely and perfectly, without discourse or searching, yet as it were, maketh experimental trial of his servants, to make them in some sort to know him, and to know themselves. And so here holy David or other faithful man, acknowledgeth God's Omniscience, that is, perfect knowledge of all things, without exception, past,\f* hast proved me, and hast known me:
\v 2 thou hast known my sitting down, and my rising up.
\v 3 Thou hast understood my cogitations far off: my path, and \f c \fr 138.3 \ft The uttermost measure and reach of mine intention.\f* my cord thou hast searched out.
\v 4 And thou hast foreseen all my ways: because there is not a word in my \f d \fr 138.4 \ft The word holden in by the tongue, and not uttered by mouth, is not hidden from God.\f* tongue.
\v 5 Behold, o Lord, thou hast known all the last things, & them of old: thou hast formed me, and hast put thy hand upon me.
\v 6 Thy knowledge is \f e \fr 138.6 \ft By experience we see that God's knowledge exceedeth our reach.\f* become marvelous of me: it is made great, and I cannot reach to it.
\v 7 \f f \fr 138.7 \ft As God's knowledge comprehendeth all things, so his presence extendeth itself to all places, neither is contained in place, but exceedeth all place, in his divine immensity.\f* Whither shall I go from thy spirit? And whither shall I fly from thy face?
\v 8 If I shall ascend into heaven, thou art there: if I descend into hell, thou art present.
\v 9 If I shall take my wings early, and dwell in the extreme parts of the sea.
\v 10 Certes thither also shall thy hand conduct me: and thy right hand shall hold me.
\v 11 \f g \fr 138.11 \ft The Prophet also in the person of any curious imaginative man, examineth and findeth that no darkness nor cover can hide any thing from God.\f* And I said: Perhaps darkness shall tread over me: and the night is mine illumination in my delights.
\v 12 For darkness shall not be darkened from thee, and the night shall be lightened as the day: as the darkness thereof, so also the light thereof.
\v 13 Because thou hast possessed \f h \fr 138.13 \ft Nothing seemeth more hidden, than a man's entrails,\f* my reins: thou hast received me from \f i \fr 138.13 \ft or a child in the mother's womb.\f* my mother's womb.
\v 14 I will confess to thee, because thou art terribly magnified: thy works are marvelous, & my soul knoweth exceedingly.
\v 15 My \f k \fr 138.15 \ft Or bones in the flesh.\f* bone is not hid from thee, which thou madest in secret: and my substance in the lower parts of the earth.
\v 16 \f l \fr 138.16 \ft Or man's bodily imperfection before his birth,\f* Mine \f 1 \fr 138.16 \ft \+it Colem, Embryonem.\+it*\f* imperfection thine eyes have seen, & in thy book \f 2 \fr 138.16 \ft \+it Of knowledge.\+it*\f* all shall be written: \f m \fr 138.16 \ft daily formed by God, not by man. \+it Job. 10. v. 8. 2. Mac. 7. v. 22. 23.\+it*\f* days shall be formed, & no man in them.
\v 17 \f n \fr 138.17 \ft Above all considerations it most exceedeth, that God so high and infinite, honoureth his humble poor servants so exceedingly, that it seemeth to themselves far more than can be due. For he rewardeth even over & above merits; which merits also are founded in God's mercy given without merit.\f* But to me thy \f 1 \fr 138.17 \ft New translaters pervert this place, translating (thoughts) for (friends) contrary to the Hebrew, Greek, and Latin, and all ancient Fathers, only pretending that the same word in the Chaldee tongue also signifieth thoughts.\f* friends, o God, are become honourable exceedingly: their principality is exceedingly strengthened.
\v 18 \f o \fr 138.18 \ft The number also of Saints whom God hath chosen, called, justified and will glorify, exceed man's conceit. \+it Apoc. 7.\+it*\f* I will number them, and they shall be multiplied above the sand: \f p \fr 138.18 \ft Incensed with this excellent glory, and desiring to be of this innumerable multitude, by thy grace I have risen from sin, and in confidence of thy perpetual help, I stand and hope to persevere in thy service.\f* I rose up and I am yet with thee.
\v 19 \f q \fr 138.19 \ft And if it be so, yea seeing it is so, that as thou, o God, doest exalt thy Saints to exceeding and unspeakable honour: so thou hast also decreed to punish obstinate sinners with eternal death and damnation:\f* If thou shalt kill sinners, o God: \f r \fr 138.19 \ft I renounce all wicked association, get ye away from me all cruel bloody men, that would draw me into everlasting torments.\f* ye men of blood depart from me.
\v 20 \f s \fr 138.20 \ft Away from me, you that think,\f* Because you say in thought: they \f t \fr 138.20 \ft the glorious mansions in heaven, prepared and promised to the just, are vain hopes, and in vain expected.\f* shall receive thy cities in vain.
\v 21 \f v \fr 138.21 \ft This hate of such sinners the just shall confidently plead, and happy are they that shall be able truly to allege for themselves in the day of judgement, that they hated all, whom God hateth,\f* Did not I hate them that hate thee, o Lord: and \f w \fr 138.21 \ft yea hated them with fervent zeal, that are God's enemies.\f* pined away because of thine enemies?
\v 22 \f x \fr 138.22 \ft Still the Prophet inculcateth this necessary perfect hatred\f* with perfect hatred did I hate them: they are become \f y \fr 138.22 \ft and enmity towards God's enemies.\f* enemies to me.
\v 23 \f z \fr 138.23 \ft For that none in this life (without special and extraordinary revelation) knoweth certainly their own state, whether they be worthy of God's love or hatred \+it (Eccle. 9.)\+it* the just submit themselves to God's examination of their heart and actions:\f* Prove me, o God, and know my heart: examine me, and know my paths.
\v 24 And \f a \fr 138.24 \ft Humbly praying God, that if they be in the way of iniquity,\f* see, if the way of iniquity be in me: \f b \fr 138.24 \ft he will vouchsafe to reduce and guide them into the right way of everlasting life.\f* and conduct me in the everlasting way.
\c 139
\cd The just diversly afflicted by the wicked, pray to be defended, 7. repose their confidence in God. 10. Who will adjudge the reprobate to eternal punishment, 13. and reward the good with the fruition of himself. \f - \ft Eternal pain of the wicked, and joy of the blessed. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Unto the end, a Psalm of David.
\v 2 Deliver me, o Lord, from the evil man: from the unjust man rescue me.
\v 3 Which \f a \fr 139.3 \ft Unquiet evil disposed, men still devise wicked plots\f* have devised iniquity in their heart: \f b \fr 139.3 \ft and never cease to make discord and debates.\f* all the day they did appoint battles. They have whet their tongues as that of a serpent:
\v 4 the venom of asps is under their lips.
\v 5 Keep me, o Lord, from the hand of the sinner: and from unjust men deliver me.
\v 6 Who have devised to supplant my steps: the proud have hid a snare for me: And they have stretched out ropes for a snare: they have laid \f 1 \fr 139.6 \ft \+it Scandalum.\+it*\f* a stumbling block for me near the way.
\v 7 \f c \fr 139.7 \ft In time of tentation prayer is most necessary.\f* I said to our Lord: Thou art my God: hear, o Lord, the voice of my petition.
\v 8 O Lord, Lord \f d \fr 139.8 \ft Man is not able to resist tentations,\f* the strength of my salvation: \f e \fr 139.8 \ft unless God by his grace, as by a helmet, defend him from yielding consent.\f* thou hast overshadowed my head in the day of battle.
\v 9 Yield me not, o Lord, from \f f \fr 139.9 \ft Suffer me not to fall from that which I now desire (which is to be constant in virtue) not to consent to sinners' persuasions.\f* my desire to the sinner: they have devised against me, forsake me not, \f g \fr 139.9 \ft Who then would triumph over me.\f* lest they perhaps be proud.
\v 10 \f h \fr 139.10 \ft The sum of their mischievous devices,\f* The head of their compass: \f i \fr 139.10 \ft consisting in their subtle persuasions with sweet words,\f* the labour of their lips \f k \fr 139.10 \ft shall overwhelm, and bring themselves to perdition.\f* shall cover them.
\v 11 \f l \fr 139.11 \ft Hell fire shall be their lot,\f* Coals shall fall upon them, \f m \fr 139.11 \ft into which they shall fall,\f* thou shalt cast them down into fire: in miseries \f n \fr 139.11 \ft & ever be in intolerable torments.\f* they shall not stand up.
\v 12 Aman full of tongue shall not be directed in the earth: evils shall take the unjust man into destruction.
\v 13 \f o \fr 139.13 \ft Ihave learned by good instructions, and by experience, that in the end thou, o God, wilt comfort the just, who are now afflicted, and punish the unjust, that live in temporal delights, as it happened to Lazarus and the glutton.\f* I have known that our Lord will do the judgment of the needy: and the revenge of the poor.
\v 14 But as for the just, they shall confess to thy name: and the righteous shall dwell with \f p \fr 139.14 \ft Eternal glory consisteth in seeing God.\f* thy countenance.
\c 140
\cd The Church prayeth that her children may avoid sinful words, 4. not make excuses of sins committed, not communicate with others in sin, nor to hearken to flatterers: 6. but to pray that they may amend (the Psalmist by the way prophesieth that many shall be converted.) 8. Though sometimes persecution be great, the Church faileth not. \f - \ft The Church prayeth and prevaileth. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm of David. Lord I have cried to thee, hear me: attend to my voice when I shall cry to thee.
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 140.2 \ft In these words the Church prayeth, offering incense in her solemn Offices.\f* Let my prayer be directed as \f a \fr 140.2 \ft As the fume of incense is sweet and ascendeth upwards: so the Church prayeth that her petitions may be grateful, and ascend to God.\f* incense in thy sight: the elevation of my hands as \f b \fr 140.2 \ft In the old testament morning sacrifice was rather more solemn, and more frequent, but the Prophet seemeth to allude unto Christ's Sacrifice, which he was to offer towards evening on the Cross; and the same also in unbloody manner, the evening before his Passion, in the Eucharist.\f* evening sacrifice.
\v 3 Set o Lord \f c \fr 140.3 \ft Ajust care and consideration what to speak, before the mouth be opened.\f* a watch to my mouth: and \f d \fr 140.3 \ft Lips must not be always stopt, for it is a sin sometimes not to speak, but as a door that is to be opened and shut at due seasons, for example, opened to confess our sins, and God's truth.\f* a door round about to my lips.
\v 4 Decline not my heart \f e \fr 140.4 \ft Shut from words of malice, and from excusing sins committed.\f* into words of malice, to make excuses in sins. With men that work iniquity: and I will not communicate with the chief of them.
\v 5 The just shall rebuke me in mercy, and \f f \fr 140.5 \ft Admonition of the just is a profitable reprehension,\f* shall reprehend me: but let not the oil of a sinner \f g \fr 140.5 \ft but the fawning of flatterers is pernicious.\f* fat my head. Because \f h \fr 140.5 \ft The Church ceaseth not to pray for sinners,\f* yet also my prayer is in their \f i \fr 140.5 \ft though as yet they take pleasure in their sins,\f* good pleasures:
\v 6 \f k \fr 140.6 \ft but the very chiefest of them, which seem most potent, if they remain obstinate to their death, shall then perish, as men thrown against rocks.\f* their judges are swallowed up joined to the rock. \f l \fr 140.6 \ft They shall then hear and understand, that the prayers of the Church\f* They shall hear my words \f m \fr 140.6 \ft are effectual, obtaining grace of constancy to her children, not to fear any persecution, nor any kind of death; obtaining also justification of their cause, when the persecuters shall see that the words and doctrine of the Church are true, and prevail in true judgement, as true, modest, peaceable, not seditious, turbulent, nor against the commonwealth.\f* because they have prevailed:
\v 7 \f n \fr 140.7 \ft As much earth sticking together is made fruitful by breaking it into small mould, so the children of the Church by persecution bring forth more fruit than before, S. Aug.\f* as the grossness of the earth is broken out upon the earth. Our \f o \fr 140.7 \ft Some persecuters are so cruel as to rage against the bones, and other relics of Martyrs,\f* bones are dissipated \f p \fr 140.7 \ft casting them into the vilest places they can, willing if they could to throw them into hell,\f* near to hell:
\v 8 \f q \fr 140.8 \ft but the Church and all her members repose confidence in God.\f* for to thee, o Lord, Lord are mine eyes; in thee have I hoped, take not away my \f 1 \fr 140.8 \ft \+it * life.\+it*\f* soul.
\v 9 Keep me from \f r \fr 140.9 \ft Therefore she prayeth that her children be not entrapped by guileful deceits,\f* the snare, which they have set for me: and from \f s \fr 140.9 \ft nor overthrown by any stumbling block cast in their way.\f* the scandals of them that work iniquity.
\v 10 \f t \fr 140.10 \ft In the end all persecuters and other wicked shall be caught in their own net of perdition,\f* Sinners shall fall in his net: \f v \fr 140.10 \ft the Church is\f* I am alone until I pass.
\c 141
\cd King David (or any other) in spiritual or temporal tribulation, not trusting in his own justice, layeth open his calamity, 5. considering God's benignity, 6. prayeth
\p
\v 1 Of \f a \fr 141.1 \ft This Psalm in form of a prayer, sheweth what cogitations David had in extreme danger.\f* understanding to David, when he was in the cave, a prayer. \x - \xt 1 Reg. 24.\x*
\v 2 With my voice I \f b \fr 141.2 \ft Not with exterior voice, for so he should have detected himself, but with fervour of spirit.\f* have cried to our Lord: with my voice I have prayed to our Lord:
\v 3 I pour out my prayer in his sight, and I pronounce my tribulation before him.
\v 4 \f c \fr 141.4 \ft When by reason of extremity I was not able to think how to help myself,\f* When my spirit faileth of myself, and \f d \fr 141.4 \ft thou o God knowing my actions and demeanor, didst deliver me.\f* thou hast known my paths. In this way, which I walked, they hid a snare for me.
\v 5 I looked toward \f e \fr 141.5 \ft Ilooked for help,\f* the right hand, and \f f \fr 141.5 \ft and diligently looked about me,\f* saw: and \f g \fr 141.5 \ft but none would seem to know me, when I required their help.\f* there was none that would know me. \f h \fr 141.5 \ft Endeavouring to save myself by flight, I found no secure place, for being in a cave or hole of a mountain, the whole army besieged me; all seek to take away my life, none to save it.\f* Flight hath failed me: and there is none to require my soul.
\v 6 \f i \fr 141.6 \ft Thus left desolate of all man's help, and destitute of all worldly shift, I cried to thee, o Lord,\f* I have cried to thee, o Lord, I have said: Thou art \f k \fr 141.6 \ft my only hopeful refuge,\f* my hope, \f l \fr 141.6 \ft neither do I desire to live for any worldly respect, but having chosen thee, o God, for my portion, and inheritance,\f* my portion \f m \fr 141.6 \ft I desire to be out of this desert place, and to be in the land, where is right use of religious divine service.\f* in the land of the living.
\v 7 Attend to my petition: because I am \f n \fr 141.7 \ft Afflicted.\f* humbled exceedingly. Deliver me from them that persecute me: because they are made strong over me.
\v 8 Bring forth my soul out of prison, \f o \fr 141.8 \ft David's desire of liberty, was especially to this end; that he might have convenient place, and other means to serve and praise God:\f* to confess unto thy name: \f p \fr 141.8 \ft the good, and well disposed people of Israel, cannot now serve thee as they desire, but expect me,\f* the just expect me, \f q \fr 141.8 \ft whom thou, o Lord, wilt advance to the Kingdom, that then we may serve thee more freely and more commodiously. All this happened in figure of Christ, of whom prophetically S. Augustin, S. Hilary, Cassianus, Cassiodorus, and others expound the whole Psalm.\f* till thou reward me.
\c 142
\cd to be speedily delivered; 11. and confidently assureth \it himself thereof.\it* \f - \ft The seventh penitential Psalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm of David, when Absalom his son persecuted him. \x - \xt 2. Reg. 17.\x* Lord hear my prayer: with thine ears receive my petition in \f a \fr 142.1 \ft God having so promised is bound by his truth\f* thy truth: hear me in \f b \fr 142.1 \ft and his justice, to hear penitents praying for remission of sins.\f* thy justice.
\v 2 And \f c \fr 142.2 \ft Deal not with me in rigor of justice,\f* enter not into judgment with thy servant: \f d \fr 142.2 \ft for no mortal man is able of himself to be justified, abstracting from God's mercy.\f* because no man living shall be justified in thy sight.
\v 3 Because the enemy \f e \fr 142.3 \ft Sought my life,\f* hath persecuted my soul: he \f f \fr 142.3 \ft and brought it into great danger. Spiritually, the devil as a roaring lion still seeking whom he may devour, hath tempted me vehemently.\f* hath humbled my life in the earth. \f g \fr 142.3 \ft Iam pressed with great calamities, temporal or spiritual.\f* He hath set me in obscure places as the dead of the world:
\v 4 and my spirit is in anguish upon me, within me my heart is troubled.
\v 5 \f h \fr 142.5 \ft In this case I consider, how God hath heretofore shewed his goodness towards me and others.\f* I was mindful of old days, I have meditated in all thy works: in the facts of thy hands did I meditate.
\v 6 I have \f i \fr 142.6 \ft Stretching forth hands a ceremony in prayer, whereby the suppliant is made more attentive, and also endureth some pain for part of satisfaction.\f* stretched forth my hands to thee: my soul is \f k \fr 142.6 \ft Man's mind without God's illumination is dry and barren.\f* as earth without water unto thee.
\v 7 Hear me \f l \fr 142.7 \ft In great tentations God's grace and help is more presently needful to prevent our weakness, lest we yield consent.\f* quickly, o Lord: my spirit hath fainted. Turn not away thy face from me: \f m \fr 142.7 \ft If God leave man without special and continual grace, he will fall\f* and I shall be like to them that descend into \f n \fr 142.7 \ft into sin, as into a deep lake from whence without help he cannot rise up again.\f* the lake.
\v 8 Make me hear thy mercy \f o \fr 142.8 \ft In the first assault of tentation.\f* in the morning: because I have hoped in thee. Make the way known to me, wherein I may walk: \f p \fr 142.8 \ft Seeing by thy grace I have begun to pray unto thee.\f* because I have lifted up my soul to thee.
\v 9 Deliver me from mine enemies, o Lord, to thee I have fled:
\v 10 teach me to do thy will, because thou art my God. Thy \f q \fr 142.10 \ft The penitent thus humbling himself and praying may assuredly trust that God doth remit his sins by the holy Sacraments, and justifieth him, protecteth him, and will bring him out of all dangers of spiritual or temporal enemies.\f* good spirit will conduct me into the right way:
\v 11 for thy name sake, o Lord, thou wilt quicken me in thine equity. Thou wilt bring forth my soul out of tribulation:
\v 12 and in thy mercy thou wilt destroy mine enemies. And thou wilt destroy all that afflict my soul: because I am thy servant.
\c 143
\cd The royal Prophet thanketh God for all his victories, & possession of the Kingdom. 3. Admiring God's benignity towards man, 5. prayeth to be still defended from all enemies, 9. promiseth a new song of praise, 11. describeth the vanity of worldly men, 15. concluding that true felicity is in serving God. \f - \ft King David praiseth God for his victories. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Psalm of David, \f a \fr 143.1 \ft Because this was David's first, and a very notable victory, the Septuagint Interpreters make mention of Goliath in this title.\f* against Goliath. Blessed be our Lord my God, who \f b \fr 143.1 \ft God made David a warrior, and victorer against Goliath, without any former training in arms.\f* teacheth my hands to battle, and my fingers to war.
\v 2 My mercy, and my refuge: my defender, and my deliverer. My protector; and I have hoped in him, who \f c \fr 143.2 \ft Made him afterwards King of a great people.\f* subdueth my people under me.
\v 3 Lord \f d \fr 143.3 \ft All mankind was unworthy before Christ,\f* what is man, that thou art \f e \fr 143.3 \ft That God should be revealed unto them,\f* made known to him? Or the son of man, \f f \fr 143.3 \ft especially that he should have care of the progeny of men, after their sin.\f* that thou esteemest him?
\v 4 \f g \fr 143.4 \ft Indeed man in himself, in his own nature and frailty, is but a vain and transitory creature, passing from life to death, as a shadow that cannot consist of itself, neither can man without God.\f* Man is made like to vanity: his days pass as a shadow.
\v 5 \f h \fr 143.5 \ft By a poetical description he prayeth for God's help, as if God should make the heavens to bow, and so descend; or make the mountains smoke, as when Moyses received the law; or declare himself by Meteors, as followeth:\f* Lord incline thy heavens, and descend: touch the mountains, and they will smoke.
\v 6 Lighten lightning, and thou shalt disperse them: shoot out thine arrows, and thou shalt destroy them.
\v 7 Send forth thy hand from on high, take me out, and deliver me from many \f i \fr 143.7 \ft Tribulations or tentations.\f* waters: from the hand \f k \fr 143.7 \ft Children of the Church,\f* of children \f l \fr 143.7 \ft but of evil life.\f* strangers.
\v 8 Whose mouth hath spoken \f m \fr 143.8 \ft They speak in vanity that promise to keep God's law, and perform it not.\f* vanity: and their right hand is the right hand of iniquity.
\v 9 O God I will sing to thee a new song: in \f n \fr 143.9 \ft In that principal instrument, apt for a new song, and for extraordinary benefits.\f* the psalter of ten strings, I will sing to thee.
\v 10 Who givest salvation to kings: who hast redeemed David thy servant from the malignant sword:
\v 11 deliver me. And rescue me out of the hand of \f o \fr 143.11 \ft Both Jews & Christians that live not well, are as strangers that frame to themselves such as false felicity, as is here described: making riches or worldly pleasures their God.\f* children strangers, whose mouth hath spoken vanity: and their right hand, is the right hand of iniquity.
\v 12 Whose sons, are as new plants in their youth. Their daughters comely trimmed: decked about after the similitude of a temple.
\v 13 Their store-houses full, flowing out of this into that. Their ewes full of young, abounding in their going forth:
\v 14 their oxen are fat. There is no ruin of wall, nor \f 1 \fr 143.14 \ft \+it * transmigration.\+it*\f* passage, nor cry in their streets.
\v 15 They have said, that it is a happy people, which hath these things: \f p \fr 143.15 \ft True happiness consisteth not in worldly things.\f* blessed is the people, whose \f q \fr 143.15 \ft But in preferring God before all.\f* God is our Lord.
\c 144
\cd God is, and for ever ought to be praised, 3. for his immensive, infinite, glorious Majesty, marvelous works, merciful benefits; for his power, wisdom, justice, 19. who will reward the good, and destroy the wicked. \f - \ft God's Majesty excelleth all things. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Praising, \f a \fr 144.1 \ft By this title Esdras signifieth that the Holy Ghost, who indited all the Psalms to God's praise, more specially in these seven last suggested to David, & by him to all God's servants, that all their other service must ten and be directed to the praise of God: and that therein we must continue, and finally rest, as in the sabbath of the seventh day, signified (as S. Beda supposeth) by these seven last Psalms of praise, eternally praising our Lord God. For\f* to David himself. I will exalt thee my God \f b \fr 144.1 \ft King is the proper epitheton of Christ, the Son of God, to whom, in his humanity, God the Father promised the Church of all nations for his kingdom. \+it Psal. 2.\+it* in whom also the whole Blessed Trinity is praised.\f* the King: and I will bless thy name \f c \fr 144.1 \ft All the time of this world they praise God,\f* for ever and \f d \fr 144.1 \ft & after in eternity.\f* for ever. \ef - \fr 144.1 \fq will exalt thee. \ft As this Psalm is the first of the seven, which contain more particular instruction of perpetually praising God: ¹ so it is the seventh of those, which are composed in order of the Alphabet, to wit, the 24. 33. 36. 110. 111. 118. and this 144, Of which the three former want some letters: signifying (as Cassiodorus interpreteth) such in God's Church, as sing his praises, but with some imperfections: the other four have the perfect Alphabet, signifying those, that sing God's praises with perfect devotion. Which only four S. Jerome calleth Alphabetical Psalms. \+it Epist. ad Paulam Urbicam, & Prooem. in Lament. Jerem.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The seven last Psalms pertain more specially to praises. This Psalm and other six are composed in order of the Alphabet. It is probable that the Hebrew text now wanteth a verse in this Psalm. And therefore is not more certain than the Greek or Latin.\ef*
\v 2 Every day will I bless thee: and will praise thy name for ever, and for ever and ever.
\v 3 Great is our Lord and exceeding laudable, and of his greatness there is no end.
\v 4 Generation and generation shall praise thy works: and they shall pronounce thy power.
\v 5 They shall speak the magnificence of the glory of thy holiness: and shall tell thy marvelous works.
\v 6 And they shall tell the force of thy \f e \fr 144.6 \ft Of wonderful and miraculous things, which strike terror into men's minds.\f* terrible things: and shall declare thy greatness:
\v 7 They shall utter the memory of the aboundance of thy sweetness: and in thy justice they shall rejoice.
\v 8 Our Lord is pitiful and merciful: patient and very merciful.
\v 9 Our Lord is sweet to all: and his \f f \fr 144.9 \ft The effects of God's mercy in redeeming, and recalling sinners, are eminent above all other works.\f* commiserations are over all his works.
\v 10 Let all thy works, o Lord, confess to thee: and let thy \f g \fr 144.10 \ft Therefore the sanctified have special cause to praise\f* Saints bless thee.
\v 11 They shall tell the glory of thy kingdom: and shall speak thy might.
\v 12 That they may make thy might known to the children of men: and the glory of the magnificence of thy kingdom.
\v 13 Thy kingdom is a kingdom \f h \fr 144.13 \ft Christ's kingdom the militant Church is magnified, but much more the triumphant which is eternal.\f* of all worlds: and thy dominion in all generation and generation. Our Lord is faithful in all his words, and holy in all his works. \ef - \fr 144.13 \fq Our Lord is faithful. \ft This verse is not now in the ordinary Hebrew text, and therefore either the same is defective, or else this Psalm should seem not to be composed with a perfect Alphabet in the fountain tongue. For here it wanteth the letter Nun. But seeing S. Jerome counteth this one of the four Alphabetical Psalms, omitting the other three, which consist of unperfect Alphabets, it is very probable that this verse was once in the Hebrew text, as it is both in Greek & Latin. Whereby amongst other places appeareth, that there is no certainty to correct the Greek or Latin Bible by the Hebrew, which is now extant; but rather by them, that may be supplied, which the Hebrew wanteth.\ef*
\v 14 Our Lord \f i \fr 144.14 \ft God is ready of his part to lift up all.\f* lifteth up all that fall: and setteth up all that are bruised.
\v 15 The eyes of all hope in thee, o Lord: and thou givest their meat in time convenient.
\v 16 Thou openest thy hand: and fillest \f k \fr 144.16 \ft He giveth necessary things to all living creatures, even to brute beasts.\f* every living creature with blessing.
\v 17 Our Lord is just in all his ways: and holy in all his works.
\v 18 Our Lord is near to all that invocate him: to all that invocate him in truth.
\v 19 He will do the will of them that fear him, and will hear their prayer, and save them.
\v 20 Our Lord keepeth all that love him: and he will destroy all sinners.
\v 21 My mouth shall speak the praise of our Lord: and let all flesh bless his holy name for ever, and for ever and ever.
\c 145
\cd God is also to be praised by his peculiar people, for particular benefits, 4. & for his omnipotent power, wisdom, goodness, in creating and governing this whole world, 11. and most special benignity towards those that trust in him. \f - \ft God's excellency in creating and governing the world. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia, \f a \fr 145.1 \ft The Septuagint added the names of these two Prophets, for the like reason as they added Jeremy, \+it Psal. 136.\+it* because Aggæus and Zacharias prophesying in the reduction of the people from captivity, exhorted them, as here the Psalmist doth, to trust in God's providence, and prefer his service before worldly cares.\f* of Aggæus and Zacharie.
\v 2 My soul praise thou our Lord, I will praise our Lord in my life: I will sing to my God as long as I shall be. Put not confidence in Princes:
\v 3 in \f b \fr 145.3 \ft In one Son of Man (Christ) is salvation (saith S. Augustin) and in him, not because he is the son of man, but because he is the Son of God.\f* the sons of men: in whom there is no salvation.
\v 4 His spirit shall go forth, and he shall return into \f c \fr 145.4 \ft He saith not that the spirit or soul shall turn into the earth, but the soul shall depart from the body, and so every one (in whom worldly men put their trust) shall according to the body, return into his earth.\f* his earth: in that day all their cogitations shall perish.
\v 5 Blessed is he, whose helper is the God of Jacob, his hope in our Lord his God:
\v 6 who made heaven and earth, the sea, and all things that are in them.
\v 7 Which keepeth truth for ever, doth judgment for them that suffer wrong: giveth food to the hungry. Our Lord looseth the fettered:
\v 8 our Lord illuminateth the blind. Our Lord lifteth up the bruised, our Lord loveth the just.
\v 9 Our Lord keepeth strangers, the pupil and widow he will receive: and the ways of sinners he will destroy.
\v 10 Our Lord will reign for ever thy God, o Sion in generation & generation.
\c 146
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Praise ye our Lord because Psalm \f a \fr 146.1 \ft It is good to sing Psalms of praise to God.\f* is good: to our God let there be pleasant and comely praise.
\v 2 Our Lord building up Jerusalem: \f b \fr 146.2 \ft A prophecy of the restoration of Jerusalem after the captivity.\f* will gather together the dispersions of Israel.
\v 3 Who \f c \fr 146.3 \ft Remitteth sins to the penitent.\f* healeth the contrite of heart: and bindeth up their sores.
\v 4 Who \f d \fr 146.4 \ft Besides experience of every one that shall behold the firmament in a clear night, the holy Scripture \+it (Gen. 15. v. 5.)\+it* sheweth, that the stars are innumerable to man. For albeit Ptolomey and other Astronomers numbereth certain more notorious stars, which serve especially for some knowledge in the science of Astronomy, numbering \+it 349.\+it* such in the Zodiach; \+it 316.\+it* in the South part thereof; and \+it 360.\+it* on the North part, which are in all \+it 1025.\+it* Yet all acknowledge that no man can come near to any probable conjecture of the whole number, nor is able to attain any perfect knowledge of their natural influences, and special proprieties. And therefore the Psalmist proposeth here the admirable and unsearchable knowledge of God: who both most exactly knoweth the number,\f* numbereth the multitude of stars: & \f e \fr 146.4 \ft and so perfectly their nature, that his divine Omniscience giveth to every star a proper name, according to their singular differences and proprieties.\f* giveth names to them all.
\v 5 Great is our Lord, and great is his strength: and of his wisdom there is no \f f \fr 146.5 \ft Things subject to God's knowledge and wisdom are innumerable.\f* number.
\v 6 Our Lord receiving the meek: & humbling sinners even to the ground.
\v 7 Sing ye to our Lord in confession: sing ye to our God on harp.
\v 8 Who \f g \fr 146.8 \ft All these and the like benefits do shew God's incomparable greatness, wisdom, and goodness.\f* covereth the heaven with clouds: and prepareth rain for the earth. Who bringeth forth grass in the mountains: and herb for the service of men.
\v 9 Who giveth to beasts their food: and to \f h \fr 146.9 \ft Both sacred and profane authors testify, that ravens seeing their young ones, either without feathers, or to have whitish unlike to theirs, as suspecting that they are not their own birds, but of some other kind, leave them destitute of meat; therefore God the author of nature, and conserver of all kinds of creatures, by his special providence, feedeth them: either by a certain dew hanging near them in the air, as Isidorus supposeth; or by little beasts, or flies, sent by God's providence, which they catching into their mouths, are nourished and brought up as S. Chrysostom teacheth, ser. in Heliam; or by what other means soever, all agree that young ravens are neglected by their parents, and are fed marvelously by God's ordinance; by which example the Psalmist sheweth, that much more God hath care of men: especially of such men (saith S. Chrysostom.) as honour him with hymns and praises,\f* the young ravens that call upon him.
\v 10 He shall not have pleasure in the strength of an horse: nor in the legs of a man shall he be well pleased.
\v 11 Our Lord is well pleased toward them that fear him: and in them that hope upon his mercy.
\c 147
\cd Again God is to be praised for his goodness towards his peculiar people, 4. yea towards all the world: but most aboundantly towards his Church. \f - \ft God's providence especially towards the Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. O \f a \fr 147.1 \ft Jerusalem in the latter part of David's time, all the time of Salomon, and part of other Kings' reigns till the captivity, had peace & prospered. Again after the captivity, the City was repaired, the Temple re-edified, and the whole land received and enjoyed many blessings. But all this was no more than a figure of the excellent benefits here prophesied, and more evidently verified in Christ's Catholic Church: partly here militant in the whole world, & especially in the glorious Jerusalem, and Sion, the perfect vision of peace, and contemplation of God in eternal life.\f* Jerusalem praise our Lord: praise thy God, o Sion. \f 1 \fr 147.1 \ft The Hebrews join this Psalm with the precedent.\f*
\v 2 Because he \f b \fr 147.2 \ft In comparison of other cities and peoples of the world, the gates of Jerusalem were strongly fenced,\f* hath strengthened the locks of thy gates: he \f c \fr 147.2 \ft and the citizens blessed: much more the Church of Christ is built upon a sure rock, her faithful children endued with all spiritual graces; and most of all, heaven it-self is free from all danger of calamity, and the Saints are most secure & most happy, enjoying eternal fruition of God.\f* hath blessed thy children in thee.
\v 3 Who \f d \fr 147.3 \ft Hath given peace in thy borders,\f* hath set thy borders peace: and filleth thee with \f e \fr 147.3 \ft and the very best corn, and all other fruits: wine, oil, milk, honey, and the rest. Allegorically in the Church, reconciliation with God, by remission of sins, & peace of conscience, in the Sacraments of Baptism & Penance; with the most spiritual food of Christ's Body & Blood in the Eucharist, and graces of other Sacraments. In heaven most assured peace and joy without end.\f* the fat of corn.
\v 4 Who sendeth forth \f f \fr 147.4 \ft This pertaineth most specially to Evangelical doctrine, preached\f* his speech to the earth: his word \f g \fr 147.4 \ft and quickly received in all the world. \+it Rom. 10. v. 18.\+it*\f* runneth swiftly.
\v 5 Who giveth \f h \fr 147.5 \ft Snow nourisheth the earth, making it warm by Antiperistasis, as is evident in natural Philosophy,\f* snow as wool: scattereth \f i \fr 147.5 \ft and noisome air is changed into clear weather. In the Church by penance & austere life men are purged from sins and vices, evil spirits are also driven away. If your sins shall be as scarlet, they shall be made white as snow, and if they be red as vermilion, they shall be white as wool. \+it Isaie 1. v. 18.\+it*\f* mist as ashes.
\v 6 He casteth \f k \fr 147.6 \ft Yea some that are hardened in sins, as ice or crystal, shall be melted, broken, or made fit to be engrafted in God's Church. So S. Peter was admonished by a vision \+it (Act. 10. v. 13.)\+it* to kill and eat.\f* his crystal as morsels: before the face of his cold \f l \fr 147.6 \ft Otherwise without God's grace giving remorse and sorrow, no man can overcome his own vices.\f* who shall endure?
\v 7 He \f m \fr 147.7 \ft But God's word preached,\f* shall send forth his word, and shall melt them: \f n \fr 147.7 \ft and his grace touching men's hearts,\f* his spirit shall blow, and \f o \fr 147.7 \ft innumerable are converted.\f* waters shall flow.
\v 8 Who declareth his word to \f p \fr 147.8 \ft The Church only enjoyeth these spiritual benefits.\f* Jacob: his justices and judgments to Israel.
\v 9 He hath \f q \fr 147.9 \ft Considering that all mankind was in the mass of sin, and that God letteth many justly perish, those to whom he giveth his grace to justification, are specially bound to praise him.\f* not done in like manner to any nation: and his judgements he hath not made manifest to them. \f r \fr 147.9 \ft And therefore the Prophet concludeth this Psalm, and the rest following, with Alleluia.\f* Alleluia.
\c 148
\cd All creatures spiritual and corporal, are invited to praise God, their Creator and Conserver, 13. as incomparably excellent. \f - \ft Our Creator to be praised by all creatures. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Praise ye our Lord \f a \fr 148.1 \ft All ye heavenly spirits praise God for the excellency of your nature.\f* from the heavens: praise ye him in the high places.
\v 2 Praise ye him all his Angels: praise ye him \f b \fr 148.2 \ft And for your innumerable multitude.\f* all his hosts:
\v 3 Praise ye him \f c \fr 148.3 \ft All creatures, wanting sense or reason, shew forth the Majesty and excellency of their Creator.\f* sun and moon: praise him all ye stars, and light.
\v 4 Praise him ye heavens of heavens: and the waters that are above the heavens,
\v 5 let them praise the name of our Lord. Because he said, and they were made: he commanded, and they were created.
\v 6 He established them for ever, and for ever and ever: he put a precept, and it shall not pass.
\v 7 Praise our Lord from the earth: ye dragons, and all depths.
\v 8 Fire, hail, snow, ice, spirit of storms: which do his word:
\v 9 Mountains, and all little hills: trees that bear fruit, and all cedars.
\v 10 Beasts, and all cattle: serpents, and feathered fowls:
\v 11 \f d \fr 148.11 \ft Again God is to be praised for the diversity of states in men, whereby the whole community is conserved & governed.\f* Kings of the earth, & all peoples: Princes, and all judges of the earth.
\v 12 Young men and virgins: old with young let them praise the name of our Lord:
\v 13 because the name of him \f e \fr 148.13 \ft God only, & no false imagined god made & disposed all things in order.\f* alone is exalted.
\v 14 The confession of him above heaven and earth: and he hath exalted \f f \fr 148.14 \ft More especially for that God hath so fortified his Church.\f* the horn of his people. An hymn to all his \f g \fr 148.14 \ft Sanctified children,\f* saints: to the children of Israel a people \f h \fr 148.14 \ft that by grace and free will, which he giveth them, approach unto him.\f* approaching unto him. \f i \fr 148.14 \ft All this considered the Psalmist concluding with Alleluia, inviteth all to praise Our Lord.\f* Alleluia.
\c 149
\cd God absolutely most excellent is to be praised, 3. with all sorts of instruments, and by all other means. \f - \ft God most excellent and most laudable. The first key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Sing ye to our Lord a new song: let his praise be in the \f a \fr 149.1 \ft God our Lord whom all creatures are bound to praise, only accepteth those men's praises, that live in his holy Church.\f* Church of saints.
\v 2 Let Israel be joyful in him, that made him: and let the children of Sion rejoice in their King.
\v 3 Let them praise his name in choir: on timbrel and psalter let them sing to him.
\v 4 Because our Lord is well pleased in his people: and he will exalt the meek unto salvation.
\v 5 The saints shall rejoice in glory: they shall be joyful in \f b \fr 149.5 \ft Eternal rest.\f* their beds.
\v 6 The \f c \fr 149.6 \ft Highest praises of God shall be continually uttered by Saints in glory: for still as praises pass by their tongues and mouths, more like praises shall succeed from their throat, and heart affection, so out of the aboundance of the heart their mouth shall still speak God's praise.\f* exaltations of God in their throat: and \f d \fr 149.6 \ft Glorified Saints shall also have judiciary power. First, all in general shall like and approve God's justice in punishing the wicked. Secondly, the wicked shall be justly condemned in comparison of the blessed, who passed through and overcame the like, yea and greater tribulations and tentations than those by which the damned were overcome. Thirdly, certain more excellent Saints, namely the Apostles and all those that forsaking propriety of temporal goods, give that they have to the poor, as some religious Orders do; or into a Community as the Apostles, and many primitive Christians did \+it (Mat. 4. v. 20. cap. 19. v. 27. Act. 4. v. 34. 1. Cor. 6. v. 3.)\+it* shall sit in judgement seats, assessory judges with Christ,\f* two edged swords in their hands.
\v 7 To do revenge in the nations: chastisements among the peoples.
\v 8 To bind their kings in fetters: and their nobles in iron manacles.
\v 9 That they may do in them the judgement that is \f e \fr 149.9 \ft Decreed by God.\f* written: \f f \fr 149.9 \ft This judicial power is part of Saint's glory.\f* this glory is to all his saints. Alleluia.
\c 150
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. Praise ye our Lord \f a \fr 150.1 \ft All ye Angels and men that are in the holy and highest\f* in his holies: praise ye him in \f b \fr 150.1 \ft All ye creatures that are in and under the first moveable firmament, praise our Lord.\f* the firmament of his strength.
\v 2 Praise ye him in \f c \fr 150.2 \ft And you especially (God's peculiar people) amongst whom and for whom divine miracles have been wrought, praise our Lord,\f* his powers: praise ye him \f d \fr 150.2 \ft with all your possible endeavour, for though his infinite Excellency exceedeth the power of all creatures to praise him sufficiently, yet it resteth that you may infinitely extend your will and desire to praise our Lord, according to the multitude of his greatness.\f* according to the multitude of his greatness.
\v 3 \f e \fr 150.3 \ft Out of this your great and infinite desire, let your tongues sound and sing divine praises, as well with voice, as musical instruments.\f* Praise ye him \f f \fr 150.3 \ft Whereof six most usual in the Tabernacle and Temple were these: Trumpet, Psalter, Harp, Timbrel, Organ, and Cymbal.\f* in the sound of trumpet: praise ye him on psalter and harp.
\v 4 Praise ye him on timbrel and \f g \fr 150.4 \ft By the way the Psalmist interposeth again two especial things, which make perfect harmony, without which no instrument is grateful to God: Unity amongst his servants, signified by the Quire of consonant voices:\f* choir: praise ye him on \f h \fr 150.4 \ft and mortification of passions, signified by Strings, which are made of dead beast's bowels.\f* strings and organ.
\v 5 Praise ye him on well sounding cymbals: praise ye him on cymbals of jubilation:
\v 6 let every \f i \fr 150.6 \ft Man created of corruptible body and immortal soul, is finally admonished to praise our Lord, over and above the praises of all other corporal creatures; who also is more especially bound thereto than Angels, because God hath vouchsafed to make himself man, to redeem man that was lost by sin, and to endow him with new grace, and so bring him to everlasting glory, where with holy Angels men also for ever & ever shall praise our Lord, with heart, voice, and jubilation of spirit, singing as the Psalmist concludeth, Alleluia.\f* spirit praise our Lord. Alleluia.
\s1 S. Augustin in the conclusion of his Enarrations upon the Psalms.
\ip S. Augustin in the conclusion of his Enarrations or Sermons upon the Psalms, explicateth a mystery in the number of an hundred and fifty, signifying the concord of the two Testaments. For in the old testament they kept the Sabbath, which is the seventh day: in \f - \ft The number of Psalms signifieth the agreement of the old and new Testament.\f* the new we keep our Lord's day, after the sabbath, that is, the eighth: which seven and eight (making fifteen) multiplied by ten, signifying the Law of ten commandments, rise unto \it 150.\it*
\ip Again, seven multiplied by seven make \it 49.\it* whereto one (to wit the eight) being added make fifty, which multiplied by three , signifying the B. Trinity, make \it 150.\it* Neither \f - \ft Three fifties signify Penance. Mercy with justice, and Praises of God.\f* seemed it without cause to this great Doctor, that the first fifty end with a Psalm of Penance, craving mercy & remission of sins: the second with Mercy & Justice, which God joineth in the Redemption, Justification, and Salvation of men: the last with Divine Praises, signifying, that by condemning sins in ourselves, through God's mercy we may be justified, and so begin in this life, which is to be perfected in the next, to praise our Lord, as S. Paul admonisheth with Psalms, Hymns, and Spiritual Songs. Concluding with the two verses, appointed by S. Damasus Pope, to be added in the end of all Psalms, and is observed ever since his time by tradition in the whole Church. \f - \ft \+it Colloss. 3. Circa An. Do 380.\+it*\f*
\ip Glory to the Father, and to the Son, and to the Holy Ghost: As it was in the beginning, and now, and ever, into worlds of worlds. \it (in eternity without end.)\it* Amen. \f - \ft Gloria Patri added by tradition.\f*
